; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; checker — the type half of the reference's internal/checker.
;
; ── What this module is FOR, and what a slice of it is ──────────────────────
;
; The checker answers two questions about a bound tree, and the fifth yardstick
; compares both (tests/oracle/types.go, TESTPLAN.md's *The fifth yardstick*):
;
;   C  the SEMANTIC DIAGNOSTICS of one file — GetDiagnostics, which runs the
;      whole check of the file and answers out of the checker's own sorted list
;   T  the TYPE of every node the reference's own baseline walker may ask about —
;      GetTypeAtLocation plus TypeToString
;
; Neither is reachable through any earlier artifact, which is why the yardstick is
; a fifth one rather than a section of the fourth: a type is not in the tree and
; not on a symbol. It is computed on demand, memoised inside the checker and never
; written back, so a port that computes NO types and a port that computes WRONG
; ones compare exactly equal on the first four, in both directions.
;
; ── The dimension's size, and what that decides ─────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ internal/checker is 59 971 lines against the 12 540 of scanner + parser +
; binder — measured at slice 46, which projects 88 000–141 000 lines of Scaly, two
; to three times the whole port to date. So this file will be built over many
; slices, and the SKELETON's job is to make every one of them an ARM: the type
; representation, the intrinsic types, the printer's keyword arms, the file-level
; check, the diagnostic list with the reference's sort, and — the part that earns
; its keep — a REPORT at every arm that is not ported, so tests/triage.py names
; the arm that unlocks the most units next. That is the binder's arrangement
; (§3.5bw) one dimension over.
;
; ── What is NOT ported, and why each omission is a decision ─────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FLOW GRAPH IS NOT PORTED, AND IT IS THE CHECKER THAT NEEDS IT. The
; binder's own header says so — it builds none, and *its reader is the checker* —
; and this file is that reader arriving. Two things depend on it. Reachability:
; checkSourceElementWorker begins with checkSourceElementUnreachable, which asks
; either NodeFlagsUnreachable (a flag the binder sets from currentFlow and this
; port does not set) or the node's FlowNode (which does not exist here), so a port
; without it cannot know that a statement is dead and would silently produce no
; TS7027. Narrowing: every identifier READ goes through getFlowTypeOfReference, so
; the T section of any expression that mentions a variable depends on it — and
; TS2454 *used before being assigned* is the corpus's second most common
; diagnostic at 6 462. Both are therefore reported rather than approximated: an
; approximated answer here is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, which is
; the class §3.5bw records for is_in_top_level_context.
;
; ★★ THE RELATER AND THE NODEBUILDER ARE NOT PORTED EITHER, and each is a
; dimension rather than a gap. relater.go (5 006 lines) is assignability, which
; even `var x: string = 1` needs; nodebuilderimpl.go (3 585) is the type PRINTER,
; of which this file ports the keyword arms only — the twenty lines of
; typeToTypeNodeHelper that answer an INTRINSIC — because those are the only type
; shapes anything here can produce.
;
; ★★★ AND THE COUPLING THE SKELETON MEASURED, because it decides how this
; dimension advances: THE C SECTION GATES THE T SECTION. One dump is one
; comparison, so a unit counts as matched only when both halves agree — and the C
; half is complete only when the check of EVERY statement in the unit is ported.
; Measured over the whole corpus: 7 300 of 17 810 units — 41 % — have an EMPTY C
; section, and not one of them can be claimed without porting the checks that PROVE
; it empty. So the
; 1 230 663 type answers of the corpus wait behind its 63 127 diagnostics, and the
; checker dimension advances at the speed of checkSourceElement. ★The escape hatch
; is named here rather than taken: the ORACLE's output would not change by one
; byte if the comparison were SPLIT into two yardsticks (C and T), because the two
; sections are already separate lines — it is compare.py, triage.py, run.sh and
; ctl.sh that would each gain a column (slice 46's rule: all four in one commit,
; or the first battery lies). That is a harness decision with a measured argument
; behind it, and it belongs to whichever slice finds the coupling is actually
; holding the type machinery back.
;
; ── What the harness fixes, and what our side therefore inherits ────────────
;
; The oracle builds a STUB program: no module resolution, the per-case
; @target/@lib/@module directives ignored, and the lib set fixed to lib.es5.d.ts
; (priced at slice 46 as a 3 % diagnostic delta against the reference's default
; closure). Two of those three are symmetric for free here, because this port has
; no program layer at all. The third is not: this side reads NO lib, so every
; global name is unresolved — which is invisible while no arm resolves a name, and
; is the first thing the slice that ports name resolution has to answer. It is
; written down here so that slice does not discover it as a diff.
;
; ★ compilerOptions: the harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation
; and nothing else, so every option this file would read is at its default —
; strictNullChecks false, exactOptionalPropertyTypes false, noImplicitAny false,
; NoUnusedLocals/NoUnusedParameters false (hence checkUnused false at
; getDiagnostics). Each place that matters says which option it is reading and
; what its default answers, so that an options record arriving later is a
; substitution rather than a search.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.String
use scaly.containers.StringBuilder
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.ast.Symbol
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.binder.Binder
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList

; ── ObjectFlags: two bits, deliberately not a table ─────────────────────────
;
; ★★ The reference's ObjectFlags is a 40-entry uint32 enum in
; internal/checker/types.go, and it is NOT generated beside TypeFlags for a reason
; that is about the enum rather than about effort: its bit positions are REUSED.
; `1 << 22` is ObjectFlagsContainsSpread on a type with TypeFlagsObject,
; ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed on a union or intersection, and the comments in
; the source are what say which — so a flat table of names would read as 40
; independent bits and be wrong in exactly the way a reader cannot see. It also
; carries a FORWARD reference (ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask names
; ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType, declared below it), which gentypeflags.py
; would refuse rather than resolve.
;
; So the two bits the intrinsic block below actually SETS are declared here, with
; their positions from the source, and the table arrives with the slice that needs
; the object types — where the reuse discipline can be ported with it.
define ObjectFlagsNone: int 0

; ── the bits that require TypeFlagsObject, in the reference's own order ──────
;
; ★★ SLICE 66 BRINGS THE TABLE THE NOTE ABOVE PROMISED, and it stops where the
; reuse begins. Bits 0..21 mean one thing each; `1 << 22` and up are the reused
; positions the note names, and they are NOT declared, because the name would be
; the lie the note describes. A slice that needs ContainsSpread declares it beside
; the reader that establishes which TypeFlags it is reading it under.
define ObjectFlagsClass: int 1 << 0                   ; Class
define ObjectFlagsInterface: int 1 << 1               ; Interface
define ObjectFlagsReference: int 1 << 2               ; Generic type reference
define ObjectFlagsTuple: int 1 << 3                   ; Synthesized generic tuple type
define ObjectFlagsAnonymous: int 1 << 4               ; Anonymous
define ObjectFlagsMapped: int 1 << 5                  ; Mapped
define ObjectFlagsInstantiated: int 1 << 6            ; Instantiated anonymous or mapped type
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteral: int 1 << 7           ; Originates in an object literal
define ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray: int 1 << 8           ; Evolving array type
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteralPatternWithComputedProperties: int 1 << 9
define ObjectFlagsReverseMapped: int 1 << 10          ; Object contains a property from a reverse-mapped type
define ObjectFlagsJsxAttributes: int 1 << 11          ; Jsx attributes type
define ObjectFlagsJSLiteral: int 1 << 12              ; Object type declared in JS
define ObjectFlagsFreshLiteral: int 1 << 13           ; Fresh object literal
define ObjectFlagsArrayLiteral: int 1 << 14           ; Originates in an array literal
define ObjectFlagsPrimitiveUnion: int 1 << 15         ; Union of only primitive types
define ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType: int 1 << 16   ; Type is or contains undefined or null widening type
define ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral: int 1 << 17 ; Type is or contains object literal type
define ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType: int 1 << 18      ; Type is or contains anyFunctionType or silentNeverType
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariablesComputed: int 1 << 19
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariables: int 1 << 20
define ObjectFlagsMembersResolved: int 1 << 21        ; Members have been resolved

define ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface: int ObjectFlagsClass | ObjectFlagsInterface

; ★ ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags, whose only reader is getPropagatingFlagsOfTypes.
; ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask is still NOT declared: it NAMES two of the reused
; bits, so it cannot be written before they are.
define ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral | ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType

; ★ ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening arrives in slice 69, where getWidenedType is its
; reader — the note above listed it as having none. It is the gate that decides
; whether widening has anything to do at all.
define ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral

; ── WideningKind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's four-member iota, declared WHOLE because reportImplicitAny
; branches on GeneratorYield and every caller names one of the four. Slice 69
; passes Normal from its single call site; the other three arrive with the return
; type and the generator.
define WideningKindNormal: int 0
define WideningKindFunctionReturn: int 1
define WideningKindGeneratorNext: int 2
define WideningKindGeneratorYield: int 3

; ── CheckMode (slice 70) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's eight-member flag set, declared WHOLE for WideningKind's
; reason: `checkExpressionEx`'s two gates test Inferential and
; SkipGenericFunctions by NAME, `checkExpressionCachedEx` branches on Normal, and
; every caller in the reference names one of the eight — so a partial table would
; make the next arm's test read as a new decision rather than as a transcription.
;
; ★★ ONLY `Normal` IS EVER PASSED HERE, and that is a fact about the CALLERS
; rather than about this table: every call this port makes comes from a statement
; arm or from a declaration's initializer, i.e. from the reference's own
; `checkExpression` / `checkExpressionCached`, both of which pass Normal. The four
; modes that change an answer (Contextual, Inferential, SkipContextSensitive,
; SkipGenericFunctions) are set only by the contextual-typing and inference
; machinery, which reports. ★That is what makes the two gates in
; check_expression_ex provably inert rather than merely quiet — see there.
define CheckModeNormal: int 0
define CheckModeContextual: int 1 << 0            ; Explicitly assigned contextual type, therefore not cacheable
define CheckModeInferential: int 1 << 1           ; Inferential typing
define CheckModeSkipContextSensitive: int 1 << 2  ; Skip context sensitive function expressions
define CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions: int 1 << 3  ; Skip single signature generic functions
define CheckModeIsForSignatureHelp: int 1 << 4    ; Call resolution for purposes of signature help
define CheckModeRestBindingElement: int 1 << 5    ; Checking a type used to determine a rest binding element's type
define CheckModeTypeOnly: int 1 << 6              ; Called from getTypeOfExpression, diagnostics may be omitted
define CheckModeForceTuple: int 1 << 7

; ── the type payloads ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ SLICE 66 IS THE SECOND PAYLOAD, so the union the note below promised
; arrives with it. The reference's hierarchy is four levels of Go embedding —
; `InterfaceType` embeds `TypeReference` embeds `ObjectType` embeds
; `StructuredType` — and §3.1's sum type flattens it: each arm is ONE record
; carrying the slots its own readers ask for, exactly as `NodeData`'s 134 arms do.
;
; ★★★ WHICH SLOTS ARE HERE IS A CLAIM ABOUT READERS, NOT ABOUT THE REFERENCE'S
; STRUCT. §3.5be's rule: a field nobody reads is a claim about the future. Every
; slot below is read by a function this slice ports; every slot NOT here is named
; with the reader that would make it observable, at the arm it belongs to.

; ObjectType.instantiations, as an ASSOCIATION LIST rather than a map — and the
; divergence is worth stating because it makes the port STRONGER than the
; reference here. Upstream keys `map[CacheHashKey]*Type` on `getTypeListKey`, an
; xxh3 hash of the type ids; a hash is what buys O(1) lookup and it buys a
; collision question with it. This slice's lists are one entry long for a
; non-generic class or interface, so a linear scan comparing the ARGUMENT LISTS
; THEMSELVES is both faster and exact — there is no key to collide.
;
; ★ The successor is named: the slice that instantiates generics is the one whose
; lists get long enough for the hash to be worth its collision argument, and it is
; the slice that must port `getTypeListKey` and `keyBuilder` with it.
define TypeInstantiation
(
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(a: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, t: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set arguments: a
        set instantiated: t
    }
}

; The intrinsics, which carry no payload at all. It exists as an arm rather than
; as a null `data` so that every Type answers a `choose` — the shape ast.scaly's
; TokenData has, for the same reason.
define NoTypeData ()

; ObjectType, the reference's plain anonymous object — newObjectType's last arm
; and, in slice 67, the STATIC TYPE OF A CLASS. It carries no slots at all, which
; is a statement about readers rather than an omission: everything the
; reference's ObjectType holds (`members`, `properties`, `callSignatures`,
; `constructSignatures`, `indexInfos`) is written by resolveStructuredTypeMembers
; and read by getPropertiesOfType / getSignaturesOfType / getIndexInfosOfType,
; all three of which report here — and `target` / `mapper` belong to the
; INSTANTIATED anonymous type, whose reader is instantiateType.
;
; ★ It is an arm of its own rather than NoTypeData because the two are different
; answers to the same `choose`: NoTypeData means an INTRINSIC, which is what the
; reference's `AsObjectType()` would refuse to give. An empty arm that can be
; told apart is worth more than a shared one that cannot.
define AnonymousTypeData ()

; InterfaceType, flattened. The first three slots are the embedded ObjectType's
; and TypeReference's; the last three are InterfaceType's own.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, each with its reader: `mapper` (instantiateType), `node`
; (getTypeArguments' deferred path, which is unreachable while
; resolved_type_arguments is set at construction), and `declaredMembersResolved` /
; `declaredMembers` / `declaredCallSignatures` / `declaredConstructSignatures` /
; `declaredIndexInfos` (resolveDeclaredMembers, behind checkIndexConstraints).
;
; ★★ THE THREE BASE SLOTS BELOW ARRIVED IN SLICE 67, AND SLICE 66 HAD NAMED THIS
; SLICE AS THEIR READER. The line above used to carry `baseTypesResolved` /
; `resolvedBaseTypes` / `resolvedBaseConstructorType` with the reader
; "getBaseTypes, which is where BOTH call sites of this slice now stop" — so the
; deferral names its own expiry date, and this is it. A limit that is fixed and
; still written down as a limit is worse than no note at all.
define InterfaceTypeData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
    resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]?

    ; allTypeParameters is outer + local + thisType, in that order, and the two
    ; counts below are what slice the three apart — which is why
    ; outer_type_parameter_count is carried even though only TypeParameters()
    ; reads the array in this slice.
    all_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    outer_type_parameter_count: int
    this_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getBaseTypes' memo and its two answers. `base_types_resolved` is a BIT
    ; rather than "the list is non-null", because the reference's answer for an
    ; interface with no `extends` is an EMPTY list and asking again is what the
    ; bit prevents — a null list would make the memo miss for exactly the 97 % of
    ; the corpus that has no base type at all.
    base_types_resolved: bool
    resolved_base_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass' memo. `undefined_type` is a real answer here
    ; (the class has no `extends`), which is why the reference tests it against
    ; nil rather than keeping a second bit.
    resolved_base_constructor_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init()
    {
        set target: null
        set resolved_type_arguments: null
        set instantiations: null
        set all_type_parameters: null
        set outer_type_parameter_count: 0
        set this_type: null
        set base_types_resolved: false
        set resolved_base_types: null
        set resolved_base_constructor_type: null
    }
}

; TypeReference — createTypeReferenceEx's product. A SEPARATE record rather than a
; shared one, on §3.5n's rule: the slot lists are not identical, and an arm that
; borrowed InterfaceTypeData would carry three slots whose absence of a writer is
; exactly what tells a reader it is not an interface.
define TypeReferenceData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
    resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)
{
    init(tg: ref[Type]?, args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        set target: tg
        set resolved_type_arguments: args
    }
}

; TypeParameter. `constraint` is written by getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface for
; the `this` type and read by nothing yet; `is_this_type` is IsThisType()'s slot.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with readers: `target` and `mapper` (instantiateType),
; `resolvedDefaultType` (getDefaultFromTypeParameter).
define TypeParameterData
(
    constraint: ref[Type]?
    is_this_type: bool
)
{
    init()
    {
        set constraint: null
        set is_this_type: false
    }
}

; LiteralType — the payload of every string, number, bigint and boolean literal
; type, slice 71.
;
; ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S `value` IS A GO `any` AND IT IS THREE SLOTS HERE, NOT A
; FOUR-ARM UNION, and the precedent is this file's own: TypeResolution's
; `TypeSystemEntity` is likewise an `any` held as slots (see its note). The
; argument is the same and it is stronger here — the reference never *asks* the
; value what it is. Its four readers are `getStringLiteralValue`,
; `getNumberLiteralValue`, `getBigIntLiteralValue` and `getBooleanLiteralValue`,
; each an unchecked type ASSERTION whose correctness is the type's own FLAG. So
; the discriminator is `Type.flags`, exactly as upstream, and a second one in the
; payload would be a thing to keep in step for no reader.
;
; ★ `text` is BORROWED, never copied: every value that reaches here is an
; `AstNode.literal_text_of`, i.e. a run of bytes on the page the parse owns, and
; the checker does not outlive the parse. The bigint arm is the one exception in
; SPIRIT rather than in kind — its digits are computed by ParsePseudoBigInt into a
; buffer this file allocates on the checker's own page.
;
; ★★ `regular_type` and `fresh_type` are the reference's own two slots and they
; are what makes a literal type a PAIR. The regular type is created first and
; points at itself; the fresh type is minted lazily by getFreshTypeOfLiteralType,
; points BACK at the regular one, and points at itself as its own fresh type.
; Both booleans break the pattern by being built eagerly and in the other order
; (see initialize_intrinsic_types).
;
; ★★★ `regular_type` STILL HAS NO READER AND `fresh_type` HAS TWO — and slice 71
; wrote that pair down WRONG BY ONE, which slice 72 is what made visible. That
; slice said `regular_type` is read by getRegularTypeOfLiteralType AND
; isFreshLiteralType. It is not: isFreshLiteralType is
; `t.flags&Freshable != 0 && t.AsLiteralType().freshType == t` and reads the FRESH
; slot. ★A slot's reader list is the kind of claim that reads as checked and is
; not, so it is corrected here rather than left to be re-derived.
;
; ★★ SO THE DOOR SLICE 71 NAMED OPENED FOR HALF OF WHAT WAS BEHIND IT.
; getWidenedLiteralType is ported (slice 72) and asks isFreshLiteralType five
; times, which gives `fresh_type` its second reader beside
; getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. getRegularTypeOfLiteralType is STILL not ported, and
; its argument is unchanged: its only callers are getRegularTypeOfExpression and
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralType, its UNION arm carries a `record_unported`, and a
; work-list row in a function nothing calls is a row that can never appear — the
; product of these slices IS the work list, so a phantom entry in it is worse
; than a missing function.
define LiteralTypeData
(
    ; StringLiteral: the cooked text of the literal. BigIntLiteral:
    ; PseudoBigInt.Base10Value — the absolute value in base 10, no leading zeros,
    ; and EMPTY for zero, which is the reference's own representation and the
    ; reason pseudo_big_int_to_string has a length test rather than a value test.
    text: pointer[char]
    text_length: int
    ; NumberLiteral: the IEEE-754 bits of the jsnum.Number.
    bits: u64
    ; BigIntLiteral: PseudoBigInt.Negative.
    negative: bool
    ; BooleanLiteral: the value.
    bool_value: bool
    regular_type: ref[Type]?
    fresh_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(t: pointer[char], tl: int, b: u64, neg: bool, bv: bool)
    {
        set text: t
        set text_length: tl
        set bits: b
        set negative: neg
        set bool_value: bv
        set regular_type: null
        set fresh_type: null
    }
}

; ★★★ THE ORDER-OF-DECLARATION AUFLAGE that ast.scaly's NodeData carries applies
; here too and is stated now rather than when it bites: the tag IS the jump-table
; index, and AArch64 needs ≥14 contiguous tags per dispatch level. Five arms are
; far below that, so every `choose` over this union lowers to a compare chain
; today — which is correct and cheap at five. The arm that makes the union big
; enough to care is the one that must re-read the root CLAUDE.md's `Insn` note.
; One row of c.declaredTypeLinks. `interface_checked` is checkInterfaceDeclaration's
; own once-bit and lives here because the reference keeps it in the same links
; record — two questions about one symbol, asked from one place.
define DeclaredTypeLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    declared_type: ref[Type]?
    interface_checked: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set declared_type: null
        set interface_checked: false
    }
}

; One row of c.valueSymbolLinks — the OTHER per-symbol links table, and the one
; getTypeOfSymbol memoises into. It is a second table rather than three more
; slots on DeclaredTypeLink because the reference keys them separately and the
; two answer different questions: a class symbol has BOTH a declared type (its
; instance side) and a resolved type (its static side), and conflating them is
; the shape of a bug rather than a saving.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with its reader: `writeType` (getWriteTypeOfSymbol), `target`
; and `mapper` (getTypeOfInstantiatedSymbol, one of the four CheckFlags arms this
; port cannot represent at all).
define ValueSymbolLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    resolved_type: ref[Type]?

    ; links.functionOrConstructorChecked — the ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit of
    ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol (slice 68). It is a report memo rather than a
    ; work memo, which is what makes it load-bearing: the two call sites can reach
    ; one symbol, and diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a second visit's
    ; duplicate line is a failure.
    function_or_constructor_checked: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set resolved_type: null
        set function_or_constructor_checked: false
    }
}

; ── TypeSystemPropertyName ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's ten-member iota, declared WHOLE and in its order even though
; three of them have a writer here. That is the opposite of the ObjectFlags
; decision two hundred lines up, and the difference is what the values are FOR:
; an ObjectFlag is a bit whose meaning is its reader, so a bit nobody reads is a
; claim about the future; these are the arms of ONE switch, and a partial
; declaration is an invitation to renumber the rest when the fourth arrives.
; Nothing outside this file observes the numbers.
define TypeSystemPropertyNameType: int 0
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstructorType: int 1
define TypeSystemPropertyNameDeclaredType: int 2
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedReturnType: int 3
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstraint: int 4
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedTypeArguments: int 5
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseTypes: int 6
define TypeSystemPropertyNameWriteType: int 7
define TypeSystemPropertyNameInitializerIsUndefined: int 8
define TypeSystemPropertyNameAliasTarget: int 9

; One entry of c.typeResolutions.
;
; ★★ `TypeSystemEntity` IS `any` IN THE REFERENCE — a Symbol, a Type, a Node or a
; Signature, told apart by the propertyName that goes with it — and it is TWO
; SLOTS here rather than a four-arm union. The two that have a writer in this
; slice are the two declared; the other two are named with the arm that brings
; them (`Node` for InitializerIsUndefined, `Signature` for ResolvedReturnType),
; and the day either lands the union is the right shape. Identity comparison is
; what the reference does with the field, and two null-or-pointer slots compare
; exactly as one `any` does.
define TypeResolution
(
    target_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    target_type: ref[Type]?
    property_name: int
    result: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?, t: ref[Type]?, pn: int, r: bool)
    {
        set target_symbol: sym
        set target_type: t
        set property_name: pn
        set result: r
    }
}

; ── the STOP LOG's element (slice 75) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ WHY A LOG BESIDE A FIRST-WINS TAG. `record_unported` keeps the FIRST stop of
; a unit and `unported_count` counts them, so between the two there is a number and
; a name and no way to get from one to the other: a tag that 27 units reach reads as
; ZERO in the work list, because every one of those units stopped somewhere else
; first. Slice 74 chose its successor off that work list and named
; `isConstructorType` as the extends chapter's first wall; measured over the same
; corpus with this log, `isConstructorType` is the first wall for 2 of 92 arrivals
; and `checkIdentifier` for 78. **A histogram of firsts is not a histogram.**
;
; ★★ IT IS COLLECTED UNCONDITIONALLY and printed only by `--stops`, which is what
; keeps the instrument from having a mode: a stop is a rare event (14 878 over the
; whole of stage 1, i.e. ~11 per unit), so the log costs one small region allocation
; per report and nothing per node. The checksum this owes is that the five artifacts
; stay byte-identical with the log in place — see tests/stops.sh's header.
define StopEvent
(
    tag: pointer[const_char]
    detail: int
)
{
    init(t: pointer[const_char], d: int)
    {
        set tag: t
        set detail: d
    }
}

define TypeData union
(
    None: NoTypeData
    Interface: InterfaceTypeData
    Reference: TypeReferenceData
    TypeParameter: TypeParameterData
    Anonymous: AnonymousTypeData
    Literal: LiteralTypeData
)

; ── Type ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `Type` is a tag struct plus an interface-typed payload
; (IntrinsicType, LiteralType, ObjectType, UnionType, …), which is the shape
; CLAUDE.md §3.1 turns into a sum type — the union above.
;
; ★ `id` is not compared by the yardstick: the dump prints a type's STRING, never
; its identity. It is kept because the reference's ids are assigned in creation
; order and several of its caches are keyed on them, so a port that dropped the
; counter would have to invent one later at every such site.
;
; ★★ `intrinsic_name` STAYS ON THE TYPE rather than moving into an intrinsic arm,
; and that is the same divergence ast.scaly makes for `symbol`/`parent`: the
; reader is type_to_string, which asks it of any type it is handed, and a `choose`
; there would have to name every arm to answer "not an intrinsic". A non-intrinsic
; type carries null, which is what the reference's `AsIntrinsicType()` would refuse
; to give at all — the yardstick is the check, exactly as it is for a node's symbol.
;
; ★ `symbol` is the reference's `Type.symbol`, set by newObjectType and
; newTypeParameter. `alias` and `checker` are not carried: the first is read by
; typeToString's alias arm, which reports, and the second is this port's `this`.
define Type
(
    id: int
    flags: int
    object_flags: int
    intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char]
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    data: TypeData
)
{
    init(i: int, f: int, of: int, nm: pointer[const_char], sym: ref[Symbol]?, d: TypeData)
    {
        set id: i
        set flags: f
        set object_flags: of
        set intrinsic_name: nm
        set symbol: sym
        set data: d
    }
}

; ── ModuleKind: the values this port can currently name ─────────────────────
;
; core.ModuleKind, and only the members a ported branch reads — the enum has
; fourteen. It is a handful of `define`s rather than a generated module for the
; reason ObjectFlags above is: a table is worth generating when the port reads
; most of it, and a table nobody can verify against a reader is a claim about
; the future. The values are the reference's own numbers
; (internal/core/compileroptions.go), which is what makes them checkable.
;
; ★ ES2022 is the DERIVED default under this harness — see Checker.module_kind
; for the derivation, which is the part that surprises.
;
; ★ Node16 and NodeNext are here as the BOUNDS of a range test rather than as
; branch labels — isCommonJSContainingModuleKind asks `Node16 <= k <= NodeNext`,
; and the two intermediate members (Node18 = 101, Node20 = 102) are inside it and
; are named by nothing this port reads. Adding them would be a table nobody
; verifies against a reader, which is the rule above.
define ModuleKindNone: int 0
define ModuleKindCommonJS: int 1
define ModuleKindES2015: int 5
define ModuleKindES2020: int 6
define ModuleKindES2022: int 7
define ModuleKindESNext: int 99
define ModuleKindNode16: int 100
define ModuleKindNodeNext: int 199
define ModuleKindPreserve: int 200

; ── ScriptTarget: the four values a ported branch reads ─────────────────────
;
; core.ScriptTarget, and the same rule as ModuleKind above: the members a reader
; actually names, with the reference's own numbers
; (internal/core/compileroptions.go), because a number is checkable and a name is
; not. The enum has fifteen.
;
; ★★★ ES2025 IS THE DERIVED DEFAULT AND IT IS THE **TOP** OF THE STANDARD RANGE,
; which is why every `languageVersion` guard in this port answers the same way and
; why none of them is dead code in the reference. `GetEmitScriptTarget()` falls
; through to `ScriptTargetLatestStandard` when Target is unset, and that is
; ES2025 = 12 — so a `>= ES2017` guard is TAKEN and a `<= ES2021` guard is not.
; See Checker.language_version.
define ScriptTargetES2017: int 4
; Slice 70's reader is checkGrammarBigIntLiteral's `< ES2020` guard, which is the
; fourth branch this list serves and the first that is provably DEAD: 12 < 7 is
; false, so a BigInt literal never collects TS2737 under this harness.
define ScriptTargetES2020: int 7
define ScriptTargetES2021: int 8
; Slice 74's reader is GetUseDefineForClassFields' fallthrough, and it is the
; threshold that makes checkClassForStaticPropertyNameConflicts inert: 12 >= 9.
define ScriptTargetES2022: int 9
define ScriptTargetES2025: int 12

; ── SemanticMeaning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ast.SemanticMeaning: which of the three declaration SPACES a declaration
; declares into. Three bits, no complement, so a hand-port is the whole table.
define SemanticMeaningNone: int 0
define SemanticMeaningValue: int 1 << 0
define SemanticMeaningType: int 1 << 1
define SemanticMeaningNamespace: int 1 << 2
define SemanticMeaningAll: int SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType | SemanticMeaningNamespace

; ── ElementFlags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.ElementFlags: what a TUPLE element is. Four bits, and slice 61 needs
; them for getTupleElementFlags and checkTupleType's ordering loop.
;
; ★★ THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS HERE IS REST AGAINST VARIADIC, and it is not a
; syntactic one in general: both are spelled `...T`, and which one it is depends on
; whether `T` is an ARRAY. getTupleElementFlags decides it with
; getArrayElementTypeNode, a purely syntactic probe — so `...T[]` and `...[...U[]]`
; answer Rest without a type, and `...T` answers Variadic and is where this port
; stops.
;
; ★ The four composites the reference defines (Fixed, Variable, NonRequired,
; NonRest) have no reader in this slice and are left out (§3.5be).
define ElementFlagsRequired: int 1 << 0
define ElementFlagsOptional: int 1 << 1
define ElementFlagsRest:     int 1 << 2
define ElementFlagsVariadic: int 1 << 3

; ── DeclarationSpaces ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.DeclarationSpaces, slice 65: which of the three export SPACES one
; DECLARATION occupies. Three bits and no composites, so the table is the port.
;
; ★★★ IT IS NOT ast.SemanticMeaning ABOVE, AND THE BIT LAYOUT IS IDENTICAL, which
; is exactly why they are two tables ten lines apart rather than one shared set:
; the temptation is at its strongest where nothing would break today. The
; reference has both, with the same three bits in the same order — and the
; per-kind ANSWERS differ, which is what makes them two questions.
; getMeaningFromDeclaration gives an EnumMember Value|Type and a non-instantiated
; ModuleDeclaration Namespace alone; getDeclarationSpaces gives an EnumMember
; ExportType|ExportValue and a SourceFile all three, a kind the other function
; answers Namespace|Value for. Folding them would be a same-layout merge of two
; specifications, and the merge is invisible until one of them changes.
define DeclarationSpacesNone:            int 0
define DeclarationSpacesExportValue:     int 1 << 0
define DeclarationSpacesExportType:      int 1 << 1
define DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace: int 1 << 2

; ── slice 73: the duplicate-member map ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; One entry of checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations' three `map[string]int`s.
;
; ★★★ AN ASSOCIATION LIST RATHER THAN A MAP, for the reason TypeInstantiation's
; note gives above and with a number behind it this time. All three maps are
; GUARDED: `instanceNames` and `staticNames` take an entry only for a member whose
; symbol has more than one declaration — i.e. only for a name that is already an
; error — and `privateNames` only for a member named with a private identifier.
; The widest input the corpus has is privateNameDuplicateField.ts with 64 private
; members in one FILE, spread over several classes, so a linear scan is bounded by
; sixty-four comparisons per member and no hash can pay for itself here.
;
; ★★ THE EMPTY LIST **IS** GO'S NIL MAP, exactly, which is what lets the port
; create all three eagerly where the reference creates each on first write. A read
; of a nil Go map answers the zero value and a read of an empty list answers 0;
; every write goes through set_duplicate_name_state, which the reference guards
; with its own `if names == nil` allocation. An `Array` whose init is empty
; allocates nothing, so the eager creation is also free.
define DuplicateNameEntry
(
    name_data: pointer[char]
    name_len: int
    state: int
)
{
    init(d: pointer[char], n: int, s: int)
    {
        set name_data: d
        set name_len: n
        set state: s
    }
}

define Checker
(
    host: pointer[Page]
    file: ref[AstNode]?

    ; The bind. The checker asks it two questions in this slice — is this file a
    ; module, and does it own a symbol — and everything the type machinery will
    ; need (getSymbolOfDeclaration, the tables) comes through it.
    b: ref[Binder]

    ; ★ THE RE-SCANNING SCANNER IS THE BINDER'S, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER
    ; THAN A SAVING. grammarErrorOnFirstToken reports at
    ; scanner.GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition, which the reference builds a FRESH
    ; scanner for; the binder ported that in slice 31 as
    ; `range_of_token_at_position` and wrote out why a shared scanner is the same
    ; answer — it carries no diagnostic sink, `skip_trivia_at` does not touch
    ; scanner state, and the language variant is set from the file name at
    ; construction. All three still hold when the sharer is one phase further on,
    ; and the binder is finished with it by the time a checker exists. A second
    ; scanner over the same text would be a second thing to keep in step.

    ; sourceFile.IsDeclarationFile, read by the checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements
    ; guard.
    is_declaration_file: bool

    ; ── the three compiler options the grammar checks read (slices 49, 50) ───
    ;
    ; ★★ THEY ARE FIELDS RATHER THAN LITERAL `false`s, and the difference is what
    ; a later reader sees. The harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and
    ; NoErrorTruncation and nothing else, so ExperimentalDecorators and
    ; VerbatimModuleSyntax are both unset and both of these are false — but a
    ; branch written as `if false` reads as dead code somebody should delete,
    ; while a branch behind a named option reads as the option it is waiting for.
    ; When an options record arrives, these two are the first substitution and the
    ; three reports behind them (grammar-legacy-accessor-decorators,
    ; node-can-be-decorated-parameter, grammar-verbatim-module-syntax) become the
    ; work list for it.
    ;
    ; ★ `c.legacyDecorators = c.compilerOptions.ExperimentalDecorators ==
    ; core.TSTrue` — the reference computes it once in NewChecker, and so does
    ; create() below.
    legacy_decorators: bool
    verbatim_module_syntax: bool

    ; ★ Slice 50's addition, and it is the one of the three whose branch is
    ; FULLY PORTED rather than reported. `shouldCheckErasableSyntax` is
    ; `ErasableSyntaxOnly.IsTrue() && !IsInJSFile(node)` and its consequence is a
    ; plain c.error on the node — no program, no resolver — so the arms of
    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration and checkExportAssignment that read it carry
    ; live code behind a false option instead of a report. That is the difference
    ; the note above is drawing: a report belongs where the MACHINERY is missing,
    ; not where the option merely happens to be off.
    erasable_syntax_only: bool

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.ShouldPreserveConstEnums(), slice 65 — and it is a
    ; DERIVATION, not a plain option read: `PreserveConstEnums.IsTrue() ||
    ; IsolatedModules.IsTrue()`. Both are unset here, so the answer is false — but
    ; the pair is why it is a field rather than an omitted term: the day either
    ; option arrives, isInstantiatedModule starts counting a CONST-ENUM-ONLY module
    ; as instantiated, and the four reports behind that gate come alive together.
    ; ★It is the one of this slice's two option readings where the naive answer is
    ; also the right one, which is worth saying because the other one (module_kind,
    ; four fields up) is the reason the derivation is written out at all.
    preserve_const_enums: bool

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.NoUncheckedSideEffectImports, slice 58 — AND IT IS
    ; **TRUE** WHEN UNSET, which is the second of this slice's two option
    ; surprises and the sharper one. The reference reads it through
    ; `IsTrueOrUnknown()`, and a Tristate's zero value is TSUnknown — so the
    ; branch behind it is taken for every side-effect-only `import "m"` under this
    ; harness, and reading "unset" as false would have silently dropped a whole
    ; population out of the histogram.
    ;
    ; ★ It is a field and not a `true` literal for the same reason the four
    ; options above are fields: what the next reader needs to know is WHICH option
    ; the branch is waiting for, and here also that the answer is not the one the
    ; name suggests.
    no_unchecked_side_effect_imports: bool

    ; ★★★ c.moduleKind, slice 58 — AND IT IS NOT ZERO, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON
    ; IT IS A FIELD WITH A DERIVATION RATHER THAN AN OMITTED TERM. The harness
    ; leaves `Module` unset, and the obvious reading of that — the three options
    ; above — is *the option is off, so the branch behind it is dead*. Here the
    ; option being unset does NOT mean ModuleKindNone: NewChecker asks
    ; `compilerOptions.GetEmitModuleKind()`, which falls through to the TARGET when
    ; Module is unset, and an unset target is `ScriptTargetLatestStandard` =
    ; ES2025 = 12, which is `>= ScriptTargetES2022` — so the answer is
    ; **ModuleKindES2022**, and every branch that tests moduleKind is LIVE.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND NO INSTRUMENT IN THIS PACKAGE CAN TELL ES2022 FROM None TODAY, which
    ; is said here rather than left for the next reader to discover. The one
    ; ported reading is checkGrammarImportClause's `!= ESNext && != Preserve`, and
    ; ModuleKindNone fails that test exactly as ES2022 does — so the naive answer
    ; is RIGHT here by accident, and the battery's proof of that is a row that
    ; substitutes None and predicts nothing moves. The value is defended from the
    ; reference, not from a measurement; the first reading that would separate them
    ; is checkModuleExportName's `== ES2015 || == ES2020`, which is unported.
    ; §3.5cw's readings of an unset option get one more here, and it is the first
    ; where the option's own value is not the question at all.
    ;
    ; ★ Stored rather than recomputed, exactly as the reference stores it, and set
    ; from a named constant so the derivation is stated once. The day an options
    ; record arrives this is the line that reads it.
    module_kind: int

    ; ★★★ c.languageVersion, slice 59 — `compilerOptions.GetEmitScriptTarget()`,
    ; which is ScriptTargetLatestStandard = **ES2025 = 12** when Target is unset.
    ; It is the third derived default in this record and the one whose consequence
    ; is largest: the collision family reads it three times and all three reads
    ; come out the same way, so checkCollisionWithGlobalPromiseInGeneratedCode
    ; returns at its own first line (`>= ES2017`) and the two
    ; recordPotentialCollisionWith… functions register nothing (`<= ES2021`).
    ;
    ; ★★ SO THREE OF THE FIVE SUB-CHECKS OF checkCollisionsForDeclarationName ARE
    ; PROVABLY INERT UNDER THIS HARNESS, and each is ported as its guard plus a
    ; report rather than dropped — the shape check_class_declaration's
    ; legacy_decorators branch already has. A target arriving later is then a
    ; substitution and the reports become the work list for it.
    language_version: int

    ; ★★★ program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, slice 59 — AND THIS ONE IS **NOT** THE
    ; DERIVED ANSWER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS A SEPARATE FIELD FROM
    ; module_kind. The oracle's stub program answers **ModuleKindNone** for every
    ; file (tests/oracle/types.go), while the option-derived value one field up is
    ; ES2022 — so the two disagree, and the yardstick's reference is the STUB's
    ; answer. §3.5cy's rule (*the answer of a PROGRAM is not an option default*)
    ; read in the direction that bites: here it is not a hole, it is a VALUE, and
    ; getting it from the option would silently flip two branches.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND BOTH FLIPS ARE OBSERVABLE, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, which is what
    ; makes this the slice's load-bearing constant.
    ; checkCollisionWithRequireExportsInGeneratedCode returns at `format >= ES2015`
    ; — with ES2022 the whole check would be dead, with None it is LIVE — and
    ; checkClassNameCollisionWithObject reports at `format < ES2015`, so `class
    ; Object {}` in a module REPORTS here and would be silent under the option's
    ; own value. The two CommonJS readings (the global-object report, the two
    ; import emit-helper branches) are dead either way, None being neither.
    emit_module_format: int

    ; ★★★ program.GetSourceFileMetaData(path).ImpliedNodeFormat, slice 64 — A
    ; THIRD program answer, and a THIRD field, even though the oracle's stub gives
    ; it the same ModuleKindNone as emit_module_format one line up. The field above
    ; carries the argument in general form and this is the case that tests it: two
    ; program methods that coincide today are still two questions, and folding them
    ; would make a later slice's `@module: node16` flip a branch it does not name.
    ; ★Its one reader is checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement's Node label, which is
    ; dead under this harness — so the field is written for the DAY the option
    ; arrives, and that is exactly when a shared field would be found to be wrong.
    implied_node_format: int

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.GetUseDefineForClassFields(), slice 74 — AND IT IS
    ; **TRUE**, WHICH MAKES THE WHOLE HEAD OF THE WORK LIST INERT. The option is
    ; a Tristate and unset, so the reference falls through to
    ; `GetEmitScriptTarget() >= ScriptTargetES2022`, and that is
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 = 12 against 9 — so
    ; checkClassForStaticPropertyNameConflicts returns at its own first line for
    ; every class in this corpus, on BOTH sides (tests/oracle/types.go builds its
    ; CompilerOptions with SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and nothing
    ; else). This is the fourth derived default in this record and the first whose
    ; consequence is a whole reference function rather than a branch.
    ;
    ; ★ A field rather than the literal `true` for the reason module_kind is one:
    ; what the next reader needs is WHICH option the guard waits for. The day an
    ; options record arrives, or a `@target: es5` case reaches the checker, this is
    ; the line that flips and the report behind the guard becomes the work list.
    use_define_for_class_fields: bool

    ; ★★ compilerOptions.NoImplicitOverride, slice 74 — read through `IsTrue()`,
    ; so an unset Tristate is FALSE and this one is not a surprise. Its single
    ; reader is checkMemberForOverrideModifier's early return, and what the false
    ; value buys is exactly the reachable half of that function: a member with no
    ; `override` modifier is not the checker's business, so the only report the
    ; family can raise here is the one about a member that HAS the modifier.
    no_implicit_override: bool

    ; c.diagnostics — the checker's OWN collection, which is neither the parse's
    ; nor the bind's. Appended through add_direct: the reference's addDiagnostic
    ; has no dedup (that is the PARSER's parseErrorAtRange), and its
    ; serializationLevel guard cannot fire here because nothing recurses into the
    ; printer.
    diagnostics: ref[DiagnosticList]?

    ; nextTypeId, in creation order.
    type_count: int

    ; ── the intrinsic types, in the reference's own creation order ───────────
    ;
    ; NewChecker builds these before anything else, and the ORDER is theirs
    ; because the ids are assigned along it. What is missing from the run is named
    ; at initialize_intrinsic_types.
    any_type: ref[Type]
    auto_type: ref[Type]
    wildcard_type: ref[Type]
    blocked_string_type: ref[Type]
    error_type: ref[Type]
    unresolved_type: ref[Type]
    non_inferrable_any_type: ref[Type]
    intrinsic_marker_type: ref[Type]
    unknown_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_widening_type: ref[Type]
    missing_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_or_missing_type: ref[Type]
    optional_type: ref[Type]
    null_type: ref[Type]
    null_widening_type: ref[Type]
    string_type: ref[Type]
    number_type: ref[Type]
    bigint_type: ref[Type]
    es_symbol_type: ref[Type]
    void_type: ref[Type]
    never_type: ref[Type]
    silent_never_type: ref[Type]
    implicit_never_type: ref[Type]
    unreachable_never_type: ref[Type]
    non_primitive_type: ref[Type]
    unique_literal_type: ref[Type]

    ; ── the boolean literal pair, slice 71 ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ FOUR TYPES FOR TWO VALUES, and the asymmetry with every other literal is
    ; the reference's: `false` and `true` are the only literal types built EAGERLY,
    ; in NewChecker, and they are built REGULAR-FIRST-THEN-FRESH rather than lazily
    ; through getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. `c.falseType` is what `case
    ; ast.KindFalseKeyword` answers, and it is the FRESH one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `booleanType` IS STILL MISSING AND STILL NAMED, which is what keeps
    ; type_to_string's Boolean arm reporting: it is `getUnionType([regularFalse,
    ; regularTrue])`, and a union is a dimension away. So this slice makes the two
    ; boolean LITERAL types without making the type they union into — the same cut
    ; slice 69 drew when it ported getTypeFromTypeNode's keyword arms and left
    ; `boolean` out.
    regular_false_type: ref[Type]
    false_type: ref[Type]
    regular_true_type: ref[Type]
    true_type: ref[Type]

    ; ── the three intern tables, slice 71 ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.stringLiteralTypes, c.numberLiteralTypes, c.bigintLiteralTypes. The
    ; reference keys three Go maps on the VALUE; these are association lists
    ; scanned linearly, which is `reported_ambient`'s idiom and this file's
    ; standing answer to a small per-file map.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE TABLE BUYS IS IDENTITY, NOT AN ANSWER — two occurrences of `"a"`
    ; in one file must be the SAME type, because every relation the checker will
    ; grow (isTypeIdenticalTo, containsType, the union reducer) compares types by
    ; pointer. Nothing in this slice can observe a hit, and that is exactly why the
    ; table is built now rather than when a reader appears: a literal type minted
    ; twice is indistinguishable from one minted once until the day it is not.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NUMBER TABLE IS KEYED ON THE BIT PATTERN AND THEREFORE NEEDS NO
    ; SEPARATE NaN CELL, which is a divergence from the reference that is provably
    ; the same answer (§3.5ag). `c.nanType` exists upstream because NaN != NaN in
    ; IEEE-754 and a Go map lookup on a NaN key therefore always misses; a bit
    ; comparison does not miss, and every NaN this port can make is jsnum's single
    ; canonical BITS_NAN. ★The sibling fact is why the arm is unreachable from this
    ; slice at all: a NumericLiteral's text is a number the scanner accepted, so
    ; `jsnum_from_string` of it is neither NaN nor negative zero — the two inputs a
    ; value-keyed table and a bit-keyed table would disagree about.
    string_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    number_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    bigint_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; c.nodeLinks[node].hasReportedStatementInAmbientContext, as a SET of the
    ; nodes that have it. The reference keeps a links record per node and this
    ; slice reads exactly one bit off it, so a set is the whole of it; the record
    ; arrives with the first arm that needs a second field.
    ;
    ; ★ Linear membership, deliberately. The set only ever holds nodes in an
    ; AMBIENT context that have already reported, which is a handful per file even
    ; in a `.d.ts` — and the reference's own map is keyed by pointer identity,
    ; which is what this compares.
    reported_ambient: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; c.renamedBindingElementsInTypes — the list checkVariableLikeDeclaration's
    ; binding-element block appends to and checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements drains
    ; at the end of the file. Slice 56 is the slice that gives it a WRITER, which is
    ; what turns the empty reader at the other end into a report.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS A LIST AND NOT A COUNTER EVEN THOUGH ONE READER ASKS ONLY WHETHER IT
    ; IS EMPTY, because the reader the reference has walks it and reports per
    ; ELEMENT — with the element's own name and property name in the message — so a
    ; counter would be a shape the next slice has to undo. §3.5be's argument runs
    ; the other way here: what may not be built is machinery no instrument can
    ; reach, and this one is reached the moment a `.ts` file carries a renamed
    ; binding element in a body-less signature.
    renamed_binding_elements_in_types: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; c.sourceFileLinks[file].deferredNodes — the nodes checkNodeDeferred parks so
    ; that a check needing a type runs after every declaration is bound into the
    ; walk. Slice 60 gives it its first writer (a type parameter) and its first
    ; reader (check_deferred_nodes).
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's is a SET (ordered, deduplicating) and this is a list, which
    ; is the same thing for the one caller there is: check_type_parameter runs once
    ; per type parameter and a node is therefore added once. A second caller that
    ; can reach the same node twice is what makes the distinction matter, and the
    ; reference's own type says so.
    deferred_nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; ── c.declaredTypeLinks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's per-symbol links table, as an association LIST keyed on the
    ; symbol itself, scanned linearly — `reported_ambient`'s idiom, for the same
    ; reason and with the same cost profile (a file's classes and interfaces).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT KEYED ON THE SYMBOL ID, and that is a correctness point rather
    ; than a preference. `Symbol.id` is drawn LAZILY on first request (slice 41),
    ; and the numbers are observable through GetSymbolNameForPrivateIdentifier — so
    ; a table that asked every class's symbol for its id would make every later
    ; class's private-name mangling shift. The pointer is the identity the
    ; reference's map has anyway.
    ;
    ; ★★ NOTHING IN THIS SLICE CAN OBSERVE A HIT. Each of the two call sites asks
    ; once per symbol, and isThislessInterface's recursive ask is behind a report —
    ; so the memo is written for fidelity and its READER is named: the slice that
    ; ports getBaseTypes, where an interface's declared type is asked for a second
    ; time through its base, and the identity of the answer is what makes
    ; `t.AsInterfaceType().thisType` the same object on both routes.
    declared_type_links: ref[Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]]

    ; ── c.valueSymbolLinks (slice 67) ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; declared_type_links' idiom exactly, and keyed on the SYMBOL POINTER for the
    ; same reason: Symbol.id is drawn lazily and its numbers are observable
    ; through private-name mangling.
    ;
    ; ★★ THIS TABLE'S MEMO IS OBSERVABLE WHERE THE OTHER ONE'S WAS NOT, and the
    ; difference is a good miniature of what a memo is for. declared_type_links'
    ; note says "nothing in this slice can observe a hit"; here
    ; getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule is asked once per class by
    ; checkClassLikeDeclaration and again by checkClassExpression, and the two
    ; must answer the SAME object — a class's static type is compared by identity
    ; against the base's in checkMembersForOverrideModifier.
    value_symbol_links: ref[Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?]]

    ; ── c.typeResolutions (slice 67) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The resolution stack, as a backing array plus a DEPTH. Scaly's Array has no
    ; pop, so the array only ever grows and `type_resolution_count` is the logical
    ; length; a push past the high-water mark adds and one below it overwrites.
    ; The reference clears the popped entry "to avoid memory leaks" and the
    ; overwrite is the same statement under RBMM.
    type_resolutions: ref[Array[ref[TypeResolution]?]]
    type_resolution_count: int

    ; c.resolutionStart. It has a READER here (findResolutionCycleStartIndex) and
    ; no writer, which is the reverse of §3.5be's forbidden shape and is why it is
    ; a field rather than a literal 0: both of its writers are named
    ; (getResolvedSignature, and the relater's recursion guard), each temporarily
    ; resets the stack floor around a nested resolution, and a literal here would
    ; become a silent divergence the day either lands.
    resolution_start: int

    ; The unported report, in the parser's and the binder's shape: the FIRST
    ; construct this slice does not implement, its tag, and a detail (a kind, in
    ; almost every case). The dump is suppressed once this is set — an incomplete
    ; port that answered a well-formed dump would be reporting agreement it has
    ; not earned.
    unported_pos: int
    unported_tag: pointer[const_char]
    unported_detail: int

    ; ★★★ THE COUNT OF REPORTS, AND SLICE 69 IS WHAT MADE IT NECESSARY. The FLAG
    ; answers *has this unit stopped*, which is what every caller before this slice
    ; needed — they compare it before and after a call to decide whether to carry
    ; on. It cannot answer *did THIS call stop*, because record_unported is
    ; first-wins: after the first report the flag no longer moves, and
    ; check_source_elements deliberately keeps walking (slice 49's expiry).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THAT DIFFERENCE ONLY CHANGES AN ANSWER WHERE A NULL IS AMBIGUOUS, and
    ; slice 69 is the first place it is: getTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration answers
    ; nil BOTH for *nothing can be inferred* — which the widener turns into `any`
    ; plus an implicit-any diagnostic — and for a stop. Measured: with the flag
    ; comparison, every declaration after a file's first report INVENTED a TS7005,
    ; and diagcheck went red on 377 units. The count is monotone, so `mark before,
    ; mark after` is exact.
    unported_count: int

    ; ── c.suggestionDiagnostics, as a COUNT (slice 70) ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference keeps a second diagnostic list for CategorySuggestion, which
    ; GetDiagnostics does not read — so a suggestion must never reach the C
    ; section, and this port keeps only what makes a suggestion SITE provable:
    ; how many fired. checkGrammarNumericLiteral is the first of them.
    ;
    ; ★ A list would be a field with no reader (§3.5be); the counter has one, and
    ; it is a control rather than the port — which is the honest description of
    ; what a suggestion is worth to this yardstick.
    suggestion_count: int

    ; ── the stop log (slice 75) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Every stop in the order it happened, where `unported_tag` is the first and
    ; `unported_count` the total. See StopEvent for why both are needed.
    stop_log: ref[Array[ref[StopEvent]?]]
)
{
    ; ── entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checker.NewChecker, minus the forty-method Program: the oracle's stub answers
    ; every resolution question with nothing, and this side has no program layer to
    ; answer them from either, so the two agree by having the same hole. See the
    ; header for what that costs and where it will first be visible.
    function create(host: pointer[Page], file: ref[AstNode], file_name: String, b: ref[Binder]) returns ref[Checker]
    {
        let c host.allocate(sizeof Checker, alignof Checker) as pointer[Checker]
        set c.host: host
        set c.file: file
        set c.b: b
        set c.is_declaration_file: is_declaration_file_name(file_name)
        set c.legacy_decorators: false
        set c.verbatim_module_syntax: false
        set c.erasable_syntax_only: false
        set c.preserve_const_enums: false
        set c.module_kind: ModuleKindES2022
        set c.no_unchecked_side_effect_imports: true
        set c.language_version: ScriptTargetES2025
        set c.emit_module_format: ModuleKindNone
        set c.implied_node_format: ModuleKindNone
        set c.use_define_for_class_fields: ScriptTargetES2025 >= ScriptTargetES2022
        set c.no_implicit_override: false
        let dl host.allocate(sizeof DiagnosticList, alignof DiagnosticList) as pointer[DiagnosticList]
        set *dl: DiagnosticList(host)
        set c.diagnostics: dl
        set c.type_count: 0
        let ra host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[AstNode]?], alignof Array[ref[AstNode]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        set *ra: Array[ref[AstNode]?]()
        set c.reported_ambient: ra
        let rb host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[AstNode]?], alignof Array[ref[AstNode]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        set *rb: Array[ref[AstNode]?]()
        set c.renamed_binding_elements_in_types: rb
        let dn host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[AstNode]?], alignof Array[ref[AstNode]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        set *dn: Array[ref[AstNode]?]()
        set c.deferred_nodes: dn
        let dt host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?], alignof Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]]
        set *dt: Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]()
        set c.declared_type_links: dt
        let vl host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?], alignof Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?]]
        set *vl: Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?]()
        set c.value_symbol_links: vl
        let tr host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[TypeResolution]?], alignof Array[ref[TypeResolution]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[TypeResolution]?]]
        set *tr: Array[ref[TypeResolution]?]()
        set c.type_resolutions: tr
        set c.type_resolution_count: 0
        set c.resolution_start: 0
        set c.unported_pos: 0 - 1
        set c.unported_tag: null
        set c.unported_detail: 0
        set c.unported_count: 0
        set c.suggestion_count: 0
        let stops host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[StopEvent]?], alignof Array[ref[StopEvent]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[StopEvent]?]]
        set *stops: Array[ref[StopEvent]?]()
        set c.stop_log: stops
        let sl host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[Type]?], alignof Array[ref[Type]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        set *sl: Array[ref[Type]?]()
        set c.string_literal_types: sl
        let nl host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[Type]?], alignof Array[ref[Type]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        set *nl: Array[ref[Type]?]()
        set c.number_literal_types: nl
        let bl host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[Type]?], alignof Array[ref[Type]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        set *bl: Array[ref[Type]?]()
        set c.bigint_literal_types: bl
        c.initialize_intrinsic_types()
        c
    }

    ; ── the unported report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The binder's mechanism, verbatim in intent: the FIRST report wins, so the
    ; tag names the construct that stopped the check rather than the last one
    ; noticed. `pos` is 0 rather than a real position for the same reason the
    ; binder's is — the dump's consumer wants the TAG, and a checker report is
    ; about a construct rather than about a place in the text.
    procedure record_unported(this, tag: pointer[const_char], detail: int)
    {
        ; Incremented OUTSIDE the first-wins guard, which is the whole point of it
        ; — see the field's own note. The LOG is appended outside it for the same
        ; reason, one notch further: the count says how many and the log says which.
        set unported_count: unported_count + 1
        let ev host.allocate(sizeof StopEvent, alignof StopEvent) as pointer[StopEvent]
        set *ev: StopEvent(tag, detail)
        stop_log.add(ev as ref[StopEvent])
        if unported_pos < 0
        {
            set unported_pos: 0
            set unported_tag: tag
            set unported_detail: detail
        }
    }

    function is_unported(this) returns bool
        unported_pos >= 0

    ; The monotone report count, for the callers that need *did THIS call stop*
    ; rather than *has this unit stopped*.
    function unported_mark(this) returns int
        unported_count

    function unported_at(c: ref[Checker]) returns int
        c.unported_pos

    function unported_tag_at(c: ref[Checker]) returns pointer[const_char]
        c.unported_tag

    function unported_detail_at(c: ref[Checker]) returns int
        c.unported_detail

    ; ── the stop log's readers (slice 75) ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Statics for the reason every other dumper accessor is one: a generic instance
    ; handed to a program root reads back as garbage, so TypeDump asks through
    ; these rather than touching the Array.
    function stop_count(c: ref[Checker]) returns int
        c.stop_log.get_length() as int

    function stop_tag_at(c: ref[Checker], i: int) returns pointer[const_char]
    {
        let ev *(c.stop_log.get_buffer() + i)
        if ev = null
            return null
        (ev as ref[StopEvent]).tag
    }

    function stop_detail_at(c: ref[Checker], i: int) returns int
    {
        let ev *(c.stop_log.get_buffer() + i)
        if ev = null
            return 0
        (ev as ref[StopEvent]).detail
    }

    ; ── the intrinsic types ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; newType, reduced to what an intrinsic needs. The reference threads a payload
    ; interface through here; this slice has one payload kind (see Type's note).
    function new_intrinsic_type_ex(this, flags: int, intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char], object_flags: int) returns ref[Type]
    {
        set type_count: type_count + 1
        let t host.allocate(sizeof Type, alignof Type) as pointer[Type]
        set *t: Type(type_count, flags, object_flags, intrinsic_name, null, TypeData.None(NoTypeData()))
        t
    }

    function new_intrinsic_type(this, flags: int, intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char]) returns ref[Type]
        this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(flags, intrinsic_name, ObjectFlagsNone)

    ; createWideningType. Its whole body is `if c.strictNullChecks { return
    ; nonWideningType }` and then a fresh intrinsic carrying
    ; ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType — and strictNullChecks is TRUE under this
    ; harness's options (see the header), so the FIRST branch is the one that runs.
    ; It is written as the reference's conditional anyway, with the option spelled
    ; as a constant, so an options record arriving later is a substitution.
    function create_widening_type(this, non_widening_type: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]
    {
        if Checker.strict_null_checks()
            return non_widening_type
        this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(non_widening_type.flags, non_widening_type.intrinsic_name, ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType)
    }

    ; ── the object types ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 66. Every type before this one is an intrinsic minted once at
    ; startup; these are the first types a SOURCE FILE causes to exist.

    ; newType, for a payload that is not the intrinsic. The reference's `newType`
    ; takes the data interface; here the arm is passed in already built.
    function new_type(this, flags: int, object_flags: int, sym: ref[Symbol]?, d: TypeData) returns ref[Type]
    {
        set type_count: type_count + 1
        let t host.allocate(sizeof Type, alignof Type) as pointer[Type]
        set *t: Type(type_count, flags, object_flags, null, sym, d)
        t
    }

    ; newObjectType. The reference's switch has EIGHT arms selecting the payload
    ; struct off the object flags, in that order, and this slice mints exactly two
    ; of them — so the other six report rather than being left out, which is this
    ; file's rule for an arm that stands in place (§3.5v). The reference's `default`
    ; is a panic, and it is a report here for the same reason every unhandled kind
    ; is: a panic would be a claim that the input is impossible.
    function new_object_type(this, object_flags: int, sym: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface) <> 0
            return this.new_type(TypeFlagsObject, object_flags, sym, TypeData.Interface(InterfaceTypeData()))
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsTuple) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("new-object-type-tuple", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsReference) <> 0
            return this.new_type(TypeFlagsObject, object_flags, sym, TypeData.Reference(TypeReferenceData(null, null)))
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsMapped) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("new-object-type-mapped", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsReverseMapped) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("new-object-type-reverse-mapped", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("new-object-type-evolving-array", 0)
            return null
        }
        ; ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType is `1 << 24`, one of the REUSED
        ; positions the flags table above deliberately does not name — so this arm
        ; is written by the slice that declares the bit, beside its reader.
        ;
        ; ★ Slice 67 fills the anonymous arm, which used to report here. Its
        ; caller is getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModuleWorker and the first thing this
        ; port makes with it is the STATIC SIDE of a class.
        if (object_flags & ObjectFlagsAnonymous) <> 0
            return this.new_type(TypeFlagsObject, object_flags, sym, TypeData.Anonymous(AnonymousTypeData()))
        this.record_unported("new-object-type-unhandled", object_flags)
        null
    }

    ; newTypeParameter. Four lines in the reference and four here.
    function new_type_parameter(this, sym: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Type]
        this.new_type(TypeFlagsTypeParameter, ObjectFlagsNone, sym, TypeData.TypeParameter(TypeParameterData()))

    ; ── literal types (slice 71) ────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; newLiteralType, and the two-slot dance at its tail is the reference's:
    ; `regularType` is the argument when one is given and the NEW TYPE ITSELF
    ; otherwise, so a regular literal type is its own regular type and a fresh one
    ; points back at the regular one it was minted from.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference passes ONE `value any` here; this takes the three slots
    ; LiteralTypeData holds, and each caller fills the one its flags name (see the
    ; record's note for why the flags are the discriminator).
    function new_literal_type(this, flags: int, text: pointer[char], text_length: int, bits: u64, negative: bool, bool_value: bool, regular_type: ref[Type]?) returns ref[Type]
    {
        let t this.new_type(flags, ObjectFlagsNone, null, TypeData.Literal(LiteralTypeData(text, text_length, bits, negative, bool_value)))
        if regular_type = null
            Checker.set_literal_regular_type(t, t)
        else
            Checker.set_literal_regular_type(t, regular_type)
        t
    }

    ; The LiteralType accessors — `AsLiteralType().value` and its two links, as
    ; questions about the union TAG, exactly as this file's other payload
    ; accessors are. Each answers a zero for an arm that is not Literal, where the
    ; reference's unchecked assertion would panic.
    function literal_text_of(t: ref[Type]) returns pointer[char]
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.text
        null
    }

    function literal_text_length_of(t: ref[Type]) returns int
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.text_length
        0
    }

    function literal_bits_of(t: ref[Type]) returns u64
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.bits
        0 as u64
    }

    function literal_negative_of(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.negative
        false
    }

    ; getBooleanLiteralValue.
    function literal_bool_of(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.bool_value
        false
    }

    function literal_regular_type_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.regular_type
        null
    }

    function literal_fresh_type_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Literal
                return d.fresh_type
        null
    }

    ; The two writers. Copy the arm out, mutate the copy, put the whole arm back —
    ; a `when` binding is a COPY and the direct spelling stores through the
    ; payload's first eight bytes (root CLAUDE.md).
    procedure set_literal_regular_type(t: ref[Type], rt: ref[Type]?)
    {
        choose t.data
            when l: Literal
            {
                var d l
                set d.regular_type: rt
                set t.data: TypeData.Literal(d)
            }
    }

    procedure set_literal_fresh_type(t: ref[Type], ft: ref[Type]?)
    {
        choose t.data
            when l: Literal
            {
                var d l
                set d.fresh_type: ft
                set t.data: TypeData.Literal(d)
            }
    }

    ; getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. The fresh type is minted ONCE per regular type and
    ; cached on it; it carries the same flags and value, points back at the regular
    ; type, and — the line that makes isFreshLiteralType work at all — points at
    ; ITSELF as its own fresh type.
    ;
    ; ★ `f.symbol = t.symbol` is the reference's and is ported even though every
    ; literal type this slice mints has a null symbol: the arm that gives one a
    ; symbol is getEnumLiteralType, and a copy that silently dropped it would be
    ; found the day that arm lands rather than here.
    function get_fresh_type_of_literal_type(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]
    {
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsFreshable) = 0
            return t
        let cached Checker.literal_fresh_type_of(t)
        if cached <> null
            return cached as ref[Type]
        let f this.new_literal_type(t.flags, Checker.literal_text_of(t), Checker.literal_text_length_of(t), Checker.literal_bits_of(t), Checker.literal_negative_of(t), Checker.literal_bool_of(t), t)
        set f.symbol: t.symbol
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(f, f)
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(t, f)
        f
    }

    ; getStringLiteralType. The table is scanned linearly and compared on the
    ; BYTES, which is what a Go map keyed on a string compares.
    function get_string_literal_type(this, text: pointer[char], text_length: int) returns ref[Type]
    {
        let len string_literal_types.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let row *(string_literal_types.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let t row as ref[Type]
                if Checker.literal_text_length_of(t) = text_length
                {
                    if Checker.text_equals(Checker.literal_text_of(t), text, text_length)
                        return t
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let t this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsStringLiteral, text, text_length, 0 as u64, false, false, null)
        string_literal_types.add(t)
        t
    }

    ; getNumberLiteralType — see the table's own note for why the NaN cell the
    ; reference keeps beside it is not here.
    function get_number_literal_type(this, bits: u64) returns ref[Type]
    {
        let len number_literal_types.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let row *(number_literal_types.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let t row as ref[Type]
                if Checker.literal_bits_of(t) = bits
                    return t
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let t this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsNumberLiteral, null, 0, bits, false, false, null)
        number_literal_types.add(t)
        t
    }

    ; getBigIntLiteralType. The key is the whole PseudoBigInt — the sign AND the
    ; digits — because that is what a Go map keyed on the struct compares.
    function get_bigint_literal_type(this, text: pointer[char], text_length: int, negative: bool) returns ref[Type]
    {
        let len bigint_literal_types.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let row *(bigint_literal_types.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let t row as ref[Type]
                if Checker.literal_negative_of(t) = negative
                {
                    if Checker.literal_text_length_of(t) = text_length
                    {
                        if Checker.text_equals(Checker.literal_text_of(t), text, text_length)
                            return t
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let t this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsBigIntLiteral, text, text_length, 0 as u64, negative, false, null)
        bigint_literal_types.add(t)
        t
    }

    function text_equals(a: pointer[char], b: pointer[char], n: int) returns bool
    {
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            if (*(a + i) as int) <> (*(b + i) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; ── the payload accessors ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's `AsInterfaceType()` / `AsTypeReference()` downcasts, as
    ; questions about the union TAG. Each answers null for an arm that is not the
    ; one asked about, where the reference would panic — the same choice
    ; new_object_type's default makes, and for the same reason.

    ; InterfaceType.thisType.
    function this_type_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Interface
                return d.this_type
        null
    }

    ; ObjectType.target, which both object arms carry.
    function target_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
                return i.target
            when r: Reference
                return r.target
        null
    }

    ; TypeReference.resolvedTypeArguments, likewise.
    function resolved_type_arguments_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
                return i.resolved_type_arguments
            when r: Reference
                return r.resolved_type_arguments
        null
    }

    ; InterfaceType.baseTypesResolved / resolvedBaseTypes /
    ; resolvedBaseConstructorType, plus the two writers getBaseTypes and
    ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass need. Each write is copy / mutate / put the
    ; whole arm back — a `when` binding is a COPY, and the direct spelling stores
    ; through the payload's first eight bytes (see mark_this_type's note).
    function base_types_resolved_of(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Interface
                return d.base_types_resolved
        false
    }

    function resolved_base_types_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Interface
                return d.resolved_base_types
        null
    }

    function resolved_base_constructor_type_of(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Interface
                return d.resolved_base_constructor_type
        null
    }

    procedure mark_base_types_resolved(t: ref[Type])
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
            {
                var d i
                set d.base_types_resolved: true
                set t.data: TypeData.Interface(d)
            }
    }

    procedure set_resolved_base_types(t: ref[Type], types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
            {
                var d i
                set d.resolved_base_types: types
                set t.data: TypeData.Interface(d)
            }
    }

    procedure set_resolved_base_constructor_type(t: ref[Type], bct: ref[Type]?)
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
            {
                var d i
                set d.resolved_base_constructor_type: bct
                set t.data: TypeData.Interface(d)
            }
    }

    ; InterfaceType.TypeParameters() — allTypeParameters minus the trailing
    ; thisType, and nil when the array is empty. The two siblings
    ; (OuterTypeParameters, LocalTypeParameters) are not written: their readers
    ; are getTypeArguments' deferred path and getBaseTypes, both of which report
    ; in this slice, and a slicing accessor nobody calls is a claim about the
    ; future (§3.5be).
    function type_parameter_count_of(t: ref[Type]) returns int
    {
        choose t.data
            when d: Interface
            {
                if d.all_type_parameters = null
                    return 0
                let all d.all_type_parameters as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
                let n all.get_length() as int
                if n = 0
                    return 0
                return n - 1
            }
        0
    }

    ; ── c.declaredTypeLinks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    function declared_type_link_of(this, sym: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[DeclaredTypeLink]
    {
        let len declared_type_links.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let row *(declared_type_links.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let r row as ref[DeclaredTypeLink]
                if r.symbol = sym
                    return r
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let fresh host.allocate(sizeof DeclaredTypeLink, alignof DeclaredTypeLink) as pointer[DeclaredTypeLink]
        set *fresh: DeclaredTypeLink(sym)
        declared_type_links.add(fresh)
        fresh
    }

    ; ── getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 66's HEAD, and the histogram that chose it is in CLAUDE.md §3.5dl:
    ; over all 17 973 units the tag `get-declared-type-of-symbol` carried, the
    ; symbol's flags select ONE of the six arms below — Class 3 007, Interface
    ; 1 432, and NOTHING else, at either stage. So the switch is written whole and
    ; five of its six arms report; only getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface is code.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ZERO ON TypeParameter IS AN ORDER, NOT A CORPUS PROPERTY, and it is
    ; the reason this slice can leave the type-parameter collectors' inner call
    ; unported: `check_type_parameters` runs BEFORE `check_exports_on_merged_
    ; declarations` in both arms, and check_type_parameter reports
    ; `get-declared-type-of-type-parameter` unconditionally — so a GENERIC class or
    ; interface is claimed by that earlier row and can never reach this head. The
    ; day that row lands, generics arrive here and appendTypeParameters below stops
    ; being a loop that never runs.
    function get_declared_type_of_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let result this.try_get_declared_type_of_symbol(symbol)
        if result = null
            return error_type
        result
    }

    function try_get_declared_type_of_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let f Symbol.flags_of(symbol)
        if (f & (SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsInterface)) <> 0
            return this.get_declared_type_of_class_or_interface(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsTypeParameter) <> 0
            return this.get_declared_type_of_type_parameter(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsTypeAlias) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-type-alias", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (f & SymbolFlagsEnum) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-enum", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (f & SymbolFlagsEnumMember) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-enum-member", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (f & SymbolFlagsAlias) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-alias", 0)
            return null
        }
        ; The reference falls out of the switch answering nil, which
        ; getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol turns into errorType. That is an ANSWER rather
        ; than a hole, so it does not report.
        null
    }

    ; getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter — four lines in the reference and four here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE HEAD OF A 1 317-UNIT WORK-LIST ROW AND IT IS FOUR LINES, which
    ; is slice 65's finding about getSymbolOfDeclaration arriving a second time: a
    ; row's DEPTH counts the callers waiting behind it, never the code in it. What
    ; still stops those units is check_type_parameter's three READERS of the type
    ; (getBaseConstraintOfType, the circular-default report, the constraint/default
    ; assignability), not the type itself — so the row does not close here, and the
    ; tag stays where it is until they land.
    function get_declared_type_of_type_parameter(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let links this.declared_type_link_of(symbol)
        if links.declared_type = null
            set links.declared_type: this.new_type_parameter(symbol)
        links.declared_type
    }

    ; getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE GUARD IS A DISJUNCTION AND ITS THREE TERMS DO NOT COST THE SAME. A
    ; CLASS short-circuits on the second term, so isThislessInterface is never
    ; asked about one — which is why the only units in this slice that can reach
    ; resolveEntityName are the 28 interfaces WITH a heritage clause, of 17 973.
    function get_declared_type_of_class_or_interface(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let links this.declared_type_link_of(symbol)
        if links.declared_type <> null
            return links.declared_type
        var kind ObjectFlagsInterface
        let is_class (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsClass) <> 0
        if is_class
            set kind: ObjectFlagsClass
        let made this.new_object_type(kind, symbol)
        if made = null
            return null
        let t made as ref[Type]
        ; The reference stores the type in the links BEFORE resolving the type
        ; parameters, and that order is load-bearing rather than incidental: a
        ; class whose own type parameter's constraint mentions the class closes the
        ; recursion on the half-built type instead of running forever.
        set links.declared_type: t
        ; ★★★ THE GUARDS BELOW ASK WHETHER *THIS CALL* REPORTED, NOT WHETHER THE
        ; CHECKER HAS EVER REPORTED, and the file already paid for the difference
        ; once — see check_grammar_modifiers' note. record_unported is FIRST-WINS
        ; and unit-wide, so by the time a file's second declaration is checked the
        ; flag is usually already set; a blanket `is_unported()` test would return
        ; here for every declaration after the first. Measured while writing this
        ; slice, on the guard one level up: two TS2368 reports vanished from
        ; checker_type_parameter_reserved_name.ts, because the second and third
        ; type parameter returned before checkTypeNameIsReserved.
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        let outer this.get_outer_type_parameters_of_class_or_interface(symbol)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return t
        let type_parameters this.append_local_type_parameters_of_class_or_interface_or_type_alias(outer, symbol)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return t

        var give_this_type false
        if type_parameters <> null
            set give_this_type: true
        if is_class
            set give_this_type: true
        if give_this_type = false
        {
            let thisless this.is_thisless_interface(symbol)
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return t
            if thisless = false
                set give_this_type: true
        }
        if give_this_type
        {
            set t.object_flags: t.object_flags | ObjectFlagsReference
            let this_type this.new_type_parameter(symbol)
            Checker.mark_this_type(this_type)
            Checker.set_type_parameter_constraint(this_type, t)
            ; `d.resolvedTypeArguments = d.TypeParameters()` is allTypeParameters
            ; WITHOUT the trailing thisType — which is exactly the list that went
            ; in, so it is built once and handed over as both.
            let tps this.new_type_list(type_parameters)
            let all this.new_type_list(tps)
            all.add(this_type)
            var outer_count 0
            if outer <> null
                set outer_count: (outer as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
            let insts host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?], alignof Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]
            set *insts: Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]()
            let seed host.allocate(sizeof TypeInstantiation, alignof TypeInstantiation) as pointer[TypeInstantiation]
            set *seed: TypeInstantiation(tps, t)
            insts.add(seed)
            Checker.fill_interface_data(t, all, tps, insts, outer_count, this_type)
        }
        t
    }

    ; A fresh type list seeded with an existing one — `append(typeParameters,
    ; d.thisType)`'s allocation, written out because Scaly's Array is a value the
    ; caller owns rather than a slice the callee may share.
    function new_type_list(this, seed: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    {
        let a host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[Type]?], alignof Array[ref[Type]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        set *a: Array[ref[Type]?]()
        if seed <> null
        {
            let src seed as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
            let n src.get_length() as int
            var i 0
            while i < n
            {
                a.add(*(src.get_buffer() + i))
                set i: i + 1
            }
        }
        a
    }

    ; The three writes into a payload arm, each its own procedure because a
    ; `choose` binding is the only way to reach the arm's slots.
    ; ★★★ THE WRITE-BACK IS THE IDIOM AND IT IS NOT DECORATION — a `when` binding is
    ; a COPY of the payload, so a `set` through it mutates a temporary. ast.scaly's
    ; `set_type` has written it this way since slice 17 (copy, mutate, put the whole
    ; arm back) and the other eighty-eight payload writes in this tree follow it.
    ; Spelled the direct way — `set d.is_this_type: true` — the emitter GEPs through
    ; the LOADED STRUCT VALUE (`getelementptr %PayloadA, %PayloadA %variant.val`) and
    ; the store lands on whatever the payload's first eight bytes point at. It
    ; SIGSEGVs here, which is the lucky half; the same spelling over a payload whose
    ; first slot is a valid address is a silent wrong write.
    procedure mark_this_type(t: ref[Type])
    {
        choose t.data
            when tp: TypeParameter
            {
                var d tp
                set d.is_this_type: true
                set t.data: TypeData.TypeParameter(d)
            }
    }

    procedure set_type_parameter_constraint(t: ref[Type], c: ref[Type])
    {
        choose t.data
            when tp: TypeParameter
            {
                var d tp
                set d.constraint: c
                set t.data: TypeData.TypeParameter(d)
            }
    }

    ; d.allTypeParameters / d.outerTypeParameterCount / d.thisType, plus the two
    ; TypeReference slots the reference sets in the same block:
    ; `resolvedTypeArguments = d.TypeParameters()`, `target = t`, and the one-entry
    ; instantiations seed.
    ;
    ; ★ resolvedTypeArguments is d.TypeParameters() — allTypeParameters WITHOUT the
    ; trailing thisType — so it is the seed list before `all.add(this_type)`, which
    ; is why the caller hands both.
    procedure fill_interface_data(t: ref[Type], all: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]], tps: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]], insts: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]], outer_count: int, this_type: ref[Type])
    {
        choose t.data
            when i: Interface
            {
                var d i
                set d.all_type_parameters: all
                set d.outer_type_parameter_count: outer_count
                set d.this_type: this_type
                set d.resolved_type_arguments: tps
                set d.instantiations: insts
                set d.target: t
                set t.data: TypeData.Interface(d)
            }
    }

    ; ── the type-parameter collectors ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ EVERY LOOP BELOW IS EMPTY FOR EVERY UNIT THIS SLICE REACHES, by the ORDER
    ; proof at get_declared_type_of_symbol — and they are written out anyway rather
    ; than folded to `null`, because the day the type-parameter row lands is the day
    ; they run, and a folded version would be a silent wrong answer rather than a
    ; missing one.

    ; getOuterTypeParametersOfClassOrInterface. The `Find` picks the INTERFACE
    ; declaration (or a variable declaration initialised to a function) when the
    ; symbol is neither a class nor a function — which is what makes a reopened
    ; interface use one of its own declarations rather than a value declaration it
    ; may not have.
    function get_outer_type_parameters_of_class_or_interface(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        var declaration Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
        if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & (SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsFunction)) = 0
        {
            set declaration: null
            let n Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
            var i 0
            while i < n
            {
                let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
                if d <> null
                {
                    let dn d as ref[AstNode]
                    if AstNode.kind_of(dn) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                    {
                        set declaration: dn
                        set i: n
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        if AstNode.kind_of(dn) = KindVariableDeclaration
                        {
                            let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(dn)
                            if initializer <> null
                            {
                                let ik AstNode.kind_of(initializer as ref[AstNode])
                                if ik = KindFunctionExpression
                                {
                                    set declaration: dn
                                    set i: n
                                }
                                if ik = KindArrowFunction
                                {
                                    set declaration: dn
                                    set i: n
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                set i: i + 1
            }
        }
        ; The reference asserts here ("Class was missing valueDeclaration -OR-
        ; non-class had no interface declarations"). A report rather than a trap,
        ; on this file's rule: an assertion is a claim that the input is impossible.
        if declaration = null
        {
            this.record_unported("outer-type-parameters-no-declaration", 0)
            return null
        }
        this.get_outer_type_parameters(declaration as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; getOuterTypeParameters, with includeThisTypes FALSE — its only caller here
    ; passes false, and the true branch is the one that asks
    ; getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface back, so leaving the parameter out keeps
    ; the recursion this slice cannot have out of the shape entirely. ★The reader
    ; that brings it back is getTypeOfSymbol's class arm.
    function get_outer_type_parameters(this, start: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        var node AstNode.parent_node_of(start)
        while node <> null
        {
            let n node as ref[AstNode]
            let kind AstNode.kind_of(n)
            if Checker.kind_carries_outer_type_parameters(kind)
            {
                let before this.is_unported()
                let outer this.get_outer_type_parameters(n)
                if this.is_unported() <> before
                    return null
                ; The three branches that need machinery this slice does not have,
                ; each reporting in place: a context-sensitive function expression
                ; or object-literal method (getSignaturesOfType over
                ; getTypeOfSymbol), a mapped type, and a conditional type's infer
                ; parameters.
                if kind = KindFunctionExpression
                {
                    this.record_unported("outer-type-parameters-context-sensitive", kind)
                    return null
                }
                if kind = KindArrowFunction
                {
                    this.record_unported("outer-type-parameters-context-sensitive", kind)
                    return null
                }
                if kind = KindMappedType
                {
                    this.record_unported("outer-type-parameters-mapped", kind)
                    return null
                }
                if kind = KindConditionalType
                {
                    this.record_unported("outer-type-parameters-conditional", kind)
                    return null
                }
                return this.append_type_parameters(outer, AstNode.type_parameters_of(n))
            }
            set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        }
        null
    }

    ; The reference's `case` list, as a predicate. A MethodDeclaration is in it and
    ; is also what IsObjectLiteralMethod tests for above — the two questions are
    ; separate and only the first is asked here.
    function kind_carries_outer_type_parameters(kind: int) returns bool
    {
        if kind = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindClassExpression
            return true
        if kind = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if kind = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if kind = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if kind = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if kind = KindConstructorType
            return true
        if kind = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if kind = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if kind = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if kind = KindMappedType
            return true
        if kind = KindConditionalType
            return true
        false
    }

    ; appendLocalTypeParametersOfClassOrInterfaceOrTypeAlias.
    function append_local_type_parameters_of_class_or_interface_or_type_alias(this, types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        var acc types
        let n Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let dn d as ref[AstNode]
                let k AstNode.kind_of(dn)
                var takes false
                if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                    set takes: true
                if k = KindClassDeclaration
                    set takes: true
                if k = KindClassExpression
                    set takes: true
                if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
                    set takes: true
                if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                    set takes: true
                if takes
                {
                    set acc: this.append_type_parameters(acc, AstNode.type_parameters_of(dn))
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        acc
    }

    ; appendTypeParameters. `AppendIfUnique` is the reference's, and the identity
    ; it compares is the TYPE's — which is why the memo in declared_type_link_of
    ; has to be the one answering, not a fresh type per ask.
    function append_type_parameters(this, types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, declarations: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        if declarations = null
            return types
        let list declarations as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        let n list.get_length() as int
        if n = 0
            return types
        var acc types
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d *(list.get_buffer() + i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let sym this.symbol_of_declaration(d as ref[AstNode])
                if sym <> null
                {
                    let tp this.get_declared_type_of_type_parameter(sym as ref[Symbol])
                    if tp <> null
                    {
                        if acc = null
                            set acc: this.new_type_list(null)
                        let a acc as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
                        var seen false
                        let m a.get_length() as int
                        var j 0
                        while j < m
                        {
                            if *(a.get_buffer() + j) = tp
                                set seen: true
                            set j: j + 1
                        }
                        if seen = false
                            a.add(tp)
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        acc
    }

    ; isThislessInterface. The loop body is reached only by an interface WITH an
    ; extends clause — 28 units of 17 973 — and it stops there, because
    ; resolveEntityName is the first name resolution this port would owe and it is
    ; a dimension rather than a call (see the file header on the unresolved global).
    function is_thisless_interface(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns bool
    {
        let n Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let dn d as ref[AstNode]
                if AstNode.kind_of(dn) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                {
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(dn) & NodeFlagsContainsThis) <> 0
                        return false
                    if Checker.has_extends_heritage(dn)
                    {
                        this.record_unported("resolve-entity-name", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
                        return true
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; ast.GetExtendsHeritageClauseElements — the list, at last with readers.
    ;
    ; ★★ SLICE 66 DEFERRED THIS AND SAID SO: its note read "reduced to the
    ; question the caller asks — does the loop run at all … pulling them out would
    ; be a list nobody reads". Slice 67 has three readers (getBaseTypeNodeOfClass
    ; through FirstOrNil, resolveBaseTypesOfInterface's loop, and the predicate
    ; below), so the deferral has expired and the reduced form is now the derived
    ; one rather than the primitive.
    function get_extends_heritage_clause_elements(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        Checker.get_heritage_elements(node, KindExtendsKeyword)
    }

    ; ast.GetImplementsHeritageClauseElements — the second of the two wrappers.
    ;
    ; ★ SLICE 74 IS WHAT SPLIT THE SHARED HALF OUT. The reference has always had
    ; GetHeritageElements(node, kind) with the two wrappers over it; this port
    ; inlined it while `extends` was the only kind anybody asked for, and the
    ; implements loop of checkClassLikeDeclaration is the second caller that makes
    ; the reference's own decomposition the cheaper one to keep.
    function get_implements_heritage_clause_elements(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        Checker.get_heritage_elements(node, KindImplementsKeyword)
    }

    ; ast.GetHeritageElements — GetHeritageClause(node, kind) and then its Types.
    ; The clause SEARCH is inlined into the walk, exactly as before the split: the
    ; reference's GetHeritageClause has no other caller here.
    function get_heritage_elements(node: ref[AstNode], kind: int) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        let clauses AstNode.heritage_clauses_of(node)
        if clauses = null
            return null
        let list clauses as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        let n list.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let c *(list.get_buffer() + i)
            if c <> null
            {
                if AstNode.heritage_clause_token_of(c as ref[AstNode]) = kind
                    return AstNode.heritage_clause_types_of(c as ref[AstNode])
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.GetExtendsHeritageClauseElement — core.FirstOrNil of the list.
    function get_extends_heritage_clause_element(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let elems Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_elements(node)
        if elems = null
            return null
        let list elems as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        if list.get_length() = 0
            return null
        *(list.get_buffer() + 0)
    }

    function has_extends_heritage(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_element(node) <> null
    }

    ; ── the type reference ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; getTypeArguments, for the case this slice can reach: resolvedTypeArguments is
    ; written at construction by getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface, so the deferred
    ; branch — which resolves them off the type NODE and needs getTypeFromTypeNode —
    ; is unreachable and reports instead of being left out.
    function get_type_arguments(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        let args Checker.resolved_type_arguments_of(t)
        if args = null
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-arguments-deferred", 0)
            return null
        }
        args
    }

    ; getPropagatingFlagsOfTypes, whole. Its caller passes TypeFlagsNone as the
    ; exclusion, so the guard is `t.flags & 0 == 0` — always true — and the loop is
    ; a plain or-fold. It is written with the parameter anyway: the OTHER callers
    ; (createArrayType, createTupleType) pass a real mask, and a version specialised
    ; to this one would have to be un-specialised by the first of them.
    function get_propagating_flags_of_types(types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, exclude_kinds: int) returns int
    {
        if types = null
            return ObjectFlagsNone
        let list types as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        let n list.get_length() as int
        var result ObjectFlagsNone
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let t *(list.get_buffer() + i)
            if t <> null
            {
                let ty t as ref[Type]
                if (ty.flags & exclude_kinds) = 0
                    set result: result | ty.object_flags
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        result & ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags
    }

    ; createTypeReferenceEx. The lookup is over the target's instantiation LIST,
    ; comparing the argument lists themselves — see TypeInstantiation for why the
    ; reference's hash key is not ported and what would bring it back.
    function create_type_reference(this, target: ref[Type], type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, object_flags: int) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let existing Checker.find_instantiation(target, type_arguments)
        if existing <> null
            return existing
        let propagating Checker.get_propagating_flags_of_types(type_arguments, 0)
        let made this.new_object_type(ObjectFlagsReference | object_flags | propagating, target.symbol)
        if made = null
            return null
        let t made as ref[Type]
        Checker.fill_reference_data(t, target, type_arguments)
        Checker.add_instantiation(target, type_arguments, t)
        t
    }

    procedure fill_reference_data(t: ref[Type], target: ref[Type], args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        choose t.data
            when r: Reference
            {
                var d r
                set d.target: target
                set d.resolved_type_arguments: args
                set t.data: TypeData.Reference(d)
            }
    }

    function find_instantiation(target: ref[Type], args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        choose target.data
            when d: Interface
            {
                if d.instantiations = null
                    return null
                let list d.instantiations as ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]
                let n list.get_length() as int
                var i 0
                while i < n
                {
                    let row *(list.get_buffer() + i)
                    if row <> null
                    {
                        let r row as ref[TypeInstantiation]
                        if Checker.type_lists_equal(r.arguments, args)
                            return r.instantiated
                    }
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                return null
            }
        null
    }

    procedure add_instantiation(target: ref[Type], args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, t: ref[Type])
    {
        choose target.data
            when d: Interface
            {
                if d.instantiations <> null
                {
                    let list d.instantiations as ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]
                    let row Page.get(list).allocate(sizeof TypeInstantiation, alignof TypeInstantiation) as pointer[TypeInstantiation]
                    set *row: TypeInstantiation(args, t)
                    list.add(row)
                }
            }
    }

    ; The key comparison the reference's hash stands in for: two argument lists are
    ; the same when they are the same length and their entries are the same TYPE
    ; OBJECTS. Identity, not structural equality — which is what upstream's hash of
    ; the type IDS means too.
    function type_lists_equal(a: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, b: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?) returns bool
    {
        var an 0
        if a <> null
            set an: (a as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
        var bn 0
        if b <> null
            set bn: (b as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
        if an <> bn
            return false
        if an = 0
            return true
        let al a as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        let bl b as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
        var i 0
        while i < an
        {
            if *(al.get_buffer() + i) <> *(bl.get_buffer() + i)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; getTypeWithThisArgument. Both call sites in this slice pass needApparentType
    ; false and a nil thisArgument, and the type they pass is the declared type of a
    ; class or interface — which carries ObjectFlagsReference exactly when
    ; getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface gave it a `this` type. The intersection arm
    ; reports; getApparentType is behind the needApparentType this slice never sets,
    ; so it is not written at all and the parameter is not carried.
    function get_type_with_this_argument(this, t: ref[Type], this_argument: ref[Type]?) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsReference) <> 0
        {
            let target Checker.target_of(t)
            if target = null
            {
                this.record_unported("type-with-this-argument-no-target", 0)
                return null
            }
            let tg target as ref[Type]
            let before this.is_unported()
            let type_arguments this.get_type_arguments(t)
            if this.is_unported() <> before
                return null
            var argn 0
            if type_arguments <> null
                set argn: (type_arguments as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
            if Checker.type_parameter_count_of(tg) <> argn
                return t
            var arg this_argument
            if arg = null
                set arg: Checker.this_type_of(tg)
            let combined this.new_type_list(type_arguments)
            combined.add(arg)
            return this.create_type_reference(tg, combined, ObjectFlagsNone)
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsIntersection) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-with-this-argument-intersection", 0)
            return null
        }
        t
    }


    ; ── slice 67: the resolution stack ──────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.typeResolutions, pushTypeResolution / popTypeResolution and the cycle
    ; search, whole. It arrives with the first two memoised type questions that
    ; need it rather than with either one of them, because it is one stack shared
    ; by nine of them and a per-question guard would be a shape the next slice has
    ; to undo.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS WHAT MAKES `interface I extends I {}` REPORT ONCE INSTEAD OF
    ; RECURSING, and the reference's design is worth restating because it is not
    ; the obvious one: the cycle is not broken by refusing to re-enter, it is
    ; broken by MARKING every frame from the cycle's start as failed, so each of
    ; them learns on the way out that its answer is not to be memoised.

    function push_type_resolution(this, target_symbol: ref[Symbol]?, target_type: ref[Type]?, property_name: int) returns bool
    {
        let start this.find_resolution_cycle_start_index(target_symbol, target_type, property_name)
        if start >= 0
        {
            var i start
            while i < type_resolution_count
            {
                let row *(type_resolutions.get_buffer() + i)
                if row <> null
                {
                    let r row as ref[TypeResolution]
                    set r.result: false
                }
                set i: i + 1
            }
            return false
        }
        let r host.allocate(sizeof TypeResolution, alignof TypeResolution) as pointer[TypeResolution]
        set *r: TypeResolution(target_symbol, target_type, property_name, true)
        if type_resolution_count < (type_resolutions.get_length() as int)
            type_resolutions.put(type_resolution_count as size_t, r)
        else
            type_resolutions.add(r)
        set type_resolution_count: type_resolution_count + 1
        true
    }

    function pop_type_resolution(this) returns bool
    {
        set type_resolution_count: type_resolution_count - 1
        let row *(type_resolutions.get_buffer() + type_resolution_count)
        if row = null
            return true
        let r row as ref[TypeResolution]
        r.result
    }

    ; findResolutionCycleStartIndex. The FIRST test in the loop is the one that
    ; ends the search: an entry whose property has since been ANSWERED means the
    ; frames above it are not part of a cycle at all, so the walk stops rather
    ; than continuing past it.
    function find_resolution_cycle_start_index(this, target_symbol: ref[Symbol]?, target_type: ref[Type]?, property_name: int) returns int
    {
        var i type_resolution_count - 1
        while i >= resolution_start
        {
            let row *(type_resolutions.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let r row as ref[TypeResolution]
                if this.type_resolution_has_property(r)
                    return 0 - 1
                if r.property_name = property_name
                {
                    var same true
                    if r.target_symbol <> target_symbol
                        set same: false
                    if r.target_type <> target_type
                        set same: false
                    if same
                        return i
                }
            }
            set i: i - 1
        }
        0 - 1
    }

    ; typeResolutionHasProperty. Three arms have a PUSH in this slice; the other
    ; seven cannot be on the stack at all, so they report in place rather than
    ; being left out (§3.5v) — and the reference's own tail is a panic, which this
    ; file never ports as a trap because an assertion is a claim that the input is
    ; impossible.
    function type_resolution_has_property(this, r: ref[TypeResolution]) returns bool
    {
        if r.property_name = TypeSystemPropertyNameType
        {
            if r.target_symbol = null
                return false
            let links this.value_symbol_link_of(r.target_symbol as ref[Symbol])
            return links.resolved_type <> null
        }
        if r.property_name = TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseTypes
        {
            if r.target_type = null
                return false
            return Checker.base_types_resolved_of(r.target_type as ref[Type])
        }
        if r.property_name = TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstructorType
        {
            if r.target_type = null
                return false
            return Checker.resolved_base_constructor_type_of(r.target_type as ref[Type]) <> null
        }
        this.record_unported("type-resolution-has-property", r.property_name)
        false
    }

    ; ── c.valueSymbolLinks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    function value_symbol_link_of(this, sym: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[ValueSymbolLink]
    {
        let len value_symbol_links.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let row *(value_symbol_links.get_buffer() + i)
            if row <> null
            {
                let r row as ref[ValueSymbolLink]
                if r.symbol = sym
                    return r
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let made host.allocate(sizeof ValueSymbolLink, alignof ValueSymbolLink) as pointer[ValueSymbolLink]
        set *made: ValueSymbolLink(sym)
        value_symbol_links.add(made)
        made
    }

    ; ── getTypeOfSymbol ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The dispatch, whole — and porting it is what SPLITS the work list's largest
    ; row into the questions the reference actually asks. Until this slice the
    ; three call sites all reported one tag, `get-type-of-symbol`, and §3.5dl had
    ; to build a throwaway instrument to say which arm each unit needed; the tag
    ; says it now.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FOUR CheckFlags ARMS ARE NOT MERELY UNREACHED, THEY ARE
    ; UNREPRESENTABLE. This port has no CheckFlags at all, because nothing in it
    ; creates a TRANSIENT symbol — which is getMergedSymbol's empty table seen
    ; from the other side, and carries the same date. They are therefore left out
    ; rather than reported: a report is a promise that an input can reach the
    ; line, and no input can.
    function get_type_of_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let f Symbol.flags_of(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsAccessor) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-accessors", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (f & (SymbolFlagsVariable | SymbolFlagsProperty)) <> 0
            return this.get_type_of_variable_or_parameter_or_property(symbol)
        if (f & (SymbolFlagsFunction | SymbolFlagsMethod | SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsEnum | SymbolFlagsValueModule)) <> 0
            return this.get_type_of_func_class_enum_module(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsEnumMember) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-enum-member", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (f & SymbolFlagsAlias) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-alias", 0)
            return null
        }
        ; The reference falls out of the chain answering errorType, which is an
        ; ANSWER rather than a hole — so it does not report, exactly as
        ; try_get_declared_type_of_symbol's tail does not.
        error_type
    }

    ; getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule. The memo is the whole function, and its hit is
    ; OBSERVABLE here (see value_symbol_links' note) — which is what makes this
    ; the first links table in this port that earns its keep.
    function get_type_of_func_class_enum_module(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let links this.value_symbol_link_of(symbol)
        if links.resolved_type = null
        {
            let made this.get_type_of_func_class_enum_module_worker(symbol)
            if made = null
                return null
            set links.resolved_type: made
        }
        links.resolved_type
    }

    function get_type_of_func_class_enum_module_worker(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let f Symbol.flags_of(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsModule) <> 0
        {
            if Checker.is_shorthand_ambient_module_symbol(symbol)
                return any_type
        }
        ; ★ The CommonJS branch. Unreachable in this slice — only a CLASS symbol
        ; reaches this function, from checkClassLikeDeclaration — and its body is
        ; resolveExternalModuleSymbol, which is a work-list row of its own. The
        ; `CommonJSModuleIndicator != nil` term is asked of the BINDER, which is
        ; this file's binder: for a one-unit compilation that is the same file the
        ; value declaration is in, and a multi-file program is where the two come
        ; apart. That is the reader for a per-file links record, named here rather
        ; than papered over.
        if (f & SymbolFlagsValueModule) <> 0
        {
            let vd Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
            if vd <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(vd as ref[AstNode]) = KindSourceFile
                {
                    if b.is_external_or_common_js_module()
                    {
                        this.record_unported("resolve-external-module-symbol", 0)
                        return null
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        let made this.new_object_type(ObjectFlagsAnonymous, symbol)
        if made = null
            return null
        let t made as ref[Type]
        if (f & SymbolFlagsClass) <> 0
        {
            let before this.is_unported()
            let base_type_variable this.get_base_type_variable_of_class(symbol)
            if this.is_unported() <> before
                return null
            ; A class whose base is a TYPE VARIABLE is a mixin, and its static
            ; type is the intersection of the anonymous type with that variable.
            ; Unreachable while getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass reports for every
            ; class that HAS a base — the reader that brings it alive is
            ; checkExpression.
            if base_type_variable <> null
            {
                this.record_unported("get-intersection-type", 0)
                return null
            }
            return t
        }
        ; strictNullChecks is true under this harness, so the guard's SECOND term
        ; is the one that decides — and an optional function or module symbol is
        ; not something this slice can be handed.
        if Checker.strict_null_checks()
        {
            if (f & SymbolFlagsOptional) <> 0
            {
                this.record_unported("get-optional-type", 0)
                return null
            }
        }
        t
    }

    ; isShorthandAmbientModuleSymbol — a module declaration with no body, which is
    ; the only kind that can be missing one.
    function is_shorthand_ambient_module_symbol(symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns bool
    {
        let vd Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
        if vd = null
            return false
        let n vd as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindModuleDeclaration
            return false
        AstNode.body_of(n) = null
    }

    ; ── slice 69: getTypeOfVariableOrParameterOrProperty ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS IS THE WORK LIST'S HEAD AND IT IS ONE FUNCTION WITH ONE FORK. Slice
    ; 67 named it and slice 68 walked past it; what stands here is the ANNOTATED
    ; half of a variable-like declaration's type, the widening that follows it, and
    ; the IMPLICIT-ANY family the un-annotated half reports instead. The
    ; INITIALIZER half is a stop, because it is checkExpression — a dimension
    ; rather than a step.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MEMO IS PART OF THE SEMANTICS HERE, not a speed-up. Everything behind
    ; this function can REPORT — the implicit-any diagnostics below are its whole
    ; product — so a second computation of one symbol's type is a duplicated line,
    ; and diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE. The reference guards the write
    ; with isParameterOfContextSensitiveSignature and the guard is transcribed.
    ;
    ; ★ `t == nil` is the reference's panic ("Unexpected nil type") and is this
    ; port's STOP: a worker that reported answers null, and the caller tells the
    ; two apart by is_unported(), which is this file's idiom everywhere.
    function get_type_of_variable_or_parameter_or_property(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let links this.value_symbol_link_of(symbol)
        if links.resolved_type = null
        {
            let t this.get_type_of_variable_or_parameter_or_property_worker(symbol)
            if t = null
                return null
            ; "For a contextually typed parameter it is possible that a type has
            ; already been assigned … we need to _prefer_ that type, but it won't
            ; be assigned until contextual typing is complete" — the reference's own
            ; comment, and the RE-READ of links.resolvedType is what it is about.
            if links.resolved_type = null
            {
                let sensitive this.is_parameter_of_context_sensitive_signature(symbol)
                if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                    return null
                if sensitive = false
                    set links.resolved_type: t
            }
            return t
        }
        links.resolved_type
    }

    ; isParameterOfContextSensitiveSignature.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST CONJUNCT IS THE WHOLE OF IT TODAY, AND THAT IS A PROVABLE
    ; UNREACHABILITY RATHER THAN A GAP. isContextSensitiveFunctionOrObjectLiteral
    ; Method opens `IsFunctionExpressionOrArrowFunction(fn) || IsObjectLiteralMethod
    ; (fn)`, and NO ARM OF THIS PORT WALKS A PARAMETER OF ANY OF THE THREE: a
    ; function expression and an arrow function are EXPRESSIONS, so the only route
    ; into their parameter list is checkExpression, which stops; an object-literal
    ; method is reached the same way. What this port does walk — a function or
    ; method DECLARATION, a signature, a function TYPE node — fails the test at its
    ; first term. So the report below stands in place and cannot fire, which is why
    ; it is a report and not a guess: the slice that ports checkExpression is the
    ; slice that makes it observable.
    function is_parameter_of_context_sensitive_signature(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns bool
    {
        let vd Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
        if vd = null
            return false
        var decl vd as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(decl) = KindBindingElement
        {
            let up Checker.walk_up_binding_elements_and_patterns(decl)
            if up = null
                return false
            set decl: up as ref[AstNode]
        }
        if AstNode.kind_of(decl) <> KindParameter
            return false
        let fn AstNode.parent_node_of(decl)
        if fn = null
            return false
        this.is_context_sensitive_function_or_object_literal_method(fn as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; ast.WalkUpBindingElementsAndPatterns — from a binding element up through the
    ; patterns to the variable declaration or parameter that roots them. It is the
    ; same climb Binder.get_root_declaration makes and is written here because the
    ; reference's spelling at this call site is the walk rather than the root.
    function walk_up_binding_elements_and_patterns(binding: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var node AstNode.parent_node_of(binding)
        while node <> null
        {
            let n node as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindBindingElement
            {
                set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
                continue
            }
            if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(n)
            {
                set node: AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
                continue
            }
            return n
        }
        null
    }

    function is_context_sensitive_function_or_object_literal_method(this, fn: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(fn)
        var admits false
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            set admits: true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            set admits: true
        if Checker.is_object_literal_method(fn)
            set admits: true
        if admits = false
            return false
        ; isContextSensitiveFunctionLikeDeclaration — three syntactic walks over
        ; expressions (HasContextSensitiveParameters, the return-expression walk and
        ; the yield one). See the header: no input reaches this line today.
        this.record_unported("is-context-sensitive-function-like-declaration", k)
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsObjectLiteralMethod — a MethodDeclaration whose parent is an object
    ; literal, which is what separates it from a class member of the same kind.
    function is_object_literal_method(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindMethodDeclaration
            return false
        Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(n)) = KindObjectLiteralExpression
    }

    ; getTypeOfVariableOrParameterOrPropertyWorker.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE RESOLUTION PUSH IS THE REFERENCE'S CIRCULARITY GUARD AND IT IS LIVE
    ; HERE: `var x: typeof x` pushes, re-enters through its own annotation and the
    ; second push finds the cycle. Both exits — the failed push and the failed pop
    ; — go through reportCircularityError, which is a DIAGNOSTIC and therefore this
    ; slice's product rather than a stop.
    ;
    ; ★★ EVERY REPORTING EXIT AFTER THE PUSH POPS FIRST, and that line is OURS
    ; rather than the reference's — slice 67's g19 priced the same shape: a report
    ; is an exit the reference does not have, so its unwinding is ours to write,
    ; and a leaked frame can only bite a LATER push in the same file.
    ;
    ; ★ Two arms of the reference's prefix have no input here and say so at their
    ; line rather than being left out: `symbol == c.requireSymbol` (this port has no
    ; require symbol — it comes from a lib this compilation does not load) and the
    ; JSON source file (a SourceFile is a value declaration only for a JSON module,
    ; which needs a program).
    function get_type_of_variable_or_parameter_or_property_worker(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        ; ★★★ THE MARK, NOT THE FLAG — see unported_count's own note. The flag says
        ; whether the UNIT has stopped and this chapter has to know whether THIS
        ; call did, because its nil is ambiguous. Both wrong readings were built and
        ; measured: an absolute is_unported() test made every declaration after a
        ; file's first report a silent no-op, and a before/after test on the FLAG
        ; invented a TS7005 for each of them (377 units red).
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let f Symbol.flags_of(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsPrototype) <> 0
        {
            ; getTypeOfPrototypeProperty — the declared type of the symbol's PARENT,
            ; instantiated with `any` per type parameter. The parent walk is
            ; getParentOfSymbol, which this port does not have.
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-prototype-property", 0)
            return null
        }
        ; `symbol == c.requireSymbol` — see the header.
        let vd Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
        if vd = null
        {
            ; debug.Assert(symbol.ValueDeclaration != nil). An assertion is a claim
            ; that the input is impossible, so it is reported rather than trapped
            ; (§3.5v) — the treatment typeResolutionHasProperty's tail gets.
            this.record_unported("value-declaration-of-variable-symbol", f)
            return null
        }
        let declaration vd as ref[AstNode]
        let dk AstNode.kind_of(declaration)
        if dk = KindSourceFile
        {
            ; isJsonSourceFile — see the header.
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-json-source-file", 0)
            return null
        }
        if this.push_type_resolution(symbol, null, TypeSystemPropertyNameType) = false
            return this.report_circularity_error(symbol)
        if (f & SymbolFlagsModuleExports) <> 0
        {
            ; The `module.exports` symbol: its own `exports` member resolves through
            ; resolveExternalModuleSymbol and everything else becomes an anonymous
            ; type over the symbol's MEMBERS — newAnonymousType with a member table,
            ; which is the object-type dimension rather than this one.
            this.pop_type_resolution_defensively()
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-module-exports", 0)
            return null
        }
        var result: ref[Type]? null
        var variable_like false
        if dk = KindParameter
            set variable_like: true
        if dk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            set variable_like: true
        if dk = KindPropertySignature
            set variable_like: true
        if dk = KindVariableDeclaration
            set variable_like: true
        if dk = KindBindingElement
            set variable_like: true
        if variable_like = false
        {
            ; The eight remaining arms, each of them an EXPRESSION check or a
            ; declaration kind this port does not reach: PropertyAssignment,
            ; ShorthandPropertyAssignment, MethodDeclaration (an object-literal
            ; method), ExportAssignment, the two assignment-declaration kinds
            ; (BinaryExpression and CallExpression), JsxAttribute and EnumMember.
            ; The reference's default arm is a panic.
            this.pop_type_resolution_defensively()
            this.record_unported("get-type-of-variable-worker-arm", dk)
            return null
        }
        set result: this.get_widened_type_for_variable_like_declaration(declaration, true)
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
        {
            this.pop_type_resolution_defensively()
            return null
        }
        if this.pop_type_resolution() = false
            return this.report_circularity_error(symbol)
        result
    }

    ; The unwinding the header names. It is not popTypeResolution's own answer that
    ; matters here — the frame is being discarded because the unit is over — so the
    ; result is deliberately dropped.
    procedure pop_type_resolution_defensively(this)
    {
        let popped this.pop_type_resolution()
    }

    ; reportCircularityError. Its two message ARGUMENTS are symbolToString calls
    ; and no instrument this package has compares one (slice 68's note), so the
    ; reports are their code and their location alone.
    ;
    ; ★ The alias branch needs getDeclarationOfAliasSymbol and sits behind a symbol
    ; flag this arm cannot carry — getTypeOfSymbol routes an alias to its own arm —
    ; so it is reported rather than written: the caller that can reach it is
    ; getTypeOfAlias, which is a work-list row of its own.
    function report_circularity_error(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let vd Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol)
        if vd = null
        {
            if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsAlias) <> 0
            {
                this.record_unported("get-declaration-of-alias-symbol", 0)
                return null
            }
            ; "Circularities could also result from parameters in function
            ; expressions that end up having themselves as contextual types … we
            ; have already reported an implicit any error so we don't report
            ; anything here."
            return any_type
        }
        let declaration vd as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.type_of(declaration) <> null
        {
            this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagX_0_is_referenced_directly_or_indirectly_in_its_own_type_annotation)
            return error_type
        }
        if Checker.no_implicit_any()
        {
            var report true
            if AstNode.kind_of(declaration) = KindParameter
            {
                if AstNode.initializer_of(declaration) = null
                    set report: false
            }
            if report
                this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagX_0_implicitly_has_type_any_because_it_does_not_have_a_type_annotation_and_is_referenced_directly_or_indirectly_in_its_own_initializer)
        }
        any_type
    }

    ; getWidenedTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration — one line upstream, and the two
    ; halves it composes are the whole of this slice.
    function get_widened_type_for_variable_like_declaration(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], report_errors: bool) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let t this.get_type_for_variable_like_declaration(declaration, true, CheckModeNormal)
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        this.widen_type_for_variable_like_declaration(t, declaration, report_errors)
    }

    ; getTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration — "the inferred type for a variable,
    ; parameter, or property declaration".
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS ANSWER IS NULLABLE IN THE REFERENCE TOO, so this port's two nulls
    ; MEAN different things here: `null` with is_unported() set is a stop, and
    ; `null` without it is the reference's own *"No type specified and nothing can
    ; be inferred"* — which the widener turns into `any` plus an implicit-any
    ; report. Every caller therefore compares is_unported() rather than the value.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE checkMode PARAMETER IS CARRIED SINCE SLICE 72, and slice 69's reason
    ; for leaving it out is recorded there rather than deleted: its two readers
    ; upstream were checkExpressionEx on a for-in right-hand side and
    ; checkDeclarationInitializer, and while BOTH were stops the parameter was a
    ; claim about a signature nothing could exercise (§3.5ar). The second is ported
    ; now, so the alternative is not an absent claim but a FOLDED CONSTANT — every
    ; call would pass Normal because that is all the port has, and a folded constant
    ; leaves nothing to grep. The one non-Normal producer, getTypeForBindingElement-
    ; Parent's CheckModeRestBindingElement, is still behind a stop, so nothing
    ; observable moves. includeOptionality was always carried, because the reference
    ; calls this with both values.
    function get_type_for_variable_like_declaration(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], include_optionality: bool, check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let dk AstNode.kind_of(declaration)
        if dk = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            ; "A variable declared in a for..in statement is of type string, or of
            ; type keyof T when the right hand expression is of a type parameter
            ; type." The GRANDPARENT is the statement, because the parent is the
            ; declaration LIST.
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(declaration)
            let gk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
            if gk = KindForInStatement
            {
                this.record_unported("get-index-type", gk)
                return null
            }
            if gk = KindForOfStatement
            {
                this.record_unported("check-right-hand-side-of-for-of", gk)
                return null
            }
        }
        else
        {
            if dk = KindBindingElement
            {
                this.record_unported("get-type-for-binding-element-parent", dk)
                return null
            }
        }
        var is_property false
        if dk = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(declaration, ModifierFlagsAccessor) = false
                set is_property: true
        }
        if dk = KindPropertySignature
            set is_property: true
        var is_optional false
        if include_optionality
            set is_optional: Checker.is_optional_declaration(declaration)
        ; Use type from type annotation if one is present.
        let declared_type this.try_get_type_from_type_node(declaration)
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        if Checker.is_catch_clause_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(declaration)
        {
            if declared_type <> null
            {
                let dt declared_type as ref[Type]
                ; "If the catch clause is explicitly annotated with any or unknown,
                ; accept it, otherwise error." The error IS the errorType it
                ; returns; the diagnostic about it belongs to a caller.
                if (dt.flags & TypeFlagsAnyOrUnknown) <> 0
                    return dt
                return error_type
            }
            ; "If the catch clause is not explicitly annotated, treat it as though
            ; it were explicitly annotated with unknown or any, depending on
            ; useUnknownInCatchVariables."
            if Checker.use_unknown_in_catch_variables()
                return unknown_type
            return any_type
        }
        if declared_type <> null
            return this.add_optionality_ex(declared_type as ref[Type], is_property, is_optional)
        if Checker.no_implicit_any()
        {
            if dk = KindVariableDeclaration
            {
                var eligible true
                if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(declaration))
                    set eligible: false
                if (b.combined_modifier_flags(declaration) & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                    set eligible: false
                if (AstNode.flags_of(declaration) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    set eligible: false
                if eligible
                {
                    ; "use control flow tracked 'any' type for non-ambient,
                    ; non-exported var or let variables with no initializer or a
                    ; 'null' or 'undefined' initializer"
                    let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(declaration)
                    if (Binder.combined_node_flags(declaration) & NodeFlagsConstant) = 0
                    {
                        if initializer = null
                            return auto_type
                        if this.is_null_or_undefined(initializer as ref[AstNode])
                            return auto_type
                        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                            return null
                    }
                    ; "Use control flow tracked 'any[]' type for non-ambient,
                    ; non-exported variables with an empty array literal
                    ; initializer."
                    if initializer <> null
                    {
                        if Checker.is_empty_array_literal(initializer as ref[AstNode])
                        {
                            ; autoArrayType is createArrayType(anyType), which needs
                            ; the global Array — from a lib this compilation does
                            ; not load.
                            this.record_unported("auto-array-type", dk)
                            return null
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        ; ★★★ THE PARAMETER ARM FALLS THROUGH, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF TS7006.
        ; Its four questions all answer NOTHING for an ordinary parameter — a set
        ; accessor's paired getter, a JSDoc full signature, a contextual `this` and
        ; a contextual parameter type — and the reference then carries on to the
        ; initializer arm and finally to `return nil`, which is what makes the
        ; widener report. A stop covering the block whole was written first and
        ; MEASURED: it takes the slice's largest report family with it, because
        ; `function f(a) {}` reaches every one of the four and is refused by all of
        ; them at a SYNTACTIC test.
        if dk = KindParameter
        {
            if AstNode.symbol_of(declaration) = null
            {
                ; "parameters of function types defined in JSDoc in TS files don't
                ; have symbols" — a real nil answer rather than a stop.
                return null
            }
            let fnp AstNode.parent_node_of(declaration)
            if fnp = null
                return null
            let fn fnp as ref[AstNode]
            let fk AstNode.kind_of(fn)
            ; "For a parameter of a set accessor, use the type of the get accessor
            ; if one is present"
            if fk = KindSetAccessor
            {
                var bindable false
                if b.has_dynamic_name(fn) = false
                    set bindable: true
                if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                    return null
                if bindable = false
                {
                    ; hasLateBindableName, whose answer is checkComputedPropertyName
                    ; or checkExpressionCached — an expression either way.
                    this.record_unported("has-late-bindable-name", fk)
                    return null
                }
                let acc_sym this.symbol_of_declaration(fn)
                if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                    return null
                if acc_sym <> null
                {
                    let getter Checker.declaration_of_kind(acc_sym as ref[Symbol], KindGetAccessor)
                    if getter <> null
                    {
                        ; getSignatureFromDeclaration, and then either the getter's
                        ; `this` parameter's type or the signature's return type.
                        this.record_unported("get-signature-from-declaration", KindGetAccessor)
                        return null
                    }
                }
            }
            ; getParameterTypeOfFullSignature → getSignatureOfFullSignatureType,
            ; whose three conjuncts are all syntactic: a JavaScript file, one of
            ; four function-like kinds, and a FullSignature slot that is filled. A
            ; TypeScript file answers nothing at the first term.
            if Parser.is_in_js_file(fn)
            {
                var full_kind false
                if fk = KindFunctionDeclaration
                    set full_kind: true
                if fk = KindMethodDeclaration
                    set full_kind: true
                if fk = KindFunctionExpression
                    set full_kind: true
                if fk = KindArrowFunction
                    set full_kind: true
                if full_kind
                {
                    if AstNode.full_signature_of(fn) <> null
                    {
                        this.record_unported("get-signature-of-full-signature-type", fk)
                        return null
                    }
                }
            }
            ; "Use contextual parameter type if one is available". The branch is
            ; chosen on the SYMBOL's name rather than on the syntax, which is the
            ; reference's spelling — InternalSymbolNameThis is the unprefixed word
            ; `this` (ast/symbol.go), so it is one of the few internal names that
            ; is not behind the 0xFE prefix.
            var contextual: ref[Type]? null
            var this_data null as pointer[char]
            let this_len Binder.internal_name_unprefixed(host, "this", &this_data)
            let psym AstNode.symbol_of(declaration)
            var is_this_symbol false
            if psym <> null
            {
                let ps psym as ref[Symbol]
                if SymbolTable.names_equal(Symbol.name_data_of(ps), Symbol.name_len_of(ps), this_data, this_len)
                    set is_this_symbol: true
            }
            if is_this_symbol
                set contextual: this.get_contextual_this_parameter_type(fn)
            else
                set contextual: this.get_contextually_typed_parameter_type(declaration)
            if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                return null
            if contextual <> null
                return this.add_optionality_ex(contextual as ref[Type], false, is_optional)
        }
        ; "Use the type of the initializer expression if one is present and the
        ; declaration is not a parameter of a contextually typed function."
        if AstNode.initializer_of(declaration) <> null
        {
            let init_type this.check_declaration_initializer(declaration, check_mode, null)
            if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                return null
            let widened this.widen_type_inferred_from_initializer(declaration, init_type)
            if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                return null
            if widened = null
                return null
            return this.add_optionality_ex(widened as ref[Type], is_property, is_optional)
        }
        if Checker.no_implicit_any()
        {
            if dk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            {
                ; The control-flow analysis of `this.xxx` assignments in the
                ; constructor or in a static block. Two of its arms are flow types
                ; and the third is getTypeOfPropertyInBaseClass; the arm can also
                ; answer NOTHING, which is why it is a stop rather than a
                ; fallthrough — the difference is observable in the report.
                this.record_unported("get-flow-type-in-constructor", dk)
                return null
            }
        }
        if dk = KindJsxAttribute
        {
            ; "if JSX attribute doesn't have initializer, by default the attribute
            ; will have boolean value of true". trueType needs newLiteralType, i.e.
            ; the value payload initialize_intrinsic_types names as missing.
            this.record_unported("jsx-attribute-true-type", dk)
            return null
        }
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(declaration))
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-from-binding-pattern", dk)
            return null
        }
        ; "No type specified and nothing can be inferred."
        null
    }

    ; widenTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NIL BRANCH IS THIS SLICE'S PRODUCT. A declaration with no annotation,
    ; no initializer and nothing to infer from gets `any` AND AN IMPLICIT-ANY
    ; DIAGNOSTIC — TS7005/7006/7008/7019/7031 — which is the first family of
    ; reports this port produces from the TYPE side rather than from a grammar
    ; check.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE TESTS ON THE NON-NIL SIDE ARE LIVE CODE WITH NO INPUT, and each
    ; names the term that is zero rather than being dropped: `t.flags&ESSymbol`
    ; reaches isGlobalSymbolConstructor, whose global symbol comes from a lib this
    ; compilation does not load; `t.flags&UniqueESSymbol` is set only by
    ; getESSymbolLikeTypeForNode, which no ported arm calls; and
    ; reportErrorsFromWidening stands behind ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType, which
    ; no type this slice can make carries — create_widening_type answers the
    ; NON-widening type under strictNullChecks, which is its own header's finding.
    function widen_type_for_variable_like_declaration(this, t: ref[Type]?, declaration: ref[AstNode], report_errors: bool) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if t <> null
        {
            let ty t as ref[Type]
            if (ty.flags & TypeFlagsESSymbol) <> 0
            {
                ; isGlobalSymbolConstructor — see the header.
                this.record_unported("is-global-symbol-constructor", 0)
                return null
            }
            if report_errors
            {
                if (ty.object_flags & ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType) <> 0
                {
                    ; reportErrorsFromWidening — see the header.
                    this.record_unported("report-errors-from-widening", 0)
                    return null
                }
            }
            if (ty.flags & TypeFlagsUniqueESSymbol) <> 0
            {
                ; the unique-symbol rewrite — see the header.
                this.record_unported("unique-es-symbol-widening", 0)
                return null
            }
            return this.get_widened_type(ty)
        }
        ; "Rest parameters default to type any[], other parameters default to type
        ; any"
        if AstNode.kind_of(declaration) = KindParameter
        {
            if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(declaration) <> null
            {
                ; anyArrayType — createArrayType over the global Array again.
                this.record_unported("any-array-type", KindParameter)
                return null
            }
        }
        ; "Report implicit any errors unless this is a private property within an
        ; ambient declaration"
        if report_errors
        {
            if this.declaration_belongs_to_private_ambient_member(declaration) = false
                this.report_implicit_any(declaration, any_type, WideningKindNormal)
        }
        any_type
    }

    ; ── slice 72: the declaration's INITIALIZER ──────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkDeclarationInitializer and the widening in front of it. The work list's
    ; head — `check-declaration-initializer`, 202 units at stage 1 — is ONE
    ; statement of getTypeOfVariableOrParameterOrPropertyWorker:
    ;
    ;   t := c.widenTypeInferredFromInitializer(declaration,
    ;            c.checkDeclarationInitializer(declaration, checkMode, nil))
    ;   return c.addOptionalityEx(t, isProperty, isOptional)
    ;
    ; and both halves of it are this slice. What it buys is the type of `let x = e`
    ; for every `e` slice 71 gave a type to, and what it costs is the widening
    ; decision on top: a literal type is FRESH out of the expression dispatch, and
    ; a non-const declaration widens it back to its base type. So `let s = "a"` is
    ; `string`, `const s = "a"` is `"a"`, and the difference is one bit read off
    ; the declaration.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS IS WHERE SLICE 71's DEFERRED PAIR HALF EXPIRES, and only half.
    ; That slice stored `regular_type` and `fresh_type` on a literal type and wrote
    ; down that neither getRegularTypeOfLiteralType nor isFreshLiteralType was
    ; ported, both being "behind the same door". getWidenedLiteralType is that
    ; door, and it opens for exactly ONE of them: it asks isFreshLiteralType five
    ; times and never asks getRegularTypeOfLiteralType at all. ★So `fresh_type`
    ; gains its second reader here and `regular_type` still has NONE — its only
    ; reader in the whole reference is getRegularTypeOfLiteralType, whose own only
    ; callers are getRegularTypeOfExpression and getBaseTypeOfLiteralType. The
    ; phantom-row argument for leaving that function out therefore still holds, and
    ; the field's note is corrected rather than retired.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND SLICE 71's ATTRIBUTION OF THE TWO SLOTS WAS WRONG BY ONE, which is
    ; only visible from here: it wrote that `regular_type` is read by
    ; getRegularTypeOfLiteralType AND isFreshLiteralType. isFreshLiteralType is
    ; `t.flags&Freshable != 0 && t.AsLiteralType().freshType == t` — it reads
    ; `freshType`. A slot's reader list is the kind of claim that reads as checked
    ; and is not, so it is fixed at the field.

    ; checkDeclarationInitializer, whole — the two padding arms included, each as
    ; the gate it is rather than as a comment.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE checkMode PARAMETER IS CARRIED HERE AND THROUGH
    ; getTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration, WHICH IS A REVERSAL, and §3.5ar is the
    ; reason. Slice 69's header left it out with an argument that was true then:
    ; *"its two readers upstream are checkExpressionEx on a for-in right-hand side
    ; and checkDeclarationInitializer, and both are stops in this slice — so the
    ; parameter would be a claim about a signature nothing can exercise."* One of
    ; the two readers is this function. Leaving the parameter out now would not
    ; leave a claim behind, it would FOLD A CONSTANT: every call would pass Normal
    ; because that is all the port has, and §3.5ar's second half is precisely the
    ; measurement that a folded constant leaves nothing to grep and no comment to
    ; expire. ★The non-Normal producer is still exactly one and still a stop —
    ; getTypeForBindingElementParent passes CheckModeRestBindingElement for a rest
    ; binding element — so nothing OBSERVABLE changes; the signature now matches
    ; the reference's and the day that stop lifts, the value flows on its own.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CONTEXTUAL BRANCH IS A STOP AND ITS ARGUMENT IS ITS OWN CALLER LIST:
    ; every one of the reference's five other callers passes nil for contextualType
    ; except getTypeFromBindingElement, which is behind `get-type-from-binding-
    ; pattern`. So the parameter is carried, the branch is transcribed, and the
    ; report names checkExpressionWithContextualType rather than pretending the
    ; null path is the whole function.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO PADDING ARMS ARE PROVABLY INERT AND ARE WRITTEN AS GATES ANYWAY,
    ; the way check_expression_ex writes its two: isObjectLiteralType is
    ; `objectFlags&ObjectLiteral`, isTupleType is `objectFlags&Reference &&
    ; Target().objectFlags&Tuple`, and NOTHING in this port sets either
    ; ObjectFlagsObjectLiteral or ObjectFlagsTuple — a `grep` over checker.scaly
    ; finds both names only in their own `define` and in tests of them. The
    ; producers are checkObjectLiteral and createTupleType, both a dimension away.
    ; So the slice that gives either flag a producer meets a report instead of a
    ; silently skipped step.
    function check_declaration_initializer(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int, contextual_type: ref[Type]?) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(declaration)
        if initializer = null
            return null
        ; ★`init` is a reserved word here (§3.5d's family), so the local is named
        ; for what it is rather than for the reference's `initializer`.
        let expr initializer as ref[AstNode]
        var t this.get_quick_type_of_expression(expr)
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        if t = null
        {
            if contextual_type <> null
            {
                ; checkExpressionWithContextualType — pushContextualType, the
                ; inference context and a checkExpressionEx under
                ; CheckModeContextual. Its only reachable caller is above.
                this.record_unported("check-expression-with-contextual-type", AstNode.kind_of(expr))
                return null
            }
            set t: this.check_expression_cached_ex(expr, check_mode)
            if t = null
                return null
        }
        let ty t as ref[Type]
        if Binder.is_part_of_parameter_declaration(declaration)
        {
            let name AstNode.name_of(declaration)
            let nk Checker.kind_or_unknown(name)
            if nk = KindObjectBindingPattern
            {
                if Checker.is_object_literal_type(ty)
                {
                    this.record_unported("pad-object-literal-type", nk)
                    return null
                }
            }
            if nk = KindArrayBindingPattern
            {
                if Checker.is_tuple_type(ty)
                {
                    this.record_unported("pad-tuple-type", nk)
                    return null
                }
            }
        }
        ty
    }

    ; isObjectLiteralType and isTupleType, both one line and both false today —
    ; see the header above for why they are here at all.
    function is_object_literal_type(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
        (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsObjectLiteral) <> 0

    function is_tuple_type(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsReference) = 0
            return false
        ; `t.Target()` is the reference's target slot. ObjectFlagsReference DOES
        ; have producers here (slice 66's type reference), so this conjunct is the
        ; live one and the tuple bit is what cannot be set.
        let target Checker.target_of(t)
        if target = null
            return false
        ((target as ref[Type]).object_flags & ObjectFlagsTuple) <> 0
    }

    ; checkExpressionCachedEx. ★★ THE MEMO IS STILL NOT ONE and the reader that
    ; would change that is still the one slice 70 named — the first arm that writes
    ; an error into a C section twice for one node. What this slice adds is the
    ; MODE ARGUMENT, which is the half of the function that is not the cache: with
    ; anything other than Normal the reference does not consult the cache at all,
    ; so forwarding the mode is the whole body under every value the port can pass.
    function check_expression_cached_ex(this, node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]?
        this.check_expression_ex(node, check_mode)

    ; getQuickTypeOfExpression — "optimize for the common case", five arms over a
    ; parenthesis-stripped expression.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS NULL MEANS *NO QUICK ANSWER* AND NOT *I STOPPED*, so this is slice
    ; 69's two-meaning nil again and the caller takes a MARK rather than testing
    ; the value. That is forced rather than chosen: the reference's own answer is
    ; nil for every expression that is none of the five shapes, and
    ; checkDeclarationInitializer's next line depends on telling that apart from a
    ; stop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LAST ARM READS `node` AND EVERY OTHER ARM READS `expr`, and it is the
    ; reference's own asymmetry rather than a transcription slip: `case
    ; ast.IsLiteralExpression(node) || ast.IsBooleanLiteral(node)`. So `("a")` gets
    ; NO quick answer while `"a"` does — both end in the same type through
    ; checkExpressionCached, but only one of them takes this path, and a port that
    ; "fixed" it would answer identically and stop reproducing the reference's
    ; call graph.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ASSERTION ARM IS LIVE, and it is the only arm here that is not a
    ; literal: `x as string` answers getTypeFromTypeNode of the type node, whose
    ; keyword arms slice 69 ported. `x as const` is excluded by IsConstTypeReference
    ; and falls through to the dispatch, where KindAsExpression reports.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CALL ARM's FOURTH CONJUNCT IS THE ONLY THING IN THIS FUNCTION THAT
    ; NEEDS A GLOBAL, and it is reported at exactly the term that needs one rather
    ; than in front of the arm: isSymbolOrSymbolForCall is two syntactic tests and
    ; then `globalESSymbol == c.resolveName(left, "Symbol", …)`. A call whose callee
    ; is not the identifier `Symbol` (or `Symbol.for`) fails a syntactic test, so
    ; the term is PROVED false and the arm's own report stands; a call to `Symbol`
    ; cannot be decided here, and the difference matters — the conjunct chooses
    ; between this arm's stop and the dispatch's `check-call-expression`, i.e.
    ; between two rows of the work list.
    function get_quick_type_of_expression(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let e Parser.skip_parentheses(node)
        if e = null
            return null
        let expr e as ref[AstNode]
        let ek AstNode.kind_of(expr)
        if ek = KindAwaitExpression
        {
            ; `t := c.getQuickTypeOfExpression(expr.Expression()); if t != nil {
            ; return c.getAwaitedType(t) }; return nil` — the RECURSION is ported
            ; and the awaited type is not, which is what makes the report exact:
            ; only an await whose operand HAS a quick type reaches getAwaitedType,
            ; and the rest answers nothing here and lands on the dispatch's
            ; `check-await-expression`.
            let inner AstNode.expression_of(expr)
            if inner = null
                return null
            let mark this.unported_mark()
            let inner_type this.get_quick_type_of_expression(inner as ref[AstNode])
            if this.unported_mark() <> mark
                return null
            if inner_type <> null
            {
                this.record_unported("get-awaited-type", ek)
                return null
            }
            return null
        }
        if ek = KindCallExpression
        {
            var eligible true
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.expression_of(expr)) = KindSuperKeyword
                set eligible: false
            if Parser.is_require_call(expr, true)
                set eligible: false
            if Parser.is_import_call(expr)
                set eligible: false
            if eligible
            {
                if this.symbol_call_undecidable(expr)
                {
                    ; getGlobalESSymbolConstructorSymbolOrNil and resolveName — the
                    ; globals table, which this port does not build.
                    this.record_unported("get-global-es-symbol-constructor-symbol", ek)
                    return null
                }
                ; isCallChain picks getReturnTypeOfSingleNonGenericSignatureOf-
                ; CallChain and everything else getReturnTypeOfSingleNonGeneric-
                ; Signature; both end in getSingleSignature, so the fork is one row.
                this.record_unported("get-return-type-of-single-non-generic-signature", ek)
                return null
            }
        }
        if ek = KindNewExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("get-return-type-of-single-non-generic-signature", ek)
            return null
        }
        var assertion false
        if ek = KindTypeAssertionExpression
            set assertion: true
        if ek = KindAsExpression
            set assertion: true
        if assertion
        {
            let type_node AstNode.type_of(expr)
            if type_node <> null
            {
                if Parser.is_const_type_reference(type_node) = false
                    return this.get_type_from_type_node(type_node as ref[AstNode])
            }
        }
        ; ★ `node`, not `expr` — see the header.
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        var literal false
        if k >= KindFirstLiteralToken
        {
            if k <= KindLastLiteralToken
                set literal: true
        }
        if k = KindTrueKeyword
            set literal: true
        if k = KindFalseKeyword
            set literal: true
        if literal
            return this.check_expression(node)
        null
    }

    ; isSymbolOrSymbolForCall, up to the term that needs a global — see
    ; get_quick_type_of_expression's header. TRUE means *the syntactic half holds
    ; and the answer is a global lookup away*, which is a stop; false means the
    ; reference's own answer is false.
    function symbol_call_undecidable(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindCallExpression
            return false
        var left AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if left = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(left as ref[AstNode]) = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            let nm AstNode.property_access_name_of(left as ref[AstNode])
            if nm <> null
            {
                if AstNode.text_is(nm as ref[AstNode], "for")
                    set left: AstNode.expression_of(left as ref[AstNode])
            }
        }
        if left = null
            return false
        let l left as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(l) <> KindIdentifier
            return false
        AstNode.text_is(l, "Symbol")
    }

    ; widenTypeInferredFromInitializer, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS SECOND HALF IS A JAVASCRIPT-ONLY IMPLICIT-ANY REPORT, and both of
    ; its tests are decided WITHOUT the globals table — one exactly and one by a
    ; sufficient condition that is proved rather than asserted (§3.5ag).
    ; isEmptyLiteralType is `t == c.implicitNeverType` under strictNullChecks,
    ; which is a pointer comparison against an intrinsic this port builds, so the
    ; arm is transcribed whole and is live code with no input today — nothing
    ; answers implicitNeverType, whose producers are the empty array literal and
    ; the object-literal inference. isEmptyArrayLiteralType goes through
    ; getElementTypeOfArrayType, whose first conjunct is isArrayType's
    ; `objectFlags&Reference`: a type WITHOUT that bit answers nil whatever
    ; globalArrayType is, so the false answer is proved and only a reference-typed
    ; widened type needs the global.
    function widen_type_inferred_from_initializer(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], t: ref[Type]?) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if t = null
            return null
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        let widened this.get_widened_literal_type_for_initializer(declaration, t as ref[Type])
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        if widened = null
            return null
        let w widened as ref[Type]
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(declaration)
        {
            if this.is_empty_literal_type(w)
            {
                this.report_implicit_any(declaration, any_type, WideningKindNormal)
                return any_type
            }
            if (w.object_flags & ObjectFlagsReference) <> 0
            {
                ; getElementTypeOfArrayType → isArrayType → globalArrayType.
                this.record_unported("get-element-type-of-array-type", AstNode.kind_of(declaration))
                return null
            }
            ; anyArrayType is createArrayType over the same global, so the arm it
            ; guards is behind the row above rather than beside it.
        }
        w
    }

    ; isEmptyLiteralType. strictNullChecks is TRUE under this harness, so the
    ; second arm is the dead one — and it is transcribed because the option is a
    ; harness fact and not a language one.
    function is_empty_literal_type(this, t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.strict_null_checks()
            return t = implicit_never_type
        t = undefined_widening_type
    }

    ; getWidenedLiteralTypeForInitializer — the one bit that decides whether a
    ; literal type survives.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MEMO IN FRONT OF getCombinedNodeFlags IS SKIPPED WITH ITS ARGUMENT:
    ; `c.lastGetCombinedNodeFlagsNode`/`Result` is a one-entry cache over a pure
    ; function of the node, so it can change no answer, and is_var_const_like above
    ; already calls Binder.combined_node_flags directly. A two-field cache that
    ; cannot be observed is two fields that cannot be wrong and cannot be right.
    function get_widened_literal_type_for_initializer(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if (Binder.combined_node_flags(declaration) & NodeFlagsConstant) <> 0
            return t
        if this.is_declaration_readonly(declaration)
            return t
        this.get_widened_literal_type(t)
    }

    ; getWidenedLiteralType — five freshness arms and a union.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THREE OF THE FIVE ARE LIVE AND THIS IS THE FIRST WIDENING IN THE PORT
    ; THAT ANSWERS A DIFFERENT TYPE THAN IT WAS GIVEN. A fresh string literal
    ; becomes `string`, a fresh number literal `number`, a fresh bigint literal
    ; `bigint` — and the freshness is what makes the answer depend on the
    ; DECLARATION and not only on the expression, which is the whole content of the
    ; caller above.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE BOOLEAN ARM IS THE ONE STOP THAT IS REACHABLE, and it is reachable
    ; often: `let b = true` gets the fresh true type from the dispatch and needs
    ; `booleanType` to widen to. That type is `getUnionType([regularFalse,
    ; regularTrue])` and is still the same missing union slice 71 left named at the
    ; field and at type_to_string's Boolean arm. So this slice makes a THIRD reader
    ; for it, and all three report.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ENUM-LIKE AND UNION ARMS ARE INERT FOR THE SAME REASON THEIR FLAGS
    ; ARE: nothing here sets TypeFlagsEnum, TypeFlagsEnumLiteral or TypeFlagsUnion
    ; on a type. They are gates with reports rather than comments, so the slice that
    ; makes an enum member type or a union meets one.
    ;
    ; ★ THE ORDER IS THE REFERENCE'S SWITCH ORDER AND IT MATTERS ONCE: EnumLike is
    ; tested before the four literal kinds, and an enum LITERAL type carries both
    ; TypeFlagsEnumLiteral and its base literal's flag, so a reordering would widen
    ; an enum member to `string` or `number` instead of to its enum.
    function get_widened_literal_type(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let fresh Checker.is_fresh_literal_type(t)
        if fresh
        {
            if (t.flags & TypeFlagsEnumLike) <> 0
            {
                ; getBaseTypeOfEnumLikeType → getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol of the
                ; member's parent.
                this.record_unported("get-base-type-of-enum-like-type", t.flags)
                return null
            }
            if (t.flags & TypeFlagsStringLiteral) <> 0
                return string_type
            if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNumberLiteral) <> 0
                return number_type
            if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBigIntLiteral) <> 0
                return bigint_type
            if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral) <> 0
            {
                ; c.booleanType — see the header.
                this.record_unported("boolean-type", t.flags)
                return null
            }
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUnion) <> 0
        {
            ; mapType over getWidenedLiteralType, i.e. getUnionType again.
            this.record_unported("map-type", t.flags)
            return null
        }
        t
    }

    ; isFreshLiteralType, whole. ★It reads `fresh_type`, which is the correction
    ; the chapter header records: a literal type is FRESH exactly when its fresh
    ; slot points at itself, and the regular member of the pair points at the fresh
    ; one only in the boolean case, where both are built eagerly.
    function is_fresh_literal_type(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsFreshable) = 0
            return false
        let f Checker.literal_fresh_type_of(t)
        if f = null
            return false
        (f as ref[Type]) = t
    }

    ; getContextuallyTypedParameterType. ★★★ ITS FIRST LINE IS THE WHOLE FUNCTION
    ; TODAY: isContextSensitiveFunctionOrObjectLiteralMethod refuses every
    ; parameter this port walks (see its own header), so the IIFE branch and the
    ; contextual-signature branch behind it are unreachable rather than unwritten.
    function get_contextually_typed_parameter_type(this, parameter: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let fn AstNode.parent_node_of(parameter)
        if fn = null
            return null
        if this.is_context_sensitive_function_or_object_literal_method(fn as ref[AstNode]) = false
            return null
        ; getEffectiveCallArguments / getContextualSignature — the call dimension.
        this.record_unported("get-contextual-signature", KindParameter)
        null
    }

    ; getContextualThisParameterType, and every one of its tests is syntactic until
    ; the one that reports.
    ;
    ; ★★ noImplicitThis IS TRUE, so the second half of this function is LIVE — it
    ; is a strict-FAMILY option, the fourth one strict_null_checks' header names.
    ; What keeps it quiet is the shape: an object-literal container or an
    ; `obj.xxx = function (…)` assignment, both of which stop, and everything else
    ; answers nothing.
    function get_contextual_this_parameter_type(this, fn: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let mark this.unported_mark()
        if AstNode.kind_of(fn) = KindArrowFunction
            return null
        if this.is_context_sensitive_function_or_object_literal_method(fn)
        {
            this.record_unported("get-contextual-signature", AstNode.kind_of(fn))
            return null
        }
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        var considered: bool Checker.no_implicit_this()
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(fn)
            set considered: true
        if considered
        {
            if Checker.containing_object_literal(fn) <> null
            {
                ; getApparentTypeOfContextualType and the ThisType<T> lookup behind
                ; it, then checkExpressionCached on the literal itself.
                this.record_unported("get-this-type-of-object-literal", AstNode.kind_of(fn))
                return null
            }
            ; "In an assignment of the form 'obj.xxx = function(...)' or
            ; 'obj[xxx] = function(...)', the contextual type for 'this' is 'obj'."
            let parent Checker.walk_up_parenthesized_expressions(AstNode.parent_node_of(fn))
            if Parser.is_assignment_expression_node(parent)
            {
                let target AstNode.binary_left_of(parent as ref[AstNode])
                if Parser.is_access_expression(target)
                {
                    this.record_unported("check-expression-cached", AstNode.kind_of(target as ref[AstNode]))
                    return null
                }
            }
        }
        null
    }

    ; getContainingObjectLiteral — two shapes, and the second reaches one level
    ; further up (a function EXPRESSION is the property assignment's initializer,
    ; so the literal is its grandparent).
    function containing_object_literal(f: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(f)
        var member false
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set member: true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            set member: true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            set member: true
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(f)
        if member
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindObjectLiteralExpression
                return parent
            return null
        }
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindPropertyAssignment
                return AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.WalkUpParenthesizedExpressions.
    function walk_up_parenthesized_expressions(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var n node
        while n <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(n as ref[AstNode]) <> KindParenthesizedExpression
                return n
            set n: AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        }
        null
    }

    ; getWidenedType / getWidenedTypeWithContext, reduced to the gate that decides
    ; whether there is anything to do. ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening is the union of
    ; the two "contains" bits, and no type this slice makes carries either — so the
    ; identity arm is the ported one and the five widening arms (any/nullable, the
    ; object literal, the union, the intersection and the array or tuple) stand
    ; behind one report. The memo on CachedTypeKindWidened goes with them: it keys
    ; a computation that cannot run.
    function get_widened_type(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("get-widened-type", t.flags)
            return null
        }
        t
    }

    ; addOptionalityEx. getOptionalType is a UNION and this port has none, so the
    ; optional half is one report — and it stands behind strictNullChecks, which is
    ; TRUE here, so `x?: string` reaches it and `x: string` does not.
    function add_optionality_ex(this, t: ref[Type], is_property: bool, is_optional: bool) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        if Checker.strict_null_checks()
        {
            if is_optional
            {
                ; getOptionalType's own first test is against undefinedOrMissingType
                ; or undefinedType, which is a plain identity — but its answer is
                ; getUnionType in every other case, and is_property is what selects
                ; between the two sentinels. Reported whole rather than half.
                this.record_unported("get-optional-type", t.flags)
                return null
            }
        }
        t
    }

    ; ── getTypeFromTypeNode, and the arms that need no printer ───────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE KEYWORD ARMS ARE PORTED AND NOTHING ELSE, and the boundary is the one
    ; type_to_string already draws: an intrinsic answers a NAME the printer prints
    ; unchanged, and every other arm makes a type the printer cannot render. So the
    ; two halves of the checker dimension are cut at the same place, on purpose —
    ; porting an arm here whose result the T section cannot print would move a unit
    ; from an honest UNPORTED to a FAIL.
    ;
    ; ★ The `get-type-from-type-node` tag is NOT new: eleven call sites in this file
    ; already report it, and they keep reporting it for the arms below that stay
    ; unported. What changes is that the tag names the ARMS rather than the
    ; function.
    function try_get_type_from_type_node(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if type_node = null
            return null
        this.get_type_from_type_node(type_node as ref[AstNode])
    }

    function get_type_from_type_node(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let t this.get_type_from_type_node_worker(node)
        if t = null
            return null
        this.get_conditional_flow_type_of_type(t as ref[Type], node)
    }

    function get_type_from_type_node_worker(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindAnyKeyword
            return any_type
        if k = KindJSDocAllType
            return any_type
        if k = KindUnknownKeyword
            return unknown_type
        if k = KindStringKeyword
            return string_type
        if k = KindNumberKeyword
            return number_type
        if k = KindBigIntKeyword
            return bigint_type
        if k = KindSymbolKeyword
            return es_symbol_type
        if k = KindVoidKeyword
            return void_type
        if k = KindUndefinedKeyword
            return undefined_type
        if k = KindNullKeyword
            return null_type
        if k = KindNeverKeyword
            return never_type
        if k = KindObjectKeyword
            return non_primitive_type
        if k = KindIntrinsicKeyword
            return intrinsic_marker_type
        ; ★ KindBooleanKeyword IS NOT HERE, and its absence is a measurement rather
        ; than an omission: booleanType is a UNION of the two boolean literal types,
        ; and initialize_intrinsic_types' header already names it among the types
        ; this port cannot make. So the keyword reports with its neighbours, and the
        ; same sentence retires the TypePredicate arm — two lines answering voidType
        ; or booleanType, of which the second is that missing union.
        ;
        ; ★ ParenthesizedType and the three JSDoc wrappers RECURSE into
        ; getTypeFromTypeNode and would work; they are left out deliberately,
        ; because each is a one-line forward whose value is exactly the inner arm's,
        ; so porting them buys nothing this slice can print and costs a row the work
        ; list would then have to explain twice.
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", k)
        null
    }

    ; getConditionalFlowTypeOfType — the substitution walk from a type NODE up to
    ; its enclosing statement.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS ONLY EFFECT IS THE CONSTRAINT LIST, so the walk is transcribed and
    ; the two places that would APPEND report instead. That is what makes the port
    ; exact rather than conservative: ending the walk early (or late) changes which
    ; ancestors are examined, and a report at the append is the one point where the
    ; reference's answer stops being `t`.
    ;
    ; ★ `covariant` flips on a PARAMETER parent and is read only by the first arm,
    ; whose guard is `covariant || t is a type variable`. No type this slice makes
    ; is a type variable, so the flag is what decides — and it is transcribed
    ; rather than folded away, because an annotation reached through one parameter
    ; list is one flip.
    function get_conditional_flow_type_of_type(this, t: ref[Type], node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        var n: ref[AstNode]? node
        var covariant true
        while n <> null
        {
            let cur n as ref[AstNode]
            if Checker.is_statement(cur)
                return t
            if AstNode.kind_of(cur) = KindJSDoc
                return t
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(cur)
            if p = null
                return t
            let parent p as ref[AstNode]
            let pk AstNode.kind_of(parent)
            if pk = KindParameter
            {
                if covariant
                    set covariant: false
                else
                    set covariant: true
            }
            var conditional_true false
            if pk = KindConditionalType
            {
                if AstNode.conditional_true_type_of(parent) = cur
                    set conditional_true: true
            }
            var substitutes false
            if conditional_true
            {
                ; "Always substitute on type parameters, regardless of variance,
                ; since even in contravariant positions they may rely on
                ; substituted constraints to be valid"
                if covariant
                    set substitutes: true
                if (t.flags & TypeFlagsTypeVariable) <> 0
                    set substitutes: true
            }
            if substitutes
            {
                this.record_unported("get-implied-constraint", pk)
                return null
            }
            if conditional_true = false
            {
                if (t.flags & TypeFlagsTypeParameter) <> 0
                {
                    if pk = KindMappedType
                    {
                        this.record_unported("get-type-parameter-from-mapped-type", pk)
                        return null
                    }
                }
            }
            set n: parent
        }
        t
    }

    ; ── the predicates this chapter needed ───────────────────────────────────

    ; ast.IsCatchClauseVariableDeclarationOrBindingElement.
    function is_catch_clause_variable_declaration_or_binding_element(declaration: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let root Binder.get_root_declaration(declaration)
        if root = null
            return false
        let n root as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(n) <> KindVariableDeclaration
            return false
        Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(n)) = KindCatchClause
    }

    ; isEmptyArrayLiteral.
    function is_empty_array_literal(expression: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(expression) <> KindArrayLiteralExpression
            return false
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(expression)
        if elements = null
            return true
        AstNode.list_count(elements) = 0
    }

    ; declarationBelongsToPrivateAmbientMember, and isPrivateWithinAmbient with it.
    ; A method rather than a static because the modifier flags live on the binder.
    function declaration_belongs_to_private_ambient_member(this, declaration: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let root Binder.get_root_declaration(declaration)
        if root = null
            return false
        var member root as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(member) = KindParameter
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(member)
            if p = null
                return false
            set member: p as ref[AstNode]
        }
        var private_name false
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsPrivate)
            set private_name: true
        if AstNode.is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(member)
            set private_name: true
        if private_name = false
            return false
        (AstNode.flags_of(member) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
    }

    ; isNullOrUndefined. Its Identifier arm compares the RESOLVED symbol against
    ; the undefined symbol, a name resolution this port does not have — so
    ; `let x = null` answers true, `let x = 1` answers false and `let x = y`
    ; reports.
    function is_null_or_undefined(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let expr Checker.skip_parentheses(node)
        let k AstNode.kind_of(expr)
        if k = KindNullKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("get-resolved-symbol", k)
            return false
        }
        false
    }

    ; ast.SkipParentheses, without the JSDoc-type-assertion exemption: that arm
    ; asks IsJSDocTypeAssertion, whose answer only ever KEEPS more parentheses. It
    ; is named rather than written because its one reader here is the test above,
    ; which reports on the very shape it would preserve.
    function skip_parentheses(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]
    {
        var n node
        while AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindParenthesizedExpression
        {
            let e AstNode.expression_of(n)
            if e = null
                return n
            set n: e as ref[AstNode]
        }
        n
    }

    ; ast.IsStatement — the two kind lists plus the Block test, transcribed. It is
    ; the TERMINATOR of getConditionalFlowTypeOfType's walk and therefore decides
    ; which ancestors that walk examines at all.
    function is_statement(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if Checker.is_statement_kind_but_not_declaration_kind(k)
            return true
        if Checker.is_declaration_statement_kind(k)
            return true
        Checker.is_block_statement(node)
    }

    function is_statement_kind_but_not_declaration_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindBreakStatement
            return true
        if k = KindContinueStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDebuggerStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDoStatement
            return true
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
            return true
        if k = KindEmptyStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForInStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForOfStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForStatement
            return true
        if k = KindIfStatement
            return true
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
            return true
        if k = KindReturnStatement
            return true
        if k = KindSwitchStatement
            return true
        if k = KindThrowStatement
            return true
        if k = KindTryStatement
            return true
        if k = KindVariableStatement
            return true
        if k = KindWhileStatement
            return true
        if k = KindWithStatement
            return true
        k = KindNotEmittedStatement
    }

    function is_declaration_statement_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMissingDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
    }

    ; isBlockStatement — a Block that is neither a try or catch body nor the
    ; Block-like body of a function. Its two parent tests are why IsStatement is
    ; asked of the NODE rather than of the kind.
    function is_block_statement(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindBlock
            return false
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
        if pk = KindTryStatement
            return false
        if pk = KindCatchClause
            return false
        Checker.is_function_block(node) = false
    }

    function is_function_block(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindBlock
            return false
        AstNode.is_function_like(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
    }

    ; ast.IsCheckJSEnabledForFile. ★★★ ITS FIRST TERM IS THE FIELD THE PARSER USED
    ; TO DROP, and the note it dropped it under named this function as the reader
    ; (parser.scaly, extract_pragmas). checkJs is unset under this harness, so the
    ; second term is false and the whole answer is the directive: a JavaScript file
    ; without `// @ts-check` gets no implicit-any diagnostic at all.
    function is_check_js_enabled_for_file(this) returns bool
    {
        let d AstNode.check_js_directive_of(file)
        if d <> CheckJsDirectiveNone
            return d = CheckJsDirectiveEnabled
        Checker.check_js()
    }

    ; reportImplicitAny, whole but for the two things no instrument compares.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENTS ARE NOT BUILT, and here that saves a CALL rather
    ; than a paragraph: `typeAsString := c.TypeToString(c.getWidenedType(t))` runs
    ; BEFORE the switch upstream, and the dump's C line is `C pos end code`. Slice
    ; 68 wrote the rule down; this is the first place it changes what the code does.
    ; The `t` parameter is kept because it is the reference's signature and because
    ; the slice that gives this port a message renderer needs it back.
    ;
    ; ★★ noImplicitAny IS TRUE UNDER THIS HARNESS, so every `errorOrSuggestion(c.
    ; noImplicitAny, …)` here is an ERROR and the five SUGGESTION codes (7043,
    ; 7044, 7045, 7047, 7050) are dead. They are named rather than written: this
    ; port has no suggestion list, and the reference routes a suggestion to one
    ; GetDiagnosticsForFile does not return — so writing them would be inventing a
    ; line no instrument could ever see, which is the single failure diagcheck's
    ; subsequence relation exists to catch.
    ;
    ; ★ THE TS7051 BRANCH IS THE ONE STOP. It fires for a parameter of a call
    ; signature, a method signature or a function type whose name reads as a TYPE,
    ; and the second disjunct of that test is resolveName. The FIRST disjunct is
    ; decidable here (the name is a type keyword) — but it is not asked separately,
    ; because a report has to stand where EITHER disjunct can send the reference,
    ; and a `||` cannot be half-evaluated into a different answer.
    procedure report_implicit_any(this, declaration: ref[AstNode], t: ref[Type], widening_kind: int)
    {
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(declaration)
        {
            ; "Only report implicit any errors/suggestions in TS and ts-check JS
            ; files"
            if this.is_check_js_enabled_for_file() = false
                return
        }
        let dk AstNode.kind_of(declaration)
        var code 0
        if dk = KindBinaryExpression
            set code: DiagMember_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        if dk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            set code: DiagMember_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        if dk = KindPropertySignature
            set code: DiagMember_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        if dk = KindParameter
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(declaration)) = KindIdentifier
            {
                let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(declaration)
                let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
                var signature_like false
                if pk = KindCallSignature
                    set signature_like: true
                if pk = KindMethodSignature
                    set signature_like: true
                if pk = KindFunctionType
                    set signature_like: true
                if signature_like
                {
                    if Checker.parameter_index_of(parent as ref[AstNode], declaration) >= 0
                    {
                        this.record_unported("resolve-name", KindParameter)
                        return
                    }
                }
            }
            if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(declaration) <> null
                set code: DiagRest_parameter_0_implicitly_has_an_any_type
            else
                set code: DiagParameter_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        }
        if dk = KindBindingElement
            set code: DiagBinding_element_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        if Checker.kind_is_function_like_implicit_any(dk)
        {
            if AstNode.name_of(declaration) = null
            {
                if widening_kind = WideningKindGeneratorYield
                    this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagGenerator_implicitly_has_yield_type_0_Consider_supplying_a_return_type_annotation)
                else
                    this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagFunction_expression_which_lacks_return_type_annotation_implicitly_has_an_0_return_type)
                return
            }
            if (AstNode.flags_of(declaration) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0
            {
                this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagThis_overload_implicitly_returns_the_type_0_because_it_lacks_a_return_type_annotation)
                return
            }
            if widening_kind = WideningKindGeneratorYield
                set code: DiagX_0_which_lacks_return_type_annotation_implicitly_has_an_1_yield_type
            else
                set code: DiagX_0_which_lacks_return_type_annotation_implicitly_has_an_1_return_type
        }
        if dk = KindMappedType
        {
            this.error_on_node(declaration, DiagMapped_object_type_implicitly_has_an_any_template_type)
            return
        }
        ; The switch's DEFAULT arm, and it is reached by every kind none of the
        ; labels above named — which is why the variable starts at 0 rather than at
        ; the default code: a kind that IS named and left the code at 0 would be a
        ; missing arm, and this way there is no such shape.
        if code = 0
            set code: DiagVariable_0_implicitly_has_an_1_type
        this.error_on_node(declaration, code)
    }

    ; The reference's fifth switch label, as a predicate: the seven function-like
    ; kinds whose implicit `any` is a RETURN type rather than a value type.
    function kind_is_function_like_implicit_any(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        k = KindArrowFunction
    }

    ; ── the base constructor type ───────────────────────────────────────────

    ; getBaseTypeVariableOfClass. It answers NULL for the ordinary class, and the
    ; caller tells that apart from a REPORT by comparing is_unported() — this
    ; file's idiom everywhere a legitimate nil and a stop share a return type.
    function get_base_type_variable_of_class(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let before this.is_unported()
        let declared this.get_declared_type_of_class_or_interface(symbol)
        if this.is_unported() <> before
            return null
        if declared = null
            return null
        let base this.get_base_constructor_type_of_class(declared as ref[Type])
        if this.is_unported() <> before
            return null
        if base = null
            return null
        let bt base as ref[Type]
        if (bt.flags & TypeFlagsTypeVariable) <> 0
            return bt
        if (bt.flags & TypeFlagsIntersection) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("base-type-variable-of-intersection", 0)
            return null
        }
        null
    }

    ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NO-BASE EARLY RETURN IS 97.5 % OF THE CORPUS'S CLASSES and it is
    ; three lines: a class with no `extends` answers undefinedType and memoises
    ; it. Everything below that line — the resolution push, checkExpression on
    ; the base clause, resolveStructuredTypeMembers, the TS2507 report and its
    ; type-parameter related-info — is behind ONE call this port cannot make yet.
    function get_base_constructor_type_of_class(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let cached Checker.resolved_base_constructor_type_of(t)
        if cached <> null
            return cached
        let base_type_node Checker.get_base_type_node_of_class(t)
        if base_type_node = null
        {
            Checker.set_resolved_base_constructor_type(t, undefined_type)
            return undefined_type
        }
        if this.push_type_resolution(null, t, TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstructorType) = false
            return error_type
        ; ★★ THE POP IS OURS RATHER THAN THE REFERENCE'S, and it is here because
        ; record_unported is first-wins but the WALK CONTINUES: an entry left on
        ; the stack by a reporting arm would sit under every later declaration's
        ; cycle search in the same file and could turn a later push into a
        ; spurious cycle. A report is an exit the reference does not have, so the
        ; unwinding it needs is ours to write.
        ; ★★★ SLICE 70 TURNS THIS STOP INTO A CALL, and one shape of it now runs
        ; to the reference's next question: `class C extends null {}`. The null
        ; keyword answers nullWideningType, and the reference's TS2507 guard reads
        ; `baseConstructorType != c.nullWideningType` — so `extends null` is
        ; deliberately NOT a *not a constructor function type* error, which is a
        ; sentence this port could not even ask before the dispatch existed.
        let base_expr AstNode.expression_of(base_type_node)
        if base_expr = null
        {
            ; The reference dereferences `baseTypeNode.Expression()` unconditionally;
            ; an ExpressionWithTypeArguments always has one, so this row is a claim
            ; rather than a hole — it is here for the reason the JsxOpeningElement
            ; arm reports instead of trapping.
            this.pop_type_resolution()
            this.record_unported("base-type-node-without-expression", KindExpressionWithTypeArguments)
            return null
        }
        let base_ctor this.check_expression(base_expr as ref[AstNode])
        this.pop_type_resolution()
        if base_ctor = null
            return null
        ; resolveStructuredTypeMembers (only for an Object or Intersection type,
        ; which nullWideningType is not) and then isConstructorType, which is the
        ; first question this function asks that the dispatch cannot answer.
        this.record_unported("is-constructor-type", KindExpressionWithTypeArguments)
        null
    }

    ; getBaseTypeNodeOfClass.
    function get_base_type_node_of_class(t: ref[Type]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if t.symbol = null
            return null
        let decl Checker.get_class_like_declaration_of_symbol(t.symbol as ref[Symbol])
        if decl = null
            return null
        Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_element(decl as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; ast.GetClassLikeDeclarationOfSymbol — Find(symbol.Declarations, IsClassLike).
    function get_class_like_declaration_of_symbol(symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let n Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let dn d as ref[AstNode]
                if Parser.is_class_like_node(dn)
                    return dn
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; ── getBaseTypes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ THE INTERFACE ARM IS THE WHOLE OF IT ON THIS CORPUS, AND THE CLASS ARM IS
    ; UNREACHABLE RATHER THAN UNWRITTEN. getBaseTypes has exactly two callers this
    ; port can reach, and both are in checkInterfaceDeclaration; the class's own
    ; caller sits inside `if baseTypeNode != nil`, which is behind
    ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol's stop. So resolveBaseTypesOfClass reports
    ; IN PLACE, which keeps the reference's switch shape rather than pretending
    ; the arm does not exist.
    function get_base_types(this, t: ref[Type]) returns ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    {
        if (t.object_flags & (ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface | ObjectFlagsTuple)) = 0
            return null
        if Checker.base_types_resolved_of(t) = false
        {
            if this.push_type_resolution(null, t, TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseTypes) = false
                return Checker.resolved_base_types_of(t)
            let before this.is_unported()
            if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsTuple) <> 0
            {
                this.pop_type_resolution()
                this.record_unported("get-tuple-base-type", 0)
                return null
            }
            if t.symbol = null
            {
                ; The reference's `default` here is a panic. A report, on this
                ; file's rule.
                this.pop_type_resolution()
                this.record_unported("get-base-types-no-symbol", 0)
                return null
            }
            let sym t.symbol as ref[Symbol]
            let sf Symbol.flags_of(sym)
            if (sf & (SymbolFlagsClass | SymbolFlagsInterface)) = 0
            {
                this.pop_type_resolution()
                this.record_unported("get-base-types-unhandled", 0)
                return null
            }
            if (sf & SymbolFlagsClass) <> 0
            {
                this.pop_type_resolution()
                this.record_unported("resolve-base-types-of-class", 0)
                return null
            }
            if (sf & SymbolFlagsInterface) <> 0
            {
                this.resolve_base_types_of_interface(t)
                if this.is_unported() <> before
                {
                    this.pop_type_resolution()
                    return null
                }
            }
            if this.pop_type_resolution() = false
            {
                let dn Symbol.declaration_count(sym)
                var di 0
                while di < dn
                {
                    let d Symbol.declaration_at(sym, di)
                    if d <> null
                    {
                        let dr d as ref[AstNode]
                        var reportable false
                        if AstNode.kind_of(dr) = KindClassDeclaration
                            set reportable: true
                        if AstNode.kind_of(dr) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                            set reportable: true
                        if reportable
                        {
                            ; reportCircularBaseType — TS2310, whose argument is
                            ; typeToStringEx with WriteArrayAsGenericType. The
                            ; printer ports the KEYWORD arms only, so a class or
                            ; interface type has no name to put in the message.
                            this.record_unported("report-circular-base-type", AstNode.kind_of(dr))
                            return null
                        }
                    }
                    set di: di + 1
                }
            }
            ; `t.objectFlags &^= ObjectFlagsMembersResolved`. Spelled as a
            ; conditional xor because this file has no bitwise complement — and
            ; the reference's comment is the reason the line exists at all: a
            ; circularity in a type-parameter default can leave members half
            ; resolved, and this resets that.
            if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsMembersResolved) <> 0
                set t.object_flags: t.object_flags xor ObjectFlagsMembersResolved
            Checker.mark_base_types_resolved(t)
        }
        Checker.resolved_base_types_of(t)
    }

    ; resolveBaseTypesOfInterface. The OUTER loop runs on every unit this slice
    ; reaches and the INNER one runs on none of them: all 43 interfaces that get
    ; here at stage 1 have no `extends` clause at all. It is written out as the
    ; nested walk it is rather than as a test on the element count, because the
    ; reference's loop BODY begins with getTypeFromTypeNode and that is exactly
    ; where the stop belongs.
    procedure resolve_base_types_of_interface(this, t: ref[Type])
    {
        if t.symbol = null
            return
        let sym t.symbol as ref[Symbol]
        let n Symbol.declaration_count(sym)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(sym, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let dn d as ref[AstNode]
                if AstNode.kind_of(dn) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                {
                    let elems Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_elements(dn)
                    if elems <> null
                    {
                        let list elems as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
                        let en list.get_length() as int
                        var j 0
                        while j < en
                        {
                            ; baseType := c.getReducedType(c.getTypeFromTypeNode(node)),
                            ; and behind it isValidBaseType, hasBaseType, the
                            ; append and the two reports.
                            this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", KindExpressionWithTypeArguments)
                            return
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkInheritedPropertiesAreIdentical. `len(baseTypes) < 2` is the whole
    ; function for an interface with fewer than two bases, which is every
    ; interface on this corpus — and the rest of it (resolveDeclaredMembers,
    ; getPropertiesOfType, isPropertyIdenticalTo and the TS2320 chain) is one
    ; call deep behind that test.
    function check_inherited_properties_are_identical(this, t: ref[Type], type_node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        let before this.is_unported()
        let base_types this.get_base_types(t)
        if this.is_unported() <> before
            return false
        var n 0
        if base_types <> null
            set n: (base_types as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
        if n < 2
            return true
        this.record_unported("resolve-declared-members", 0)
        false
    }

    ; The two compilerOptions this slice reads, as functions rather than fields:
    ; there is no options record yet, and a field would claim one exists. Each
    ; answers the DEFAULT, which is what the oracle's stub program sets (it passes
    ; SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and nothing else).
    ;
    ; ★★★ strictNullChecks WAS `false` HERE FROM SLICE 47 TO SLICE 52 AND THE
    ; DEFAULT IS `true` — corrected in slice 52, and the shape of the mistake is
    ; worth more than the line. The two options are read through DIFFERENT
    ; accessors and only one of them takes the family default:
    ; `c.strictNullChecks = GetStrictOptionValue(options.StrictNullChecks)`, whose
    ; body is `if value != TSUnknown { return value == TSTrue }; return
    ; options.Strict != TSFalse` — so an UNSET option answers TRUE, because Strict
    ; is unset too and the test is `!= TSFalse` rather than `== TSTrue`. Meanwhile
    ; `c.exactOptionalPropertyTypes = options.ExactOptionalPropertyTypes ==
    ; core.TSTrue` is a plain equality and DOES answer false. Reading "the harness
    ; sets no options" as "every option is off" is therefore wrong for the four
    ; strict-family options (strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes, noImplicitAny,
    ; strictBindCallApply) and right for the rest — a third reading of "unset", next
    ; to the two check_signature_declaration meets (a FALSE default that kills a
    ; branch, and a target default at its MAXIMUM that kills a `<` comparison).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS UNOBSERVABLE, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED SIX SLICES, and the reason
    ; is worth writing down: its one reader is create_widening_type, and a wrong
    ; answer there does not produce a wrong TYPE — it produces two EXTRA types
    ; (a fresh widening intrinsic instead of the non-widening one returned
    ; unchanged), so the type COUNTER and every type ID after undefinedWideningType
    ; shift by two. The dump's T section prints `TypeToString(t)`, a NAME, and
    ; nothing in this suite compares an id or the counter. Correcting it leaves all
    ; five yardsticks byte-identical — which is the same fact from the other side,
    ; and is the checksum this correction carries instead of a gate.
    function strict_null_checks() returns bool
        true

    function exact_optional_property_types() returns bool
        false

    ; ★★ SLICE 69 NEEDS THREE MORE, and they split along exactly the line
    ; strict_null_checks' header draws. noImplicitAny and useUnknownInCatch
    ; Variables are strict-FAMILY options read through GetStrictOptionValue, so an
    ; unset value falls through to `Strict != TSFalse` and both answer TRUE — which
    ; is what makes this slice produce diagnostics at all. checkJs is a plain
    ; equality against TSTrue and answers FALSE, which is why a JavaScript file
    ; without `// @ts-check` gets none of them.
    ;
    ; ★ check_signature_declaration's header already stated noImplicitAny's value
    ; and called the two arms behind it *"live code behind a live option"*; this is
    ; the slice that reads it.
    function no_implicit_any() returns bool
        true

    function use_unknown_in_catch_variables() returns bool
        true

    function check_js() returns bool
        false

    ; noImplicitThis — the fourth strict-family option, TRUE for the same reason.
    function no_implicit_this() returns bool
        true

    ; NewChecker's intrinsic block. The order is the reference's, because the type
    ; ids are assigned along it.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS MISSING FROM THE RUN, and each omission is one mechanism rather
    ; than one type: the BOOLEAN family (regularFalseType, falseType,
    ; regularTrueType, trueType) needs newLiteralType, i.e. a value payload on
    ; Type — §3.5be's rule says a field with no reader is DECLARED rather than
    ; ported, and nothing in this slice can produce a literal type, so the payload
    ; arrives with the arm that does. booleanType, stringOrNumberType,
    ; stringNumberSymbolType, numberOrBigIntType and templateConstraintType need
    ; getUnionType; numericStringType needs getTemplateLiteralType; and the
    ; fifteen empty/marker OBJECT types need newAnonymousType and newTypeParameter.
    ; Every one of them is reachable only from an arm that reports, so their
    ; absence cannot be observed — which is exactly why it is listed here instead.
    procedure initialize_intrinsic_types(this)
    {
        set any_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        set auto_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsAny, "any", ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType)
        set wildcard_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        set blocked_string_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        ; ★ errorType's intrinsic NAME is "error" and its printed form is "any":
        ; the printer branches on TypeFlagsAny and never reads the name except to
        ; recognise intrinsicMarkerType. That is why a `T … any` line in the dump
        ; is most often this type rather than anyType — see get_type_of_node's
        ; fallthrough.
        set error_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "error")
        set unresolved_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "unresolved")
        set non_inferrable_any_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsAny, "any", ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType)
        set intrinsic_marker_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "intrinsic")
        set unknown_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUnknown, "unknown")
        set undefined_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        set undefined_widening_type: this.create_widening_type(undefined_type)
        set missing_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        ; core.IfElse(c.exactOptionalPropertyTypes, c.missingType, c.undefinedType)
        set undefined_or_missing_type: undefined_type
        if Checker.exact_optional_property_types()
            set undefined_or_missing_type: missing_type
        set optional_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        set null_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNull, "null")
        set null_widening_type: this.create_widening_type(null_type)
        set string_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsString, "string")
        set number_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNumber, "number")
        set bigint_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsBigInt, "bigint")
        set es_symbol_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsESSymbol, "symbol")
        set void_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsVoid, "void")
        set never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set silent_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsNever, "never", ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType)
        set implicit_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set unreachable_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set non_primitive_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNonPrimitive, "object")
        ; Special `never` flagged by union reduction to behave as a literal.
        set unique_literal_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")

        ; ★★★ THE BOOLEAN PAIR IS BUILT HERE AND IN THE REFERENCE'S OWN ORDER,
        ; which is REGULAR then FRESH and then two writes that close the cycle —
        ; `regularFalseType.freshType = falseType` and
        ; `falseType.freshType = falseType`. It is the one literal type
        ; getFreshTypeOfLiteralType never mints, so the eight lines below are the
        ; only place in this port where that function's postcondition is
        ; established by hand. ★Six lines of NewChecker are transcribed and the
        ; SEVENTH is not: `c.booleanType = c.getUnionType(...)` needs a union.
        set regular_false_type: this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, null, 0, 0 as u64, false, false, null)
        set false_type: this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, null, 0, 0 as u64, false, false, regular_false_type)
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(regular_false_type, false_type)
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(false_type, false_type)
        set regular_true_type: this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, null, 0, 0 as u64, false, true, null)
        set true_type: this.new_literal_type(TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, null, 0, 0 as u64, false, true, regular_true_type)
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(regular_true_type, true_type)
        Checker.set_literal_fresh_type(true_type, true_type)
    }

    ; ── the type printer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; TypeToString → typeToString → typeToStringEx → nodeBuilder.TypeToTypeNode →
    ; the emit printer. THIS PORTS THE KEYWORD ARMS OF THAT WORKER AND NOTHING
    ; ELSE, and the boundary is not arbitrary: those arms answer a bare keyword
    ; that the printer prints unchanged, so they are the shapes for which the
    ; nodebuilder and the printer are both identity. Every other arm builds a NODE
    ; and needs a printer, which is a dimension of its own (3 585 + 486 lines).
    ;
    ; ★ THE ORDER OF THE TESTS IS THE REFERENCE'S, and it is load-bearing rather
    ; than stylistic: the flags are not disjoint. A fresh boolean literal carries
    ; TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, and an enum literal carries StringLiteral or
    ; NumberLiteral as well as EnumLiteral, so testing Void before the literal
    ; flags would answer a keyword for a literal type. Every arm this slice does
    ; not port therefore stands IN PLACE, reporting, rather than being left out.
    ;
    ; ★ Two things typeToStringEx does before the worker are no-ops here and say
    ; so at their line: the serializationLevel cutoff (nothing recurses into the
    ; printer, so the level is always 0) and the truncation (NoErrorTruncation is
    ; set by the harness, which is the whole reason the oracle passes it — a
    ; truncated name would make the T section depend on a display budget).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A BUILDER SINCE SLICE 71, AND THE ARM THAT WIDENED IT IS THE ONE
    ; THIS FUNCTION'S OWN HEADER NAMED: *"the arm that first needs composition is
    ; the one that widens this to a builder"*. Four of the arms below now compose
    ; a name out of a VALUE — a quoted and escaped string, a number's
    ; ECMAScript-to-String, a bigint's digits plus `n`, and the two boolean
    ; keywords — so a `pointer[const_char]` can no longer be the answer.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CALLER OWNS THE BUFFER AND MUST HAND IN A SCRATCH ONE. The answer is
    ; written into `out` and the function reports `false` when it cannot name the
    ; type — by which point it may already have written part of a name, so `out`
    ; must not be the caller's real sink. TypeDump obeys this at its one call site
    ; and says so. ★A `String` return would have been the smaller change and the
    ; worse one: the name is appended to a builder either way, so a String would be
    ; an allocation and a copy per node of the walk.
    function type_to_string(this, t: ref[Type]?, out: ref[StringBuilder]) returns bool
    {
        if t = null
        {
            ; The reference PANICS here ("should always get typenode"), because a
            ; nil type reaches the worker only with FlagsAllowEmptyUnionOrIntersection
            ; set and typeToString does not set it. Reported rather than trapped:
            ; the dump's caller skips a nil type exactly as the oracle does, so
            ; this is unreachable from the walk and a report says so without
            ; inventing a keyword.
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-null", 0)
            return false
        }

        ; getReducedType, which the worker calls when FlagsNoTypeReduction is
        ; absent — as it is here. It is the identity on everything but a union or
        ; an intersection with a possibly-never member, and both of those report
        ; below, so the call is the identity for every type this slice can hold.
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsAny) <> 0
        {
            ; t.alias — the type-alias name a type may carry — is not represented
            ; yet, and cannot be: an alias is set by getTypeFromTypeNode, which
            ; reports. The arm stands here so the next reader sees where it goes.
            if t = unresolved_type
            {
                ; The reference wraps the keyword in a SYNTHETIC LEADING COMMENT
                ; ("/*unresolved*/ any") and prints it with a printer that keeps
                ; comments, where every other type is printed with one that
                ; removes them. That is a printer feature rather than a name, so
                ; it reports.
                this.record_unported("type-to-string-unresolved", 0)
                return false
            }
            if t = intrinsic_marker_type
                return Checker.write_keyword(out, "intrinsic")
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "any")
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUnknown) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "unknown")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsString) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "string")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNumber) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "number")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBigInt) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "bigint")
        ; `t.flags&Boolean != 0 && t.alias == nil` — booleanType is a UNION of the
        ; two boolean literals, so this arm needs getUnionType before it can ever
        ; be entered, and no boolean type exists here to enter it with. ★Slice 71
        ; makes the two boolean LITERAL types and still not this one, which is why
        ; the arm four below it is live and this one is not.
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBoolean) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-boolean", 0)
            return false
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsEnumLike) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-enum", 0)
            return false
        }
        ; ★★★ THE STRING LITERAL IS THE ONLY ARM WITH AN ESCAPE, and it is a port
        ; of the PRINTER rather than of the nodebuilder. The builder makes a
        ; synthetic StringLiteral node and adds EFNoAsciiEscaping to it; the
        ; printer then cannot reach an original source text (the node is
        ; synthesized) and falls into getLiteralText's StringLiteral arm, which is
        ; a double quote, escapeStringWorker, and a double quote. The emit flag is
        ; what makes NeverAsciiEscape true, which is the one bit of the worker's
        ; behaviour that would otherwise depend on it.
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsStringLiteral) <> 0
        {
            Checker.write_string_literal_name(out, Checker.literal_text_of(t), Checker.literal_text_length_of(t))
            return true
        }
        ; The number's own String(). ★The reference's arm SPLITS a negative value
        ; into a PrefixUnaryExpression over the digits without the sign, which is
        ; two nodes printing the one text `-N` — so the split is a property of the
        ; node tree it builds and not of the name, and the name is the whole
        ; String() either way (§3.5ag).
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNumberLiteral) <> 0
        {
            var nb char[JSNUM_MAX_TEXT]
            let nn jsnum_to_string(Checker.literal_bits_of(t), &nb[0])
            out.append(&nb[0], nn as size_t)
            return true
        }
        ; pseudoBigIntToString(value) + "n".
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBigIntLiteral) <> 0
        {
            Checker.write_pseudo_big_int(out, t)
            out.append("n" as char)
            return true
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral) <> 0
        {
            if Checker.literal_bool_of(t)
                return Checker.write_keyword(out, "true")
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "false")
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUniqueESSymbol) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-unique-symbol", 0)
            return false
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsVoid) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "void")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUndefined) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "undefined")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNull) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "null")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNever) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "never")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsESSymbol) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "symbol")
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNonPrimitive) <> 0
            return Checker.write_keyword(out, "object")
        ; Everything past this point is the this-type, the alias, and the object /
        ; union / intersection / instantiable machinery — the rest of
        ; nodebuilderimpl.
        this.record_unported("type-to-string", t.flags)
        false
    }

    ; The fourteen keyword arms, which used to BE the answer and are now the
    ; degenerate case of composing one.
    function write_keyword(out: ref[StringBuilder], name: pointer[const_char]) returns bool
    {
        out.append(name)
        true
    }

    ; PseudoBigInt.String(): the empty Base10Value is the value ZERO, and a
    ; non-empty one is prefixed with a minus when the sign is set. The length test
    ; is the reference's and is not interchangeable with a digit test — see
    ; bigint_literal_arm for why zero is stored empty in the first place.
    procedure write_pseudo_big_int(out: ref[StringBuilder], t: ref[Type])
        Checker.write_pseudo_big_int_value(out, Checker.literal_text_of(t), Checker.literal_text_length_of(t), Checker.literal_negative_of(t))

    ; ★★ THE THREE COMPOSING BODIES ARE STATICS OVER BYTES rather than over a
    ; Type, and that is what lets tests/litcheck.sh measure THIS code instead of a
    ; copy of it. An instrument that re-implements what it checks agrees with
    ; itself, which is the one thing a second producer must not do — and the first
    ; draft of that battery did exactly that until g17 refused to apply and said
    ; so.
    procedure write_pseudo_big_int_value(out: ref[StringBuilder], text: pointer[char], n: int, negative: bool)
    {
        if n = 0
        {
            out.append("0" as char)
            return
        }
        if negative
            out.append("-" as char)
        out.append(text, n as size_t)
    }

    ; The string literal's whole name: a double quote, the escape worker, a double
    ; quote.
    procedure write_string_literal_name(out: ref[StringBuilder], text: pointer[char], n: int)
    {
        out.append("\"" as char)
        Checker.escape_string_worker(out, text, n)
        out.append("\"" as char)
    }

    ; `jsnum.ParsePseudoBigInt(text)` followed by NewPseudoBigInt's
    ; `strings.TrimLeft(value, "0")` — the two steps between a bigint literal's
    ; TEXT and the digits a PseudoBigInt holds, with the all-zero case answering
    ; the EMPTY string the reference represents zero as. Answers the length and
    ; writes the START OFFSET into `out` through `out_start`, or -1 when
    ; ParsePseudoBigInt refuses the text.
    function pseudo_big_int_digits(text: pointer[char], n: int, out: pointer[char], out_start: pointer[int]) returns int
    {
        set *out_start: 0
        let m jsnum_parse_pseudo_big_int(text, n, out)
        if m < 0
            return 0 - 1
        var i 0
        while i < m
        {
            if (*(out + i) as int) <> (("0" as char) as int)
                break
            set i: i + 1
        }
        set *out_start: i
        m - i
    }

    ; ── printer/utilities.go's escapeStringWorker ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Narrowed to its ONE caller (§3.5br) and the narrowing is stated so the next
    ; reader can widen it honestly: the quote character is always the double quote
    ; (nodeBuilder passes isSingleQuote false and never sets
    ; FlagsUseSingleQuotesForStringLiteralType), and the flags are always
    ; NeverAsciiEscape and never JsxAttributeEscape (the emit flag the builder adds
    ; is exactly EFNoAsciiEscaping). So the three arms that read `quoteChar ==
    ; backtick`, the whole JSX branch and the `ch > 0x7f` half of the default test
    ; are all decided here rather than carried.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT SURVIVES THE NARROWING IS THE PART THAT ACTUALLY BITES: the canonical
    ; escapes, the sub-0x20 controls, the three line-ish code points U+2028,
    ; U+2029 and U+0085, and the SURROGATE cases — a lone surrogate and a
    ; supplementary code point both become `\uXXXX` sequences, uppercase hex, and a
    ; supplementary one becomes TWO of them.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DECODER IS `DecodeJSStringRune` AND NOT PLAIN UTF-8, which is the one
    ; place this could have been silently wrong: the scanner writes a lone
    ; surrogate out as its three-byte sentinel (see value_put_codepoint), and Go's
    ; own utf8 decoder REFUSES that encoding and answers RuneError — so a port
    ; reaching for the ordinary decoder would print U+FFFD where the reference
    ; prints `\uD800`.
    procedure escape_string_worker(out: ref[StringBuilder], s: pointer[char], slen: int)
    {
        var i 0
        while i < slen
        {
            var size 0
            let ch Checker.decode_js_string_rune(s, slen, i, &size)

            var escape false
            if (ch >= 0xD800) and (ch <= 0xDFFF)
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0xFFFD
            {
                ; `ch == utf8.RuneError && size == 1` — a stray byte that is not
                ; valid UTF-8. A REAL U+FFFD in the text decodes with size 3 and is
                ; not escaped, which is why the size is part of the test.
                if size = 1
                    set escape: true
            }
            if ch = 0x5C
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x22
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x2028
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x2029
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x0085
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x0D
                set escape: true
            if ch = 0x0A
                set escape: true
            if ch <= 0x1F
                set escape: true

            if escape = false
            {
                out.append(s + i, size as size_t)
                set i: i + size
                continue
            }

            ; ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S SUPPLEMENTARY-PLANE ARM IS NOT HERE AND THE
            ; BATTERY IS WHY. `ch > 0xffff` encodes a surrogate PAIR by hand, and
            ; under this narrowing nothing can reach it: a code point above
            ; 0x7f is escaped only by the default test's second disjunct, which
            ; NeverAsciiEscape switches off, and none of the other conditions can
            ; be true above 0xffff. It was transcribed first and removed after
            ; g13 came back UNGATED — the row now breaks the NARROWING instead,
            ; which is the condition that actually decides this. ★A LONE
            ; surrogate is a different matter and IS escaped: it fails the
            ; UTF-8 decoder's own range test rather than the ascii one.
            if (ch >= 0xD800) and (ch <= 0xDFFF)
            {
                Checker.write_utf16_escape(out, ch)
                set i: i + size
                continue
            }
            if ch = 0
            {
                ; "If the null character is followed by digits, print as a hex
                ; escape to prevent the result from parsing as an octal".
                var digit false
                if (i + size) < slen
                {
                    let nx *(s + (i + size)) as int
                    if (nx >= 0x30) and (nx <= 0x39)
                        set digit: true
                }
                if digit
                    out.append("\\x00")
                else
                    out.append("\\0")
                set i: i + size
                continue
            }
            Checker.write_escaped_char(out, ch)
            set i: i + size
        }
    }

    ; escapedCharsMap, plus encodeUtf16EscapeSequence as its default. `$` and the
    ; backtick are in the reference's table and unreachable here — both are escaped
    ; only when the quote character is a backtick.
    procedure write_escaped_char(out: ref[StringBuilder], ch: int)
    {
        if ch = 0x09
        {
            out.append("\\t")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x0B
        {
            out.append("\\v")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x0C
        {
            out.append("\\f")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x08
        {
            out.append("\\b")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x0D
        {
            out.append("\\r")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x0A
        {
            out.append("\\n")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x5C
        {
            out.append("\\\\")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x22
        {
            out.append("\\\"")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x2028
        {
            out.append("\\u2028")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x2029
        {
            out.append("\\u2029")
            return
        }
        if ch = 0x0085
        {
            out.append("\\u0085")
            return
        }
        Checker.write_utf16_escape(out, ch)
    }

    ; encodeUtf16EscapeSequence — `\u` and FOUR UPPERCASE hex digits, zero-padded.
    ; Every code point that reaches it is below 0x10000, and that is now a
    ; property of the CALLERS rather than a hope — see escape_string_worker, whose
    ; supplementary-plane arm is unreachable under this narrowing and is therefore
    ; not written.
    procedure write_utf16_escape(out: ref[StringBuilder], ch: int)
    {
        out.append("\\u")
        var shift 12
        while shift >= 0
        {
            let d (ch >> shift) & 0xF
            if d < 10
                out.append((d + 0x30) as char)
            else
                out.append(((d - 10) + 0x41) as char)
            set shift: shift - 4
        }
    }

    ; stringutil.DecodeJSStringRune. The three-byte SENTINEL for a lone surrogate
    ; is recognised FIRST — `ED A0..BF 80..BF` — and everything else is Go's own
    ; utf8.DecodeRuneInString, which jsnum already ports arm for arm.
    function decode_js_string_rune(s: pointer[char], stop: int, i: int, out_size: pointer[int]) returns int
    {
        if (i + 2) < stop
        {
            let b0 *(s + i) as int
            if b0 = 0xED
            {
                let b1 *(s + (i + 1)) as int
                let b2 *(s + (i + 2)) as int
                if (b1 >= 0xA0) and (b1 <= 0xBF)
                {
                    if (b2 >= 0x80) and (b2 <= 0xBF)
                    {
                        set *out_size: 3
                        return (0xD000 | ((b1 & 0x3F) << 6)) | (b2 & 0x3F)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        jsnum_decode_rune(s, stop, i, out_size)
    }

    ; ── GetTypeAtLocation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getTypeOfNode, transcribed as a CHAIN because that is what it is: fifteen
    ; predicates in a fixed order, ending in errorType. A kind switch would be a
    ; different function — several of the predicates depend on a node's PARENT, not
    ; on its kind — so the shape is kept and the arms report.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE GATE IN THE MIDDLE IS THE HONEST PART. Ten of those predicates
    ; are not ported (IsExpressionNode, IsDeclarationNode, IsTypeDeclaration,
    ; IsTypeDeclarationName, IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName,
    ; IsBindingPattern, isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment, IsMetaProperty,
    ; IsImportAttributes, and the class-extends pair), and a chain that skipped a
    ; predicate it cannot evaluate would fall through to errorType and answer
    ; `any` — a well-formed WRONG answer, on the most common line the yardstick
    ; compares (`any` is 43 % of the corpus's 1 230 663 type answers, so a wrong
    ; `any` is the single easiest way for this port to look right). So a kind
    ; INVENTORY stands in front of them, listing the kinds for which every
    ; unported predicate is provably false; every other kind reports with its own
    ; number as the detail, which is the histogram tests/triage.py groups.
    function get_type_of_node(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        ; `ast.IsSourceFile(node) && !ast.IsExternalOrCommonJSModule(...)`. The
        ; walk never asks about the source file itself (both dumpers skip it), so
        ; this arm is unreachable from the yardstick and is ported because it is
        ; one line and because the next reader would otherwise have to prove that
        ; again.
        if k = KindSourceFile
        {
            if b.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
                return error_type
        }

        ; "We cannot answer semantic questions within a with block, do not proceed
        ; any further" — the reference's own comment. The flag is the parser's, so
        ; this arm is complete.
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsInWithStatement) <> 0
            return error_type

        if Checker.kind_has_ported_type_arm(k) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("type-of-node", k)
            return null
        }

        ; The chain's own end: "if we get here, the node is not something we have
        ; a type for". Reached by exactly the kinds the inventory admits.
        error_type
    }

    ; ★★★ THE INVENTORY, AND WHAT ADMITTING A KIND COSTS. A kind belongs here only
    ; when every predicate of getTypeOfNode that this slice cannot evaluate is
    ; provably false for it — in EVERY context, since the predicates read parents.
    ; Slice 47 admits exactly one, and writes the proof out because the next slice
    ; will extend the list and needs the standard:
    ;
    ; KindEndOfFile (1). It is the SourceFile's endOfFileToken, so its parent is
    ; always the source file. IsPartOfTypeNode: the kind is outside
    ; [FirstTypeNode, LastTypeNode] and is in none of the switch's cases.
    ; IsExpressionNode: not in its list, and its default is false.
    ; TryGetClassImplementingOrExtendingExpressionWithTypeArguments requires an
    ; ExpressionWithTypeArguments. IsTypeDeclaration and IsDeclarationNode are
    ; properties of the node LAYOUT and the endOfFileToken is a plain token node.
    ; IsTypeDeclarationName and IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName both require
    ; the node to BE a name, i.e. an identifier or a literal.
    ; IsBindingElement / IsBindingPattern: the two binding kinds.
    ; isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment walks up from an entity NAME.
    ; IsMetaProperty(node.Parent) is false because the parent is the source file.
    ; IsImportAttributes is one kind. So the token reaches the fallthrough and its
    ; type is errorType, printed `any` — which is what the reference answers for
    ; it in every unit of the corpus.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 48 ADDS TWO, AND THE WAY IT EARNS THEM IS THE POINT. Slice 47's
    ; note here read *the statement kinds almost certainly belong, but "almost
    ; certainly" is not the standard, and the honest way to admit them is with
    ; IsDeclarationNode ported rather than with a second enumeration by hand*.
    ; It is ported now (DeclarationKinds.scaly, derived from the reference's own
    ; struct graph and verified against its own DeclarationData()), so
    ; `is_declaration` answers the tenth predicate instead of a paragraph, and
    ; that is what the two admissions below rest on.
    ;
    ; KindEmptyStatement (243) and KindDebuggerStatement (260). The proof is the
    ; end-of-file token's, re-run for a STATEMENT, and it must hold in every
    ; context — a `;` can be the body of an `if`, an arm of a case clause or a
    ; statement of a block, not only a top-level element:
    ;
    ;   IsPartOfTypeNode        ported, and answers false: neither kind is in
    ;                           [FirstTypeNode, LastTypeNode] nor in the switch.
    ;   IsDeclaration           PORTED as of this slice, and false: neither kind
    ;                           is among the fifty-six.
    ;   IsExpressionNode        the switch lists no statement kind and its default
    ;                           is false.
    ;   TryGetClassImplementing…  requires an ExpressionWithTypeArguments.
    ;   IsTypeDeclaration       a six-kind list plus the import/export type-only
    ;                           arms; a statement is in none of them.
    ;   IsTypeDeclarationName   requires KindIdentifier.
    ;   IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName  either the parent is an
    ;                           import/export specifier and the node must then be
    ;                           an identifier or a string literal, or it reduces to
    ;                           IsDeclarationName, whose last test is
    ;                           `parent.Name() == node` — and a declaration's Name()
    ;                           is a name node, never a statement.
    ;   IsBindingElement / IsBindingPattern   three kinds, none of them these.
    ;   isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment  the parent must be an
    ;                           ImportEqualsDeclaration whose ModuleReference this
    ;                           is, or an ExportAssignment whose Expression it is;
    ;                           both slots take an entity name or an expression.
    ;   IsMetaProperty(parent)  a MetaProperty's children are tokens.
    ;   IsImportAttributes      one kind.
    ;
    ; So both reach the fallthrough and answer errorType, printed `any` — which is
    ; what the reference answers for them in every unit of the corpus, and what
    ; `checker_ambient_statements_only.d.ts` is there to hold it to.
    function kind_has_ported_type_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindEndOfFile
            return true
        if k = KindEmptyStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDebuggerStatement
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ── ast.GetMeaningFromDeclaration ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ IT LIVES HERE AND UPSTREAM HAS IT IN internal/ast, and the reason is this
    ; port's own layout rather than a preference: its ModuleDeclaration arm asks
    ; GetModuleInstanceState, which the reference keeps in `ast` and slice 31 put on
    ; the BINDER (it needs the visited set and the ancestor chain that the binder
    ; carries). The `ast` module is declared before `binder`, so a function in
    ; ast.scaly could not reach it — a module-level name from a module declared
    ; LATER is a hard `unknown identifier` and, in call-argument position, a
    ; silently dropped call. So the one arm that needs the binder decides where the
    ; whole function goes, and it goes with its dependency.
    ;
    ; Its reader is the type walk's exclusion for an IDENTIFIER: a name that
    ; declares nothing into the VALUE space is not asked for its type, with the
    ; type-alias name as the one exception.
    function get_meaning_from_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if node = null
            return SemanticMeaningAll
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        if Checker.kind_declares_a_value_only(k)
            return SemanticMeaningValue
        if Checker.kind_declares_a_type_only(k)
            return SemanticMeaningType
        if k = KindEnumMember
            return SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType

        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if Binder.is_ambient_module(node)
                return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue
            if b.get_module_instance_state(node) = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue
            return SemanticMeaningNamespace
        }

        ; The reference's list here returns SemanticMeaningAll, which is also the
        ; function's default — the kinds are enumerated upstream to say that the
        ; answer is deliberate, and the enumeration changes no answer, so it is a
        ; comment here rather than eight more comparisons: EnumDeclaration,
        ; NamedImports, ImportSpecifier, ImportEqualsDeclaration, ImportDeclaration,
        ; JSImportDeclaration, ExportAssignment, ExportDeclaration. ★ And the
        ; SourceFile arm — "an external module can be a Value" — answers
        ; Namespace|Value, which IS different from the default and is therefore
        ; below rather than in this comment.
        if k = KindSourceFile
            return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue

        SemanticMeaningAll
    }

    ; The two long `case` lists of GetMeaningFromDeclaration, as functions for the
    ; reason ast.scaly's is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node is one.
    function kind_declares_a_value_only(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        if k = KindBindingElement
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if k = KindCatchClause
            return true
        if k = KindJsxAttribute
            return true
        false
    }

    function kind_declares_a_type_only(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
            return true
        false
    }

    ; checker.IsTypeAny, which the type walk's class-extends workaround asks: the
    ; reference re-asks the node itself when the parent's answer is `any`.
    function is_type_any(t: ref[Type]?) returns bool
    {
        if t = null
            return false
        (t.flags & TypeFlagsAny) <> 0
    }

    ; ── GetDiagnostics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getDiagnostics: checkUnused is false under this harness's options (see the
    ; header), so the whole suggestion/unused branch is not entered, and what is
    ; left is checkSourceFile followed by the collection's sorted view.
    procedure get_diagnostics(this)
    {
        this.check_source_file()
        ; GetDiagnosticsForFile sorts the collection before answering — see
        ; DiagnosticList.sort_by_position for the comparison and for why this list
        ; sorts while the parser's does not.
        diagnostics.sort_by_position()
    }

    ; checkSourceFile. The `links.typeChecked` once-guard is dropped for the
    ; binder's reason: a Checker here is one object, used once, on the caller's
    ; page.
    procedure check_source_file(this)
    {
        ; Grammar checking. The result is discarded upstream too — the bool is
        ; there for the `&&` inside checkGrammarSourceFile and nothing reads it.
        this.check_grammar_source_file()

        ; `c.renamedBindingElementsInTypes = nil` — the list
        ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration appends to and
        ; checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements reads. Slice 56 ported the WRITER, and
        ; there is still nothing here to clear: this Checker is built per file and
        ; the list is empty when it arrives, where the reference reuses one Checker
        ; across the program and has to. ★The line is kept as a comment rather than
        ; dropped because the day this port checks a second file with one Checker,
        ; a list that survives the file boundary reports on nodes of the previous
        ; one — and the clear is the whole fix.

        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.statements_of(file))
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        this.check_deferred_nodes()
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        if b.is_external_or_common_js_module()
        {
            ; checkExternalModuleExports plus registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck.
            ; The second is observable only through checkUnusedIdentifiers, which
            ; checkUnused gates off here, so what this arm owes is the first: the
            ; module's export table walked for a name exported twice with
            ; different meanings. It needs the module symbol's exports and
            ; getSymbolFlags, so it reports — and note it is reached only AFTER
            ; every statement has been checked, which is why a module's dump is
            ; usually stopped by one of its statements rather than by this line.
            this.record_unported("check-external-module-exports", 0)
            return
        }

        if is_declaration_file = false
            this.check_unused_renamed_binding_elements()

        this.produce_deferred_diagnostics()
    }

    procedure check_source_elements(this, nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)
    {
        if nodes = null
            return
        var i 0
        while i < AstNode.list_count(nodes)
        {
            ; ★★★ SLICE 47 STOPPED HERE ON A REPORT AND SLICE 49 DOES NOT, AND THE
            ; REASON IT GAVE IS WHY. It read *the reference breaks on cancellation;
            ; a report is this port's equivalent stopping condition and there is
            ; nothing to gain from checking the rest of a file whose dump is
            ; already suppressed* — true on the day it was written, and slice 48
            ; built the instrument that makes it false: tests/diagcheck.sh compares
            ; the C section of every unit, suppressed dump or not, so the
            ; statements after the first report are exactly where a wrong span or
            ; an invented diagnostic would hide. It is also the more faithful
            ; reading: the reference checks every element, and cancellation is not
            ; a thing this port has.
            ;
            ; ★ The relation diagcheck asserts survives the change and gets
            ; stronger. With the stop, our lines were a PREFIX of the reference's;
            ; without it they are a SUBSET, and both sides are sorted, so a
            ; subsequence test holds either way — over more of the file.
            ; §3.5cp's lesson, one dimension on: a deferral's REASON expires, and
            ; nothing re-reads it when it does.
            this.check_source_element(AstNode.child_in_list(nodes, i))
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkSourceElement's whole body is the save/restore of two registers around
    ; checkSourceElementWorker plus `c.instantiationCount = 0`. currentNode is read
    ; by the instantiation-depth machinery and withinUnreachableCode by the
    ; reachability check — neither of which exists here, for the reason the header
    ; gives — so the saves have nothing to save yet and the worker is called
    ; directly. The function is kept because every arm of the checker calls it and
    ; the registers arrive with the flow graph.
    procedure check_source_element(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        this.check_source_element_worker(node)
    }

    ; ★★★ checkSourceElementWorker. FIFTY-ONE OF THE REFERENCE'S FIFTY-NINE
    ; `case` LABELS ARE PORTED (three in slice 47, five in slice 49, eight in
    ; slice 50 — the import/export/module family plus the two blocks — the class in
    ; slice 51, the function declaration in slice 52, the variable DECLARATION in
    ; slice 54, the PARAMETER in slice 55, the BINDING ELEMENT in slice 56, the type
    ; parameter in slice 60, the whole TYPE half in slice 61, the two type MEMBERS in
    ; slice 62, the eight JUMP-AND-GUARD statements in slice 63 and the five loop,
    ; return and switch arms in slice 64; slice 53 added none and finished one of
    ; the eighteen it found instead). checkVariableStatement
    ; runs to its last line, so the VariableStatement arm is the third in this file
    ; that is COMPLETE rather than a prefix — the count is a count of arms REACHED,
    ; not of arms finished, and those are two different numbers. ★Slice 63 makes it
    ; five: checkBreakOrContinueStatement and checkTryStatement run to their last
    ; lines too; slice 64 makes it six with checkForStatement.
    ;
    ; ★ THE UNIT OF THE COUNT CHANGED IN SLICE 61 and the two figures are not
    ; comparable: it used to read *twenty-one arms of its sixty*, counting some
    ; multi-kind cases (Block/ModuleBlock, TypeAlias/JSTypeAlias) as separate arms
    ; and some not. A `case` LABEL is countable against the reference's own text —
    ; fifty-nine of them, `case ConstructorType, FunctionType, CallSignature,
    ; ConstructSignature, IndexSignature:` being ONE — so that is the unit now. In
    ; the old unit slice 61 added nineteen cases; in the new one the total went from
    ; nineteen to thirty-eight.
    ;
    ; ★ THE STATEMENT CHAPTER IS CLOSED AS OF SLICE 64 and what is left is strays:
    ; the accessor pair, the constructor, the class static block, the enum member,
    ; the missing declaration and the four JSDoc type kinds. ★The namespace export
    ; declaration is NOT among them and used to be listed here — the reference's
    ; switch has no `case` for it at all, so it moved to kind_has_no_check_arm; see
    ; that function's own note, which is the more interesting half.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SINCE SLICE 54 THE COUNT HAS A THIRD CATEGORY BESIDE `ported` AND `not
    ; ported`: the twelve kinds the switch has NO CASE for (kind_has_no_check_arm).
    ; They are not arms and they are not holes — doing nothing IS the reference's
    ; answer — and they are the reason the gate below can say true for a kind this
    ; file has no code for at all.
    ;
    ; What had to be settled before ANY of them
    ; could be was the two steps that stand in FRONT of the switch and run for
    ; every node. ★The count is the one kind_has_ported_check_arm answers true
    ; for, counted rather than incremented: it read *fifteen … seven in slice 50*
    ; for two slices while the table held sixteen and then seventeen, because the
    ; slice-50 figure was one short of its own parenthetical (six arms plus two
    ; blocks) and nothing re-added the sum.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REACHABILITY CHECK PRODUCES NOTHING THIS YARDSTICK COMPARES, and
    ; that is read off the reference rather than hoped: checkSourceElementUnreachable
    ; ends in `c.addErrorOrSuggestion(c.compilerOptions.AllowUnreachableCode ==
    ; core.TSFalse, diagnostic)`, and AllowUnreachableCode is UNSET under this
    ; harness (the oracle passes SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and
    ; nothing else), so the flag is TSUnknown, `== TSFalse` is false, and the
    ; diagnostic goes to `c.suggestionDiagnostics` with CategorySuggestion. The
    ; oracle calls GetDiagnostics, which reads `c.diagnostics`. Its only other
    ; effect is `c.withinUnreachableCode`, whose three readers are the guard
    ; itself and the save/restore around it. So TS7027 cannot appear in a C
    ; section at all here — which is why an arm may be claimed without the flow
    ; graph, and why this is stated at the line rather than left as a hole. ★It is
    ; conditional on the OPTION: a slice that lets `@allowUnreachableCode: false`
    ; through would make the whole family observable and owe the flow graph.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JSDOC PASS IS GATED ON THE FLAG THE REFERENCE GATES IT ON.
    ; `node.EagerJSDoc(nil)` answers nil unless NodeFlagsHasJSDoc is set, so a node
    ; without the flag cannot enter the loop and the step is a no-op we can prove
    ; rather than assume. With the flag set it reports: checkJSDocComment acts only
    ; on the three @link kinds and resolves with ignoreErrors, so it very probably
    ; adds nothing either — but *very probably* is not the standard the kind
    ; inventory below is held to, and resolveJSDocMemberName reaches
    ; getTypeOfSymbol, which reports at ITS own sites. The tighter gate (walk the
    ; comment parts and admit a JSDoc that carries no link) is a refinement with a
    ; measurement attached, not a guess.
    ;
    ; ★ The kind test comes FIRST, before both, and the order is ours rather than
    ; the reference's: for a kind whose arm is not ported the unit is unported
    ; either way, and the tag should name what actually blocks it. Putting the
    ; JSDoc gate in front would relabel a VariableStatement carrying a doc comment
    ; as `check-jsdoc-comments`, which fragments the histogram this column exists
    ; to be.
    procedure check_source_element_worker(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_has_ported_check_arm(k) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("check", k)
            this.spend_ambient_report_of_container(node)
            return
        }
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsHasJSDoc) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsdoc-comments", k)
            this.spend_ambient_report_of_container(node)
            return
        }
        ; The kinds the reference's switch has no CASE for, which is a different
        ; fact from an arm this port has not written — see kind_has_no_check_arm.
        ; The JSDoc gate stays in FRONT of it: the two steps before the switch run
        ; for every kind, and a keyword type carrying a doc comment would reach
        ; checkJSDocComments there as any other node does.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_check_arm(k)
            return

        ; case ast.KindBlock, ast.KindModuleBlock: c.checkBlock(node)
        ;
        ; ★★★ THIS ARM IS WHAT LIFTS SLICE 49's LAST BOUND. It wrote *only
        ; TOP-LEVEL statements are ever checked … anything inside a namespace body
        ; is reached through checkModuleDeclaration -> checkSourceElement(body) ->
        ; checkBlock, and neither arm is ported*, and named the next slice as the
        ; one that lifts it. Both arms are here, and they had to arrive together:
        ; the module arm's FIRST step is checkSourceElement(body), so porting it
        ; without this one would have measured the module prefix on a body that
        ; reports immediately.
        if k = KindBlock
        {
            this.check_block(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindModuleBlock
        {
            this.check_block(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindVariableStatement
        {
            this.check_variable_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
        {
            this.check_expression_statement(node)
            return
        }
        ; ── slice 63: the JUMP-AND-GUARD statement arms ─────────────────────
        ;
        ; The reference's own cases are eight: `IfStatement`, `DoStatement`,
        ; `WhileStatement`, `ContinueStatement, BreakStatement` (one label over one
        ; function), `WithStatement`, `LabeledStatement`, `ThrowStatement` and
        ; `TryStatement`. What is NOT here is the five that carry a loop head or a
        ; return type — see the section header.
        if k = KindIfStatement
        {
            this.check_if_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindDoStatement
        {
            this.check_do_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindWhileStatement
        {
            this.check_while_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindContinueStatement
        {
            this.check_break_or_continue_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindBreakStatement
        {
            this.check_break_or_continue_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindWithStatement
        {
            this.check_with_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
        {
            this.check_labeled_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindThrowStatement
        {
            this.check_throw_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTryStatement
        {
            this.check_try_statement(node)
            return
        }
        ; ── slice 64: the LOOP, RETURN and SWITCH arms ──────────────────────
        ;
        ; The reference's remaining five statement cases — `ForStatement`,
        ; `ForInStatement`, `ForOfStatement`, `ReturnStatement` and
        ; `SwitchStatement`. With them all thirteen statement `case` labels of this
        ; switch are reached.
        if k = KindForStatement
        {
            this.check_for_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindForInStatement
        {
            this.check_for_in_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindForOfStatement
        {
            this.check_for_of_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindReturnStatement
        {
            this.check_return_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindSwitchStatement
        {
            this.check_switch_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
        {
            this.check_interface_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
        {
            this.check_type_alias_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
        {
            this.check_type_alias_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
        {
            this.check_enum_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 65. Reached by the enum declaration's own members walk, which is why
        ; it arrives with that arm rather than in a slice of its own.
        if k = KindEnumMember
        {
            this.check_enum_member(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 50 — the six arms whose head is checkGrammarModuleElementContext.
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            this.check_module_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_equals_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_export_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExportAssignment
        {
            this.check_export_assignment(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 51 — the class declaration, whose head is
        ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration.
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
        {
            this.check_class_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 52 — the function declaration, whose head is
        ; checkSignatureDeclaration.
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
        {
            this.check_function_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 54 — the variable declaration, which the VariableStatement arm's
        ; own walk reaches.
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            this.check_variable_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 55 — the parameter, which checkSignatureDeclaration's parameter
        ; list walk (slice 52) has been handing to this switch ever since.
        if k = KindParameter
        {
            this.check_parameter(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 56 — the binding element, which the element walk of slices 54 and
        ; 55 has been handing to this switch ever since.
        if k = KindBindingElement
        {
            this.check_binding_element(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 60 — the type parameter. It became REACHABLE in slice 61, through
        ; checkMappedType's own type parameter and through an InferType's — the two
        ; callers slice 60 named when it wrote the arm down inert.
        if k = KindTypeParameter
        {
            this.check_type_parameter(node)
            return
        }
        ; ── slice 62: the type-MEMBER arms ──────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The two kinds slice 61's members walk has been handing here ever since,
        ; plus the two class kinds that share their bodies and are INERT (nothing
        ; walks a class body or an object literal yet). The reference's own cases
        ; are `PropertyDeclaration`/`PropertySignature` separately and
        ; `MethodDeclaration, MethodSignature` together.
        if k = KindPropertySignature
        {
            this.check_property_signature(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            this.check_property_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindMethodSignature
        {
            this.check_method_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
        {
            this.check_method_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; ── slice 61: the type-node half of the switch ──────────────────────
        ;
        ; case ConstructorType, FunctionType, CallSignature, ConstructSignature,
        ; IndexSignature: c.checkSignatureDeclaration(node) — five kinds and one
        ; call, the function slice 52 wrote whole and nothing had reached with a
        ; kind other than FunctionDeclaration.
        if k = KindConstructorType
        {
            this.check_signature_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindFunctionType
        {
            this.check_signature_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindCallSignature
        {
            this.check_signature_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindConstructSignature
        {
            this.check_signature_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindIndexSignature
        {
            this.check_signature_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeReference
        {
            this.check_type_reference_node(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypePredicate
        {
            this.check_type_predicate(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeQuery
        {
            this.check_type_query(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
        {
            this.check_type_literal(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindArrayType
        {
            this.check_array_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTupleType
        {
            this.check_tuple_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindUnionType
        {
            this.check_union_or_intersection_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindIntersectionType
        {
            this.check_union_or_intersection_type(node)
            return
        }
        ; case ParenthesizedType, OptionalType, RestType:
        ;     node.ForEachChild(c.checkSourceElement)
        if k = KindParenthesizedType
        {
            this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindOptionalType
        {
            this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindRestType
        {
            this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindThisType
        {
            this.check_this_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeOperator
        {
            this.check_type_operator(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindConditionalType
        {
            this.check_conditional_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindInferType
        {
            this.check_infer_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTemplateLiteralType
        {
            this.check_template_literal_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindImportType
        {
            this.check_import_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindNamedTupleMember
        {
            this.check_named_tuple_member(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindIndexedAccessType
        {
            this.check_indexed_access_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindMappedType
        {
            this.check_mapped_type(node)
            return
        }
        ; case JSDocNonNullableType, JSDocNullableType, JSDocAllType,
        ; JSDocTypeLiteral: c.checkJSDocType(node)
        if k = KindJSDocNonNullableType
        {
            this.check_jsdoc_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSDocNullableType
        {
            this.check_jsdoc_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSDocAllType
        {
            this.check_jsdoc_type(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSDocTypeLiteral
        {
            this.check_jsdoc_type(node)
            return
        }
        ; case ast.KindEmptyStatement:    c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
        ; case ast.KindDebuggerStatement: c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
        ;
        ; Two arms whose whole body is that one call — so they are COMPLETE, which
        ; is the first time anything in this file can say so.
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
    }

    ; ★★★ THE ONCE-BIT OF checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext IS A PROPERTY OF
    ; THE WHOLE BLOCK, AND AN UNPORTED ARM MAKES ITS OWNER UNKNOWABLE. Slice 50
    ; found this the moment it ported the BLOCK arm, on
    ; `compiler_ambientWithStatements`: `declare namespace M { break; continue;
    ; debugger; … }` — the reference reports ONE TS1036, on the FIRST statement, and
    ; our port reported it on the `debugger`, because that is the first statement
    ; whose arm exists. A diagnostic at a position the reference does not have is
    ; the one thing diagcheck can fail on, and it did.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SO THE HONEST ANSWER IS TO SAY NOTHING, and the reason is exactly the
    ; reason the port reports rather than approximates everywhere else: the
    ; reference calls checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext from the FIRST LINE of
    ; some twenty statement arms (break/continue, return, if, do, while, for, throw,
    ; try, switch, with, …), so a statement whose arm this slice does not implement
    ; MIGHT have spent the bit. Marking the container is the only claim that cannot
    ; be wrong: it makes our C section a strict SUBSET of the reference's, which is
    ; the relation the instrument asserts, instead of a line in the wrong place.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT USES THE reported_ambient SET AND MEANS SOMETHING ELSE BY IT — *this
    ; container's report is spent or unknowable*, not *we reported it* — which is
    ; why it is its own procedure with its own name rather than a bare
    ; mark_reported_ambient at two call sites.
    ;
    ; ★ THE SUCCESSOR IS NAMED: porting the first line of those twenty arms puts the
    ; report back where the reference puts it and retires this function. Until then
    ; the number it costs is visible — it is read off diagcheck's *units we speak
    ; on*, which is what that counter is printed for.
    ;
    ; ★★ SLICE 63 TOOK EIGHT OF THE TWENTY and slice 64 the remaining five, and the
    ; function STILL stays — which is the ordinary shape of this retirement rather
    ; than a shortfall: the bit belongs to a BLOCK, and a block is unknowable while
    ; ANY of its statements has an unported arm, statement or not.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT IS LEFT IS NOT A STATEMENT AT ALL, and that is the successor stated
    ; precisely rather than by kind-count. Slice 63 wrote *the five loop/return/
    ; switch arms plus NamespaceExportDeclaration and MissingDeclaration*; of those
    ; the five are ported, and NamespaceExportDeclaration turned out to have no arm
    ; in the reference either — it is in kind_has_no_check_arm now, so it reaches
    ; this function only through the JSDoc gate. What remains that can genuinely
    ; spend the bit is MissingDeclaration, whose arm is unported and sits in
    ; statement position.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND THE SECOND CALLER IS THE ONE THAT WILL OUTLIVE THE FIRST: the JSDoc
    ; gate. Any statement of an ambient block that carries a doc comment is
    ; unknowable regardless of whether its arm is written, because the port stops
    ; at `check-jsdoc-comments` in FRONT of the switch — which is a claim about
    ; checkJSDocComments and not about the statement chapter at all. So the
    ; retirement this function is waiting for is now two independent things, and
    ; naming them apart is the point: an inventory that says *the statement arms*
    ; would read as finished today and the function would still be load-bearing.
    ;
    ; ★ Only an AMBIENT node can spend the bit (the check returns at its own first
    ; line otherwise), and only the in-block half of it is a property of the
    ; CONTAINER: the signature half marks the node itself, so an unported statement
    ; there can affect nothing else.
    procedure spend_ambient_report_of_container(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        var in_block false
        if pk = KindBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_block: true
        if in_block = false
            return
        if this.has_reported_ambient(parent)
            return
        this.mark_reported_ambient(parent)
    }

    function kind_has_ported_check_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        ; ★★★ THE KINDS WITH NO ARM AT ALL ANSWER TRUE HERE, and the word `ported`
        ; is what makes that look wrong: what this gate decides is whether the port
        ; SPEAKS on the kind, and for a kind the reference's switch does not
        ; mention, doing nothing IS the whole arm. Slice 54 needed the distinction
        ; because it is the first slice whose walk reaches a TYPE node.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_check_arm(k)
            return true
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
            return true
        if k = KindEmptyStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDebuggerStatement
            return true
        ; Slice 63. The JUMP-AND-GUARD statement arms — the eight `case` labels of
        ; the statement chapter whose bodies reach neither a loop head nor a return
        ; type. Every one of them is REACHED (a statement list walks into it), which
        ; is what separates this row from slices 60 and 62's inert ones.
        if k = KindIfStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDoStatement
            return true
        if k = KindWhileStatement
            return true
        if k = KindContinueStatement
            return true
        if k = KindBreakStatement
            return true
        if k = KindWithStatement
            return true
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
            return true
        if k = KindThrowStatement
            return true
        if k = KindTryStatement
            return true
        ; Slice 64. The five that carry a loop head or a return type — the rest of
        ; the statement chapter, and with them every statement `case` label of
        ; checkSourceElementWorker is reached.
        if k = KindForStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForInStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForOfStatement
            return true
        if k = KindReturnStatement
            return true
        if k = KindSwitchStatement
            return true
        ; Slice 49. The five kinds whose arm BEGINS with checkGrammarModifiers and
        ; has nothing in front of it — so the ported prefix is the whole of the
        ; arm up to the first thing this slice does not implement, and the report
        ; names that thing.
        if k = KindVariableStatement
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 65. The enum MEMBER, reached by the enum declaration's members walk.
        if k = KindEnumMember
            return true
        ; Slice 50. The BLOCK pair plus the six arms of the import/export/module
        ; family — the whole set that reaches checkGrammarModuleElementContext, and
        ; the two blocks that carry the statements those arms recurse into.
        if k = KindBlock
            return true
        if k = KindModuleBlock
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        ; Slice 51. The class declaration — one arm, three functions of prefix,
        ; and the first reader of a list's RANGE outside the parser.
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 52. The function declaration — the last of the switch's arms to be
        ; a HEAD, and the first whose grammar `||` this port runs to its end.
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 54. The variable declaration, reached by the VariableStatement
        ; arm's own walk through checkVariableDeclarationList.
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 55. The parameter — the first arm this file reaches through a
        ; SIGNATURE rather than through a statement list, and the first whose
        ; reports are `c.error`s rather than grammar errors.
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        ; Slice 56. The binding element — the other half of
        ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration, and the first arm whose own walk feeds
        ; ITSELF (a nested pattern's elements are binding elements too).
        if k = KindBindingElement
            return true
        ; Slice 60. The type parameter. ★★★It is INERT and it is here because the
        ; table is a claim about which kinds this port SPEAKS on, not about which
        ; ones it has been asked about: checkTypeParameters calls checkTypeParameter
        ; DIRECTLY, as the reference does, so nothing hands a type parameter to
        ; checkSourceElement today and neither this row nor the switch arm below can
        ; be reached. Leaving them out would make the first slice that does walk into
        ; one — checkMappedType's own type parameter, or an InferType's — report a
        ; hole for an arm that exists. ★The consequence of the direct call is worth
        ; the line: the two steps in FRONT of the switch do not run for a type
        ; parameter, so one carrying a doc comment does NOT take the
        ; check-jsdoc-comments tag. That is the reference's shape, not an omission.
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            return true
        ; Slice 62. The type-MEMBER arms — the property signature and the method
        ; signature, which slice 61's members walk reaches, plus the two CLASS kinds
        ; that share their bodies. ★★★The class pair is INERT for slice 60's reason:
        ; nothing walks a class body's members or an object literal's properties, so
        ; neither kind can reach the switch today — and the row is here because the
        ; table says which kinds this port SPEAKS on, not which ones it has been
        ; asked about.
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 61. The switch's whole TYPE half, plus the five signature kinds
        ; whose case is a call to checkSignatureDeclaration. ★★★It is the largest
        ; single addition this table has taken — twenty-seven kinds — and it is one
        ; slice for the reason the section header gives: eleven of the arms are a
        ; WALK, and a walk stopped one kind short of its neighbour is not a smaller
        ; slice, it is a broken one.
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindTypeReference
            return true
        if k = KindTypePredicate
            return true
        if k = KindTypeQuery
            return true
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
            return true
        if k = KindArrayType
            return true
        if k = KindTupleType
            return true
        if k = KindUnionType
            return true
        if k = KindIntersectionType
            return true
        if k = KindParenthesizedType
            return true
        if k = KindOptionalType
            return true
        if k = KindRestType
            return true
        if k = KindThisType
            return true
        if k = KindTypeOperator
            return true
        if k = KindConditionalType
            return true
        if k = KindInferType
            return true
        if k = KindTemplateLiteralType
            return true
        if k = KindImportType
            return true
        if k = KindNamedTupleMember
            return true
        if k = KindIndexedAccessType
            return true
        if k = KindMappedType
            return true
        if k = KindJSDocNonNullableType
            return true
        if k = KindJSDocNullableType
            return true
        if k = KindJSDocAllType
            return true
        if k = KindJSDocTypeLiteral
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ★★★ THE KINDS checkSourceElementWorker's SWITCH HAS NO CASE FOR — so
    ; `checkSourceElement(x)` on one of them runs the two steps in front of the
    ; switch and then does NOTHING, and a report would be a claim that the unit is
    ; unmeasured where the reference measures nothing either. That is §3.5cx's
    ; checkExternalEmitHelpers argument at a place where it costs units rather than
    ; a comment: over stage 1 the three commonest annotations alone —
    ; `any` 35, `number` 16, `string` 14 — plus `void` 5 and a literal type 2 are
    ; **72 units** that would have carried a tag naming a function that does not
    ; exist. Measured on the way in, with the family switched off.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE REFERENCE'S SWITCH AND IT IS ENUMERABLE, which is
    ; what separates it from a guess: the twelve kinds below are the ELEVEN keyword
    ; types a type annotation can be — the ten of the parser's own
    ; `keyword_type_start` table, plus `void`, which that table deliberately omits
    ; because there is no `void.Foo` fallback, plus `intrinsic`, which only a type
    ; alias's right-hand side can be — and the LITERAL TYPE wrapper. Every OTHER
    ; kind a type annotation can be HAS a case (TypeReference, ArrayType,
    ; TupleType, UnionType, TypeLiteral, FunctionType, TypeOperator, MappedType, …
    ; twenty-six of them).
    ;
    ; ★★ SINCE SLICE 61 THOSE TWENTY-SIX ARE PORTED RATHER THAN REPORTED, which
    ; inverts what this list is FOR without changing what it says. It used to keep
    ; seventy-two units of stage 1 from carrying a tag naming a function that does
    ; not exist while their neighbours carried honest ones; now every neighbour is
    ; an arm, and the list is the only thing standing between a keyword type and a
    ; hole that is not there. The measurement is unchanged and so is the argument.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE LIST, because the list is only worth what
    ; its argument is: every other kind the switch omits — an EXPRESSION kind, a
    ; token, a JSX kind. Those are no-arm kinds too, but nothing in this port hands
    ; one to checkSourceElement, so admitting them would be a claim no instrument
    ; here can reach. The list grows with the arm that first walks into such a
    ; position.
    ;
    ; ★ A LiteralType's CHILD is not walked, and that is the reference's answer
    ; rather than an omission: no case means no ForEachChild, so `var x: "a"` never
    ; reaches the string literal. The three kinds whose case IS a bare
    ; `node.ForEachChild(c.checkSourceElement)` — Parenthesized/Optional/Rest — are
    ; not here for exactly that reason.
    function kind_has_no_check_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindAnyKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUnknownKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindStringKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNumberKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBigIntKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindSymbolKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBooleanKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUndefinedKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNeverKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindObjectKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindVoidKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindIntrinsicKeyword
            return true
        ; ★★★ SLICE 64: NamespaceExportDeclaration BELONGS HERE AND HAD BEEN
        ; COUNTED AS AN UNPORTED ARM FOR SIXTEEN SLICES. `export as namespace N`
        ; has NO `case` in checkSourceElementWorker — the only mention of the kind
        ; anywhere in internal/checker's switch statements is
        ; getTargetOfDeclaration's — so doing nothing IS the reference's whole
        ; answer, and the seventeen units of stage 2 that were sitting at
        ; `check NamespaceExportDeclaration` were sitting on a row that names no
        ; work. ★It was found by asking what is left AFTER the statement chapter
        ; rather than by looking at the row, which is the point: a work-list row is
        ; a claim about the reference, and nothing re-checks a claim that has been
        ; on the list since slice 47.
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        k = KindLiteralType
    }

    ; ── slice 70: the EXPRESSION DISPATCH ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `check-expression` was ONE row of 4 118 units at the head of the work list
    ; and it is a CHAPTER rather than a function: checkExpression → checkExpressionEx
    ; → checkExpressionWorker, whose switch has forty-four `case` labels over
    ; thirty-eight kinds. This slice ports the three functions in front of the
    ; switch and the switch itself, so that the column stops naming a call and
    ; starts naming the ARM the corpus reaches — which is the move slice 47 made
    ; when it put the kind on `check`, slice 63 when it split the statement arms,
    ; and the reason a work-list row is only as good as its resolution.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE SLICE PRODUCES IS A HISTOGRAM, AND THAT IS ITS WHOLE POINT.
    ; Three of the forty-six `case` labels answer a type this port already has, so
    ; the slice finishes three expression shapes; two more run their GRAMMAR check
    ; and stop at the type; every other label reports under its own reference
    ; function's name. The one number that decides the next slice is therefore not
    ; a diagnostic count but which arm the units land on.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE KIND COVERAGE IS DIFFED RATHER THAN EYEBALLED: the forty-six labels
    ; name forty-nine kinds, and the set below is IDENTICAL to the reference's,
    ; compared mechanically (its `ast.Kind*` mentions against this function's
    ; `if k = Kind*`). That check is worth keeping for the next reader — a missing
    ; kind here does not fail, it falls into the default and answers `any`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE FOUR ARMS COMPLETE THREE STATEMENT ARMS WITH THEM. An expression
    ; STATEMENT, a `throw` and a `with` hand the type to nothing — the reference
    ; discards it — so for a ported expression those arms now run to their last
    ; line rather than stopping, which is the first time in this dimension that
    ; porting an expression finishes a STATEMENT.
    ;
    ; ★★ NULL MEANS *I STOPPED* HERE AND NOTHING ELSE, which is what makes the
    ; chain readable after slice 69's two-meaning nil: the reference's worker ends
    ; in `return c.errorType` and never answers nil, so a null coming out of this
    ; chain is unambiguous and a caller tests it directly instead of taking a mark.
    ; ★The one caller that still needs the mark is the one whose own answer may be
    ; legitimately empty — check_variable_like_declaration — and it takes it for
    ; the reason slice 69 wrote down, not for this chain's sake.

    ; checkExpression, whole. The only mode any caller in this port passes.
    function check_expression(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
        this.check_expression_ex(node, CheckModeNormal)

    ; checkExpressionEx, and its four steps split two and two.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO IT SKIPS ARE FIELDS WITH NO READER (§3.5be), each named with the
    ; reader that would make it observable: `c.currentNode` is read by
    ; getTypeOfSymbol's circularity report and by the tracer, both of which report
    ; here; `c.instantiationCount = 0` is the budget instantiateType charges
    ; against, and nothing instantiates yet. A save/restore of a field this file
    ; does not have would be two lines that cannot be wrong and cannot be right.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO IT PORTS ARE BOTH GATES, AND BOTH ARE PROVABLY INERT RATHER THAN
    ; MERELY QUIET — which is why they stand here reporting instead of being left
    ; out. instantiateTypeWithSingleGenericCallSignature returns its argument
    ; unchanged unless the mode carries Inferential or SkipGenericFunctions, and
    ; CheckMode's own note says only Normal is ever passed; isConstEnumObjectType
    ; needs an ANONYMOUS object type whose symbol is a const enum, and the only
    ; producer of one is getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule's enum arm, which reports.
    ; So neither can fire today, and a slice that gives either a producer meets a
    ; report rather than a silently skipped step.
    function check_expression_ex(this, node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let t this.check_expression_worker(node, check_mode)
        if t = null
            return null
        if (check_mode & (CheckModeInferential | CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions)) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("instantiate-type-with-single-generic-call-signature", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return null
        }
        if Checker.is_const_enum_object_type(t as ref[Type])
        {
            this.record_unported("check-const-enum-access", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return null
        }
        t
    }

    ; isConstEnumObjectType + isConstEnumSymbol, both whole and both one line.
    function is_const_enum_object_type(t: ref[Type]) returns bool
    {
        if (t.object_flags & ObjectFlagsAnonymous) = 0
            return false
        let sym t.symbol
        if sym = null
            return false
        (Symbol.flags_of(sym as ref[Symbol]) & SymbolFlagsConstEnum) <> 0
    }

    ; checkExpressionCached — the MEMO, and it is deliberately not one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE CACHE BUYS IS EXACTLY WHAT NOTHING HERE CAN OBSERVE YET, and the
    ; reader that changes that is named rather than guessed at. Its body is
    ; `links.resolvedType == nil` around a checkExpressionEx, with the flow-loop
    ; stack and the flow type cache saved and cleared across the call — neither of
    ; which exists here, because nothing in this port does flow analysis. A memo's
    ; only OBSERVABLE effect on a C section is a diagnostic reported twice when the
    ; same node is checked twice, and every arm this slice ports either answers a
    ; type in silence or reports unported; the first arm that writes an error into
    ; the C section (checkIdentifier's TS2304 is the nearest) is the arm that owes
    ; this function its links table.
    ;
    ; ★ The `checkMode != CheckModeNormal` short-circuit in front of the memo is
    ; kept as the whole function, which is the same statement from the other side:
    ; with Normal the reference consults the cache, and with anything else it does
    ; not — and Normal is all this port passes.
    function check_expression_cached(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
        this.check_expression_ex(node, CheckModeNormal)

    ; ★★★ checkExpressionWorker. THREE OF THE FORTY-SIX `case` LABELS ARE PORTED
    ; and the fourth thing ported is the switch's DEFAULT, which is not a case at
    ; all: `return c.errorType` for a kind the switch does not name. That default
    ; matters more than it looks — every expression kind this port will never see
    ; in expression position answers `any` through it, exactly as upstream.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THESE THREE. A ported arm needs a type this port can MAKE, and the
    ; intrinsic table (see initialize_intrinsic_types) holds every widening and
    ; keyword type and no literal type at all: `null` and an omitted element
    ; answer nullWideningType and undefinedWideningType, a parenthesised expression
    ; answers its operand's type — one line in the reference and a recursion here —
    ; and the default answers errorType. The literal arms are the FIRST thing the
    ; next slice needs and they are one mechanism, not five: newLiteralType, a value
    ; payload on Type, the fresh/regular pair, three intern tables, and — the part
    ; that reaches outside the checker — type_to_string composing a NAME instead of
    ; answering a static string, which its own header already names as the arm that
    ; widens it to a builder.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO LITERAL GRAMMAR CHECKS ARE PORTED ANYWAY, ahead of the types they
    ; stand in front of, because they are reached BEFORE the type in the reference's
    ; own order and neither needs one. Both are complete; neither can put a line
    ; into a C section, and the two reasons are different — see each at its site.
    ; They are ported rather than deferred so the numeric arm's stop names the TYPE
    ; and not a grammar check that would then have to be re-decided under it.
    ;
    ; ★ THE TAG IS THE REFERENCE FUNCTION'S NAME AND THE DETAIL IS THE KIND, even
    ; where the kind is implied by the tag (`check-identifier` is only ever asked
    ; about an Identifier). Two arms need the detail to tell them apart —
    ; checkCallExpression serves both CallExpression and NewExpression, checkAssertion
    ; both TypeAssertionExpression and AsExpression — and a column that is uniform
    ; is worth more than eight saved zeroes.
    function check_expression_worker(this, node: ref[AstNode], check_mode: int) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        if k = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("check-identifier", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindPrivateIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("check-private-identifier-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindThisKeyword
        {
            this.record_unported("check-this-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindSuperKeyword
        {
            this.record_unported("check-super-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        ; `case ast.KindNullKeyword: return c.nullWideningType` — the first
        ; expression in this port with a type.
        if k = KindNullKeyword
            return null_widening_type
        if k = KindStringLiteral
            return this.string_literal_arm(node)
        if k = KindNoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
            return this.string_literal_arm(node)
        ; The grammar check runs FIRST and the type second, which is the
        ; reference's order: `c.checkGrammarNumericLiteral(...)` then
        ; `getFreshTypeOfLiteralType(getNumberLiteralType(jsnum.FromString(
        ; node.Text())))`.
        if k = KindNumericLiteral
        {
            this.check_grammar_numeric_literal(node)
            let bits jsnum_from_string(AstNode.literal_text_of(node), AstNode.literal_text_length_of(node))
            return this.get_fresh_type_of_literal_type(this.get_number_literal_type(bits))
        }
        if k = KindBigIntLiteral
        {
            this.check_grammar_bigint_literal(node)
            return this.bigint_literal_arm(node)
        }
        ; c.trueType / c.falseType — the FRESH member of each boolean pair, built
        ; eagerly in initialize_intrinsic_types.
        if k = KindTrueKeyword
            return true_type
        if k = KindFalseKeyword
            return false_type
        if k = KindTemplateExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-template-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindRegularExpressionLiteral
        {
            this.record_unported("check-regular-expression-literal", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindArrayLiteralExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-array-literal", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindObjectLiteralExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-object-literal", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-property-access-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindQualifiedName
        {
            this.record_unported("check-qualified-name", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-indexed-access", k)
            return null
        }
        ; ast.IsImportCall picks a DIFFERENT function, so the fork is in the work
        ; list rather than behind one row. ★★★SLICE 72 REPLACES THE KIND TEST THAT
        ; USED TO STAND HERE with the predicate itself: `import("m")`'s callee is
        ; the `import` KEYWORD and `import.defer("m")`'s is a MetaProperty, and the
        ; reference sends BOTH here — so the kind test, whose note read *"which is
        ; a kind test and needs nothing else"*, sent the deferred form to
        ; check-call-expression. See Parser.is_import_call.
        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            if Parser.is_import_call(node)
            {
                this.record_unported("check-import-call-expression", k)
                return null
            }
            this.record_unported("check-call-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindNewExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-call-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindTaggedTemplateExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-tagged-template-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        ; checkParenthesizedExpression, whole:
        ;   `return c.checkExpressionEx(node.Expression(), checkMode)`
        ;
        ; ★ THE JSDOC TYPE-ASSERTION ARM THAT USED TO STAND HERE IS GONE FROM THE
        ; REFERENCE at this pin — the function is one line — so `(null)` answers
        ; the same type `null` does, and a parenthesised unported expression takes
        ; ITS arm's tag rather than one naming the parenthesis. That is the same
        ; property the statement arms have: the tag names the first thing not
        ; implemented, however deep it sits.
        if k = KindParenthesizedExpression
        {
            let inner AstNode.expression_of(node)
            if inner = null
                return error_type
            return this.check_expression_ex(inner as ref[AstNode], check_mode)
        }
        if k = KindClassExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-class-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-function-expression-or-object-literal-method", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindArrowFunction
        {
            this.record_unported("check-function-expression-or-object-literal-method", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindTypeAssertionExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-assertion", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindAsExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-assertion", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindTypeOfExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-type-of-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindNonNullExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-non-null-assertion", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
        {
            this.record_unported("check-expression-with-type-arguments", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindSatisfiesExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-satisfies-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindMetaProperty
        {
            this.record_unported("check-meta-property", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindDeleteExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-delete-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindVoidExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-void-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindAwaitExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-await-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-prefix-unary-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindPostfixUnaryExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-postfix-unary-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-binary-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindConditionalExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-conditional-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindSpreadElement
        {
            this.record_unported("check-spread-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        ; `case ast.KindOmittedExpression: return c.undefinedWideningType` — the
        ; elision in `[, x]` and in `let [, a] = xs`.
        if k = KindOmittedExpression
            return undefined_widening_type
        if k = KindYieldExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-yield-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindSyntheticExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-synthetic-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindJsxExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-expression", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindJsxElement
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-element", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindJsxSelfClosingElement
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-self-closing-element", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindJsxFragment
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-fragment", k)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindJsxAttributes
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-attributes", k)
            return null
        }
        ; ★★ THE REFERENCE PANICS HERE ("Should never directly check a
        ; JsxOpeningElement") and this port REPORTS, which is the same choice
        ; type_to_string's null arm and reportCircularityError made: a panic is an
        ; unreachability CLAIM, and the port's way of holding a claim is a row that
        ; would appear if it were false. A trap here would be a crash class the
        ; suite handles through accepted.txt — the wrong instrument for our own
        ; claim about the reference's own tree.
        if k = KindJsxOpeningElement
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsx-opening-element-unreachable", k)
            return null
        }
        ; The switch's default. Not a hole: `return c.errorType` is the reference's
        ; answer for every kind above, printed `any`.
        error_type
    }

    ; The two string-literal `case` labels, which share a body in the reference and
    ; therefore share one here (§3.5bb: a shared record buys a shared BODY only —
    ; here it is the labels that are shared, and the body is the same one).
    ;
    ; ★★ isSkipDirectInferenceNode IS PROVABLY FALSE AND IS THEREFORE NOT ASKED.
    ; It is a membership test in `c.skipDirectInferenceNodes`, a set whose only
    ; writer is the inference machinery's `skipDirectInference` path; nothing here
    ; infers, so the set is empty and the blockedStringType arm cannot be taken.
    ; ★It is one of the few places in this port where the honest port of a test is
    ; NO test — the set does not exist, so a test against it would be a test
    ; against a literal false. The reader that changes that is the inference
    ; dimension, and it arrives with the set.
    function string_literal_arm(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
        this.get_fresh_type_of_literal_type(this.get_string_literal_type(AstNode.literal_text_of(node), AstNode.literal_text_length_of(node)))

    ; `getFreshTypeOfLiteralType(getBigIntLiteralType(jsnum.NewPseudoBigInt(
    ; jsnum.ParsePseudoBigInt(node.Text()), false)))`, which is three routines
    ; deep and is written out because two of the three do something to the digits.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ParsePseudoBigInt IS THE STAND-IN SLICE 44 NAMED THIS ARM AS THE READER
    ; OF, and it is earned here (§3.5p). The scanner's own call on it is earned in
    ; the same commit — see set_bigint_token_value, and the note there for why the
    ; two together are belt and braces rather than a doubled conversion.
    ;
    ; ★★ NewPseudoBigInt TRIMS LEADING ZEROS AND ZERO IS THE EMPTY STRING, which is
    ; not tidiness: `PseudoBigInt`'s own comment says *"The value zero is
    ; represented as an empty string"*, and pseudo_big_int_to_string reads exactly
    ; that — a length test, not a value test. A port that stored `"0"` here would
    ; print `0n` correctly and compare UNEQUAL to a zero that came the other way.
    ;
    ; ★ `negative` is false at this call site and always will be: a minus sign is a
    ; PrefixUnaryExpression, so the literal itself never carries one. The slot is
    ; filled from the argument rather than hard-coded because ParseValidBigInt —
    ; the type-position entry point, unported — is the caller that passes true.
    function bigint_literal_arm(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Type]?
    {
        let text AstNode.literal_text_of(node)
        let n AstNode.literal_text_length_of(node)
        let scratch String^host(((n + (n / 4)) + 4) as size_t)
        let out scratch.get_buffer()
        var start 0
        let m Checker.pseudo_big_int_digits(text, n, out, &start)
        if m < 0
        {
            ; The reference PANICS. Unreachable from here — the scanner accepted
            ; the digits — so it reports rather than trapping, which is this file's
            ; standing answer to a reference panic.
            this.record_unported("parse-pseudo-big-int-invalid", 0)
            return null
        }
        this.get_fresh_type_of_literal_type(this.get_bigint_literal_type(out + start, m, false))
    }

    ; checkGrammarNumericLiteral, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS REPORT IS A SUGGESTION AND CANNOT REACH A C SECTION, which is why the
    ; whole function is ported and its measurement is zero: the last line is
    ; `c.addErrorOrSuggestion(false, …)`, and with isError false that sets
    ; CategorySuggestion and appends to `c.suggestionDiagnostics` — a list
    ; GetDiagnostics does not read. TS80008's own generated line says `Suggestion`.
    ; It is ported rather than skipped because the arm around it is ported, and a
    ; skipped step under a ported arm is the shape §3.5ae warns about: invisible
    ; from both sides.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO TEXTS ARE DIFFERENT TEXTS AND THE REFERENCE SAYS SO. The
    ; fractional test reads the RAW source (`scanner.GetTextOfNode`) because
    ; `node.Text` of `1100000000000000000000` is the scanner's cooked `1.1e21`,
    ; which contains a `.` the source does not — so asking the cooked text would
    ; return early on a literal that must be reported. The VALUE is read from the
    ; cooked text, because that is the one jsnum parses. Two questions, two texts,
    ; and swapping them is a wrong answer in both directions.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE COMPARISON IS ON THE BIT PATTERN and it is exact rather than
    ; approximate: jsnum_from_string answers an IEEE-754 double's BITS, and for two
    ; non-negative finite doubles the unsigned ordering of the bits IS the ordering
    ; of the values. MaxSafeInteger = 2^53 - 1 is 0x433FFFFFFFFFFFFF. A literal is
    ; never negative (the minus sign is a PrefixUnaryExpression) and never NaN, so
    ; the two exclusions the bit trick needs are properties of the input.
    procedure check_grammar_numeric_literal(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if (AstNode.numeric_token_flags_of(node) & TokenFlagsScientific) <> 0
            return
        let start b.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(node))
        let stop AstNode.end_of(node)
        var i: int start
        while i < stop
        {
            if b.byte_at(i) = 0x2E
                return
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let bits jsnum_from_string(AstNode.literal_text_of(node), AstNode.literal_text_length_of(node))
        if bits <= 0x433FFFFFFFFFFFFF
            return
        ; addErrorOrSuggestion(false, …) — see the header. The diagnostic is
        ; deliberately NOT added to this checker's list, and this is the first
        ; SUGGESTION site in the port: writing it into `diagnostics` would invent a
        ; C line the reference does not have, which is the one thing diagcheck
        ; fails on.
        this.record_suggestion(DiagNumeric_literals_with_absolute_values_equal_to_2_53_or_greater_are_too_large_to_be_represented_accurately_as_integers)
    }

    ; The suggestion sink. It counts and drops, and BOTH halves are the point: a
    ; suggestion must not reach the C section, and a suggestion site that did
    ; nothing at all would be indistinguishable from an unported one.
    ;
    ; ★ The counter is the only way a control can prove this function ran — see
    ; slice 70's g-rows.
    procedure record_suggestion(this, code: int)
        set suggestion_count: suggestion_count + 1

    ; checkGrammarBigIntLiteral, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS REPORT IS DEAD UNDER THIS HARNESS AND THE PROOF IS ONE COMPARISON:
    ; `c.languageVersion < core.ScriptTargetES2020` with languageVersion at the
    ; DERIVED DEFAULT ES2025 = 12 and ES2020 = 7. So TS2737 cannot appear, and the
    ; function's answer is false for every input. ★It is the fourth reading of an
    ; unset option in this file (see ScriptTarget's note) and the second that kills
    ; a branch outright rather than steering one.
    ;
    ; ★ The literalType test is two kind comparisons and is ported because it is
    ; the reference's own short-circuit: a bigint in `type T = 1n` or `type T = -1n`
    ; is exempt whatever the target is, so it is not part of what the target kills.
    function check_grammar_bigint_literal(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var literal_type: bool false
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindLiteralType
            set literal_type: true
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindPrefixUnaryExpression
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)) = KindLiteralType
                set literal_type: true
        }
        if literal_type
            return false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return false
        if language_version < ScriptTargetES2020
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagBigInt_literals_are_not_available_when_targeting_lower_than_ES2020)
        false
    }

    ; checkExpressionStatement, whole:
    ;
    ;   c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
    ;   c.checkExpression(node.Expression())
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SECOND LINE IS THE CHECKER'S WHOLE EXPRESSION DIMENSION, and the
    ; report is placed there rather than on the statement so that the unported
    ; column stops saying `ExpressionStatement` — a kind nobody can act on — and
    ; starts naming the EXPRESSION kinds the corpus actually holds. That is the
    ; same move slice 47 made when it put the kind on `check`: this column is the
    ; work list, and a work list is only as useful as its resolution.
    procedure check_expression_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if e = null
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 70: THE RESULT IS DISCARDED, SO THIS ARM IS COMPLETE for every
        ; expression the dispatch can type. It is the fourth statement arm in this
        ; file that runs to its last line, and the first whose completeness came
        ; from the EXPRESSION side.
        this.check_expression(e as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; ── slice 63: the JUMP-AND-GUARD statement arms ──────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Eight `case` labels of checkSourceElementWorker — if / do / while /
    ; (continue, break) / with / labeled / throw / try — and ten reference functions.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THESE EIGHT AND NOT THE WHOLE STATEMENT CHAPTER. What separates them
    ; from the six that stay is the LOOP HEAD: `for`, `for-in` and `for-of` reach
    ; checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement, whose await-modifier branch reads
    ; `c.moduleKind`, `c.languageVersion`, the file's implied node format and
    ; IsEffectiveExternalModule — a second option dimension with its own
    ; measurement to make — and `return` and `switch` reach the TYPE dimension in
    ; their own bodies (getSignatureFromDeclaration, isTypeEqualityComparableTo).
    ; The eight here reach neither: every report they carry is decided by the
    ; SYNTAX and by the binder's tables, which is why the whole of
    ; checkGrammarBreakOrContinueStatement and the whole of checkLabeledStatement's
    ; duplicate-label walk can be ported rather than prefixed.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE ARM THAT MOVES THE MOST IS NOT ONE OF THE ARMS — IT IS THE FIRST
    ; LINE THEY SHARE. checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext is called from the
    ; first line of every one of them, and spend_ambient_report_of_container exists
    ; precisely because an UNPORTED statement arm might have spent that
    ; container's once-bit: it marks the block *unknowable* and says nothing. Eight
    ; of the thirteen statement `case` labels stop being unknowable here, so an
    ; ambient block whose first statement is an `if`, a `try` or a `break` reports
    ; its TS1036 at the reference's own position instead of falling silent. ★The
    ; function is NOT retired — the five loop/return/switch kinds still reach it, and
    ; retiring it needs the whole chapter plus NamespaceExportDeclaration and
    ; MissingDeclaration. That is the successor, and it is named here rather than
    ; guessed at.
    ;
    ; ★★ TWO OF THE TEN FUNCTIONS ARE COMPLETE and the rest are prefixes, which is
    ; worth separating because this file has said *complete* only three times
    ; before: checkBreakOrContinueStatement (with the whole of its grammar check)
    ; and checkTryStatement. checkLabeledStatement is complete except for a
    ; SUGGESTION that cannot reach a C section — see its own note.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHERE THE HOLES ARE, AND THAT THEY ARE ALL ONE HOLE. Five of the eight
    ; arms stop at `check-expression`: the `if`, `do` and `while` conditions through
    ; checkTruthinessExpression, the `throw` operand and the `with` subject
    ; directly. The sixth, the catch clause's type annotation, stops at
    ; `get-type-from-type-node`. So this slice adds no new DIMENSION to the work
    ; list — it moves 179 units of stage 2 from `check <statement kind>`, a row
    ; nobody can act on, onto the expression kinds the corpus actually holds, which
    ; is the move slice 47 made when it put the kind on `check` and slice 50 made
    ; when it ported the block.

    ; checkTruthinessExpression, whole:
    ;   `return c.checkTruthinessOfType(c.checkExpression(node), node)`
    ;
    ; ★ Both halves are the type dimension and the INNER one is evaluated first, so
    ; the tag names checkExpression — the port's ordinary rule that a tag names the
    ; FIRST thing not implemented, and the same tag check_expression_statement
    ; carries. checkTruthinessOfType's own report (*this condition will always
    ; return true*) sits behind it.
    procedure check_truthiness_expression(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        ; checkTruthinessOfType is the outer half and it is still the type
        ; dimension (*this condition will always return true*, TS2801 and the
        ; always-truthy family), so a condition whose expression NOW has a type
        ; moves from `check-expression` to the row naming the half that is left.
        let t this.check_expression(node as ref[AstNode])
        if t = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-truthiness-of-type", AstNode.kind_of(node as ref[AstNode]))
    }

    ; checkIfStatement, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE EMPTY-STATEMENT REPORT IS ABOUT THE THEN-BRANCH AND IS ASKED AFTER IT
    ; HAS BEEN WALKED, which is the reference's order and not a tidiness question:
    ; an EmptyStatement's own arm is checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext, so in an
    ; ambient block `if (x) ;` produces the TS1036 first and the TS1313 second, and
    ; the two land in that order in the sorted list because the semicolon sits
    ; where it sits.
    ;
    ; ★ checkTestingKnownTruthyCallableOrAwaitableOrEnumMemberType takes the type
    ; checkTruthinessExpression answers as its second argument, so it is behind that
    ; hole rather than beside it — no separate report.
    procedure check_if_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        this.check_truthiness_expression(AstNode.expression_of(node))
        let then_statement AstNode.then_statement_of(node)
        this.check_source_element(then_statement)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(then_statement) = KindEmptyStatement
            this.error_on_node(then_statement, DiagThe_body_of_an_if_statement_cannot_be_the_empty_statement)
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.else_statement_of(node))
    }

    ; checkDoStatement and checkWhileStatement, both whole.
    ;
    ; ★ THEIR TWO STEPS ARE IN OPPOSITE ORDERS and that is the reference's, not an
    ; oversight to normalise: a `do` walks its BODY and then checks its condition,
    ; a `while` the other way round. It decides which of the two a unit's tag names
    ; when both are unported, which is the whole reason the order of a prefix
    ; matters at all in this port.
    procedure check_do_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
        this.check_truthiness_expression(AstNode.expression_of(node))
    }

    procedure check_while_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        this.check_truthiness_expression(AstNode.expression_of(node))
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
    }

    ; checkThrowStatement, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REPORT IS ABOUT A TOKEN THAT IS NOT THERE. `throw` followed by a line
    ; break parses as a throw of a MISSING identifier — an Identifier node with
    ; empty text and a zero-length span — and the diagnostic is placed at that
    ; span's start with length 0, which is why it goes through grammar_error_at_pos
    ; rather than through either node-shaped reporter. A zero-length diagnostic is
    ; exactly what the reference emits here and the yardstick compares spans, so
    ; rounding it up to the statement would be a wrong answer that looks right.
    procedure check_throw_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let throw_expr AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node) = false
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(throw_expr) = KindIdentifier
            {
                if AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(throw_expr) = 0
                    this.grammar_error_at_pos(AstNode.pos_of(throw_expr), 0, DiagLine_break_not_permitted_here)
            }
        }
        if throw_expr = null
            return
        ; Discarded, exactly as the reference discards it — so `throw null` is a
        ; finished statement.
        this.check_expression(throw_expr as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; checkWithStatement, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS LAST REPORT IS UNCONDITIONAL AND IS THE ONLY ONE IN THIS FILE THAT
    ; SPANS TWO NODES. `with` is not supported by the checker at all, so every
    ; `with` statement collects TS2410 — over the head only: from the statement's
    ; trivia-skipped start to the POS of its body, i.e. `with (x)` including the
    ; parenthesis and excluding the block. That is a span no node has, which is
    ; what grammar_error_at_pos exists for, and it is the second of its two
    ; callers (§ its own note names `len(",")` as the first).
    ;
    ; ★ The parse-diagnostic guard is asked TWICE — once by this function and once
    ; inside grammar_error_at_pos — and the duplication is the reference's. It is
    ; kept rather than folded, because folding it would make the port's shape
    ; differ from the text it is checked against for no gain.
    procedure check_with_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node) = false
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAwaitContext) <> 0
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagX_with_statements_are_not_allowed_in_an_async_function_block)
        }
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if e <> null
            this.check_expression(e as ref[AstNode])
        let body AstNode.statement_of(node)
        if body = null
            return
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return
        let start b.skip_trivia_at(AstNode.pos_of(node))
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(start, AstNode.pos_of(body) - start, DiagThe_with_statement_is_not_supported_All_symbols_in_a_with_block_will_have_type_any)
    }

    ; checkTryStatement, whole — the second function in this file that is complete
    ; rather than a prefix, and the first whose completeness is inherited (every
    ; step of it is a call to something already written).
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE BLOCKS GO TO check_block DIRECTLY AND NOT THROUGH
    ; check_source_element, which is the reference's spelling and has a consequence
    ; worth the line: the two steps in FRONT of the switch do not run for them, so
    ; a try block carrying a doc comment does NOT take the check-jsdoc-comments tag.
    ; That is the same fact slice 60 recorded about checkTypeParameters' direct call.
    procedure check_try_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        let try_block AstNode.try_block_of(node)
        if try_block <> null
            this.check_block(try_block)
        let catch_clause AstNode.catch_clause_of(node)
        if catch_clause <> null
            this.check_catch_clause(catch_clause)
        let finally_block AstNode.finally_block_of(node)
        if finally_block <> null
            this.check_block(finally_block)
    }

    ; checkCatchClause, whole in shape and a prefix in one branch.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS THREE BRANCHES ARE AN else-CHAIN OVER THE CATCH VARIABLE'S SHAPE, and
    ; only the FIRST of them is behind the type system: an annotation has to be
    ; resolved before *must be any or unknown* can be decided, so that arm reports
    ; `get-type-from-type-node` and says nothing. The other two are ours whole — an
    ; initializer is a syntactic fact, and the redeclaration walk is a question
    ; about the binder's own tables, which this port has.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REDECLARATION WALK IS THE FIRST TIME THE CHECKER READS A LOCALS TABLE
    ; OF A NODE THAT IS NOT ITS ARGUMENT, and the pairing is the point: it walks the
    ; CATCH CLAUSE's locals (the caught names) and looks each up in the BLOCK's, so
    ; `try {} catch (e) { let e; }` reports on the inner `let`. The reference
    ; iterates a Go MAP, i.e. in an unspecified order, while this walks the table in
    ; insertion order — invisible, because a catch clause binds one name in every
    ; shape but a destructuring pattern, and the collection is sorted by position
    ; before it is answered.
    procedure check_catch_clause(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let declaration AstNode.catch_variable_declaration_of(node)
        if declaration <> null
        {
            this.check_variable_like_declaration(declaration)
            let type_node AstNode.type_of(declaration)
            if type_node <> null
                this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", AstNode.kind_of(type_node))
            else
            {
                let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(declaration)
                if initializer <> null
                    this.grammar_error_on_first_token(initializer, DiagCatch_clause_variable_cannot_have_an_initializer)
                else
                    this.report_catch_clause_redeclarations(node)
            }
        }
        let block AstNode.catch_block_of(node)
        if block <> null
            this.check_block(block)
    }

    ; The `else` arm of checkCatchClause, as its own procedure because the nesting
    ; is four deep in the reference and the walk is the interesting half.
    procedure report_catch_clause_redeclarations(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let block_locals AstNode.locals_of(AstNode.catch_block_of(node))
        if block_locals = null
            return
        let caught AstNode.locals_of(node)
        var i 0
        while i < SymbolTable.length(caught)
        {
            let block_local SymbolTable.get(block_locals, SymbolTable.entry_name_data_at(caught, i), SymbolTable.entry_name_len_at(caught, i))
            if block_local <> null
            {
                let value_declaration Symbol.value_declaration_of(block_local)
                if value_declaration <> null
                {
                    if (Symbol.flags_of(block_local) & SymbolFlagsBlockScopedVariable) <> 0
                        this.grammar_error_on_node(value_declaration, DiagCannot_redeclare_identifier_0_in_catch_clause)
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkBreakOrContinueStatement, whole — two lines, and the whole of what they
    ; guard is below.
    procedure check_break_or_continue_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node) = false
            this.check_grammar_break_or_continue_statement(node)
    }

    ; checkGrammarBreakOrContinueStatement, whole. An ancestor walk with four ways
    ; to answer *this jump is fine* and five diagnostics for the ways it is not.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE Go `switch` HAS A `default` AND IT IS NOT A FALLTHROUGH. A
    ; SwitchStatement that does not satisfy the case's inner condition — an
    ; unlabeled `continue`, or any labeled jump — ends the switch and walks ON to
    ; the parent; it does NOT reach the `default` arm's iteration test. Reading the
    ; three arms as an if/else-if/else chain is the only faithful spelling, and
    ; reading them as three independent tests would make `switch` count as an
    ; enclosing iteration statement for `continue`, which is exactly the error
    ; TS1104 exists to report.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FUNCTION-BOUNDARY TEST COMES BEFORE THE KIND SWITCH, so a jump out of
    ; a function reports TS1107 even when the label it names is genuinely there —
    ; the walk never reaches it. That order is what makes the report *cannot cross
    ; function boundary* rather than *no such label*.
    ;
    ; ★ It answers the RESULT of its report, and every `return` of it is a report or
    ; a `false`. The caller ignores the value; the reference's own signature keeps
    ; it because grammarErrorOnNode's answer is what a grammar check's `&&` chains
    ; read, and this one is written at the reference's shape so a later caller does
    ; not have to re-derive it.
    function check_grammar_break_or_continue_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let target_label AstNode.label_name_of(node)
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        var current: ref[AstNode]? node
        while current <> null
        {
            if Checker.is_function_like_or_class_static_block_declaration(current)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagJump_target_cannot_cross_function_boundary)
            let ck AstNode.kind_of(current)
            if ck = KindLabeledStatement
            {
                if target_label <> null
                {
                    if Checker.identifier_names_equal(AstNode.label_name_of(current), target_label)
                    {
                        ; A matching label: `continue` may only name one that sits on
                        ; an iteration statement, `break` may name any.
                        if k = KindContinueStatement
                        {
                            if Checker.is_iteration_statement(AstNode.statement_of(current), true) = false
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_continue_statement_can_only_jump_to_a_label_of_an_enclosing_iteration_statement)
                        }
                        return false
                    }
                }
            }
            else
            {
                if ck = KindSwitchStatement
                {
                    if k = KindBreakStatement
                    {
                        if target_label = null
                            return false
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    if Checker.is_iteration_statement(current, false)
                    {
                        if target_label = null
                            return false
                    }
                }
            }
            set current: AstNode.parent_node_of(current)
        }
        if target_label <> null
        {
            if k = KindBreakStatement
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_break_statement_can_only_jump_to_a_label_of_an_enclosing_statement)
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_continue_statement_can_only_jump_to_a_label_of_an_enclosing_iteration_statement)
        }
        if k = KindBreakStatement
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_break_statement_can_only_be_used_within_an_enclosing_iteration_or_switch_statement)
        this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_continue_statement_can_only_be_used_within_an_enclosing_iteration_statement)
    }

    ; checkLabeledStatement, whole except for a report no C section can hold.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE UNUSED-LABEL REPORT IS A SUGGESTION UNDER THIS HARNESS, which is the
    ; reachability check's argument at a second site and is read off the reference
    ; the same way: `c.errorOrSuggestion(c.compilerOptions.AllowUnusedLabels ==
    ; core.TSFalse, labelNode, Unused_label)`. AllowUnusedLabels is UNSET (the
    ; oracle passes SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and nothing else), so
    ; the flag is TSUnknown, `== TSFalse` is false, and the diagnostic goes to
    ; c.suggestionDiagnostics — a list GetDiagnostics does not read. So TS7028
    ; cannot appear in a C section here, and a report would claim the unit is
    ; unmeasured where the reference emits nothing. ★It is conditional on the
    ; OPTION, exactly as the reachability check is: a slice that lets
    ; `@allowUnusedLabels: false` through would make it observable and owe the
    ; NodeFlagsUnreachable half of the binder with it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DUPLICATE-LABEL WALK STOPS AT THE FIRST FUNCTION and reports on the
    ; LABEL rather than on the statement, which is the same asymmetry slice 62's
    ; span table produced: the two spellings do not coincide here, because a
    ; LabeledStatement's error range is its whole `l: for (…) …` and the label's is
    ; two characters.
    procedure check_labeled_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let label_node AstNode.label_name_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node) = false
        {
            var current AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            while current <> null
            {
                if AstNode.is_function_like(current)
                    break
                if AstNode.kind_of(current) = KindLabeledStatement
                {
                    if Checker.identifier_names_equal(AstNode.label_name_of(current), label_node)
                    {
                        this.grammar_error_on_node(label_node, DiagDuplicate_label_0)
                        break
                    }
                }
                set current: AstNode.parent_node_of(current)
            }
        }
        ; The unused-label suggestion — see the header.
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
    }

    ; ast.IsIterationStatement, whole — the five loop kinds, plus a LabeledStatement
    ; whose own statement is one when the caller asks to look through labels.
    ;
    ; ★ The recursion passes the flag ON, so `a: b: for (…) …` is an iteration
    ; statement through both labels. Written as the reference writes it rather than
    ; unrolled to one level.
    function is_iteration_statement(node: ref[AstNode], look_in_labeled_statements: bool) returns bool
    {
        let k Checker.kind_or_unknown(node)
        if k = KindForStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForInStatement
            return true
        if k = KindForOfStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDoStatement
            return true
        if k = KindWhileStatement
            return true
        if k = KindLabeledStatement
        {
            if look_in_labeled_statements = false
                return false
            return Checker.is_iteration_statement(AstNode.statement_of(node), look_in_labeled_statements)
        }
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsFunctionLikeOrClassStaticBlockDeclaration, whole.
    function is_function_like_or_class_static_block_declaration(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if node = null
            return false
        if AstNode.is_function_like(node)
            return true
        AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
    }

    ; `a.Text() == b.Text()` for two label identifiers — the node-to-node twin of
    ; identifier_text_equals, which compares against a C string. Both operands are
    ; a LabeledStatement's or a break/continue's label, i.e. an Identifier, and a
    ; node that is not one answers a null text: names_equal then compares lengths
    ; first, so a null never reaches the byte loop.
    function identifier_names_equal(a: ref[AstNode]?, b: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if a = null
            return false
        if b = null
            return false
        SymbolTable.names_equal(AstNode.identifier_text_of(a), AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(a), AstNode.identifier_text_of(b), AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(b))
    }

    ; ── slice 64: the LOOP, RETURN and SWITCH arms — the statement chapter closes ─
    ;
    ; The five `case` labels slice 63 named as its remainder: `ForStatement`,
    ; `ForInStatement`, `ForOfStatement`, `ReturnStatement` and `SwitchStatement`,
    ; and six reference functions (the three loop arms plus
    ; checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement, checkReturnStatement and
    ; checkSwitchStatement). With them the thirteen statement `case` labels of
    ; checkSourceElementWorker are all reached.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 63 NAMED TWO BLOCKERS AND ONE OF THEM WAS ALREADY PAID FOR. It
    ; deferred the three loop arms because checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement's
    ; await branch reads `c.moduleKind`, `c.languageVersion`, the file's implied
    ; node format and IsEffectiveExternalModule — *a second option dimension with
    ; its own measurement to make*. Three of those four FIELDS already exist and
    ; already carry their measured default: `module_kind` = ModuleKindES2022 (slice
    ; 50's derivation, which is what makes TS18060 live), `language_version` =
    ; ScriptTargetES2025 and `emit_module_format` = ModuleKindNone (slice 58). So
    ; the dimension was opened two slices earlier by functions that had nothing to
    ; do with loops, and the deferral outlived its reason — §3.5cp's shape again,
    ; and the reason it is worth a paragraph is that a deferral names the work it
    ; is waiting for and NOTHING re-reads that name when the work lands somewhere
    ; else.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE DIMENSION ACTUALLY ANSWERS, AND IT COLLAPSES. With
    ; moduleKind = ES2022 the switch over it takes the `ES2022, ESNext, Preserve,
    ; System` label, whose body is `if languageVersion >= ES2017 { break }` — and
    ; ES2025 is 12 against ES2017's 4, so it BREAKS and reports nothing. TS1432
    ; (*top-level for-await is only allowed when module is …*) is therefore
    ; unreachable under this harness, and so is TS1309 (*the current file is a
    ; CommonJS module*), which needs one of Node16/Node18/Node20/NodeNext.
    ; ★IsEffectiveExternalModule collapses the same way: it is
    ; `IsExternalModule(file) || (isCommonJSContainingModuleKind(ES2022) &&
    ; CommonJSModuleIndicator != nil)`, and ES2022 = 7 is neither CommonJS (1) nor
    ; in 100..199, so the second disjunct is dead and the question is the binder's
    ; `is_external_module` alone. What SURVIVES is TS1431, and it survives BECAUSE
    ; of the derivation rather than in spite of it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE OTHER BLOCKER WAS REAL AND IS A PREFIX, NOT A DEFERRAL. `return` and
    ; `switch` do reach the type system in their own bodies, but not in their FIRST
    ; steps: checkReturnStatement's two grammar reports (TS18041 inside a class
    ; static block, TS1108 outside a function body) are decided by the ancestor
    ; walk alone and stand in front of getSignatureFromDeclaration, and
    ; checkSwitchStatement's duplicate-default report and its per-clause
    ; `checkSourceElements` are both in front of isTypeEqualityComparableTo. So the
    ; two arrive as ordinary prefixes and the chapter closes on the same terms as
    ; every other one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE ARM THAT MOVES THE MOST IS AGAIN NOT AN ARM — IT IS THE WALK.
    ; Four of the five recurse into a statement the port had never reached: a
    ; loop's body, and every statement of every `switch` clause. That is the
    ; slice-50 move (the block) and the slice-63 move (the guards) at the last
    ; container the statement grammar has, and it is why a slice whose own reports
    ; are five codes long changes the count on statements it does not mention.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS UNREACHABLE, AND IT IS SLICE 63's ONE FACT AGAIN. TS18038
    ; (*for-await cannot be used inside a class static block*) needs a class static
    ; block's body, and TS1103 (*for-await only within async functions*) needs a
    ; for-of that is NOT in a top-level context, i.e. one inside a function body.
    ; Nothing in this port walks into either. Both are ported at the reference's
    ; shape and both are measured from both sides in the battery, exactly as slice
    ; 63's TS1107 was.
    ;
    ; ★ TS1103's RELATED INFORMATION is deliberately not built: the oracle's C
    ; section is `pos end code` and nothing else (tests/oracle/types.go), so a
    ; related-info record is invisible to every instrument this port has. It is an
    ; absence with a reason rather than a hole, and the reason is the INSTRUMENT's
    ; rather than the port's — the day a yardstick compares it, this line is where
    ; to look.

    ; getContainingFunctionOrClassStaticBlock —
    ; `FindAncestor(node.Parent, IsFunctionLikeOrClassStaticBlockDeclaration)`,
    ; written out as the walk it is (§3.9d), starting at the PARENT. The twin of
    ; get_containing_function above; the predicate it walks on is slice 63's, which
    ; checkGrammarBreakOrContinueStatement already needed.
    function get_containing_function_or_class_static_block(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if node = null
            return null
        var p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        while p <> null
        {
            if Checker.is_function_like_or_class_static_block_declaration(p)
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.IsEffectiveExternalModule, whole:
    ;
    ;   IsExternalModule(file) ||
    ;     (isCommonJSContainingModuleKind(options.GetEmitModuleKind()) &&
    ;      file.CommonJSModuleIndicator != nil)
    ;
    ; ★ Both halves are written even though the second is provably false here (see
    ; the section header: ES2022 is neither CommonJS nor in the Node16..NodeNext
    ; range), because the module kind is a FIELD — a slice that lets `@module:`
    ; through changes the answer without touching this function, and a folded
    ; version would silently keep answering the old one.
    function is_effective_external_module(this) returns bool
    {
        if b.is_external_module_of()
            return true
        if Checker.is_common_js_containing_module_kind(module_kind) = false
            return false
        b.common_js_module_indicator_of() <> null
    }

    ; isCommonJSContainingModuleKind, whole.
    function is_common_js_containing_module_kind(kind: int) returns bool
    {
        if kind = ModuleKindCommonJS
            return true
        if kind < ModuleKindNode16
            return false
        kind <= ModuleKindNodeNext
    }

    ; checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement, whole — the head all three loop arms
    ; below share, and the only function of this slice that reports more than
    ; twice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS AWAIT BRANCH IS THE OPTION DIMENSION AND IT ANSWERS WITH ONE
    ; DIAGNOSTIC. See the section header for the derivation; what is left in the
    ; code is the shape, with each dead label carrying the number that kills it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `async` TEST IS ABOUT A KEYWORD THAT IS NOT ONE. `for (async of […])`
    ; is a for-of whose initializer parses as an ordinary Identifier spelled
    ; `async`, and the report is TS1106; the test is a TEXT comparison because
    ; there is no AsyncKeyword token in that position to ask about. It is asked
    ; only outside an await context, where `for await (async of …)` is a different
    ; error already reported above.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DECLARATION-LIST BRANCH IS GUARDED BY ANOTHER GRAMMAR CHECK'S ANSWER —
    ; `if !c.checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList(list)` — which is slice 53's
    ; function, and the guard is what stops a second report on a list that has
    ; already produced one. ★An EMPTY declaration list returns without a report and
    ; the reference says why at its own line: `for (let of [1,2,3])` parses `let`
    ; as the loop variable's NAME and leaves the list empty, so a report here would
    ; be about a construct the user did not write.
    ;
    ; ★ It answers a bool that all three callers IGNORE. Kept at the reference's
    ; signature for the reason check_grammar_break_or_continue_statement's is: the
    ; value is what a grammar check's `&&` chains read, and rebuilding it later is
    ; the more expensive half.
    function check_grammar_for_in_or_for_of_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
            return true
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        let await_modifier AstNode.for_await_modifier_of(node)
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if k = KindForOfStatement
        {
            if await_modifier <> null
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAwaitContext) = 0
                {
                    if Binder.is_in_top_level_context(node)
                    {
                        if this.has_parse_diagnostics() = false
                        {
                            if this.is_effective_external_module() = false
                                this.error_on_node(await_modifier, DiagX_for_await_loops_are_only_allowed_at_the_top_level_of_a_file_when_that_file_is_a_module_but_this_file_has_no_imports_or_exports_Consider_adding_an_empty_export_to_make_this_file_a_module)
                            ; ★★★ The `switch c.moduleKind`, and its two
                            ; FALLTHROUGHS are the whole of its meaning — read as
                            ; three independent labels it says something else. The
                            ; Go text is
                            ;
                            ;   case Node16, Node18, Node20, NodeNext:
                            ;       if impliedNodeFormat == CommonJS { TS1309; break }
                            ;       fallthrough
                            ;   case ES2022, ESNext, Preserve, System:
                            ;       if languageVersion >= ES2017 { break }
                            ;       fallthrough
                            ;   default:
                            ;       TS1432
                            ;
                            ; so a NODE module whose file is not CommonJS is judged
                            ; by the ES label's target test, and a module kind in
                            ; NEITHER set reports TS1432 without any test at all.
                            ; Written as the two membership tests plus one flag,
                            ; because the flag is what a fallthrough IS.
                            ;
                            ; ★★ Both labels are dead under this harness and each
                            ; for its own number: module_kind is ES2022 = 7, so the
                            ; Node label is never taken; language_version is
                            ; ES2025 = 12 against ES2017 = 4, so the ES label
                            ; breaks. TS1309 and TS1432 are therefore both
                            ; unreachable here — and neither is folded away,
                            ; because module_kind and language_version are FIELDS
                            ; and a slice that lets `@module:` or `@target:` through
                            ; moves them without touching this line.
                            var report_es_target false
                            if Checker.module_kind_reaches_the_node_label(module_kind)
                            {
                                if implied_node_format = ModuleKindCommonJS
                                    this.error_on_node(await_modifier, DiagThe_current_file_is_a_CommonJS_module_and_cannot_use_await_at_the_top_level)
                                else
                                {
                                    if language_version < ScriptTargetES2017
                                        set report_es_target: true
                                }
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                if Checker.module_kind_reaches_the_es_label(module_kind)
                                {
                                    if language_version < ScriptTargetES2017
                                        set report_es_target: true
                                }
                                else
                                    set report_es_target: true
                            }
                            if report_es_target
                                this.error_on_node(await_modifier, DiagTop_level_for_await_loops_are_only_allowed_when_the_module_option_is_set_to_es2022_esnext_system_node16_node18_node20_nodenext_or_preserve_and_the_target_option_is_set_to_es2017_or_higher)
                        }
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        ; UNREACHABLE under this port and not under the reference:
                        ; a for-of outside a top-level context sits inside a
                        ; function body, and nothing here walks into one. Ported at
                        ; the reference's shape; the related-info record it carries
                        ; is not built — see the section header.
                        if this.has_parse_diagnostics() = false
                        {
                            this.error_on_node(await_modifier, DiagX_for_await_loops_are_only_allowed_within_async_functions_and_at_the_top_levels_of_modules)
                            return true
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAwaitContext) = 0
            {
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(initializer) = KindIdentifier
                {
                    if Checker.identifier_text_equals(initializer, "async", 5)
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_of_statement_may_not_be_async)
                }
            }
        }
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(initializer) <> KindVariableDeclarationList
            return false
        if this.check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(initializer)
            return false
        let declarations AstNode.variable_declarations_of(initializer)
        let count AstNode.list_count(declarations)
        if count = 0
            return false
        if count > 1
        {
            if k = KindForInStatement
                return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(AstNode.child_in_list(declarations, 1), DiagOnly_a_single_variable_declaration_is_allowed_in_a_for_in_statement)
            return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(AstNode.child_in_list(declarations, 1), DiagOnly_a_single_variable_declaration_is_allowed_in_a_for_of_statement)
        }
        let first AstNode.child_in_list(declarations, 0)
        if AstNode.initializer_of(first) <> null
        {
            if k = KindForInStatement
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(first), DiagThe_variable_declaration_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_have_an_initializer)
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(first), DiagThe_variable_declaration_of_a_for_of_statement_cannot_have_an_initializer)
        }
        if AstNode.type_of(first) <> null
        {
            if k = KindForInStatement
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(first, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_use_a_type_annotation)
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(first, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_of_statement_cannot_use_a_type_annotation)
        }
        false
    }

    ; The `ES2022, ESNext, Preserve, System` label of that switch, as the
    ; membership test it is. ★System = 4 is NOT among this port's ModuleKind
    ; constants and is deliberately not added: the rule above says a member is
    ; declared when a reader names it, and what this function needs is the SET, not
    ; four branch labels. The number is the reference's (internal/core).
    ; The `Node16, Node18, Node20, NodeNext` label of the same switch. ★It is NOT
    ; is_common_js_containing_module_kind, which the eye wants it to be: that
    ; predicate also admits ModuleKindCommonJS = 1, and CommonJS falls to this
    ; switch's `default` and reports TS1432. Two ranges one member apart, with two
    ; different answers — so they are two functions.
    function module_kind_reaches_the_node_label(kind: int) returns bool
    {
        if kind < ModuleKindNode16
            return false
        kind <= ModuleKindNodeNext
    }

    function module_kind_reaches_the_es_label(kind: int) returns bool
    {
        if kind = ModuleKindES2022
            return true
        if kind = ModuleKindESNext
            return true
        if kind = ModuleKindPreserve
            return true
        kind = 4
    }

    ; checkForStatement, whole — the third statement arm in this file that is
    ; COMPLETE rather than a prefix, and the first whose completeness needed
    ; nothing new: every step is a call to something slices 53, 54 and 63 wrote.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE GRAMMAR CHECK AND THE CHECK ARE TWO SEPARATE VISITS TO THE SAME LIST,
    ; and the reference asks the KIND twice to get there — once through the generic
    ; `node.Initializer()` inside the ambient guard and once through the record.
    ; Kept as two, because the guard runs only when the statement is NOT ambient
    ; and the walk below runs always.
    procedure check_for_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node) = false
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(initializer) = KindVariableDeclarationList
                this.check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(initializer)
        }
        if initializer <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(initializer) = KindVariableDeclarationList
                this.check_variable_declaration_list(initializer)
            else
                this.check_expression(initializer as ref[AstNode])
        }
        let condition AstNode.for_condition_of(node)
        if condition <> null
            this.check_truthiness_expression(condition)
        let incrementor AstNode.for_incrementor_of(node)
        if incrementor <> null
            this.check_expression(incrementor as ref[AstNode])
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
        ; `if node.Locals() != nil { c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(node) }` —
        ; observable only through checkUnusedIdentifiers, which checkUnused gates
        ; off under this harness (see the file header).
    }

    ; checkForInStatement, a PREFIX in three places and whole in its walk.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS ELSE-CHAIN IS WHERE THE PORT HAS TO CHOOSE, AND IT CHOOSES SILENCE.
    ; `if isArrayLiteral || isObjectLiteral -> TS2491; else if !assignable ->
    ; TS2405; else -> checkReferenceExpression` is ONE chain, so the second arm's
    ; type question stands between the first arm and the third: with the type
    ; unavailable this port can report the first arm (a syntactic fact) and must
    ; say nothing for the other two. Slice 62's g07 and slice 63's g20 said the same
    ; sentence — diagcheck forgives a missing line and fails on an invented one —
    ; and here it costs TS2405, TS2406 and TS2780.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DESTRUCTURING REPORT APPEARS TWICE, on two different nodes: once on
    ; the first declaration's NAME when the left side is a declaration list, and
    ; once on the initializer EXPRESSION when it is not. They are one message and
    ; two sites, which is why the port writes them at their two sites rather than
    ; hoisting the message.
    ;
    ; ★ The right-hand expression is walked FIRST in the reference
    ; (`rightType := …c.checkExpression(data.Expression)`), so it is what the tag
    ; names — which is why a `for-in` reports `check-expression` on its SUBJECT and
    ; not on its left side, even though the left side is the half this arm can
    ; still report about.
    procedure check_for_in_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_for_in_or_for_of_statement(node)
        let subject AstNode.expression_of(node)
        var right_type: ref[Type]? null
        if subject <> null
            set right_type: this.check_expression(subject as ref[AstNode])
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(initializer) = KindVariableDeclarationList
        {
            let declarations AstNode.variable_declarations_of(initializer)
            if AstNode.list_count(declarations) <> 0
            {
                let name AstNode.name_of(AstNode.child_in_list(declarations, 0))
                if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
                    this.error_on_node(name, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_a_destructuring_pattern)
            }
            this.check_variable_declaration_list(initializer)
        }
        else
        {
            if initializer <> null
            {
                let left_type this.check_expression(initializer as ref[AstNode])
                let ik AstNode.kind_of(initializer)
                if ik = KindArrayLiteralExpression
                    this.error_on_node(initializer, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_a_destructuring_pattern)
                if ik = KindObjectLiteralExpression
                    this.error_on_node(initializer, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_a_destructuring_pattern)
                ; ★★ THE ELSE-IF NEEDS NO ELSE HERE, and that is a proof rather than
                ; a shortcut: the chain's first arm fires exactly for an array or
                ; object literal, and both of those kinds REPORT in the dispatch —
                ; so a non-null left type is already evidence that the first arm did
                ; not fire. The two remaining arms are TS2405 and
                ; checkReferenceExpression, which is what the row names.
                if left_type <> null
                    this.record_unported("is-type-assignable-to", ik)
            }
        }
        ; `if rightType == neverType || !isTypeAssignableToKind(…) -> TS2407` is the
        ; type dimension outright, and it is the reason a `for-in` over anything is
        ; still an unported unit. ★Since slice 70 the test can be REACHED — a
        ; subject the dispatch types arrives here with a rightType — so the row
        ; names the test rather than the call in front of it.
        if right_type <> null
            this.record_unported("is-type-assignable-to-kind", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
    }

    ; checkForOfStatement, a prefix in its right half.
    ;
    ; ★★ ITS AWAIT BRANCH IS TWO ARMS AND BOTH ARE DEAD, FOR TWO DIFFERENT REASONS.
    ; The class-static-block arm (TS18038) needs a static block's body and the
    ; other arm ends in checkExternalEmitHelpers, which returns at its own first
    ; line because ImportHelpers is unset (§3.5cx priced that call). So the branch
    ; is written for its shape and reports nothing under this harness — the first
    ; reason is this port's, the second is the reference's, and the fixture says so
    ; at its own line rather than leaving the second half to be rediscovered.
    ;
    ; ★ getContainingFunctionOrClassStaticBlock is called BEFORE the null test on
    ; the await modifier in the reference, and the order is kept: it is a walk with
    ; no report, so the difference is invisible — and a reader checking this
    ; against the text should not have to notice that it was moved.
    procedure check_for_of_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_for_in_or_for_of_statement(node)
        let container Checker.get_containing_function_or_class_static_block(node)
        let await_modifier AstNode.for_await_modifier_of(node)
        if await_modifier <> null
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
                this.grammar_error_on_node(await_modifier, DiagX_for_await_loops_cannot_be_used_inside_a_class_static_block)
            ; else: GetFunctionFlags + languageVersion + checkExternalEmitHelpers,
            ; whose first line returns on the unset ImportHelpers — see the header.
        }
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(initializer) = KindVariableDeclarationList
            this.check_variable_declaration_list(initializer)
        else
        {
            ; checkRightHandSideOfForOf, then either checkDestructuringAssignment or
            ; checkExpression + checkReferenceExpression + an assignability check.
            ; All four are the type dimension, and the tag names the FIRST of them
            ; the reference reaches, which is the right-hand side.
            ;
            ; ★ THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE'S OWN FIRST STEP IS THE DISPATCH — checkRight
            ; HandSideOfForOf calls checkNonNullExpression, which is checkNonNullType
            ; over checkExpression — so the call goes in front of the row, and the
            ; row keeps the name of the function whose REMAINDER (the iteration
            ; types) is unported. That is the row slice 69 already put on the work
            ; list at 78 units.
            let subject AstNode.expression_of(node)
            if subject <> null
            {
                let right this.check_expression(subject as ref[AstNode])
                if right <> null
                    this.record_unported("check-right-hand-side-of-for-of", AstNode.kind_of(subject as ref[AstNode]))
            }
        }
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.statement_of(node))
    }

    ; checkReturnStatement's PREFIX — three steps, of which two report.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SECOND REPORT IS LIVE AND THE FIRST IS NOT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
    ; ONE FACT SLICE 63 BOUNDED ITSELF BY. TS1108 (*a return statement can only be
    ; used within a function body*) fires when the ancestor walk finds NO container
    ; at all, which is exactly a top-level `return` — reachable, and the commonest
    ; thing this arm will say. TS18041 needs a class static block's body, which
    ; nothing here walks into.
    ;
    ; ★ The stop is `getSignatureFromDeclaration`, which is where every remaining
    ; branch of this function goes through; the tag names it rather than
    ; checkExpressionCached, because the signature is fetched first.
    procedure check_return_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
            return
        let container Checker.get_containing_function_or_class_static_block(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
        {
            this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_return_statement_cannot_be_used_inside_a_class_static_block)
            return
        }
        if container = null
        {
            this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_return_statement_can_only_be_used_within_a_function_body)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("get-signature-from-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(container))
    }

    ; checkSwitchStatement, whole except for the two type questions inside its loop.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DUPLICATE-DEFAULT REPORT IS A ONCE-BIT OVER THE LOOP, not a per-clause
    ; test: the flag is set by the first report and the guard reads it, so
    ; `default: … default: … default:` reports ONCE, on the second clause. Written
    ; with the reference's two locals rather than by counting the clauses, because
    ; a count would report on the third as well.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PER-CLAUSE `checkSourceElements(clause.Statements())` IS WHAT MAKES
    ; THIS ARM WORTH ITS SIZE. A switch clause is a statement container the port
    ; had never entered — statements_of had three of its four arms until this slice
    ; (see ast.scaly), so even asking would have answered an empty list.
    ;
    ; ★ NoFallthroughCasesInSwitch is unset under this harness, so the fallthrough
    ; report is not reachable; and the flow node it reads does not exist here
    ; either, which is the ordinary shape of an option guard standing in front of a
    ; dimension.
    procedure check_switch_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        var first_default_clause: ref[AstNode]? null
        var has_duplicate_default_clause false
        let subject AstNode.expression_of(node)
        var expression_type: ref[Type]? null
        if subject <> null
            set expression_type: this.check_expression(subject as ref[AstNode])
        let clauses AstNode.case_clauses_of(AstNode.case_block_of(node))
        var i 0
        while i < AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        {
            let clause AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i)
            let ck AstNode.kind_of(clause)
            if ck = KindDefaultClause
            {
                if has_duplicate_default_clause = false
                {
                    if first_default_clause = null
                        set first_default_clause: clause
                    else
                    {
                        this.grammar_error_on_node(clause, DiagA_default_clause_cannot_appear_more_than_once_in_a_switch_statement)
                        set has_duplicate_default_clause: true
                    }
                }
            }
            if ck = KindCaseClause
            {
                let case_expression AstNode.expression_of(clause)
                if case_expression <> null
                {
                    let case_type this.check_expression(case_expression as ref[AstNode])
                    ; isTypeEqualityComparableTo / checkTypeComparableTo. BOTH types
                    ; are needed, so the row is reached only when the switch subject
                    ; and this clause's expression are both typed — `switch (null) {
                    ; case null: }` is the shape that gets there today.
                    if case_type <> null
                    {
                        if expression_type <> null
                            this.record_unported("is-type-equality-comparable-to", AstNode.kind_of(case_expression as ref[AstNode]))
                    }
                }
            }
            this.check_source_elements(AstNode.statements_of(clause))
            ; NoFallthroughCasesInSwitch — see the header.
            set i: i + 1
        }
        ; `if caseBlock.Locals() != nil { c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(…) }`
        ; — gated off, as in check_for_statement above.
    }

    ; ── slice 49: the four arms whose first grammar check is the modifier one ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THESE FIVE KINDS AND NOT THE FOUR BIGGEST. The histogram's head is
    ; VariableStatement 3 940 / ClassDeclaration 3 022 / FunctionDeclaration 2 255
    ; / ImportDeclaration 1 601 on stage 2, and only the first of those is here.
    ; The other three do NOT begin with checkGrammarModifiers: a class reaches it
    ; through checkClassLikeDeclaration, whose own first line is
    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration; a function reaches it through
    ; checkFunctionOrMethodDeclaration → checkSignatureDeclaration; and the
    ; import/export family reaches it through checkGrammarModuleElementContext,
    ; which may RETURN before it. Each of those is a second head with its own
    ; prefix, and porting one of them without its prefix would run this function
    ; where the reference does not.
    ;
    ; ★★ The import/export family had a second reason to wait, and it was the
    ; generator's: their prefix reports messages that live in checker.go, which
    ; tools/gendiagnostics.py deliberately did not scrape (some 1 000 messages,
    ; i.e. most of the table). The five kinds here report only through
    ; grammarchecks.go, which slice 48 added — so the boundary of SLICE 49 was the
    ; boundary of the message table it already had, a checkable line rather than a
    ; judgement. ★SLICE 50 PAID THAT BILL: checker.go joined the SOURCES on the
    ; condition the generator's own note set, the table went 337 -> 947 codes, and
    ; the family is ported below. This paragraph is kept in the past tense rather
    ; than deleted, because the argument it makes — let the generator's stated
    ; boundary decide the slice's — is the transferable half.

    ; checkVariableStatement, whole:
    ;
    ;   if !c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && !c.checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList(list) {
    ;       c.checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement(varStatement)
    ;   }
    ;   c.checkVariableDeclarationList(declarationList)
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 53 RUNS IT TO THE END, AND WHAT THAT BUYS IS NOT DIAGNOSTICS — IT
    ; IS THE WALK. The last line recurses into every declaration of the list, so
    ; the 276 stage-1 units this arm held move off `check-grammar-variable-
    ; declaration-list VariableStatement` and onto whatever the DECLARATIONS
    ; report, which is the histogram moving one level down exactly as slice 50's
    ; block arm and slice 52's parameter list moved it.
    ;
    ; ★ The `&&` is a SHORT-CIRCUIT and both terms are real now, so the old
    ; two-tag report is gone with them. What is left unported inside the chain is
    ; one term of checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList (the await-using tail) and
    ; nothing else; the block-scoped statement check below is complete.
    procedure check_variable_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let list AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if this.check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(list) = false
                this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_block_scoped_variable_statement(node)
        }
        this.check_variable_declaration_list(list)
    }

    ; checkVariableDeclarationList, whole:
    ;
    ;   blockScopeKind := ast.GetCombinedNodeFlags(node) & ast.NodeFlagsBlockScoped
    ;   if (blockScopeKind == Using || blockScopeKind == AwaitUsing) &&
    ;       c.languageVersion < LanguageFeatureMinimumTarget.UsingAndAwaitUsing {
    ;       c.checkExternalEmitHelpers(node, …AddDisposableResourceAndDisposeResources)
    ;   }
    ;   c.checkSourceElements(node.AsVariableDeclarationList().Declarations.Nodes)
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EMIT-HELPERS CALL IS REACHED AND IS A PROVABLE NO-OP, WHICH IS A
    ; FOURTH READING OF AN UNSET OPTION TO SET BESIDE §3.5cw's THREE. The gate is
    ; TAKEN for a `using` declaration — `c.languageVersion` defaults to
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 = 12 and the feature's minimum is
    ; ScriptTargetESNext = 99, so `12 < 99` is true, which is the OPPOSITE
    ; direction to the two `languageVersion` reads slice 52 measured. What kills it
    ; is the callee's own first line: `if !c.compilerOptions.ImportHelpers.IsTrue()
    ; { return }`, and `IsTrue()` is `== TSTrue`, so an unset option answers false
    ; and checkExternalEmitHelpers returns before it looks at anything. So the
    ; whole family — resolveHelpersModule, getExportsOfModule, the per-helper name
    ; resolution — is unreachable under this harness, and the step is written as a
    ; comment with its argument rather than as a report, because a report would
    ; claim the unit is unmeasured when the reference does nothing either.
    ;
    ; ★ `GetCombinedNodeFlags` is `Binder.combined_node_flags`; the reference's
    ; caller here uses the UNCACHED spelling while the block-scoped check below
    ; uses `getCombinedNodeFlagsCached`, which is a one-entry memo of the same
    ; function — so the two are one call here, as the two spellings are one answer
    ; there.
    procedure check_variable_declaration_list(this, node: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if node = null
            return
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.variable_declarations_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 54: the variable DECLARATION ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkVariableDeclaration, whole: two calls, and the tracer above them is
    ; nothing here (the reference builds a tracing span; `c.tracer` is nil under
    ; this harness and the whole `if` is a no-op).
    procedure check_variable_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_variable_declaration(node)
        this.check_variable_like_declaration(node)
    }

    ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration's PREFIX — the shared arm of a variable
    ; declaration, a parameter, a property and a binding element, of which only the
    ; first reaches it in this slice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THIS BUYS IS THE TYPE WALK, and it is the move §3.5cx predicted:
    ; `c.checkSourceElement(typeNode)` sends the histogram's biggest row one level
    ; down onto the TYPE kinds, exactly as the block arm and the parameter list did
    ; for statements. It also sends a destructuring declaration's ELEMENTS down, so
    ; `check BindingElement` becomes a row of its own.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT IS WHY THE NO-ARM FAMILY HAD TO ARRIVE WITH IT (see
    ; kind_has_no_check_arm). A keyword type is not a kind whose arm is unported:
    ; the reference's switch has NO case for it, so `checkSourceElement(number)`
    ; does nothing at all. Reporting `check NumberKeyword` would be §3.5cx's
    ; checkExternalEmitHelpers mistake with the sign flipped — a claim that the
    ; unit is unmeasured where the reference measures nothing either — and it would
    ; be the commonest row in the histogram.
    ;
    ; ★★ ONE OF THE REFERENCE'S BRANCHES IS STILL UNREACHABLE FOR ALL THREE KINDS
    ; THAT REACH THIS FUNCTION, and it is a comment rather than a report because
    ; the kind decides it: `IsComputedPropertyName(name)` cannot hold — a variable
    ; declaration's, a parameter's and a binding element's NAME is an identifier or
    ; a binding pattern, never a computed one. Its owner is the PropertyDeclaration
    ; arm — one of the five callers of this function, and the only one whose name
    ; CAN be computed (the fifth is checkCatchClause, whose declaration is an
    ; ordinary variable declaration). ★The OTHER branch this paragraph used to
    ; name — `IsBindingElement`, both the guard on the type walk and the block —
    ; went live with slice 56, and the line moves in that commit rather than being
    ; left to read as an open limit (§3.5cx's rule).
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 54's WORK LIST PREDICTED THREE AND checkParameter DELIVERED ONE,
    ; and the correction is left standing rather than quietly dropped. It named
    ; `IsBindingElement`, `IsPartOfParameterDeclaration` and the computed property
    ; name as *the three branches this slice could only comment on* that the
    ; parameter arm turns into live code; only the middle one is a question about
    ; the PARAMETER — the other two are questions about the BINDING ELEMENT, which
    ; is the arm the same paragraph called *the other half of the same function*.
    ; ★The transferable half: **a work-list entry that predicts what a slice
    ; unblocks is an estimate made from the caller's side, and it counts the
    ; branches the FUNCTION has rather than the branches the new KIND reaches.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND SLICE 56 FOUND THAT CORRECTION OUT BY ONE IN THE SAME DIRECTION. It
    ; reassigned BOTH remaining branches to *the binding element*, and only
    ; `IsBindingElement` was one: a binding element's NAME is an identifier or a
    ; binding pattern, so `IsComputedPropertyName(name)` is as unreachable for it
    ; as it was for the other two kinds, and its owner is the PropertyDeclaration
    ; arm. **A correction made from the caller's side is made from the caller's
    ; side too** — the only thing that settles which arm owns a branch is reading
    ; what the new kind's NAME can be, and both estimates skipped that step.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AMBIENT RETURN IS THE ONE PLACE THIS ARM COMPLETES. For a binding
    ; pattern in an ambient or type context the reference returns with nothing more
    ; to say, so a `declare namespace N { const { a } = x }` runs to the end of the
    ; arm — while the same declaration outside one needs the widened type and stops.
    procedure check_variable_like_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_decorators(node)
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if name = null
            return
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        ; ★★★ THE `!ast.IsBindingElement(node)` GUARD IS SLICE 56's, AND IT IS A
        ; CONDITION WITH NO OBSERVABLE EFFECT — which is exactly why it is
        ; transcribed. A BindingElement carries no type annotation: the accessor
        ; answers null for the kind, and check_source_element returns at its own
        ; first line on a null. So the guard cannot change an answer on any tree
        ; the parser builds, and dropping it would make the port depend on that
        ; accessor's arm list rather than on the reference's own test.
        let is_binding_element AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindBindingElement
        if is_binding_element = false
            this.check_source_element(type_node)
        ; ★★★ THE BINDING-ELEMENT BLOCK, SLICE 56. Its four steps are the whole of
        ; what a binding element adds to this shared function, and only the FIRST
        ; is fully portable — which is the ordinary shape of a checker arm here and
        ; not a shortfall of this one.
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE FIRST STEP IS A DEFERRAL AND A RETURN, and the return is the half
        ; that is observable today. `type F = ({a: string}) => void` — a renamed
        ; binding element in a signature that has no body — is confusing enough
        ; that the reference forbids it even with noUnusedLocals off, and it does
        ; so by APPENDING the node to renamedBindingElementsInTypes and returning.
        ; The report itself comes at the end of the file, from
        ; checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements, and needs the symbol; the RETURN is
        ; free, and it skips the element walk, TS2371 and getSymbolOfDeclaration
        ; for this node. ★So a port that dropped the append and kept the return
        ; would be right about the control flow and silent about the diagnostic,
        ; which is the one failure mode diagcheck cannot see (§3.5cz's f13).
        ;
        ; ★★ THE SECOND STEP IS checkExternalEmitHelpers AND IS A PROVABLE NO-OP,
        ; for the reason check_variable_declaration_list gives at its own line: the
        ; callee's first statement is `if !c.compilerOptions.ImportHelpers.IsTrue()
        ; { return }` and the option is unset under this harness. It is a comment
        ; with its argument rather than a report, because a report would claim the
        ; unit is unmeasured where the reference does nothing either. ★Its GATE is
        ; ported all the same in the third step's shape: an object pattern's rest
        ; element under a language version below ObjectSpreadRest is a real
        ; condition, and it is written down here rather than re-derived.
        ;
        ; ★★ THE COMPUTED PROPERTY NAME IS THE FIRST PLACE IN THIS FUNCTION WHERE
        ; ONE CAN OCCUR, and it is the PROPERTY name rather than the name: `{[k]: v}
        ; = o` is a binding element whose propertyName is computed, while its own
        ; name is the plain identifier `v`. That is the whole reason the paragraph
        ; above can still say the `IsComputedPropertyName(name)` branch is
        ; unreachable while this one reports.
        ;
        ; ★ THE FOURTH STEP IS THE TYPE DIMENSION and takes the rest of the block
        ; with it: getTypeForBindingElementParent is computed unconditionally, so
        ; the property-accessibility walk behind it is behind ONE report.
        if is_binding_element
        {
            let prop_name AstNode.property_name_of(node)
            var renamed_in_type false
            if prop_name <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
                {
                    if Binder.is_part_of_parameter_declaration(node)
                    {
                        if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(Checker.get_containing_function(node)))
                            set renamed_in_type: true
                    }
                }
            }
            if renamed_in_type
            {
                renamed_binding_elements_in_types.add(node)
                return
            }
            ; `IsObjectBindingPattern(node.Parent) && hasDotDotDotToken(node) &&
            ; c.languageVersion < LanguageFeatureMinimumTarget.ObjectSpreadRest`
            ; then checkExternalEmitHelpers(node, ExternalEmitHelpersRest) — the
            ; no-op above. The gate itself is true for `{...r} = o` under this
            ; harness (12 < 99, the direction slice 54 measured), so what makes the
            ; step silent is the callee and not the condition.
            if prop_name <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(prop_name) = KindComputedPropertyName
                    this.record_unported("check-computed-property-name", KindBindingElement)
            }
            this.record_unported("get-type-for-binding-element-parent", KindBindingElement)
        }
        ; ★★★ THE ELEMENT WALK AND THE BINDING-PATTERN VALIDATION ARE TWO
        ; SEPARATE `if ast.IsBindingPattern(name)` BLOCKS IN THE REFERENCE, and
        ; slice 54 merged them because the statement BETWEEN them was dead. Slice
        ; 55 makes that statement live, so the merge had to come apart: for a
        ; binding-pattern parameter with an initializer in a body-less function the
        ; reference walks the elements, then reports TS2371 and returns — and the
        ; merged form took the pattern branch and never reached the report. ★The
        ; general shape is worth more than the case: **a merge of two blocks with
        ; the same condition is sound only while everything between them is dead,
        ; so it expires with the slice that ports that code** — and nothing marks
        ; it, because the merged form is a correct transcription right up to that
        ; day.
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
            this.check_source_elements(AstNode.elements_of(name))
        ; ★★★ THE FIRST OF SLICE 54's THREE COMMENTED-OUT BRANCHES TO GO LIVE, and
        ; it is the only one of the three that this slice reaches. A parameter's
        ; name cannot be a computed property name and a parameter is not a binding
        ; element, so those two stay comments; `IsPartOfParameterDeclaration` is
        ; true for every parameter and for every binding element inside one, which
        ; is exactly the population this arm added.
        ;
        ; ★★ `ast.NodeIsMissing(fn.Body())` IS NOT `fn.Body() == nil`, and the
        ; difference is a parse error: a missing node is a nil body OR a body whose
        ; pos equals its end, so `function f(a = 1)` with no braces at all reports
        ; here too. The optional-binding-pattern test in check_parameter below asks
        ; the OTHER question (`fn.Body() != nil`) four lines later in the reference,
        ; and the two are transcribed as they stand rather than unified.
        ;
        ; ★ `fn` is read null-safely, where the reference dereferences it. A
        ; parameter's parent IS its function-like, so FindAncestor cannot come back
        ; empty for any tree the parser builds — the guard costs nothing and the
        ; null direction answers "missing", which is the safe one.
        if initializer <> null
        {
            if Binder.is_part_of_parameter_declaration(node)
            {
                if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(Checker.get_containing_function(node)))
                {
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_initializer_is_only_allowed_in_a_function_or_constructor_implementation)
                    return
                }
            }
        }
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
        {
            if this.is_in_ambient_or_type_node(node)
                return
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            var grandparent: ref[AstNode]? null
            if parent <> null
                set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
            var need_check_initializer false
            if initializer <> null
            {
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(grandparent) <> KindForInStatement
                    set need_check_initializer: true
            }
            ; !core.Some(name.Elements(), n => n.Name() != nil) — an OmittedExpression
            ; has no name, so a pattern of nothing but holes needs the widened type.
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
            var need_check_widened_type true
            var i 0
            let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
            while i < n
            {
                if AstNode.name_of(AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)) <> null
                {
                    set need_check_widened_type: false
                    break
                }
                set i: i + 1
            }
            if need_check_initializer or need_check_widened_type
                this.record_unported("get-widened-type-for-variable-like-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return
        }

        ; ★★★ SLICE 65: THE SYMBOL ARRIVES AND THE ARM MOVES TWO LINES. What the tag
        ; `get-symbol-of-declaration` named for eleven slices was the first unported
        ; CALL, and behind it stand exactly two statements before the type: the
        ; CommonJS `const x = require(...)` alias branch, which lands in the
        ; `check-alias-symbol` row the work list already has, and the bigint
        ; property-name report. Then `convertAutoToAny(getTypeOfSymbol(symbol))`,
        ; which is the type of a symbol and is where 5 500 units now stop.
        ;
        ; ★★ THE ALIAS BRANCH RETURNS, and that is the difference between a hole and
        ; a stop: a `const x = require("m")` is finished by checkAliasSymbol as far
        ; as this function is concerned, so it does not reach the type at all. Its
        ; row is therefore `check-alias-symbol` rather than `get-type-of-symbol`, and
        ; the two are different work.
        let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
        if symbol = null
            return
        if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsAlias) <> 0
        {
            if b.is_variable_declaration_initialized_to_require(node)
            {
                this.record_unported("check-alias-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                return
            }
        }
        ; ★ TS1539 is reported on the NAME and does not return — the reference
        ; carries on to the type — so it is a report inside the arm rather than a
        ; branch of it.
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindBigIntLiteral
            this.error_on_node(name, DiagA_bigint_literal_cannot_be_used_as_a_property_name)
        ; ★★★ SLICE 67 DOES NOT MOVE THIS ARM, IT RENAMES ITS STOP — and the
        ; rename is the measurement. `convertAutoToAny(getTypeOfSymbol(symbol))`
        ; now runs a real dispatch, and a variable, parameter or property lands in
        ; getTypeOfVariableOrParameterOrProperty, which is the row slice 68 has to
        ; decide about. Until this slice the tag said `get-type-of-symbol` for all
        ; three of this function's populations AND for the class, so the work list
        ; showed one 7 695-unit row where the reference asks two questions.
        let before_type this.is_unported()
        let symbol_type this.get_type_of_symbol(symbol as ref[Symbol])
        if this.is_unported() <> before_type
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 70 TAKES THE WHOLE TAIL AND STOPS AT FOUR NAMED PLACES INSIDE
        ; IT, which is the difference between porting a function and porting the
        ; call in front of it. What used to be one 146-unit row reading
        ; `check-expression <declaration kind>` — a row naming neither the
        ; expression nor the work — is now four: check-type-assignable-to (the
        ; initializer's own check), are-declaration-flags-identical, get-widened-
        ; type-for-variable-like-declaration (the secondary declaration) and
        ; check-var-declared-names-not-shadowed.
        ;
        ; ★★ THE PRIMARY/SECONDARY FORK IS PORTED because it decides WHICH
        ; unported thing comes first: a secondary declaration asks
        ; getWidenedTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration and isTypeIdenticalTo before it
        ; ever looks at an initializer, so folding the two branches would put a
        ; wrong row on the work list for every `var x: T` redeclaration.
        if symbol_type = null
            return

        ; ★★★ THE TAIL IS TRANSCRIBED WITH ITS REPORTS INLINE AND NO EARLY EXIT,
        ; because the reference has none: the primary/secondary fork and the
        ; trailing three-check block are sequential, so a stop in the fork must not
        ; take the block with it. That is worth more than a tidier shape — the
        ; block ends in checkCollisionsForDeclarationName, which slice 59 ported,
        ; so a `var x = 1` now collects its collision diagnostics even though its
        ; initializer's assignability check is a dimension away. ★record_unported
        ; does not abort; check_source_elements has walked past its own reports
        ; since slice 49, and this is the same discipline one level down.
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        let value_declaration Symbol.value_declaration_of(symbol as ref[Symbol])
        var is_primary_declaration false
        if value_declaration <> null
        {
            if value_declaration = node
                set is_primary_declaration: true
        }
        if is_primary_declaration
        {
            ; "Node is the primary declaration of the symbol, just validate the
            ; initializer" — and the for-in guard is the reference's own comment:
            ; *don't validate for-in initializer as it is already an error*. The
            ; statement is two levels up (the declaration's LIST's statement).
            if initializer <> null
            {
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))) <> KindForInStatement
                {
                    let initializer_type this.check_expression_cached(initializer as ref[AstNode])
                    if initializer_type <> null
                    {
                        ; checkTypeAssignableToAndOptionallyElaborate, i.e. the
                        ; ASSIGNABILITY RELATION — 5 006 lines of relater.go, a
                        ; dimension and not a step. convertAutoToAny is still not
                        ; written for the same reason: its value is read only here.
                        ; ★The await-using and using arms behind it need
                        ; getGlobalAsyncDisposableType and getUnionType, so they are
                        ; behind this row rather than beside it.
                        this.record_unported("check-type-assignable-to", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                    }
                }
            }
            ; `if len(symbol.Declarations) > 1 { if core.Some(…!areDeclarationFlags
            ; Identical…) { TS2687 } }` — areDeclarationFlagsIdentical is a
            ; modifier comparison over two declarations and is the cheapest thing
            ; left in this function; it is not ported because nothing else in this
            ; slice needs it and a report here names it exactly.
            if Symbol.declaration_count(symbol as ref[Symbol]) > 1
                this.record_unported("are-declaration-flags-identical", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        }
        else
        {
            ; The secondary declaration's own three steps, of which the FIRST is
            ; already a stop: getWidenedTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration is what
            ; slice 69 ported up to its RequiresWidening gate, and isTypeIdenticalTo
            ; is the relation again.
            this.record_unported("get-widened-type-for-variable-like-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        }
        ; The trailing block, which runs for every declaration that is not a
        ; property: `checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations` (ported, slice 31),
        ; `checkVarDeclaredNamesNotShadowed` for a variable or binding element
        ; (NOT ported — it resolves the name in the enclosing block scope) and
        ; `checkCollisionsForDeclarationName` (ported, slice 59).
        let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return
        if nk = KindPropertySignature
            return
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)
        if nk = KindVariableDeclaration
            this.record_unported("check-var-declared-names-not-shadowed", nk)
        if nk = KindBindingElement
            this.record_unported("check-var-declared-names-not-shadowed", nk)
        this.check_collisions_for_declaration_name(node, AstNode.name_of(node))
    }

    ; isInAmbientOrTypeNode, whole. FindAncestor with a three-kind predicate is
    ; written out as the walk it is (§3.9d) — and it starts AT the node, as
    ; FindAncestor does, which matters for a binding pattern that IS inside a type
    ; literal's member.
    function is_in_ambient_or_type_node(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return true
        var p: ref[AstNode]? node
        while p <> null
        {
            let k AstNode.kind_of(p)
            if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindTypeLiteral
                return true
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        false
    }

    ; ── slice 55: the PARAMETER ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkParameter, whole except its last term. Its first two lines are the two
    ; functions this port already had — checkGrammarModifiers (slice 49, whose
    ; parameter-property arm has been live and unreached since that slice) and
    ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration (slice 54) — and everything after them is a run
    ; of c.errors over kind tests, which is why the arm is affordable at all.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST LINE BUYS THREE DIAGNOSTICS OUT OF A FUNCTION THIS SLICE DID
    ; NOT TOUCH, and the number is measured: checkGrammarModifiers has carried its
    ; `nk = KindParameter` arm since slice 49 and nothing had ever called it on a
    ; parameter, because nothing walked one. Clipping this arm and re-running the
    ; whole of stage 2 through both binaries puts the difference at **+3 TS1090**
    ; on top of the twenty-three this function's own reports answer for. ★A ported
    ; arm no caller reaches is indistinguishable from an unported one until a
    ; caller arrives — §3.5ce's *an arm that is ported is not thereby measured*,
    ; seen from the paying end.
    ;
    ; ★★★ EIGHT REPORTS AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS A GRAMMAR ERROR, which is the
    ; distinction §3.5cw's two reporters exist for and the first arm in this file
    ; where it decides anything at scale. checkParameter calls `c.error`, not
    ; `c.grammarErrorOnNode` — so a file that FAILED TO PARSE still gets every one
    ; of these, where every report of the five grammar functions in front of them
    ; is suppressed. Routing one of them through grammar_error_on_node would be a
    ; missing diagnostic on exactly the broken files the corpus has most of, and it
    ; would look like a port that is merely conservative.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LAST TERM IS THE ONE HOLE AND IT IS THE TYPE DIMENSION, asked where
    ; the reference asks it: `hasDotDotDotToken(node) && !IsBindingPattern(name) &&
    ; !c.isTypeAssignableTo(c.getReducedType(c.getTypeOfSymbol(node.Symbol())),
    ; c.anyReadonlyArrayType)`. The two kind tests in front of it are FREE and are
    ; ported, so the report fires only for a rest parameter whose name is not a
    ; pattern — which is what makes the row a measurement of the term rather than
    ; of the statement. ★The reference's own comment says why the second test is
    ; there: a rest parameter with a binding pattern already reported from
    ; checkGrammarParameterList, so this would be a second line on one defect.
    ;
    ; ★★ `paramName` IS COMPUTED ONCE AND ASKED THREE TIMES, and it is empty unless
    ; the name is an IDENTIFIER — so a destructuring parameter answers "" and
    ; cannot be `this`, `new` or `constructor`. Transcribed as a bool plus three
    ; text comparisons rather than as a String, for §3.9a's reason: this port
    ; compares identifier text by bytes and has no reason to materialise one.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `this`/`new` QUARTET IS FOUR INDEPENDENT `if`s, NOT A CHAIN, and a
    ; parameter can collect two of them: `class C { constructor(this: C) {} }`
    ; reports TS2681 (a constructor cannot have a `this` parameter) and, when it is
    ; not first, TS2680 as well. Reading them as an else-chain would drop the
    ; second, which is the same shape as checkGrammarParameterList's discarded
    ; trailing-comma result one slice back.
    ;
    ; ★ `slices.Index(fn.Parameters(), node) != 0` is a POINTER search, and -1
    ; satisfies `!= 0` as loudly as 1 does. parameter_index_of answers -1 for a
    ; parameter it cannot find in its own function's list, which is unreachable for
    ; a parsed tree and is the reference's answer for it either way.
    procedure check_parameter(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        this.check_variable_like_declaration(node)
        let fn Checker.get_containing_function(node)
        let fk Checker.kind_or_unknown(fn)
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        var named_identifier false
        if name <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
                set named_identifier: true
        }
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsParameterPropertyModifier)
        {
            if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
            ; !(IsConstructorDeclaration(fn) && NodeIsPresent(fn.Body())) — an
            ; overload signature has no body, so a parameter property on one is a
            ; report even though the constructor it belongs to has an
            ; implementation somewhere below it.
            var in_constructor_implementation false
            if fk = KindConstructor
            {
                if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(fn)) = false
                    set in_constructor_implementation: true
            }
            if in_constructor_implementation = false
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_property_is_only_allowed_in_a_constructor_implementation)
            ; The span is the NAME here and the node everywhere else in this
            ; function, which is the reference's choice and not a simplification
            ; worth making: `constructor` as a parameter property names a member
            ; that would collide with the class's own constructor.
            if fk = KindConstructor
            {
                if named_identifier
                {
                    if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "constructor", 11)
                        this.error_on_node(name, DiagX_constructor_cannot_be_used_as_a_parameter_property_name)
                }
            }
        }
        ; `node.Initializer() == nil && isOptionalDeclaration(node) &&
        ; IsBindingPattern(node.Name()) && fn.Body() != nil` — a `?` on a
        ; destructuring parameter of a function that HAS a body. The last test is
        ; the plain null one, not NodeIsMissing: four lines above it in the
        ; reference the same question is asked the other way, and both are
        ; transcribed as they stand.
        if AstNode.initializer_of(node) = null
        {
            if Checker.is_optional_declaration(node)
            {
                if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
                {
                    if AstNode.body_of(fn) <> null
                        this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_binding_pattern_parameter_cannot_be_optional_in_an_implementation_signature)
                }
            }
        }
        var this_or_new false
        if named_identifier
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "this", 4)
                set this_or_new: true
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "new", 3)
                set this_or_new: true
        }
        if this_or_new
        {
            if Checker.parameter_index_of(fn, node) <> 0
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_0_parameter_must_be_the_first_parameter)
            var construct_like false
            if fk = KindConstructor
                set construct_like: true
            if fk = KindConstructSignature
                set construct_like: true
            if fk = KindConstructorType
                set construct_like: true
            if construct_like
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_constructor_cannot_have_a_this_parameter)
            if fk = KindArrowFunction
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagAn_arrow_function_cannot_have_a_this_parameter)
            if Checker.is_accessor_kind(fk)
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagX_get_and_set_accessors_cannot_declare_this_parameters)
        }
        ; isTypeAssignableTo(getReducedType(getTypeOfSymbol(node.Symbol())),
        ; anyReadonlyArrayType) — and the symbol is the NODE's own rather than
        ; getSymbolOfDeclaration's, which is the reference's spelling here.
        if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node) <> null
        {
            if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name) = false
            {
                let psym AstNode.symbol_of(node)
                if psym <> null
                {
                    let before_type this.is_unported()
                    let ptype this.get_type_of_symbol(psym as ref[Symbol])
                    if this.is_unported() = before_type
                        this.record_unported("is-type-assignable-to", KindParameter)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    ; ast.GetContainingFunction — `FindAncestor(node.Parent, IsFunctionLike)`,
    ; written out as the walk it is (§3.9d), starting at the PARENT rather than at
    ; the node. For a parameter that is one step; for a binding element inside a
    ; parameter's pattern it is three, which is the population the caller above
    ; shares with checkVariableLikeDeclaration's TS2371.
    function get_containing_function(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if node = null
            return null
        var p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        while p <> null
        {
            if AstNode.is_function_like(p)
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; isOptionalDeclaration, which is ast.HasQuestionToken, which is
    ; `IsQuestionToken(node.QuestionToken())`. The KIND test is transcribed rather
    ; than dropped even though a Parameter's slot can hold nothing else: the
    ; accessor serves four kinds whose slot is a field and everything else through
    ; the POSTFIX slot, and that one also holds a `!` (ast.scaly's question_token_of
    ; says so at the line). ★check_grammar_parameter_list asks the slot directly one
    ; slice back, which is the reference's spelling there — the two are the same
    ; answer for a parameter and different questions in general.
    function is_optional_declaration(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let q AstNode.question_token_of(node)
        if q = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(q) = KindQuestionToken
    }

    ; slices.Index(fn.Parameters(), node), by IDENTITY. -1 when the list has no
    ; such element, which is what the reference's `!= 0` reads as a report.
    function parameter_index_of(fn: ref[AstNode], node: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let parameters AstNode.parameters_of(fn)
        if parameters = null
            return 0 - 1
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(parameters)
        while i < n
        {
            if AstNode.child_in_list(parameters, i) = node
                return i
            set i: i + 1
        }
        0 - 1
    }

    ; ── slice 56: the BINDING ELEMENT ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkBindingElement, whole — two lines, and it is the other half of the
    ; function slice 55 ported the first half of. What makes it a slice of its own
    ; rather than a line is what the two lines REACH: checkGrammarBindingElement is
    ; four reports this file has never had a caller for, and
    ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration's binding-element block is the branch slices 54
    ; and 55 could only comment on.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE GRAMMAR RESULT IS DISCARDED, and upstream discards it too — the bool
    ; exists for the `return` spellings inside the function and nothing reads it,
    ; which is the same shape checkVariableDeclaration's first line has one slice
    ; back. It is transcribed as a `function returns bool` all the same, because the
    ; three internal returns ARE the control flow.
    procedure check_binding_element(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_binding_element(node)
        this.check_variable_like_declaration(node)
    }

    ; checkTypeNameIsReserved, whole: the eleven predefined type keywords may not
    ; be the name of a user-defined type. It is one switch on the name's TEXT, and
    ; the message is the CALLER's — six call sites in the reference pass six
    ; different ones (interface, type alias, type parameter, import, class, enum),
    ; which is why the code is a parameter here rather than a constant.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ELEVEN ARE A SPELLING LIST, NOT A KIND TEST, and that is the whole
    ; reason this reads the identifier's text instead of asking the scanner what
    ; token it was. Every one of `any unknown never number bigint boolean string
    ; symbol void object undefined` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword — `interface any {}`
    ; parses with an Identifier named `any`, not with a keyword token — so a test on
    ; the kind would answer *identifier* for all eleven and report nothing.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE NAME MAY BE ABSENT AND THE REFERENCE DEREFERENCES IT. Upstream writes
    ; `name.Text()` with no null test, on the grounds that every one of its six call
    ; sites passes a declaration name that the parser guarantees — an interface, a
    ; type alias and a type parameter all take one, and a missing one is a parse
    ; error that produces a MISSING identifier node rather than nil. This port
    ; keeps the guard anyway because AstNode.identifier_text_of dereferences and
    ; identifier_text_length_of answers 0 for a missing node, which compares equal
    ; to no spelling in the list — so the guard changes no answer and removes a way
    ; for a later caller to crash. ★It is NOT a place a report is being skipped:
    ; a missing name has no text to be one of the eleven.
    ;
    ; ★ The message ARGUMENT (`name.Text()` again, filling the `{0}`) is not
    ; computed, for check_grammar_modifiers' reason: the dump is `C pos end code`
    ; and a message's code does not depend on its arguments.
    procedure check_type_name_is_reserved(this, name: ref[AstNode]?, code: int)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        var reserved false
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "any", 3)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "unknown", 7)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "never", 5)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "number", 6)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "bigint", 6)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "boolean", 7)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "string", 6)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "symbol", 6)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "void", 4)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "object", 6)
            set reserved: true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "undefined", 9)
            set reserved: true
        if reserved
            this.error_on_node(name, code)
    }

    ; checkInterfaceDeclaration's first five lines, which is everything up to
    ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARM STOPS AT A FUNCTION THE TYPE-ALIAS ARM ALSO STOPS AT, and that
    ; is why the two land in one slice. checkTypeAliasDeclaration's own prefix is
    ; the same three steps in a different ORDER — modifiers, reserved name,
    ; container, checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations — so porting the reserved-name
    ; check for the interface and leaving the alias reporting one call in FRONT of
    ; it would have been a boundary drawn by which arm was being edited.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE containerAllowsBlockScopedVariable LINE WAS DATED HERE BY SLICE 53
    ; AND THE DATING PAID FOR ITSELF. Its note said the line was one statement
    ; away, that the tag it stood behind was reached only on the MODIFIER-ERROR
    ; path (6 units of 17 855), and that porting it alone would still leave the arm
    ; behind checkGrammarInterfaceDeclaration — all three correct, and this slice
    ; had to re-derive none of it. ★What the note could not say, because the stub's
    ; fork made it unaskable, is the line's OWN population: the `if
    ; !c.checkGrammarModifiers(node)` guards the grammar call and nothing else, so
    ; the container test is the arm's second statement, unconditional, and runs on
    ; every interface. The 6 was the tag's population, not the line's — which is
    ; what "this tag's whole stage-2 row" already said, read at the right level.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CONTAINER REPORT IS UNREACHABLE ON THIS CORPUS AND IS PORTED ANYWAY,
    ; with the argument container_allows_block_scoped_variable's own header
    ; already carries: every parent an interface can have in reach (SourceFile,
    ; ModuleBlock, Block) falls through to the permissive answer, and the refusing
    ; kinds are the seven loop/if/with statements, whose arms are unported — so
    ; nothing can put an interface under one. It is the same shape slice 53's f10
    ; measured from the other side, and the control battery holds it to a number
    ; rather than to an argument.
    procedure check_interface_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
            this.check_grammar_interface_declaration(node)
        if this.container_allows_block_scoped_variable(AstNode.parent_node_of(node)) = false
            this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_can_only_be_declared_inside_a_block)
        this.check_type_parameters(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
        this.check_type_name_is_reserved(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagInterface_name_cannot_be_0)
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)
        ; ★★★ SLICE 66 MOVES THE STOP PAST THE DECLARED TYPE, and what stops the
        ; arm now is checkInheritedPropertiesAreIdentical — whose first line is
        ; getBaseTypes. The rest of the arm
        ; (checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations, the extends heritage walk, the
        ; members walk, the duplicate index signatures) sits behind that, so it is
        ; still one row; the row's NAME is what moved.
        let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
        if symbol = null
            return
        let sym symbol as ref[Symbol]
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        this.check_type_parameter_lists_identical(sym)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        let links this.declared_type_link_of(sym)
        if links.interface_checked = false
        {
            set links.interface_checked: true
            let t this.get_declared_type_of_symbol(sym)
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return
            if t = null
                return
            let type_with_this this.get_type_with_this_argument(t as ref[Type], null)
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return
            ; ★★★ SLICE 67 MOVES THE INTERFACE PAST ITS BASE TYPES, and for every
            ; interface on this corpus that list is EMPTY — all 43 units reaching
            ; here at stage 1 carry no `extends` clause on any declaration. So
            ; checkInheritedPropertiesAreIdentical answers TRUE on its second
            ; line, the assignability loop below runs zero times, and what stops
            ; the arm is checkIndexConstraints — i.e. resolveDeclaredMembers,
            ; which is the members dimension rather than the base one.
            let identical this.check_inherited_properties_are_identical(t as ref[Type], AstNode.name_of(node))
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return
            if identical
            {
                let bases this.get_base_types(t as ref[Type])
                if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                    return
                var bn 0
                if bases <> null
                    set bn: (bases as ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]).get_length() as int
                var bi 0
                while bi < bn
                {
                    ; checkTypeAssignableTo(typeWithThis,
                    ; getTypeWithThisArgument(baseType, thisType), name, TS2430).
                    this.record_unported("check-type-assignable-to", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
                    return
                }
                this.record_unported("check-index-constraints", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
                return
            }
            ; checkInheritedPropertiesAreIdentical answering FALSE means it
            ; reported, so this line is unreachable — it is written because the
            ; reference's `if` has no else and the arm continues below it, and the
            ; continuation (checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations, the heritage
            ; walk, the members walk) is the same row the branch above names.
            this.record_unported("check-index-constraints", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
        }
    }

    ; checkTypeParameterListsIdentical. The `len(declarations) == 1` guard is the
    ; whole function for a class or interface declared once, which is the ordinary
    ; case; behind it stand getClassOrInterfaceDeclarationsOfSymbol,
    ; areTypeParametersIdentical and symbolToString.
    ;
    ; ★ The links bit is NOT set before the report: the reference sets
    ; typeParametersChecked as the first statement inside the block, and a port that
    ; set it and then reported would answer differently on a second ask — which is
    ; the shape §3.5ae warns about, a guard reading a result that has stopped
    ; failing.
    procedure check_type_parameter_lists_identical(this, symbol: ref[Symbol])
    {
        if Symbol.declaration_count(symbol) = 1
            return
        this.record_unported("check-type-parameter-lists-identical", 0)
    }

    ; checkTypeAliasDeclaration's first four lines. The arm serves both
    ; TypeAliasDeclaration and JSTypeAliasDeclaration, as the reference's switch
    ; does — one `case` with two kinds.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS ORDER IS NOT THE INTERFACE'S ORDER, AND THE FIRST READING OF WHAT
    ; THAT COSTS WAS WRONG. The alias asks the reserved name BEFORE the container
    ; test; the interface asks it AFTER checkTypeParameters, which is a hole here —
    ; so `interface never<T> {}` was predicted to report nothing while `type
    ; never<T> = T` reported TS2457. Both report. record_unported MARKS the unit
    ; and does not RETURN, so the C section runs on past a hole, which is this
    ; file's convention since slice 52 and is sound because diagcheck's relation is
    ; a subsequence. ★★What the order really moves is the TAG: the generic
    ; interface carries `check-type-parameter` and the generic alias carried
    ; `check-exports-on-merged-declarations`. The two fixtures named after that pair
    ; are the only witness, because no yardstick compares a tag. ★★★SLICE 65 MOVED
    ; THE SECOND HALF OF THAT SENTENCE TO NOTHING: this arm has no stop any more, so
    ; the generic alias carries whatever its own type node reports and a plain one
    ; carries no tag at all. The asymmetry it describes is still real and still what
    ; decides which report a `never` name gets; only the alias side's tag is gone.
    ;
    ; ★ checkTypeParameters was BELOW the stop (upstream computes typeNode and
    ; typeParameters after checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations), which is the other half
    ; of the same asymmetry and the reason the alias arm did not call it. Slice 65
    ; reaches it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 65: THIS IS THE WHOLE ARM, AND IT IS THE FIRST DECLARATION ARM OF
    ; THIS FILE THAT NEEDS NO TYPE. Everything below the head is syntax: the type
    ; parameters, the `intrinsic` keyword's two shapes, the type node's own walk.
    ; Nothing here asks for a symbol's type, so nothing stops — and what that costs
    ; is stated rather than celebrated, because a completed arm is only as complete
    ; as the walk below it (`checkSourceElement(typeNode)` reports at ITS own sites,
    ; which is where a `type X = SomeReference` still stops).
    ;
    ; ★★ registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck IS DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED, at this site
    ; and at every other one this slice reaches, and the argument is §3.5be's with a
    ; measurement rather than a guess: the list's only reader is
    ; checkUnusedIdentifiers, gated in checkSourceFileWorker by `checkUnused :=
    ; NoUnusedLocals.IsTrue() || NoUnusedParameters.IsTrue() || collection ==
    ; &c.suggestionDiagnostics`. Both options are unset under this harness and the
    ; oracle asks GetDiagnostics, whose collection is `c.diagnostics` — so the gate
    ; is false, the reader never runs, and porting the writer would be a list nobody
    ; reads. ★And even with the gate open every report goes through reportUnused,
    ; which sends a non-error to the SUGGESTION list; only NoUnusedLocals set to
    ; TRUE makes one an error. Two independent reasons, both of them the option.
    procedure check_type_alias_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        this.check_type_name_is_reserved(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagType_alias_name_cannot_be_0)
        if this.container_allows_block_scoped_variable(AstNode.parent_node_of(node)) = false
            this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_can_only_be_declared_inside_a_block)
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)

        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        let type_parameters AstNode.type_parameters_of(node)
        this.check_type_parameters(type_parameters)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(type_node) = KindIntrinsicKeyword
        {
            if this.type_alias_intrinsic_is_legal(node, type_parameters) = false
                this.error_on_node(type_node, DiagThe_intrinsic_keyword_can_only_be_used_to_declare_compiler_provided_intrinsic_types)
            return
        }
        this.check_source_element(type_node)
    }

    ; The negated condition of checkTypeAliasDeclaration's `intrinsic` report, as
    ; its own function because the reference writes it as one `!( … || … )` over two
    ; lines and the report reads backwards at the call site otherwise.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO LEGAL SHAPES AND THEY DO NOT OVERLAP: no type parameters and the name
    ; `BuiltinIteratorReturn`, or exactly ONE type parameter and a name in
    ; intrinsicTypeKinds. So `type Uppercase = intrinsic` is an error and so is
    ; `type BuiltinIteratorReturn<T> = intrinsic` — the arity is part of the name's
    ; licence, not a separate check.
    ;
    ; ★★ intrinsicTypeKinds IS FIVE NAMES AND IS PORTED AS THE FIVE, not as a table:
    ; its VALUE (which intrinsic-type kind) is read at three other sites, all of them
    ; inside getStringMappingType and its neighbours, and this caller reads only
    ; `!= IntrinsicTypeKindUnknown` — i.e. membership. A table with no reader for its
    ; values would be §3.5be again. The slice that ports the string-mapping types
    ; brings the values with it.
    function type_alias_intrinsic_is_legal(this, node: ref[AstNode], type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns bool
    {
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        let arity AstNode.list_count(type_parameters)
        if arity = 0
            return Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "BuiltinIteratorReturn", 21)
        if arity <> 1
            return false
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "Uppercase", 9)
            return true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "Lowercase", 9)
            return true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "Capitalize", 10)
            return true
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "Uncapitalize", 12)
            return true
        Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "NoInfer", 7)
    }

    ; checkEnumDeclaration's first line. Unlike the two above, the result is
    ; DISCARDED here — the reference writes `c.checkGrammarModifiers(node)` on its
    ; own line — so there is one continuation and one tag.
    ;
    ; ★ SLICE 59: the collision head is ported, so the arm walks on to
    ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations — and `enum any {}` reports TS2431 on the way,
    ; the enum half of check_type_name_is_reserved's two callers in that head.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 65 GIVES IT THE MEMBERS WALK, AND THE WALK IS WHY THE ENUM MEMBER
    ; ARM IS IN THIS SLICE. `checkSourceElements(node.Members())` is the line right
    ; after the head, so the moment the head lands every enum's members are handed
    ; to checkSourceElement — and with no EnumMember arm the whole family would have
    ; taken the bare `check` tag, regrowing the row slice 64 emptied and naming the
    ; container's walk as the blocker of the member. checkEnumMember is six lines.
    ; ★It is the §3.5cd rule from the other direction: a container arm's walk is
    ; most of what the arm delivers, so the walk decides which OTHER arm belongs in
    ; the slice.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONCE-BIT BLOCK BELOW computeEnumMemberValues IS THE SECOND OBSTACLE AND
    ; IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN TAGGED: `declaredTypeLinks.Get(enumSymbol).enumChecked`
    ; gates the two const-consistency reports (TS2473, TS2432), and it is a
    ; PER-SYMBOL side table this port has none of — the merged-declaration head one
    ; screen up needs no such table because its once-guard is a node identity test
    ; (GetDeclarationOfKind), which is the difference between "run once per symbol"
    ; and "run on the first declaration". The tag stays on the value computation,
    ; because that is what stops first.
    procedure check_enum_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        this.check_collisions_for_declaration_name(node, AstNode.name_of(node))
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.member_list_of(node))
        if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
        }
        this.record_unported("compute-enum-member-values", KindEnumDeclaration)
    }

    ; checkEnumMember, whole.
    ;
    ; ★ The private-identifier report is on the NODE and not on the name, which is
    ; the reference's span and reads as a mistake at the call site — `c.error(node,
    ; …)` under `if ast.IsPrivateIdentifier(node.Name())`. Left as it is: the span is
    ; the specification.
    ;
    ; ★★ AN ENUM MEMBER WITH NO INITIALIZER COMPLETES, and that is the only shape of
    ; this arm that does. `enum E { A, B }` is finished here; `enum E { A = 1 }`
    ; stops at check-expression, and the auto-numbering that would give A its value
    ; is not this function's business at all — computeEnumMemberValues does it from
    ; the declaration arm.
    procedure check_enum_member(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(node)) = KindPrivateIdentifier
            this.error_on_node(node, DiagAn_enum_member_cannot_be_named_with_a_private_identifier)
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if initializer <> null
            this.check_expression(initializer as ref[AstNode])
    }

    ; ── slice 50: the import/export/module family, and the block it needed ───
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS ONE HEAD AND THE ARMS IT PREFIXES, and the line it stops at
    ; is a FILE rather than a judgement — the same shape slice 49's boundary had,
    ; where the message table decided. Everything these arms call in
    ; grammarchecks.go is ported (the head, checkGrammarModifiers, the export
    ; declaration's type-only check); the first call into a CHECK of checker.go is
    ; where each arm reports. The two exceptions are named because they look like
    ; the line being crossed and are not: `c.error` is a REPORTER, the semantic
    ; twin of grammar_error_on_node, and `shouldCheckErasableSyntax` is a
    ; one-line option read. Neither is a check.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE FIRST THING THIS SLICE HAD TO PAY IS THE MESSAGE TABLE. Slice 49
    ; recorded that this family *is the only one that also needs
    ; gendiagnostics.py's SOURCES to grow*: every one of the six arms names its
    ; illegal-context message in checker.go, which the generator deliberately did
    ; not scrape. It scrapes it now — 337 codes to 947 — on the condition the old
    ; note itself set (*it joins when an arm of checker.go reports*), and widening
    ; it found a defect in the scrape that only that file could show. The
    ; generator's header has it.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE FAMILY CANNOT DO YET, said plainly: the illegal-context report is
    ; UNREACHABLE. Its condition is a parent that is none of SourceFile,
    ; ModuleBlock or ModuleDeclaration — a `{ import … }` inside a plain block, a
    ; `export = x` inside an `if` body — and while checkBlock is ported for a
    ; block that is a STATEMENT, no arm that CONTAINS a block is (checkIfStatement,
    ; checkFunctionDeclaration, …). So the head runs on every unit of the family
    ; and answers false on every one of them. That is worth a fixture the moment a
    ; containing arm lands, and it is why the corpus is the evidence for the claim
    ; rather than a reading of it.

    ; checkBlock, whole. Two of its three steps are no-ops here for reasons the
    ; file already carries: `flowAnalysisDisabled` is saved and restored around a
    ; call, and there is no flow graph to disable (see the header); and
    ; registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck is observable only through
    ; checkUnusedIdentifiers, which checkUnused gates off under this harness.
    ;
    ; ★ The grammar check is asked of a KindBlock and NOT of a KindModuleBlock —
    ; that is the reference's own `if node.Kind == ast.KindBlock`, and it is the
    ; whole difference between the two kinds here. A namespace body is not a
    ; statement, so *statements are not allowed in ambient contexts* is not a thing
    ; to say about it.
    procedure check_block(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindBlock
            this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.statements_of(node))
    }

    ; checkModuleDeclaration's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS FIRST STEP IS THE RECURSION, and that decides what the histogram
    ; says about a namespace: the body is checked BEFORE the module's own grammar,
    ; so a statement inside it that this port cannot answer is the FIRST report and
    ; therefore the tag (record_unported keeps the first). That is not a loss of
    ; resolution — it is the resolution moving one level down, which is what the
    ; block arm was ported for.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ORDER OF THE REPORTS IS NOT THE ORDER OF THE LINES, and the sort is
    ; what makes that safe: the body's diagnostics are found before the module's,
    ; while GetDiagnosticsForFile answers a list sorted by position. So a
    ; `declare module M { ; }` whose body reports TS1036 and whose header reports
    ; TS1035 comes out in text order either way.
    procedure check_module_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let body AstNode.body_of(node)
        if body <> null
            this.check_source_element(body)

        let is_global_augmentation Binder.is_global_scope_augmentation(node)
        var in_ambient_context false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            set in_ambient_context: true
        if is_global_augmentation
        {
            if in_ambient_context = false
                this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagAugmentations_for_the_global_scope_should_have_declare_modifier_unless_they_appear_in_already_ambient_context)
        }

        ; ★ The two illegal-context messages are chosen by whether this is an
        ; AMBIENT EXTERNAL MODULE — `declare module "m"`, a quoted name — rather
        ; than by the ambient flag: `declare namespace N` is ambient and is still a
        ; namespace declaration.
        var context_code: int DiagA_namespace_declaration_is_only_allowed_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Binder.is_ambient_module(node)
            set context_code: DiagAn_ambient_module_declaration_is_only_allowed_at_the_top_level_in_a_file
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, context_code)
            return

        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if in_ambient_context = false
            {
                let name AstNode.name_of(node)
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindStringLiteral
                    this.grammar_error_on_node(name, DiagOnly_ambient_modules_can_use_quoted_names)
            }
        }

        ; ★ TWO TAGS, because the identifier branch stops one call earlier than the
        ; string one. `namespace N {}` is stopped by checkCollisionsForDeclarationName
        ; — and the TS1540 module-keyword advice sits BEHIND it, so `module N {}`
        ; cannot report it yet; `declare module "m"` skips that branch entirely and
        ; is stopped by checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations.
        ; ★★ SLICE 59 CLOSED THE FIRST OF THE TWO AND THE TWO BECAME ONE. The
        ; collision head is ported, so the identifier branch runs through it and
        ; reaches the TS1540 advice that sat behind it since slice 50 — `module N
        ; {}` says *use the namespace keyword* — and then both branches meet at
        ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations. The keyword is a FIELD on the
        ; declaration (three values: namespace, module, global), so the test is a
        ; field read and not a token position.
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(node)) = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.check_collisions_for_declaration_name(node, AstNode.name_of(node))
            if AstNode.module_keyword_of(node) = KindModuleKeyword
                this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagA_namespace_declaration_should_not_be_declared_using_the_module_keyword_Please_use_the_namespace_keyword_instead)
        }
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)

        ; ★★★ SLICE 65 TAKES THE WHOLE TAIL, AND IT IS THE LONGEST ARM OF THIS FILE
        ; THAT NEEDS NO TYPE — thirty lines of the reference, seven reports, and the
        ; only thing behind it is checkModuleAugmentationElement. What makes it
        ; portable is that every question it asks is about SYNTAX or about the
        ; symbol's declaration LIST: is this module instantiated, does its name
        ; collide with a class or function declared later, is it an augmentation, is
        ; its parent a global source file.
        let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
        var value_module false
        if symbol <> null
        {
            if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsValueModule) <> 0
                set value_module: true
        }
        ; isInstantiatedModule(node, ShouldPreserveConstEnums()). ★The option is
        ; unset under this harness, so the second disjunct — a CONST-ENUM-ONLY module
        ; counting as instantiated — is false without asking, and only
        ; ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated qualifies. It is written as the disjunction
        ; it is rather than as the surviving half, because the two states are
        ; genuinely different answers and a slice that lets `@preserveConstEnums`
        ; through moves the line without touching it.
        var instantiated false
        if value_module
        {
            if in_ambient_context = false
            {
                let module_state b.get_module_instance_state(node)
                if module_state = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                    set instantiated: true
                if preserve_const_enums
                {
                    if module_state = ModuleInstanceStateConstEnumOnly
                        set instantiated: true
                }
            }
        }
        if instantiated
        {
            if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)

            ; `GetIsolatedModules() && GetSourceFileOfNode(node).ExternalModuleIndicator
            ; == nil` — TS1250, and the option is unset, so the term is false without
            ; asking. ★Its second half is the binder's is_external_module and would be
            ; free; the option is the whole of the hole, which is why this is a
            ; comment and not a tag (§3.5v: an unset option is not an unported
            ; construct).

            ; ★★ THE TWO MERGE-ORDER REPORTS ARE THE ONES THIS TAIL IS FOR. A
            ; namespace may merge with a class or a function, and it must come AFTER
            ; it — `namespace N {} class N {}` is TS2434, and the file-crossing twin
            ; TS2433 cannot fire here because one unit is one file. It is stated as
            ; a reachability fact rather than left out: the comparison is written,
            ; and with one file both source files are the same node, so the `else if`
            ; is what runs.
            if symbol <> null
            {
                if Symbol.declaration_count(symbol) > 1
                {
                    let first Checker.first_non_ambient_class_or_function_declaration(symbol)
                    if first <> null
                    {
                        if Checker.source_file_of_node(node) <> Checker.source_file_of_node(first)
                            this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagA_namespace_declaration_cannot_be_in_a_different_file_from_a_class_or_function_with_which_it_is_merged)
                        else
                        {
                            if AstNode.pos_of(node) < AstNode.pos_of(first)
                                this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagA_namespace_declaration_cannot_be_located_prior_to_a_class_or_function_with_which_it_is_merged)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            ; The verbatim-module-syntax report, behind the same option every other
            ; site of it is behind (see check_export_assignment's own note).
            if verbatim_module_syntax
                this.record_unported("grammar-verbatim-module-syntax", KindModuleDeclaration)
        }

        if Binder.is_ambient_module(node) = false
            return

        ; ★★★ THE AUGMENTATION BRANCH IS THE ONE HOLE, AND ITS GUARD COLLAPSES TO A
        ; SINGLE TERM HERE. `checkBody := isGlobalAugmentation ||
        ; getSymbolOfDeclaration(node).Flags&Transient != 0`, and the Transient flag
        ; is set by NOTHING in this port: it is the checker's own mark for a symbol
        ; created by cloneSymbol or newSymbol during the global merge, which is the
        ; same pass getMergedSymbol's empty table comes from. So the second disjunct
        ; is false for the same reason and by the same date — see get_merged_symbol.
        ; ★It is written as the disjunction anyway, so that the slice which ports the
        ; merge finds both halves in one place.
        if b.is_module_augmentation_external(node)
        {
            var check_body is_global_augmentation
            if symbol <> null
            {
                if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsTransient) <> 0
                    set check_body: true
            }
            if check_body
            {
                if body <> null
                    this.record_unported("check-module-augmentation-element", KindModuleDeclaration)
            }
            return
        }

        ; ★★ THE LAST TWO ARMS DIFFER IN ONE REPORT AND SHARE THE OTHER, which is
        ; the reference's shape and reads as a duplication until the messages are
        ; compared: a global augmentation that is not directly nested in a module is
        ; the same complaint whether its parent is a global source file or a
        ; namespace, while a NON-global ambient module gets *cannot specify relative
        ; module name* in the first case and *cannot be nested* in the second.
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if Checker.is_global_source_file(b, AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
        {
            if is_global_augmentation
                this.error_on_node(name, DiagAugmentations_for_the_global_scope_can_only_be_directly_nested_in_external_modules_or_ambient_module_declarations)
            else
            {
                if is_external_module_name_relative(AstNode.literal_text_of(name), AstNode.literal_text_length_of(name))
                    this.error_on_node(name, DiagAmbient_module_declaration_cannot_specify_relative_module_name)
            }
            return
        }
        if is_global_augmentation
            this.error_on_node(name, DiagAugmentations_for_the_global_scope_can_only_be_directly_nested_in_external_modules_or_ambient_module_declarations)
        else
            this.error_on_node(name, DiagAmbient_modules_cannot_be_nested_in_other_modules_or_namespaces)
    }

    ; getFirstNonAmbientClassOrFunctionDeclaration, whole.
    ;
    ; ★ The `&&` binds tighter than the `||` in the reference's condition, so it is a
    ; CLASS declaration, or a function declaration WITH A BODY — an overload
    ; signature is not something a namespace can be merged after.
    function first_non_ambient_class_or_function_declaration(s: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(s)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(s, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                var eligible false
                let k AstNode.kind_of(d)
                if k = KindClassDeclaration
                    set eligible: true
                if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
                {
                    if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(d)) = false
                        set eligible: true
                }
                if eligible
                {
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(d) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                        return d
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.GetSourceFileOfNode — the walk up to the SourceFile.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT ANSWERS THE SAME NODE FOR EVERY ARGUMENT IN THIS HARNESS, because a unit
    ; is one file, and it is ported anyway: the caller compares two of them, so
    ; folding the comparison to `true` would delete a report's CONDITION rather than
    ; its body, and the deletion becomes wrong on the day a second file arrives
    ; (§3.5bg). The reference reaches the file through a cached pointer on every
    ; node; this port has the parent chain and no such slot, and adding one would be
    ; a field whose only reader is a comparison that cannot fail.
    function source_file_of_node(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var p node
        while p <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(p) = KindSourceFile
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; checkImportDeclaration's prefix. The arm serves ImportDeclaration and
    ; JSImportDeclaration, as the reference's switch does — one `case` with two
    ; kinds — and the report carries the node's own kind so the histogram keeps
    ; them apart.
    ;
    ; ★ `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && node.Modifiers() != nil` is a
    ; short-circuit whose LEFT side is the call: the check always runs, and the
    ; extra report only follows when it found nothing and there is a modifier
    ; anyway. `An import declaration cannot have modifiers` is therefore about a
    ; LEGAL modifier in an illegal place (`export import x from "m"`), not about a
    ; malformed one.
    procedure check_import_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_import_declaration_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }

        ; ★★ SLICE 58's CONTINUATION. The two branches inside are the reference's
        ; `if importClause != nil && !checkGrammarImportClause(...)` and its
        ; `else if`, and BOTH of them stop at something this port does not have —
        ; but they stop at different things, so they get different tags. The first
        ; is checkImportBinding (collisions + the alias symbol, i.e. the symbol
        ; table); the second is resolveExternalModuleNameWorker behind an option
        ; that is unset, which is the shape the family's other option branches
        ; already have.
        ;
        ; ★ checkImportAttributes is OUTSIDE the whole thing, on purpose: the
        ; reference calls it unconditionally, so a declaration whose external check
        ; FAILED still gets its attributes checked. That is the one line of this
        ; arm that was already reachable before this slice.
        if this.check_external_import_or_export_declaration(node)
        {
            let import_clause AstNode.import_clause_of(node)
            if import_clause = null
            {
                if no_unchecked_side_effect_imports
                    this.record_unported("resolve-external-module-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            }
            ; ★★ SLICE 59's CONTINUATION. Three shapes and three different
            ; answers, which is why the tag could not stay one: a DEFAULT import
            ; hands the clause to checkImportBinding, a NAMESPACE import hands it
            ; the namespace node, and a NAMED import list asks the module RESOLVER
            ; first and visits its specifiers only if the module resolved — which
            ; under this harness it never does, so the loop is unreachable and the
            ; tag names the resolver.
            ;
            ; ★ Everything else in this branch is killed by the emit format being
            ; ModuleKindNone: the two CommonJS emit-helper calls (ImportStar and
            ; ImportDefault, with `needsImportStar` the flag that keeps them
            ; exclusive) and, one option further, the JSON-import error, whose gate
            ; is `Node18 <= moduleKind <= NodeNext` and whose value is ES2022. The
            ; flag is not modelled because neither reader exists.
            if import_clause <> null
            {
                if this.check_grammar_import_clause(import_clause) = false
                {
                    if AstNode.name_of(import_clause) <> null
                        this.check_import_binding(import_clause)
                    let named_bindings AstNode.named_bindings_of(import_clause)
                    if named_bindings <> null
                    {
                        if AstNode.kind_of(named_bindings) = KindNamespaceImport
                            this.check_import_binding(named_bindings)
                        if AstNode.kind_of(named_bindings) <> KindNamespaceImport
                            this.record_unported("resolve-external-module-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        this.check_import_attributes(node)
    }

    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration's prefix. It shares the import declaration's two
    ; illegal-context messages — the reference passes the same pair — and discards
    ; the modifier check's result, so there is no second modifier report here.
    procedure check_import_equals_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
        }
        ; ★★ SLICE 58 CLOSES THE FIRST OF THE TWO TAGS AND LEAVES THE SECOND. The
        ; disjunction is `IsInternalModuleImportEqualsDeclaration(node) ||
        ; checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration(node)`, and its left side is a
        ; PURE KIND TEST — an internal `import A = B.C` never asks the external
        ; check at all. So the external half is now answered and both roads meet at
        ; checkImportBinding, which is the symbol table.
        ;
        ; ★ The `||` short-circuits, so the external check must not run for an
        ; internal reference. Written as two statements rather than as one `or`,
        ; because a bare `a or b` here would evaluate b for every namespace import
        ; and the call is not free of REPORTS.
        var proceed false
        if AstNode.import_equals_reference_kind(node) <> KindExternalModuleReference
            set proceed: true
        if proceed = false
            set proceed: this.check_external_import_or_export_declaration(node)
        ; ★ SLICE 59: checkImportBinding is ported, so the stop moves one call on to
        ; checkAliasSymbol. Behind IT stand markLinkedReferences and the two arms of
        ; the module reference — the internal one resolving the alias, the external
        ; one reporting TS1202 for `import a = require("m")` under an ES module
        ; target, which is what this harness's ES2022 is. That report is therefore
        ; reachable in the reference and behind one stop here.
        if proceed
            this.check_import_binding(node)
    }

    ; checkExportDeclaration's prefix — and the ONE arm of this family that can run
    ; to the END.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `export {}` COMPLETES, and it is the only shape here that does. With no
    ; module specifier the `||` short-circuits, so checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration
    ; is never asked; with an EMPTY clause the specifier loop has nothing to visit;
    ; the three context bools then permit it at the top level of a file; and
    ; checkImportAttributes returns at its own first line because there is no
    ; `with { … }`. So the check reaches the bottom and the unit's C section becomes
    ; comparable — which is the only way the matched count can move in this slice,
    ; the same argument slice 49 made for why its count could NOT.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 58 MADE THE SPECIFIER-BEARING HALF REACHABLE, AND THAT TURNED THE
    ; SPECIFIER TEST FROM A SHORTCUT INTO A CONDITION IN TWO PLACES. The reference
    ; asks `ModuleSpecifier == nil` twice — once as the left disjunct of the guard,
    ; and once inside `inAmbientNamespaceDeclaration` — and while the arm returned
    ; at the first of them the second was a tautology, so slice 50 could write "from
    ; here the module specifier is nil" and mean it. It is not nil any more, and the
    ; test had to come back explicitly. **A condition that is true because of where
    ; the code stopped is a condition that expires when the code stops later**, and
    ; the only thing that marks such a line is that somebody wrote down WHY it was
    ; omitted — which slice 50 did.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SPECIFIER-LOOP TAG MARKS AND NO LONGER RETURNS. checkExportSpecifier is
    ; still the symbol table, but the namespace report BELOW the loop is not, and
    ; with `export { a } from "m"` now reaching the block that report has a
    ; population it never had. record_unported keeps the FIRST tag either way, and
    ; diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a report we make after one we skip
    ; is sound — the convention this file has followed since slice 52.
    procedure check_export_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_export_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_export_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_export_declaration_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }
        this.check_grammar_export_declaration(node)

        ; The `||`, short-circuiting: with no module specifier the external check
        ; is never asked.
        let module_specifier AstNode.module_specifier_of(node)
        var proceed false
        if module_specifier = null
            set proceed: true
        if proceed = false
            set proceed: this.check_external_import_or_export_declaration(node)
        if proceed = false
        {
            this.check_import_attributes(node)
            return
        }

        let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(node)
        var named false
        if clause <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(clause) <> KindNamespaceExport
                set named: true
        }
        if named = false
        {
            ; `export * from "m"` and `export * as ns from "m"`. resolveExternalModuleName
            ; needs the module resolver, so the whole branch stops here — including
            ; its two emit-helper calls, which are one level further in.
            this.record_unported("resolve-external-module-name", KindExportDeclaration)
            this.check_import_attributes(node)
            return
        }

        let els AstNode.elements_of(clause)
        if AstNode.list_count(els) > 0
            this.record_unported("check-export-specifier", KindExportDeclaration)

        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        var in_ambient_external_module false
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
        {
            if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
                set in_ambient_external_module: true
        }
        var in_ambient_namespace_declaration false
        if in_ambient_external_module = false
        {
            if pk = KindModuleBlock
            {
                if module_specifier = null
                {
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        set in_ambient_namespace_declaration: true
                }
            }
        }
        var permitted false
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set permitted: true
        if in_ambient_external_module
            set permitted: true
        if in_ambient_namespace_declaration
            set permitted: true
        if permitted = false
            this.error_on_node(node, DiagExport_declarations_are_not_permitted_in_a_namespace)

        this.check_import_attributes(node)
    }

    ; checkExportAssignment's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★ ONE PATH OF IT RETURNS, and it is unreachable today for the reason the
    ; family's header gives: the container walk finds a ModuleDeclaration only for
    ; an `export = x` inside a namespace BODY, and a body's statements are reached
    ; through this slice's own block arm — so the path exists, and the corpus is
    ; what says whether anything takes it.
    ;
    ; ★ `ast.IsExportAssignment(node)` in the modifier condition is always true
    ; here: the reference's switch routes exactly one kind to this arm. Kept as the
    ; reference's structure would read, minus a test that cannot fail.
    procedure check_export_assignment(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let is_export_equals AstNode.is_export_equals_of(node)
        var code: int DiagA_default_export_must_be_at_the_top_level_of_a_file_or_module_declaration
        if is_export_equals
            set code: DiagAn_export_assignment_must_be_at_the_top_level_of_a_file_or_module_declaration
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
        {
            if is_export_equals
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
            }
        }

        var container AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) <> KindSourceFile
            set container: AstNode.parent_node_of(container)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(container) = false
            {
                if is_export_equals
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagAn_export_assignment_cannot_be_used_in_a_namespace)
                if is_export_equals = false
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_default_export_can_only_be_used_in_an_ECMAScript_style_module)
                return
            }
        }

        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_export_assignment_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }

        ; isIllegalExportDefaultInCJS. VerbatimModuleSyntax stands in front of the
        ; whole conjunction and is unset, so the term is false without asking.
        ; Behind the option rather than omitted, and under the tag slice 49 gave the
        ; same missing machinery. ★What used to stand here added *and it reaches
        ; program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, which the stub program does not have*;
        ; slice 54 measured that and it is false — the oracle's program HAS the
        ; method and answers ModuleKindNone. The option is the whole of the hole.
        if verbatim_module_syntax
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-verbatim-module-syntax", KindExportAssignment)
            return
        }

        ; ★ TWO TAGS again, and the second names the EXPRESSION's kind rather than
        ; the statement's — check_expression_statement's argument: this column is
        ; the work list, and `ExportAssignment` is a row nobody can act on.
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(e) = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("resolve-entity-name", KindExportAssignment)
            return
        }
        ; The else branch is one call, and behind it stands checkExternalModuleExports
        ; — the container's export table — which is what the row names now.
        if e = null
            return
        let export_type this.check_expression_cached(e as ref[AstNode])
        if export_type = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-external-module-exports", KindExportAssignment)
    }

    ; checkImportAttributes' GUARD, which is the whole of it for a declaration with
    ; no `with { … }` clause — and that is every declaration in the corpus but a
    ; handful. Behind the guard stands getGlobalImportAttributesTypeChecked, i.e.
    ; the global type table and the assignability relation, so the rest is a
    ; dimension rather than a step.
    procedure check_import_attributes(this, declaration: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.import_attributes_of(declaration) = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-import-attributes", AstNode.kind_of(declaration))
    }

    ; ── slice 58: checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS ONE FUNCTION AND IT UNBLOCKS THREE ARMS AT ONCE, which is
    ; what made it the biggest row on slice 57's work list without being the
    ; biggest function: `check-external-import-or-export-declaration` stood at
    ; 1 588 units under ImportDeclaration, 411 under ImportEqualsDeclaration and
    ; 216 under ExportDeclaration. Slice 50 put those three tags there in one
    ; commit and named the boundary it was drawing — *everything these arms call in
    ; grammarchecks.go is ported; the first call into a CHECK of checker.go is
    ; where each arm reports* — so this is the first slice of the family to cross
    ; that line, and it crosses it at the one checker.go function of the six that
    ; needs neither the symbol table nor a type.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IT NEEDS INSTEAD IS A PATH PREDICATE, and that is why tspath grew
    ; three functions rather than the checker growing one: `IsExternalModuleNameRelative`
    ; is asked of a module SPECIFIER, not of a file name, and it is the same
    ; function the module resolver will ask the day that dimension opens.
    ;
    ; ★ Its result is a BOOL and all three callers read it — two as the right
    ; disjunct of an `||` whose left side can skip it entirely. That shape is the
    ; reason the two import arms could not simply be widened in place: the export
    ; arm asks it only when there IS a module specifier, and the import-equals arm
    ; only when the reference is EXTERNAL, so each caller's guard had to be ported
    ; with it.
    function check_external_import_or_export_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let module_name AstNode.get_external_module_name(node)
        if module_name = null
            return false
        if Binder.node_is_missing(module_name)
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(module_name) <> KindStringLiteral
        {
            this.error_on_node(module_name, DiagString_literal_expected)
            return false
        }

        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var in_ambient_external_module false
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
        {
            if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
                set in_ambient_external_module: true
        }
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindSourceFile
        {
            if in_ambient_external_module = false
            {
                ; ★ The message is chosen by the node's KIND and the two are not
                ; each other's negation: an export declaration says *export
                ; declarations are not permitted in a namespace* (TS1194, the same
                ; code the export arm's own tail reports for the no-specifier
                ; case), an import says *import declarations in a namespace cannot
                ; reference a module* (TS1147). The reference's `IfElse` is a
                ; two-arm choice over IsExportDeclaration, so JSImportDeclaration
                ; falls to the import side with ImportDeclaration.
                var code: int DiagImport_declarations_in_a_namespace_cannot_reference_a_module
                if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindExportDeclaration
                    set code: DiagExport_declarations_are_not_permitted_in_a_namespace
                this.error_on_node(module_name, code)
                return false
            }
        }

        if in_ambient_external_module
        {
            if is_external_module_name_relative(AstNode.literal_text_of(module_name), AstNode.literal_text_length_of(module_name))
            {
                ; ★★ The GUARD is the interesting half and it is a suppression, not
                ; a condition: checkModuleDeclaration has already reported on a
                ; top-level import/export of an external module AUGMENTATION, so
                ; this one is skipped to avoid saying it twice. The reference's own
                ; comment says exactly that, and it is why the test is
                ; `isTopLevelInExternalModuleAugmentation` — a question about the
                ; enclosing module's relationship to its FILE — rather than
                ; anything about this declaration.
                if Checker.is_top_level_in_external_module_augmentation(this.b, node) = false
                {
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagImport_or_export_declaration_in_an_ambient_module_declaration_cannot_reference_module_through_relative_module_name)
                    return false
                }
            }
        }

        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindImportEqualsDeclaration
        {
            let attributes AstNode.import_attributes_of(node)
            if attributes <> null
            {
                ; ★ The loop does NOT stop at the first bad value — every attribute
                ; whose value is not a string literal is reported, and the accumulated
                ; flag is the answer. `import x from "m" with { a: 1, b: 2 }` is two
                ; diagnostics, which is what makes a control that returns early
                ; measurable.
                var has_error false
                let attrs AstNode.import_attribute_list_of(attributes)
                let n AstNode.list_count(attrs)
                var i 0
                while i < n
                {
                    let value AstNode.import_attribute_value_of(AstNode.child_in_list(attrs, i))
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(value) <> KindStringLiteral
                    {
                        set has_error: true
                        this.error_on_node(value, DiagImport_attribute_values_must_be_string_literal_expressions)
                    }
                    set i: i + 1
                }
                return has_error = false
            }
        }
        true
    }

    ; isTopLevelInExternalModuleAugmentation, whole — three tests and a hop, and
    ; the last of them is the binder's `is_module_augmentation_external`, which
    ; already existed because bind_module_declaration asks the same question.
    ;
    ; ★ It takes the BINDER rather than being a method on it, because the caller is
    ; the checker and the reference's is a free function in checker/utilities.go.
    ; The binder is passed for the one field the answer depends on
    ; (is_external_module), which is a property of the FILE and not of the node.
    function is_top_level_in_external_module_augmentation(b: ref[Binder], node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if node = null
            return false
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if parent = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(parent) <> KindModuleBlock
            return false
        let grand AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(grand) = false
            return false
        b.is_module_augmentation_external(grand)
    }

    ; ast.IsGlobalSourceFile, slice 65: a SourceFile that is not an external or
    ; CommonJS module — i.e. a SCRIPT.
    ;
    ; ★ It takes the binder for is_top_level_in_external_module_augmentation's
    ; reason: the second half of the question is a property of the FILE, which the
    ; binder computed once at construction, and not of the node handed in.
    function is_global_source_file(b: ref[Binder], node: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(node) <> KindSourceFile
            return false
        b.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
    }

    ; checkGrammarImportClause, whole — two arms over the phase modifier, and the
    ; one grammar check of this family slice 50 could not reach.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DEFER ARM'S THIRD REPORT IS LIVE BECAUSE THE UNSET `module` OPTION
    ; DERIVES TO ES2022, not because anything sets it — see Checker.module_kind.
    ; `import defer * as ns from "m"` therefore reports TS18060 here, and a reading
    ; of the unset option as ModuleKindNone would have made that line unreachable
    ; while leaving the file compiling and every yardstick green.
    ;
    ; ★ The JSDoc guard on the type arm is the reparser's: a `@import` tag builds
    ; an ImportClause with NodeFlagsJSDoc set, where a default name and named
    ; bindings together are the tag's own syntax rather than a mistake.
    function check_grammar_import_clause(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let phase AstNode.import_clause_phase_modifier_of(node)
        let named_bindings AstNode.named_bindings_of(node)
        if phase = KindTypeKeyword
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsJSDoc) = 0
            {
                if AstNode.name_of(node) <> null
                {
                    if named_bindings <> null
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_type_only_import_can_specify_a_default_import_or_named_bindings_but_not_both)
                }
            }
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(named_bindings) = KindNamedImports
                return this.check_grammar_type_only_named_imports_or_exports(named_bindings)
        }
        if phase = KindDeferKeyword
        {
            if AstNode.name_of(node) <> null
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagDefault_imports_are_not_allowed_in_a_deferred_import)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(named_bindings) = KindNamedImports
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagNamed_imports_are_not_allowed_in_a_deferred_import)
            if module_kind <> ModuleKindESNext
            {
                if module_kind <> ModuleKindPreserve
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagDeferred_imports_are_only_supported_when_the_module_flag_is_set_to_esnext_or_preserve)
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; ── slice 51: the class declaration ─────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS THE HEAD checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration AND THE ONE ARM
    ; IT PREFIXES, and the line it stops at is the same KIND of line slice 50's
    ; was — a call into a CHECK the port does not have — but it takes three
    ; functions to get there rather than one, because two of them turn out to be
    ; guards. checkClassDeclaration's own prefix is a legacy-decorator report and
    ; the missing-name test; checkClassLikeDeclaration's first line is the head;
    ; and checkDecorators, which follows it, RETURNS at its own first line for
    ; every class that carries no decorator — which is nearly all of them. So the
    ; report lands on checkCollisionsForDeclarationName, a row this column already
    ; has for the module and enum arms.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IT COST IS NOT IN THIS FILE: the head reports at spans only a
    ; NodeList's own range knows, so §3.5bq's side table had to become readable
    ; one phase later. See the class-like grammar checks below check_grammar_modifiers.

    ; checkDecorators' GUARD, and the guard is the whole of what is portable.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE-WAY `||` IS THE COMMON CASE. `!CanHaveDecorators(node) ||
    ; !HasDecorators(node) || !NodeCanBeDecorated(...)` returns for any node
    ; without a decorator, so this function is a no-op for a plain class and the
    ; check continues past it — which is the difference between reporting here and
    ; reporting one call earlier, and the reason it is ported at all.
    ;
    ; ★ Behind the guard stand checkExternalEmitHelpers (the emit-helper table, and
    ; a languageVersion this harness has no target for), markLinkedReferences and
    ; checkDecorator, which resolves a signature. None of them is a step; the tag
    ; names the function rather than one of them, because which one comes first
    ; depends on a compiler option that is not read anywhere yet.
    ;
    ; ★ The parent and grandparent are read null-safely, for the reason
    ; kind_or_unknown exists: upstream reads `node.Parent.Parent` straight out of
    ; the call and a nil Parent is a readable zero in Go.
    procedure check_decorators(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.can_have_decorators(node) = false
            return
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsDecorator) = false
            return
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var grandparent: ref[AstNode]? null
        if parent <> null
            set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        if this.node_can_be_decorated(node, parent, grandparent) = false
            return
        if Checker.find_first_decorator(node) = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-decorators", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 73: checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; The statement slice 72's histogram named: `check-object-type-for-duplicate-
    ; declarations` was the head of the work list on BOTH stages (128 units at
    ; stage 1, 2 891 at stage 2), and it is one function plus the reporter under
    ; it. What it buys is the class's own duplicate-member family — TS2300 on a
    ; repeated property or on a property that collides with an accessor, TS2699 on
    ; a static member named `prototype`, and TS2804 on a private name used once
    ; statically and once on an instance.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THREE MAPS ARE THE FUNCTION, and each one is guarded differently,
    ; which is what makes them three rather than one keyed by a discriminator.
    ; `instanceNames` and `staticNames` hold a KIND (1 property, 2 accessor, 3
    ; already reported) and take an entry only for a symbol with more than one
    ; declaration; `privateNames` holds a BITSET (1 instance, 2 static, 3 both,
    ; reported on arrival) and takes an entry for every private-named member. Two
    ; different value alphabets over the same key type is exactly the same-layout
    ; merge §3.5n rejects. See DuplicateNameEntry for why the storage is a list.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO STATIC QUESTIONS ARE NOT ONE QUESTION, and the reference asks
    ; both, three lines apart. `ast.HasStaticModifier` — the modifier alone — picks
    ; which of the two kind maps a member goes into; `ast.IsStatic` — a class
    ; ELEMENT with that modifier, OR a static block — decides the private name's
    ; bit and the reporter's `isStatic == ast.IsStatic(member)` filter. They differ
    ; on a `static` modifier the parser accepted somewhere that is not a class
    ; element, and folding them would be invisible until it did.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE WALK OPENS A SECOND WORK-LIST ROW AND ONLY STAGE 2 HAS INPUT FOR IT:
    ; 109 units land on `get-late-bound-symbol`, zero at stage 1. A class member with
    ; a COMPUTED name carries the binder's internal name `__computed`, and
    ; symbol_of_declaration routes such a symbol through get_late_bound_symbol, which
    ; is a deliberate stop of another slice. **A work-list row is not a property of
    ; the function that reports it** — this walk is simply the first caller that ever
    ; asks a computed member for its symbol.
    ;
    ; ★ `checkPrivateNames` is a parameter rather than a constant because the
    ; reference's three call sites disagree: the class passes true, checkTypeLiteral
    ; and checkInterfaceDeclaration pass false. Only the class reaches here in this
    ; slice — the other two arms stop earlier, the interface at check-index-
    ; constraints inside its `interfaceChecked` block — so the false side has no
    ; input yet and the flag is written out rather than folded away.
    procedure check_object_type_for_duplicate_declarations(this, node: ref[AstNode], check_private_names: bool)
    {
        let members AstNode.member_list_of(node)
        if members = null
            return
        let ms members as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        let count ms.get_length() as int
        if count = 0
            return
        let ia host.allocate(sizeof Array[DuplicateNameEntry], alignof Array[DuplicateNameEntry]) as pointer[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]]
        set *ia: Array[DuplicateNameEntry]()
        let sa host.allocate(sizeof Array[DuplicateNameEntry], alignof Array[DuplicateNameEntry]) as pointer[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]]
        set *sa: Array[DuplicateNameEntry]()
        let pa host.allocate(sizeof Array[DuplicateNameEntry], alignof Array[DuplicateNameEntry]) as pointer[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]]
        set *pa: Array[DuplicateNameEntry]()
        let node_in_ambient_context (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let m *(ms.get_buffer() + i)
            set i: i + 1
            if m = null
                continue
            let member m as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.kind_of(member) = KindConstructor
            {
                ; A parameter property declares a class member without appearing in
                ; the member list, so the constructor's parameters are walked in
                ; the member's place. `is_parameter_property_declaration` reads the
                ; parameter's own PARENT where the reference is handed `member`
                ; explicitly; they are the same node, since the parameter list
                ; being walked is this constructor's.
                let params AstNode.parameters_of(member)
                if params = null
                    continue
                let ps params as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
                let pn ps.get_length() as int
                var j 0
                while j < pn
                {
                    let p *(ps.get_buffer() + j)
                    set j: j + 1
                    if p = null
                        continue
                    let param p as ref[AstNode]
                    if b.is_parameter_property_declaration(param) = false
                        continue
                    if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(param))
                        continue
                    this.check_property_or_accessor(node, this.symbol_of_declaration(param), 1, false, ia, sa)
                }
                continue
            }
            let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(member)
            let is_static b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsStatic)
            ; In non-ambient contexts, check that static members are not named
            ; 'prototype'.
            if node_in_ambient_context = false
            {
                if is_static
                {
                    if symbol <> null
                    {
                        if Checker.symbol_name_equals(symbol as ref[Symbol], "prototype", 9)
                            this.error_on_node_or_null(AstNode.name_of(member), DiagStatic_property_0_conflicts_with_built_in_property_Function_0_of_constructor_function_1)
                    }
                }
            }
            ; Check that this object type declaration doesn't contain multiple
            ; declarations of the same property, or accessor and property
            ; declarations with the same name.
            ;
            ; ★ The reference's condition is `IsPropertyDeclaration(member) &&
            ; !HasAccessorModifier(member) || IsPropertySignatureDeclaration(member)`
            ; against `IsAccessor(member) || IsPropertyDeclaration(member) &&
            ; HasAccessorModifier(member)`, i.e. an `accessor` field is an ACCESSOR
            ; and a plain field is a property. Written as nested ifs for the
            ; language guide's reason, and the else-branch is the reference's `else
            ; if` and not a second independent test.
            ;
            ; ★★★ THE MODIFIER TEST IS UNOBSERVABLE AND THE BINDER IS WHY (control
            ; g08, two wrong fixtures). Only ONE kind pair distinguishes the two
            ; readings — accessor against accessor, where the switch falls through
            ; and reports nothing — and the binder never merges that pair: two
            ; `accessor` fields conflict, and so does an `accessor` field beside a
            ; getter, so each keeps its own symbol with ONE declaration and the
            ; guard in check_property_or_accessor stops it. Every shape that DOES
            ; merge (a plain field beside an `accessor` field, either order) reports
            ; under both readings. So this test is here for the reference's sake and
            ; nothing in this suite can go red on it; the symbols yardstick matching
            ; those units is what makes that a claim about the reference.
            ;
            ; ★ KindPropertySignature has no input either: the only caller that
            ; passes a type literal or an interface is behind an earlier stop.
            let mk AstNode.kind_of(member)
            var is_property false
            if mk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsAccessor) = false
                    set is_property: true
            }
            if mk = KindPropertySignature
                set is_property: true
            if is_property
                this.check_property_or_accessor(node, symbol, 1, is_static, ia, sa)
            else
            {
                var is_accessor false
                if Checker.is_accessor_kind(mk)
                    set is_accessor: true
                if mk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                {
                    if b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsAccessor)
                        set is_accessor: true
                }
                if is_accessor
                    this.check_property_or_accessor(node, symbol, 2, is_static, ia, sa)
            }
            ; Check that each private identifier is used only for instance members
            ; or only for static members. It is an error for an instance and a
            ; static member to have the same private identifier.
            if check_private_names = false
                continue
            let nm AstNode.name_of(member)
            if nm = null
                continue
            if AstNode.is_private_identifier(nm) = false
                continue
            if symbol = null
            {
                ; The reference dereferences the symbol here and would PANIC; a
                ; private-named class element is always declared, so this reports
                ; rather than trapping (§3.5, the standing answer to a reference
                ; panic) and the histogram says so if the claim is ever false.
                this.record_unported("duplicate-declarations-private-no-symbol", mk)
                continue
            }
            let psym symbol as ref[Symbol]
            let pd Symbol.name_data_of(psym)
            let pl Symbol.name_len_of(psym)
            var flags Checker.duplicate_name_state(pa, pd, pl)
            if flags = 3
                continue
            var bit 1
            if b.is_static(member)
                set bit: 2
            set flags: flags | bit
            Checker.set_duplicate_name_state(pa, pd, pl, flags)
            if flags = 3
                this.report_duplicate_member_errors(node, pd, pl, false, false, DiagDuplicate_identifier_0_Static_and_instance_elements_cannot_share_the_same_private_name)
        }
    }

    ; checkPropertyOrAccessor, the closure — the two kind maps it captures are
    ; parameters, and `node` with them because the reporter needs it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE STATE MACHINE IS THREE-VALUED AND ITS THIRD VALUE IS "ALREADY SAID SO".
    ; `state == 0` is the absent key, so the first sighting of a name only records
    ; what KIND it was; the second reports if the pair is property/property or
    ; property/accessor and then writes 3, which no later member can move — two
    ; accessors of the same name are a getter and a setter and are NOT an error,
    ; which is the whole content of `state == 2 && kind != 2`.
    procedure check_property_or_accessor(this, node: ref[AstNode], symbol: ref[Symbol]?, kind: int, is_static: bool, instance_names: ref[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]], static_names: ref[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]])
    {
        if symbol = null
        {
            ; `len(symbol.Declarations)` on a nil symbol PANICS upstream. The
            ; reference itself is of two minds about it — the `prototype` test three
            ; lines above this call site guards with `symbol != nil` and this call
            ; does not — so the assumption is made visible rather than assumed.
            this.record_unported("duplicate-declarations-no-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return
        }
        let sym symbol as ref[Symbol]
        if Symbol.declaration_count(sym) <= 1
            return
        var names: ref[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]] instance_names
        if is_static
            set names: static_names
        let d Symbol.name_data_of(sym)
        let n Symbol.name_len_of(sym)
        let state Checker.duplicate_name_state(names, d, n)
        if state = 0
        {
            ; On first occurrence just record the kind.
            Checker.set_duplicate_name_state(names, d, n, kind)
            return
        }
        var report false
        if state = 1
            set report: true
        if state = 2
        {
            if kind <> 2
                set report: true
        }
        if report = false
            return
        ; Error on second property or combination of property and accessor.
        this.report_duplicate_member_errors(node, d, n, true, is_static, DiagDuplicate_identifier_0)
        ; Record that errors have been reported.
        Checker.set_duplicate_name_state(names, d, n, 3)
    }

    ; reportDuplicateMemberErrors, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT WALKS THE WHOLE MEMBER LIST AGAIN PER REPORTED NAME, and that is the
    ; reference's shape rather than an oversight: the report has to land on EVERY
    ; declaration of the name, not on the second one, so the diagnostics come out in
    ; member order and a name declared three times collects three. Keeping a list of
    ; the nodes seen so far would answer the same set in the same order only for the
    ; declarations BEFORE the one that tripped the state machine.
    ;
    ; ★ `checkStatic` is false only on the private-name call, where the point is
    ; precisely that a static and an instance member share the name — so the filter
    ; that the two kind maps get from being separate maps has to be switched off.
    procedure report_duplicate_member_errors(this, node: ref[AstNode], name_data: pointer[char], name_len: int, check_static: bool, is_static: bool, code: int)
    {
        let members AstNode.member_list_of(node)
        if members = null
            return
        let ms members as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        let count ms.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let m *(ms.get_buffer() + i)
            set i: i + 1
            if m = null
                continue
            let member m as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.kind_of(member) = KindConstructor
            {
                let params AstNode.parameters_of(member)
                if params = null
                    continue
                let ps params as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
                let pn ps.get_length() as int
                var j 0
                while j < pn
                {
                    let p *(ps.get_buffer() + j)
                    set j: j + 1
                    if p = null
                        continue
                    let param p as ref[AstNode]
                    if b.is_parameter_property_declaration(param) = false
                        continue
                    if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(param))
                        continue
                    let psym this.symbol_of_declaration(param)
                    if psym = null
                    {
                        ; The reference reads `symbol.Name` here without asking, and
                        ; would PANIC — the same shape check_property_or_accessor
                        ; reports rather than trapping, and the third site of it in
                        ; this pair. It is NOT covered by that one: the reporter
                        ; walks every member, including a constructor the outer walk
                        ; has not reached yet.
                        this.record_unported("duplicate-member-errors-no-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(member))
                        continue
                    }
                    if Checker.symbol_name_matches(psym as ref[Symbol], name_data, name_len)
                        this.error_on_node_or_null(AstNode.name_of(param), code)
                }
                continue
            }
            let msym this.symbol_of_declaration(member)
            ; ★ THIS guard is the REFERENCE'S own — `symbol != nil && symbol.Name ==
            ; name` — so it is a skip and not a report, unlike the parameter branch
            ; three lines up. The asymmetry is upstream's and is transcribed.
            if msym = null
                continue
            if Checker.symbol_name_matches(msym as ref[Symbol], name_data, name_len) = false
                continue
            if check_static
            {
                if is_static <> b.is_static(member)
                    continue
            }
            this.error_on_node_or_null(AstNode.name_of(member), code)
        }
    }

    ; The map read, `names[symbol.Name]`. A name the list does not carry answers 0,
    ; which is what a Go map read of an absent key does.
    function duplicate_name_state(t: ref[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]], d: pointer[char], n: int) returns int
    {
        let len t.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let e *(t.get_buffer() + i)
            if SymbolTable.names_equal(e.name_data, e.name_len, d, n)
                return e.state
            set i: i + 1
        }
        0
    }

    ; The map write, `names[symbol.Name] = state`. An existing name is overwritten
    ; in place; a new one is appended.
    procedure set_duplicate_name_state(t: ref[Array[DuplicateNameEntry]], d: pointer[char], n: int, s: int)
    {
        let len t.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let e t.get_buffer() + i
            if SymbolTable.names_equal(e.name_data, e.name_len, d, n)
            {
                set e.state: s
                return
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        t.add(DuplicateNameEntry(d, n, s))
    }

    ; `symbol.Name == <literal>`, for the one name this slice compares against a
    ; constant. The bytes are not text (§Symbol.name_data), so the comparison is
    ; SymbolTable.names_equal's and not a String's.
    function symbol_name_equals(s: ref[Symbol], lit: pointer[const_char], len: int) returns bool
    {
        if Symbol.name_len_of(s) <> len
            return false
        let d Symbol.name_data_of(s)
        if d = null
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if (*(d + i) as int) <> ((*(lit + i) as char) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; `symbol.Name == name`, symbol against the reported name's bytes.
    function symbol_name_matches(s: ref[Symbol], d: pointer[char], n: int) returns bool
        SymbolTable.names_equal(Symbol.name_data_of(s), Symbol.name_len_of(s), d, n)

    ; checkClassLikeDeclaration's prefix — two calls and then the check that stops
    ; it. It is a function of its own rather than folded into the class arm because
    ; the reference has it that way and because checkClassExpression is its second
    ; caller, which is the next slice that reaches this file.
    ;
    ; ★★ SLICE 59's CONTINUATION, and it moves the stop TWO calls on rather than
    ; one: the collision head runs, then checkTypeParameters — which is ported and
    ; whose own report is inside its loop — so a generic class stops at
    ; check-type-parameter and a plain one at checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations.
    ; ★The two reports the head adds here are the class's own: the reserved type
    ; name (`class any {}`) and `class Object {}` under a module format below
    ; ES2015, which is what this harness has.
    procedure check_class_like_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_class_like_declaration(node)
        this.check_decorators(node)
        this.check_collisions_for_declaration_name(node, AstNode.name_of(node))
        this.check_type_parameters(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
        this.check_exports_on_merged_declarations(node)
        ; ★★★ SLICE 66 MOVES THE STOP ONE LINE, AND THE LINE IT MOVES TO IS THE
        ; OTHER 4 500-UNIT HEAD. checkClassLikeDeclaration reads the declared type,
        ; then `typeWithThis`, then `staticType := c.getTypeOfSymbol(symbol)` — and
        ; a class symbol carries SymbolFlagsClass, which is getTypeOfSymbol's
        ; getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule arm. The histogram in CLAUDE.md §3.5dl
        ; measured that arm as reaching ZERO units; it reached zero because the
        ; class stopped one statement earlier, which is the same lesson slice 65
        ; wrote about a tag naming an ORDER.
        let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
        if symbol = null
            return
        let sym symbol as ref[Symbol]
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        let class_type this.get_declared_type_of_symbol(sym)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        if class_type = null
            return
        let type_with_this this.get_type_with_this_argument(class_type as ref[Type], null)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 67 MOVES THE CLASS PAST ITS STATIC SIDE. `staticType :=
        ; c.getTypeOfSymbol(symbol)` is getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule for a class
        ; symbol — the arm §3.5dl's histogram measured as reaching ZERO units,
        ; because the class stopped one statement earlier — and for a class with
        ; no `extends` it is an anonymous object type and nothing else.
        let static_type this.get_type_of_symbol(sym)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        this.check_type_parameter_lists_identical(sym)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 68 MOVES THE CLASS PAST ITS OVERLOAD CHECK. A class symbol
        ; carries SymbolFlagsClass and its declaration list is what
        ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol walks — a class merged with a function
        ; declaration is exactly the shape TS2814 and TS2813 are about, and the
        ; hasNonAmbientClass block is the only one of the worker's four that a class
        ; symbol can reach on its own.
        this.check_function_or_constructor_symbol(sym)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 73 MOVES THE CLASS PAST ITS DUPLICATE-MEMBER CHECK — the head
        ; of the work list on both stages (128 units at stage 1, 2 891 at stage 2).
        ; The class is the only one of the reference's three call sites that passes
        ; checkPrivateNames true, and the only one this port can reach yet.
        this.check_object_type_for_duplicate_declarations(node, true)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        ; ★★★ SLICE 74 CROSSES THE HEAD OF THE WORK LIST FOR THE PRICE OF ONE
        ; GUARD. checkClassForStaticPropertyNameConflicts returns at its own first
        ; line under this harness (see use_define_for_class_fields), so the row
        ; slice 73 left at 145 / 2 799 units is inert — and the two paths that row
        ; stood for collapse into one, because the ambient class and the plain one
        ; now arrive at the same next statement.
        let node_in_ambient_context (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
        if node_in_ambient_context = false
        {
            this.check_class_for_static_property_name_conflicts(node)
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return
        }
        ; ★★★ THE EXTENDS HERITAGE BLOCK IS THE NEXT SLICE AND IT IS REPORTED AS
        ; ONE ROW. Eleven statements sit inside `if baseTypeNode != nil` —
        ; getBaseTypes' CLASS arm (which reports in place today, see its note),
        ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass' isConstructorType stop, the type-argument
        ; constraint loop, checkTypeAssignableTo and
        ; checkKindsOfPropertyMemberOverrides — and not one of them is in reach. The
        ; stop has to be HERE rather than one call further on, because the block
        ; runs BEFORE checkMembersForOverrideModifier: skipping it to reach the
        ; override family would emit that family's diagnostics ahead of the
        ; block's, and diagcheck compares a SUBSEQUENCE, so the order is part of
        ; the answer.
        if Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_element(node) <> null
        {
            this.record_unported("class-extends-heritage-clause", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return
        }
        ; ★★★ SO THE CLASS THIS SLICE CAN FINISH IS THE ONE WITH NO BASE, AND THAT
        ; IS EXACTLY THE CLASS THE OVERRIDE FAMILY HAS SOMETHING TO SAY ABOUT.
        ; checkMemberForOverrideModifier's first branch is the `baseWithThis == nil`
        ; one, and its whole content is TS4112 / TS4121 — *this member cannot have
        ; an override modifier because its containing class does not extend another
        ; class*. The half of that function this port cannot reach is the half a
        ; base type would be needed for, and the caller's stop above is what proves
        ; the reachable half is the only one running.
        this.check_members_for_override_modifier(node, class_type as ref[Type], type_with_this, static_type)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        ; The implements loop. Its body is checkTypeReferenceNode,
        ; getTypeFromTypeNode and issueMemberSpecificError over
        ; checkTypeAssignableTo — the same wall as the extends block, and a separate
        ; row because a class may have either clause without the other.
        let impls Checker.get_implements_heritage_clause_elements(node)
        if impls <> null
        {
            let il impls as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
            if il.get_length() > 0
            {
                this.record_unported("class-implements-heritage-clause", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                return
            }
        }
        ; checkIndexConstraints twice, checkClassOrInterfaceForDuplicateIndexSignatures
        ; and checkPropertyInitialization. The row is the one the interface arm
        ; already stops at (slice 66), so the class joins an existing 1 354 rather
        ; than opening a row of its own.
        this.record_unported("check-index-constraints", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 74a: checkClassForStaticPropertyNameConflicts ──────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HEAD OF THE WORK LIST ON BOTH STAGES, AND IT IS INERT. The whole
    ; function is behind `if c.compilerOptions.GetUseDefineForClassFields() {
    ; return }`, and that answers TRUE under this harness — the option is an unset
    ; Tristate, so the reference falls through to `GetEmitScriptTarget() >=
    ; ScriptTargetES2022`, which is ES2025 >= ES2022. The oracle's own
    ; CompilerOptions is built with two unrelated fields and nothing else
    ; (tests/oracle/types.go), so this is not a divergence between the two sides:
    ; the REFERENCE runs no more of this function than we do.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS BEHIND THE GUARD IS REPORTED RATHER THAN WRITTEN, and the reason
    ; is that an unreachable body cannot be gated (§3.5ao). The loop asks
    ; getEffectivePropertyNameForPropertyNameNode for every static member's name
    ; and reports TS2699 for `name`, `length`, `caller` and `arguments` — the same
    ; message slice 73 already raises for `prototype`, from the other of its two
    ; sites. The computed-name arm of that helper needs tryGetNameFromType over
    ; getTypeOfExpression, so the body is not a five-line port either.
    ;
    ; ★ The row is therefore a claim about the OPTION, and the battery's way of
    ; saying so is to substitute `false` for the field: the report then fires on
    ; every class in the corpus and the tagpin goes red. That is the only
    ; instrument this function has, and it is the honest one.
    procedure check_class_for_static_property_name_conflicts(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if use_define_for_class_fields
            return
        this.record_unported("class-static-property-name-conflicts", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 74b: checkMembersForOverrideModifier ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `baseWithThis` COMPUTATION IS DEAD HERE BY THE CALLER'S STOP, and it
    ; is a report rather than an omission for the reason check_class_declaration's
    ; legacy_decorators branch is: check_class_like_declaration reports at the
    ; extends heritage block, so a class reaching this function has no `extends`
    ; clause and GetExtendsHeritageClauseElement answers nil. The condition is
    ; EVALUATED rather than assumed, so the day the block above becomes a port this
    ; function says what it still owes instead of quietly answering nil.
    ;
    ; ★ baseStaticType is computed even though nothing reads it on the reachable
    ; path: with no base type node getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass answers
    ; undefinedType and CACHES it on the type, which is a side effect the reference
    ; has at this point in the walk.
    procedure check_members_for_override_modifier(this, node: ref[AstNode], t: ref[Type], type_with_this: ref[Type]?, static_type: ref[Type]?)
    {
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        var base_with_this: ref[Type]? null
        if Checker.get_extends_heritage_clause_element(node) <> null
        {
            this.record_unported("override-modifier-base-with-this", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return
        }
        let base_static_type this.get_base_constructor_type_of_class(t)
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return
        let members AstNode.member_list_of(node)
        if members = null
            return
        let ms members as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        let count ms.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let m *(ms.get_buffer() + i)
            set i: i + 1
            if m = null
                continue
            let member m as ref[AstNode]
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsAmbient)
                continue
            ; A parameter property declares a class member without appearing in the
            ; member list, so the constructor's parameters stand in for it — the
            ; same substitution slice 73's walk makes, and with the same reading of
            ; is_parameter_property_declaration through the parameter's own parent.
            if AstNode.kind_of(member) = KindConstructor
            {
                let params AstNode.parameters_of(member)
                if params = null
                    continue
                let ps params as ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
                let pn ps.get_length() as int
                var j 0
                while j < pn
                {
                    let p *(ps.get_buffer() + j)
                    set j: j + 1
                    if p = null
                        continue
                    let param p as ref[AstNode]
                    if b.is_parameter_property_declaration(param) = false
                        continue
                    this.check_member_for_override_modifier(node, static_type, base_static_type, base_with_this, t, type_with_this, param)
                    if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                        return
                }
                continue
            }
            this.check_member_for_override_modifier(node, static_type, base_static_type, base_with_this, t, type_with_this, member)
            if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
                return
        }
    }

    ; ── slice 74c: checkMemberForOverrideModifier ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST BRANCH IS THE WHOLE OF THE REACHABLE FUNCTION, AND IT IS THE
    ; SLICE'S PRODUCT. With `baseWithThis == nil` the reference reports on a member
    ; that carries the `override` modifier and returns on one that does not: TS4112
    ; in a TypeScript file, TS4121 in a JavaScript one, *this member cannot have an
    ; override modifier because its containing class does not extend another
    ; class*.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SPAN IS `c.error(member, …)` AND THAT IS NOT THE MEMBER'S RANGE —
    ; scanner.GetErrorRangeForNode NARROWS a named declaration to its NAME, for a
    ; fixed LIST of kinds whose own comment upstream reads *"This list is a work in
    ; progress"*. PropertyDeclaration, MethodDeclaration, GetAccessor and
    ; SetAccessor are on it, so a class member reports on its name; **KindParameter
    ; is NOT**, so a parameter property reports over its whole text, modifiers
    ; included. Measured on the fixtures: `foo`, `x`, `s`, `a`, `b` — and
    ; `override readonly x: number`. ★That is why control g18, which substitutes
    ; `AstNode.name_of(member)`, goes red on exactly ONE pin file: the two spellings
    ; coincide everywhere the narrowing list already applies, and the parameter
    ; property is the only place they can disagree.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JS ARM IS NOT SPECULATIVE. hasOverrideModifier is
    ; HasSyntacticModifier — not the effective, JSDoc-aware form — so what the JS
    ; arm needs is a `.js` unit whose class member is written with the `override`
    ; keyword. The corpus has them, and the two codes differ, so the `isJs` term is
    ; gateable rather than transcribed on faith.
    ;
    ; ★★ EVERYTHING AFTER THE FIRST BRANCH IS REPORTED. The dynamic-name arm needs
    ; isNonBindableDynamicName, and behind it sit getPropertyOfType,
    ; getSuggestedSymbolForNonexistentClassMember and the whole noImplicitOverride
    ; block — none of which a class with no base can reach, since the reference's own
    ; `!memberHasOverrideModifier && !NoImplicitOverride.IsTrue()` return sends every
    ; modifier-less member home first. The `no_implicit_override` read is that
    ; return, written out so the option has a named reader.
    procedure check_member_for_override_modifier(this, node: ref[AstNode], static_type: ref[Type]?, base_static_type: ref[Type]?, base_with_this: ref[Type]?, t: ref[Type], type_with_this: ref[Type]?, member: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let is_js Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
        let member_has_override_modifier b.has_syntactic_modifier(member, ModifierFlagsOverride)
        if base_with_this = null
        {
            if member_has_override_modifier
            {
                if is_js
                    this.error_on_node(member, DiagThis_member_cannot_have_a_JSDoc_comment_with_an_override_tag_because_its_containing_class_0_does_not_extend_another_class)
                else
                    this.error_on_node(member, DiagThis_member_cannot_have_an_override_modifier_because_its_containing_class_0_does_not_extend_another_class)
            }
            return
        }
        if member_has_override_modifier = false
        {
            if no_implicit_override = false
                return
        }
        this.record_unported("member-override-modifier-base-lookup", AstNode.kind_of(member))
    }

    ; checkClassDeclaration's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MISSING-NAME REPORT IS THE ONLY DIAGNOSTIC THIS ARM ADDS ON ITS OWN,
    ; and it is about `export default class {}` versus a bare `class {}` at
    ; statement position: the second has no name and no `default` modifier, and the
    ; span is the FIRST TOKEN rather than the error range, so the report is
    ; grammar_error_on_first_token.
    ;
    ; ★ `core.Find(node.ModifierNodes(), ast.IsDecorator)` is computed before the
    ; legacy test upstream and its value is only read inside it, so the order here
    ; is the reference's order with the dead computation left where a report can
    ; stand for it.
    procedure check_class_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        ; `c.legacyDecorators && firstDecorator != nil && core.Some(node.Members(),
        ; …)` — the class-decorator-with-a-static-private-member report. The option
        ; is unset under this harness (see check_grammar_modifiers' header), so the
        ; branch is dead; it is REPORTED rather than dropped for the reason the
        ; accessor-pair decorator check one screen down is reported — an options
        ; record arriving later turns it into a port instead of a silent wrong
        ; answer. What it needs besides the option is a class-MEMBERS accessor,
        ; which nothing in this port has a live reader for yet.
        if legacy_decorators
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-class-decorators-static-private", KindClassDeclaration)
            return
        }
        if AstNode.name_of(node) = null
        {
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsDefault) = false
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_class_declaration_without_the_default_modifier_must_have_a_name)
        }
        this.check_class_like_declaration(node)
        ; `c.checkSourceElements(node.Members())` and
        ; `c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(node)` are not reached: the call
        ; above always reports, so the MEMBERS of a class stay behind this arm
        ; rather than joining the histogram one level down the way a namespace
        ; body's statements did in slice 50.
    }

    ; ── slice 59: the collision checks, and one head for four arms ───────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS checkCollisionsForDeclarationName, WHICH IS THE FIRST HEAD
    ; OF THIS CHAPTER THAT FOUR INDEPENDENT ARMS STOP AT: the class-like
    ; declaration (3 300 units), the module declaration (341), the enum
    ; declaration, and checkImportBinding, whose own first line it is and behind
    ; which slice 58's 1 506 + 429 stand. Slice 58's work list put the four at
    ; **5 235** units and named what the head needs — needCollisionCheckForIdentifier,
    ; GetDeclarationContainer, GetModuleInstanceState — all three of which were in
    ; reach, and one thing it called a stop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT ONE WAS NOT A STOP. *checkClassNameCollisionWithObject (the
    ; global symbol, i.e. a stop)* is a claim read off a NAME: the function is two
    ; terms, `name.Text() == "Object"` and `GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile(...) <
    ; ModuleKindES2015`, and it resolves nothing at all. It is ported whole in this
    ; slice, its report is LIVE, and §3.5ci's rule — *a marker whose stated blocker
    ; never existed* — arrives from the other side: there the marker outlived a
    ; blocker that had gone, here the work list invented one that was never there.
    ; A blocker is a claim about a BODY and must be read in the body.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THREE OF THE FIVE SUB-CHECKS ARE PROVABLY INERT AND TWO ARE LIVE, and
    ; which is which is decided by two constants that do NOT agree — see
    ; Checker.language_version and Checker.emit_module_format. The require/exports
    ; check runs (format None is below ES2015); the global-object check runs to its
    ; last line and cannot report (it needs CommonJS); the Promise check returns at
    ; its first line (ES2025 >= ES2017) and the two recordPotentialCollisionWith…
    ; functions register nothing (ES2025 is not <= ES2021). Each of the three inert
    ; ones is ported as its GUARD plus a report — the shape
    ; check_class_declaration's legacy_decorators branch has — because what stands
    ; behind them is real machinery (the binder's HasAsyncFunctions flag, the
    ; nodeLinks check flags, the deferred-diagnostic queue) and a lowered target
    ; must turn into a port rather than into a silent silence.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE HEAD BUYS EACH CALLER IS DIFFERENT, AND ONLY ONE OF THE FOUR IS
    ; A PURE RE-LABELLING. The enum and the class-like arm walk on to
    ; checkTypeParameters / checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations, so their rows move to
    ; tags this column already has; the module arm gains a REPORT of its own, the
    ; TS1540 *use the namespace keyword* advice that sat behind this call since
    ; slice 50; the class-like arm gains two, the reserved type name and
    ; `class Object {}`; and checkImportBinding is one line further on, at
    ; checkAliasSymbol.

    ; ast.GetDeclarationContainer. FindAncestor from the ROOT declaration through
    ; the six kinds that are not containers, and then that node's PARENT — so for
    ; `var x = 1` at the top level of a file the answer is the SourceFile, reached
    ; through the declaration, the declaration list and the statement.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference would panic if the walk ran off the top (`FindAncestor(...)
    ; .Parent` on nil); this answers null, and every caller tests the result for
    ; KindSourceFile, which null is not. It cannot happen either way — a SourceFile
    ; is not one of the six — and the difference is one dereference rather than a
    ; behaviour.
    function get_declaration_container(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var n Binder.get_root_declaration(node)
        while n <> null
        {
            let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
            var transparent false
            if k = KindVariableDeclaration
                set transparent: true
            if k = KindVariableDeclarationList
                set transparent: true
            if k = KindImportSpecifier
                set transparent: true
            if k = KindNamedImports
                set transparent: true
            if k = KindNamespaceImport
                set transparent: true
            if k = KindImportClause
                set transparent: true
            if transparent = false
                return AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            set n: AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
        }
        null
    }

    ; ast.IsTypeOnlyImportDeclaration. Four arms, and the two that read a
    ; GRANDparent are the reason it is not one call to is_type_only_of: an
    ; ImportSpecifier is type-only if it says so ITSELF (`import { type A }`) or if
    ; its clause does (`import type { A }`), and the clause is two hops up through
    ; the NamedImports.
    function is_type_only_import_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindImportSpecifier
        {
            if AstNode.is_type_only_of(n)
                return true
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            if p = null
                return false
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(p))
        }
        if k = KindNamespaceImport
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n))
        if k = KindImportClause
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(n)
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(n)
        false
    }

    ; ast.isTypeOnlyExportDeclaration. The EXPORT half of the disjunction below,
    ; ported although its one reader cannot reach it — need_collision_check_for_identifier
    ; asks the disjunction only for the three IMPORT kinds — because §3.5br's rule
    ; has been paid for six times: an accessor narrowed to the arms its callers are
    ; BELIEVED to reach is the defect that keeps coming back, and the three arms
    ; cost eleven lines.
    ;
    ; ★ The ExportDeclaration arm is the one that is not a plain flag read:
    ; `export type * from "m"` is type-only, `export type { A }` is not — a
    ; specifier list makes the declaration's own flag a statement about its
    ; specifiers rather than about itself.
    function is_type_only_export_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindExportSpecifier
        {
            if AstNode.is_type_only_of(n)
                return true
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            if p = null
                return false
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(p))
        }
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
        {
            if AstNode.is_type_only_of(n) = false
                return false
            if AstNode.module_specifier_of(n) = null
                return false
            return AstNode.export_clause_of(n) = null
        }
        if k = KindNamespaceExport
            return AstNode.is_type_only_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(n))
        false
    }

    function is_type_only_import_or_export_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.is_type_only_import_declaration(n)
            return true
        Checker.is_type_only_export_declaration(n)
    }

    ; needCollisionCheckForIdentifier, whole. The question is *would a name emitted
    ; by the down-level transform collide with this declaration*, so every arm is a
    ; reason the declaration cannot be reached by an unqualified name at runtime: a
    ; MEMBER is always qualified, an AMBIENT declaration emits nothing, a type-only
    ; import erases, and a parameter of a bodiless OVERLOAD is not emitted at all.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE NAME TEST IS THE FIRST TERM AND IT IS THE CHEAP ONE, which is why the
    ; five callers each pass a literal: the function is asked seven times per
    ; declaration name (require, exports, Object, Promise, WeakMap, WeakSet,
    ; Reflect) and six of those are answered by a length comparison.
    ;
    ; ★ `identifier.Text()` upstream answers for a string literal too, while
    ; identifier_text_equals reads the Identifier payload and answers false for
    ; anything else. No caller can pass a non-identifier — the module arm asks
    ; IsIdentifier of the name before it calls the head, and a class, an enum and an
    ; import binding all take an identifier or nothing — so the two agree
    ; everywhere they are asked, and this note is what stands in for the arm that
    ; would otherwise be written for a name that cannot arrive.
    function need_collision_check_for_identifier(node: ref[AstNode], identifier: ref[AstNode]?, name: pointer[const_char], name_len: int) returns bool
    {
        if identifier <> null
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(identifier, name, name_len) = false
                return false
        }
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return false
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return false
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return false
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return false
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return false
        var is_import_form false
        if k = KindImportClause
            set is_import_form: true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            set is_import_form: true
        if k = KindImportSpecifier
            set is_import_form: true
        if is_import_form
        {
            if Checker.is_type_only_import_or_export_declaration(node)
                return false
        }
        let root Binder.get_root_declaration(node)
        if AstNode.kind_of(root) = KindParameter
        {
            if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(root)))
                return false
        }
        true
    }

    ; checkCollisionWithRequireExportsInGeneratedCode, whole — and it is the LIVE
    ; one of the three "generated code" checks, because the emit format under this
    ; harness is ModuleKindNone and None is below ES2015.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REPORT IS ABOUT THE TOP LEVEL OF A MODULE AND NOTHING ELSE. Both
    ; conditions on the container say so: it must be the SourceFile (so a name
    ; inside a function or a namespace is safe) and the file must be a module (a
    ; script emits no `require`/`exports` of its own). So `export {}; var require =
    ; 1` reports twice over — once for each of the two spellings the down-level
    ; module transform reserves — and the same declaration in a plain script
    ; reports nothing.
    ;
    ; ★ The uninstantiated-module guard is the reference's own comment: `declare
    ; namespace N {}` whose body declares only types emits no object, so its name
    ; cannot collide. It is asked of the NODE rather than of the container, which is
    ; why it reads GetModuleInstanceState and not the ambient flag.
    ;
    ; ★ errorSkippedOnNoEmit is `c.error` plus a FLAG on the diagnostic, and the
    ; flag is invisible to this yardstick: the dump is `C pos end code`, and the
    ; flag is read by the program when deciding which diagnostics survive
    ; `--noEmit`. So error_on_node is the whole of it here, and the difference is
    ; named rather than modelled.
    procedure check_collision_with_require_exports_in_generated_code(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if emit_module_format >= ModuleKindES2015
            return
        if name = null
            return
        var wanted false
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "require", 7)
            set wanted: true
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "exports", 7)
            set wanted: true
        if wanted = false
            return
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if b.get_module_instance_state(node) <> ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                return
        }
        let parent Checker.get_declaration_container(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindSourceFile
            return
        if b.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
            return
        this.error_on_node(name, DiagDuplicate_identifier_0_Compiler_reserves_name_1_in_top_level_scope_of_a_module)
    }

    ; checkCollisionWithGlobalObjectInGeneratedCode, whole. It runs to its last
    ; line on every non-class declaration named `Object` and cannot report: the
    ; last term is `format == ModuleKindCommonJS` and the format is None.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS PORTED RATHER THAN REPORTED, which is the opposite decision to the
    ; three inert checks around it, and the difference is what stands behind the
    ; last term: nothing. Every step of this function is ported machinery, so a
    ; CommonJS format arriving later needs no code — while the Promise check would
    ; need a binder flag this port does not set and the two record… functions a
    ; deferred queue that is empty by construction. A report claims *unmeasured*;
    ; this function is measured and answers nothing.
    ;
    ; ★ The IsClassLike exclusion is this check's own and does not appear in the
    ; require/exports one above: a class named `Object` is the business of
    ; checkClassNameCollisionWithObject, which the head calls through the class-like
    ; branch and which has its own message.
    procedure check_collision_with_global_object_in_generated_code(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        if Parser.is_class_like_node(node)
            return
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "Object", 6) = false
            return
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if b.get_module_instance_state(node) <> ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                return
        }
        let parent Checker.get_declaration_container(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindSourceFile
            return
        if b.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
            return
        if emit_module_format <> ModuleKindCommonJS
            return
        this.error_on_node(name, DiagDuplicate_identifier_0_Compiler_reserves_name_1_in_top_level_scope_of_a_module)
    }

    ; checkCollisionWithGlobalPromiseInGeneratedCode's GUARD, which is the whole of
    ; what is portable here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT STANDS BEHIND IT IS A DEFERRED FIELD MEETING ITS FIRST READER AND
    ; STILL NOT BECOMING OBSERVABLE — §3.5as from the other side. The report needs
    ; `parent.Flags & NodeFlagsHasAsyncFunctions`, a SourceFile node flag the
    ; binder deliberately does not set (bind_function_declaration and
    ; bind_function_expression each say so, with the argument *no dump here can see
    ; it*). This is the reader those notes asked for — and it sits behind a guard
    ; that ES2025 closes, so the flag stays unobservable and the two binder notes
    ; stay true. That is worth writing down precisely because it looks like the
    ; moment they expire.
    procedure check_collision_with_global_promise_in_generated_code(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        if language_version >= ScriptTargetES2017
            return
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "Promise", 7) = false
            return
        this.record_unported("check-collision-with-global-promise", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; recordPotentialCollisionWithWeakMapSetInGeneratedCode's GUARD. Behind it:
    ; addDeferredDiagnostic of checkWeakMapSetCollision, which reads
    ; NodeCheckFlagsContainsClassWithPrivateIdentifiers off the enclosing
    ; block-scope container's nodeLinks — a table this port does not have, filled
    ; by setNodeLinksForPrivateIdentifierScope, which nothing calls yet.
    ;
    ; ★ TWO NAMES, ONE CALL SITE: the reference asks needCollisionCheckForIdentifier
    ; for `WeakMap` and `WeakSet` in one disjunction, so the down-level private-name
    ; transform's two reserved names are one condition.
    procedure record_potential_collision_with_weak_map_set_in_generated_code(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if language_version > ScriptTargetES2021
            return
        var wanted false
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "WeakMap", 7)
            set wanted: true
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "WeakSet", 7)
            set wanted: true
        if wanted = false
            return
        this.record_unported("check-weak-map-set-collision", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; recordPotentialCollisionWithReflectInGeneratedCode's GUARD. Behind it,
    ; checkReflectCollision, which reads
    ; NodeCheckFlagsContainsSuperPropertyInStaticInitializer off a class
    ; expression's MEMBERS or off a function expression itself — the same nodeLinks
    ; table as the WeakMap check, filled by a marker this port does not set.
    ;
    ; ★ The null test on the name is INSIDE the conjunction upstream rather than in
    ; front of it, and it is kept in that position because the guard's order is what
    ; a control substitutes; need_collision_check_for_identifier treats a null
    ; identifier as *any name*, which is exactly the reading a missing test would
    ; give it.
    procedure record_potential_collision_with_reflect_in_generated_code(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        if language_version > ScriptTargetES2021
            return
        if Checker.need_collision_check_for_identifier(node, name, "Reflect", 7) = false
            return
        this.record_unported("check-reflect-collision", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkClassNameCollisionWithObject, whole — the two-term function slice 58's
    ; work list called a stop.
    ;
    ; ★★ ITS REPORT IS LIVE AND IT IS THE FORMAT THAT MAKES IT SO: `class Object
    ; {}` reports TS2725 here because the stub program answers ModuleKindNone,
    ; which is below ES2015. Under the option-derived ES2022 the same source would
    ; be silent — see Checker.emit_module_format, where the two flips of this one
    ; constant are set out.
    ;
    ; ★ The message names the module kind (`… with module '{0}'`) and the argument
    ; is c.moduleKind.String(), i.e. the DERIVED ES2022 rather than the file's
    ; format. Arguments are not computed here (the dump is code and span), so the
    ; two values meeting in one report is a curiosity rather than a decision — but
    ; it is the reason the field and the option are kept apart.
    procedure check_class_name_collision_with_object(this, name: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "Object", 6) = false
            return
        if emit_module_format >= ModuleKindES2015
            return
        this.error_on_node(name, DiagClass_name_cannot_be_Object_when_targeting_ES5_and_above_with_module_0)
    }

    ; checkCollisionsForDeclarationName, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE NULL TEST IS THE FUNCTION'S FIRST LINE AND IT IS NOT REDUNDANT: three
    ; of the four callers pass `node.Name()` of a node that need not have one —
    ; `export default class {}` has no name, and so has the `class {}` whose own
    ; missing-name report check_class_declaration makes one call earlier.
    ;
    ; ★ The two branches at the bottom are `IsClassLike` and `IsEnumDeclaration`,
    ; which is the whole of the reference's `else if` chain — a module declaration
    ; and an import binding fall through both and are done with the five checks
    ; above.
    procedure check_collisions_for_declaration_name(this, node: ref[AstNode], name: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if name = null
            return
        this.check_collision_with_require_exports_in_generated_code(node, name)
        this.check_collision_with_global_object_in_generated_code(node, name)
        this.check_collision_with_global_promise_in_generated_code(node, name)
        this.record_potential_collision_with_weak_map_set_in_generated_code(node, name)
        this.record_potential_collision_with_reflect_in_generated_code(node, name)
        if Parser.is_class_like_node(node)
        {
            this.check_type_name_is_reserved(name, DiagClass_name_cannot_be_0)
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                this.check_class_name_collision_with_object(name)
            return
        }
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindEnumDeclaration
            this.check_type_name_is_reserved(name, DiagEnum_name_cannot_be_0)
    }

    ; checkImportBinding — the head's fourth caller, and one line long here.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE NODE IS THE BINDING AND NOT THE DECLARATION, which is what makes the
    ; collision checks above ask their kind questions of an ImportClause, a
    ; NamespaceImport or an ImportSpecifier: `import a from "m"` passes the CLAUSE,
    ; `import * as ns from "m"` the namespace import, `import { a } from "m"` each
    ; specifier. Those three kinds are exactly the type-only arm of
    ; need_collision_check_for_identifier, which is why that arm is live code rather
    ; than transcription.
    ;
    ; ★ checkAliasSymbol is the stop: it resolves the alias target and compares its
    ; meaning against the local declaration space, i.e. the symbol table. Behind it
    ; stand checkModuleExportName (the first reading that could tell ES2022 from
    ; None — see Checker.module_kind) and the ImportDefault emit helper, which the
    ; format kills.
    procedure check_import_binding(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_collisions_for_declaration_name(node, AstNode.name_of(node))
        this.record_unported("check-alias-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 60: the type parameter, the head slice 52 stubbed ───────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HEAD FOUR ARMS ALREADY CALL, PORTED FROM ITS REPORT INWARDS.
    ; checkTypeParameters was slice 52's boundary and has been the largest
    ; actionable row of the unported column ever since (1 115 at stage 2, read at
    ; KindTypeParameter). Its loop body is four steps, and the fork that decides the
    ; slice is that only ONE of them is the type dimension: checkTypeParameter's
    ; getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter. The default-ordering report, the duplicate
    ; report and the grammar half of checkTypeParameter are all reachable without a
    ; type, and two of them need no symbol table either.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DUPLICATE-NAME LOOP IS PORTED WHOLE AND IT IS THE FIRST CHECK IN THIS
    ; FILE THAT COMPARES TWO SYMBOLS. It reads `typeParameterDeclarations[j].Symbol()
    ; == node.Symbol()` — `Node.Symbol()`, the slot the BINDER wrote, NOT
    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration — so it needs neither the merge nor the late-bound
    ; lookup that gate every other symbol question in this file. That distinction is
    ; the whole reason the report is live: the same line spelled
    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration two functions away is this slice's stop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `for j := range i` REPORTS ONCE PER MATCHING PREDECESSOR, NOT ONCE PER
    ; DUPLICATE, and the first reading of that written here was WRONG. `<T, T, T>`
    ; produces THREE diagnostics — one on the second T (j=0) and TWO on the third
    ; (j=0 and j=1), both at the same span — not two. The inner loop has no break
    ; and the outer one no memory, so the count is the number of PAIRS. Measured
    ; against the reference on `checker_type_parameter_duplicate.ts`, which is why
    ; that fixture carries three parameters rather than two: at two, both readings
    ; give the same answer.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO REPORTS OF THIS LOOP BODY HAVE DIFFERENT SPANS, and a fixture
    ; without a modifier or a constraint cannot tell them apart. The ordering report
    ; spans the whole PARAMETER (`c.error(node, …)`) — `in out U` in
    ; `interface I<T = string, in out U>`, modifiers included — while the duplicate
    ; report spans the NAME alone (`c.error(node.Name(), …)`) — the bare `V` of
    ; `V extends number`. Both halves are pinned by
    ; `checker_type_parameter_spans.ts`.
    ;
    ; ★ seenDefault is a STICKY flag over the whole list and the report fires on a
    ; parameter with NO default that FOLLOWS one — `<T = string, U>` reports on `U`.
    ; §3.5k's rule with nothing to translate: the reference's own local, in the
    ; reference's own scope.
    procedure check_type_parameters(this, type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)
    {
        if type_parameters = null
            return
        let count AstNode.list_count(type_parameters)
        var seen_default false
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let node AstNode.child_in_list(type_parameters, i)
            this.check_type_parameter(node)
            let default_type_node AstNode.type_parameter_default_of(node)
            if default_type_node <> null
            {
                set seen_default: true
                this.check_type_parameters_not_referenced(default_type_node, type_parameters, i)
            }
            else
            {
                if seen_default
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagRequired_type_parameters_may_not_follow_optional_type_parameters)
            }
            var j 0
            while j < i
            {
                if AstNode.symbol_of(AstNode.child_in_list(type_parameters, j)) = AstNode.symbol_of(node)
                    this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagDuplicate_identifier_0)
                set j: j + 1
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkTypeParametersNotReferenced, ported as its WALK with the resolution as
    ; the report — which is the only shape that says something true about a corpus
    ; where most defaults contain no type reference at all.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FUNCTION IS A FULL CHILD WALK WHOSE BODY DOES NOTHING EXCEPT AT A
    ; TypeReference, so porting it as one report on entry would have claimed a hole
    ; for `<T = string>`, `<T = 1>` and `<T = keyof any>` — none of which reaches
    ; getTypeFromTypeReference at all. §3.5ag's rule (compute WHETHER, not WHICH)
    ; read one level up: the walk decides whether the resolution is ASKED, and the
    ; walk is free.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REFERENCE'S `visit` RETURNS `node.ForEachChild(visit)` AND THAT VALUE
    ; IS ALWAYS false, so the walk is exhaustive and the early-exit machinery is
    ; dead: a leaf's ForEachChild answers false, and no arm of `visit` returns true
    ; on its own. Ported as a plain recursion rather than as a bool-returning one,
    ; because a return value nothing can be is a shape the next reader has to
    ; disprove.
    ;
    ; ★ The `for i := index; i < len(typeParameters); i++` comparison against
    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration is BELOW the report — that is the loop the tag names,
    ; and it is the merged symbol rather than the binder's slot, unlike the
    ; duplicate-name loop above.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REPORT CANNOT BE FIRST EITHER, AND FOR A STRUCTURAL REASON WORTH
    ; STATING: checkSourceElement(DefaultType) runs BEFORE this call, and every type
    ; kind that can CONTAIN a type reference has an arm in the reference's switch
    ; that this port does not implement — the twelve kinds with no arm at all are the
    ; eleven keyword types and LiteralType, none of which can hold one. So the
    ; default's own walk reports before this one is entered, on every input. ★That is
    ; a claim about the whole corpus and it is measured rather than argued: slice 60's
    ; g17 replaces this report with a diagnostic and finds **one unit / two
    ; diagnostics** on stage 1, and g18 inverts the kind test to reach **10 units**,
    ; which is the only row that gates the RECURSION at all.
    procedure check_type_parameters_not_referenced(this, root: ref[AstNode]?, type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], index: int)
    {
        if root = null
            return
        if AstNode.kind_of(root) = KindTypeReference
            this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-reference", KindTypeReference)
        let count AstNode.child_count(root)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            this.check_type_parameters_not_referenced(AstNode.child_at(root, i), type_parameters, index)
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkTypeParameter, whose four ported lines are its grammar half and whose
    ; stop is its fifth.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE STOP IS UNCONDITIONAL, AND THAT IS THE FIRST OF THREE INDEPENDENT
    ; REASONS THE LAST LINE IS UNOBSERVABLE — the other two were found by the
    ; battery, one after the other, each refuting the row that had just been written.
    ; getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter(getSymbolOfDeclaration(node)) runs for every
    ; type parameter there is, so this arm ALWAYS records and the deferred
    ; registration below it can never be the first report. **(2)** Remove that stop
    ; and it is still not first: the drain runs after every declaration, and all
    ; three container kinds the deferred guard admits report from their own arms —
    ; which is the sentence check_unused_renamed_binding_elements already carries,
    ; *a deferred element does not remove a report from the unit, it hands the unit
    ; to its container's*. **(3)** And the strongest one: `check_source_file` returns
    ; as soon as any statement has reported, and `check_deferred_nodes` sits BELOW
    ; that return — so for a type parameter the drain does not report late, it never
    ; RUNS. The deferred arm is ported anyway, for the reason check_deferred_nodes
    ; was left in place empty in the first place (the arm that first defers has to
    ; find the drain there) and for §3.5cz's f14 reason (not emitting it would claim
    ; the drain is ported). ★Its cost is held to a number rather than to an
    ; argument: slice 60's g14 lifts reason (3) and probes the arm with a
    ; diagnostic — 30 units of stage 1 — g15 opens the guard and reaches 61, and g16
    ; removes the registration and returns to the baseline. ★The successor is named:
    ; lifting that early return for real is a slice of its own, and the reference has
    ; no such exit.
    ;
    ; ★★ checkTypeNameIsReserved IS BELOW THE STOP AND IS PORTED, which is this
    ; file's convention since slice 52 and is sound for the reason it always is:
    ; record_unported MARKS the unit and does not RETURN, and diagcheck's relation
    ; is a SUBSEQUENCE — so skipping the three type reports between the stop and
    ; this one leaves the order intact. `type never<T> {}` and `interface I<any> {}`
    ; therefore report TS2368 today.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO checkSourceElement CALLS ARE WHERE THIS SLICE'S HISTOGRAM GOES.
    ; A constraint or a default that is a KEYWORD type has no arm at all
    ; (kind_has_no_check_arm), so `<T extends string>` walks silently; one that is a
    ; TypeReference or a TypeLiteral lands in the two rows the work list already
    ; names. That is slice 50's *resolution moving one level down* for the third
    ; time.
    ;
    ; ★ The Expression slot is the parser's recovery for `<T extends 1>` — exactly
    ; one of constraint and expression is ever set — and its report is the FIRST
    ; token of the expression, not the whole of it.
    procedure check_type_parameter(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        let expr AstNode.type_parameter_expression_of(node)
        if expr <> null
            this.grammar_error_on_first_token(expr, DiagType_expected)
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.type_parameter_constraint_of(node))
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.type_parameter_default_of(node))

        ; typeParameter := c.getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter(c.getSymbolOfDeclaration(node))
        ; and the three checks that read it: getBaseConstraintOfType (forced for its
        ; circularity errors), the circular-default report TS2716, and the
        ; constraint/default assignability TS2344 through checkTypeAssignableTo.
        ;
        ; ★★★ SLICE 65 RE-TAGGED IT AND MOVED NOTHING; SLICE 66 ANSWERS IT AND
        ; MOVES ONE LINE, and the two facts belong to the same row.
        ; getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter is FOUR lines — it mints a type parameter
        ; and memoises it — so the 1 317 units this row carried were never waiting
        ; on code, they were waiting on the Type payload that slice 66 brings. What
        ; stops the arm now is the FIRST of the three checks that READ the type:
        ; getBaseConstraintOfType, which the reference calls purely to force the
        ; circularity errors out.
        ;
        ; ★ Asking for the type before reporting is what makes the ported function
        ; REACHED — the other caller (appendTypeParameters) is a loop that cannot
        ; run while every generic declaration stops here, so without this line the
        ; four lines would be dead code claiming to be a port (§3.5be).
        let tp_symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
        if tp_symbol <> null
            let tp this.get_declared_type_of_type_parameter(tp_symbol as ref[Symbol])
        this.record_unported("get-base-constraint-of-type", KindTypeParameter)

        this.check_type_name_is_reserved(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagType_parameter_name_cannot_be_0)
        this.check_node_deferred(node)
    }

    ; checkTypeParameterDeferred, ported as its GUARD.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE GUARD IS A PURE KIND TEST ON THE PARENT AND IT CLOSES THREE QUARTERS
    ; OF THE FUNCTION. A type parameter of a function, a method, a signature or a
    ; constructor type falls through every arm and the deferred check is a NO-OP for
    ; it — not a hole, the reference's own answer — so only an interface, a
    ; class-like or a type alias owes the variance machinery
    ; (getTypeParameterModifiers, createMarkerType, checkTypeAssignableTo), and that
    ; is what the report stands for.
    ;
    ; ★★ THIS FUNCTION DOES NOT EXECUTE TODAY — see check_type_parameter's stop for
    ; the three reasons and for the rows that price them. It is written anyway rather
    ; than left as a comment, because the alternative is a drain that silently
    ; answers nothing for a kind it was handed, which is the one shape §3.5ao says to
    ; avoid; and the guard is what a control can still measure, since g14 and g15
    ; differ by exactly it (30 units against 61).
    procedure check_type_parameter_deferred(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var owes false
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            set owes: true
        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
            set owes: true
        if AstNode.is_type_or_js_type_alias_declaration(parent)
            set owes: true
        if owes = false
            return
        this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-type-parameter", KindTypeParameter)
    }


    ; ── slice 61: the type-node arms of checkSourceElement ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CHAPTER SLICE 60's TWO RECURSIONS OPENED. checkTypeParameter hands a
    ; constraint and a default to checkSourceElement, and checkSignatureDeclaration
    ; has been handing it a return-type annotation since slice 52 — so the switch's
    ; TYPE half has been reached for nine slices with not one arm written, and its
    ; four biggest rows (`check` at TypeLiteral, TypeReference, UnionType and
    ; ArrayType) stood at 941 units of stage 2 on slice 60's work list. This slice
    ; fills the half: every `case` of checkSourceElementWorker whose node is a TYPE
    ; node, plus the five signature kinds whose case is a call to a function this
    ; port already has.
    ;
    ; ★★★ MOST OF THE CHAPTER IS RECURSION, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS ONE SLICE RATHER
    ; THAN NINETEEN. SIXTEEN of the nineteen cases walk into children — only
    ; ThisType, TypeQuery and TypePredicate do not — and FOUR of the sixteen do
    ; nothing else at all (ArrayType, the Parenthesized/Optional/Rest trio,
    ; TypeOperator and ConditionalType are complete on arrival). The type system
    ; stands behind exactly one line of each of the rest. Porting one arm and
    ; leaving its neighbour would stop a walk in the middle: `(T)[]` needs ArrayType
    ; AND ParenthesizedType, `keyof T[]` needs TypeOperator AND ArrayType, and a
    ; TypeLiteral's members are index signatures and call signatures. The unit of
    ; work here is the CLOSURE of the walk, not the function.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE ARM THAT COSTS NOTHING IS THE ONE WITH THE LARGEST YIELD PER LINE:
    ; `case ConstructorType, FunctionType, CallSignature, ConstructSignature,
    ; IndexSignature: c.checkSignatureDeclaration(node)` is FIVE kinds routed to a
    ; function slice 52 wrote whole. Nothing was missing but the wiring, and the
    ; wiring was missing because no walk reached those kinds until this one.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SEVEN STOPS ARE ONE DIMENSION UNDER SEVEN NAMES, and they are tagged
    ; per CALL rather than folded, because that is what the reference writes:
    ; checkThisType calls getTypeFromThisTypeNode, checkTypeQuery calls
    ; getTypeFromTypeQueryNode, checkIndexedAccessType calls
    ; getTypeFromIndexedAccessTypeNode, checkTypeLiteral calls
    ; getTypeFromTypeLiteralOrFunctionOrConstructorTypeNode, checkMappedType calls
    ; getTypeFromMappedTypeNode, and the rest call getTypeFromTypeNode. A single
    ; folded tag would read as one function to port and it is a table with a switch
    ; in front of it; seven rows say which entries the corpus actually reaches.

    ; ForEachChild(c.checkSourceElement) — the body of four arms
    ; (ParenthesizedType/OptionalType/RestType, ConditionalType, IndexedAccessType's
    ; first line and checkJSDocType's second), written once. §3.9d's index pair.
    procedure check_each_child_source_element(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let count AstNode.child_count(node)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            this.check_source_element(AstNode.child_at(node, i))
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkArrayType, whole — one line, and one of the four arms this slice brings
    ; that are COMPLETE on arrival with nothing written down beside them (the others
    ; are the Parenthesized/Optional/Rest trio, TypeOperator and ConditionalType).
    ; Before slice 61 this file had none.
    procedure check_array_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.array_element_type_of(node))

    ; checkUnionOrIntersectionType, whole but for its last line.
    procedure check_union_or_intersection_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkThisType, whole: one call, and the whole of it is the stop.
    procedure check_this_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-this-type-node", KindThisType)

    ; checkTypeQuery, whole, and likewise entirely its stop. ★getTypeFromTypeQueryNode
    ; resolves the entity name to a symbol and asks for its type, so this row is the
    ; symbol table and the type table at once — which is why it is not folded into
    ; the getTypeFromTypeNode row even though that switch would reach it.
    procedure check_type_query(this, node: ref[AstNode])
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-query-node", KindTypeQuery)

    ; checkConditionalType, whole — `node.ForEachChild(c.checkSourceElement)` and
    ; nothing else. ★It is the arm that makes an INFER type reachable: `T extends
    ; infer U ? U : never` walks into the extendsType, which is where the only legal
    ; `infer` lives.
    procedure check_conditional_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
        this.check_each_child_source_element(node)

    ; checkIndexedAccessType, whose second line is checkIndexedAccessIndexType over
    ; getTypeFromIndexedAccessTypeNode — the index constraint report TS2537 and the
    ; whole of the indexed-access relation.
    procedure check_indexed_access_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-indexed-access-type-node", KindIndexedAccessType)
    }

    ; checkTypeOperator, whole — its grammar check and one recursion, with nothing
    ; behind it. ★The recursion is `node.Type()`, i.e. the operand alone; a
    ; TypeOperatorNode has no other child, so this and a ForEachChild walk coincide
    ; and the reference's spelling is kept.
    procedure check_type_operator(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_type_operator_node(node)
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.type_operator_inner_of(node))
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeOperatorNode, whole — and it is the READER that closes parser
    ; slice 7's faithfulness note (1). Its entire body is a switch on the OPERATOR,
    ; a field this port did not store until this slice; a `keyof` falls through both
    ; arms and reports nothing.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `unique` ARM IS FIVE REPORTS AND EVERY ONE OF THEM IS SYNTACTIC —
    ; where the operand is not the `symbol` keyword, and then which DECLARATION the
    ; operator sits in. That is why the whole function is portable in a slice that
    ; owns no type: `unique symbol` is a claim about a declaration's shape, checked
    ; against modifiers and against the parent chain, and the reference asks for a
    ; type nowhere in it.
    ;
    ; ★★ WalkUpParenthesizedTypes CLIMBS THE PARENT CHAIN, and reading it as the
    ; descent through a parenthesized type's INNER slot is the mistake its name
    ; invites: `(unique symbol)` inside a variable declaration has to find the
    ; DECLARATION, so the walk goes up while the node it is standing on is a
    ; parenthesized type. It is spelled out here rather than given an accessor of
    ; its own because it has one caller and the loop is three lines.
    ;
    ; ★ THE SPANS ARE THREE, NOT ONE, and they do not follow the arms: the
    ; wrong-operand report is on the OPERAND, the binding-name report and the
    ; default `not allowed here` are on the OPERATOR NODE, and the const and the two
    ; property reports are on the enclosing declaration's NAME. That is what
    ; checker_type_operator_unique.ts exists to pin, and g09 is the row that moves
    ; one of them into another's place.
    function check_grammar_type_operator_node(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let op AstNode.type_operator_operator_of(node)
        if op = KindUniqueKeyword
        {
            let inner_type AstNode.type_operator_inner_of(node)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(inner_type) <> KindSymbolKeyword
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(inner_type, DiagX_0_expected)
            var parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            while Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindParenthesizedType
                set parent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
            let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
            if pk = KindVariableDeclaration
            {
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(parent)) <> KindIdentifier
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_unique_symbol_types_may_not_be_used_on_a_variable_declaration_with_a_binding_name)
                if Checker.is_variable_declaration_in_variable_statement(parent) = false
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_unique_symbol_types_are_only_allowed_on_variables_in_a_variable_statement)
                if (AstNode.flags_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)) & NodeFlagsConst) = 0
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parent), DiagA_variable_whose_type_is_a_unique_symbol_type_must_be_const)
                return false
            }
            if pk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            {
                if b.is_static(parent) = false
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parent), DiagA_property_of_a_class_whose_type_is_a_unique_symbol_type_must_be_both_static_and_readonly)
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(parent, ModifierFlagsReadonly) = false
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parent), DiagA_property_of_a_class_whose_type_is_a_unique_symbol_type_must_be_both_static_and_readonly)
                return false
            }
            if pk = KindPropertySignature
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(parent, ModifierFlagsReadonly) = false
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parent), DiagA_property_of_an_interface_or_type_literal_whose_type_is_a_unique_symbol_type_must_be_readonly)
                return false
            }
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_unique_symbol_types_are_not_allowed_here)
        }
        if op = KindReadonlyKeyword
        {
            let inner_type AstNode.type_operator_inner_of(node)
            let ik Checker.kind_or_unknown(inner_type)
            if ik = KindArrayType
                return false
            if ik = KindTupleType
                return false
            return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagX_readonly_type_modifier_is_only_permitted_on_array_and_tuple_literal_types)
        }
        false
    }

    ; isVariableDeclarationInVariableStatement, whole — `IsVariableDeclarationList(
    ; node.Parent) && IsVariableStatement(node.Parent.Parent)`. A declaration of a
    ; `for (const x of …)` head has a list parent and a FOR parent above it, which
    ; is the case the predicate exists to exclude.
    function is_variable_declaration_in_variable_statement(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindVariableDeclarationList
            return false
        Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)) = KindVariableStatement
    }

    ; checkTypeLiteral, whose first line is the whole of this arm's yield and whose
    ; other three are the object type.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MEMBERS WALK IS WHAT MAKES FIVE SIGNATURE KINDS AND TWO MEMBER KINDS
    ; REACHABLE AT ONCE, and two of them were already ported and unreached: an index
    ; signature's checkGrammarIndexSignature report (slice 52 wrote *no arm of this
    ; slice can reach it*) and a call signature's whole checkSignatureDeclaration.
    ; A property or method signature has no arm here yet and reports its own kind.
    procedure check_type_literal(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.member_list_of(node))

        ; t := c.getTypeFromTypeLiteralOrFunctionOrConstructorTypeNode(node), and the
        ; three checks that read it: checkIndexConstraints, the duplicate index
        ; signature report and checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations. The last two
        ; are syntactic in their own bodies but both are entered with `t.symbol`
        ; and the anonymous type is what this port cannot build.
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-literal-node", KindTypeLiteral)
    }

    ; checkTypeReferenceNode, whose three ported steps are its grammar check, the
    ; JSDoc-dot report and the type-argument walk.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JSDoc-DOT REPORT IS THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT RE-SCANS FOR A
    ; TOKEN KIND. `Foo.<T>` is the JSDoc spelling of a type reference, and the
    ; reference recognises it by the GAP: the type name ends before the type
    ; arguments begin, and the token in the gap is scanned to see whether it is a
    ; dot. Anything else in that gap (a comment, a stray token from a recovered
    ; parse) leaves the branch untaken.
    ;
    ; ★ `IsTypeReferenceNode(node) && node.Flags&NodeFlagsJSDoc == 0` is kept whole
    ; although the kind half is TRIVIALLY TRUE on the one route that reaches this
    ; function today — the switch's TypeReference case. It is transcribed rather
    ; than folded away because the reference's own reason for it is a second caller
    ; this port does not have yet (an ExpressionWithTypeArguments reaching
    ; checkTypeReferenceOrImport by another route), and a test dropped because it
    ; happens to hold is a test nobody puts back. The FLAGS half is live: a
    ; reparsed JSDoc type reference carries NodeFlagsJSDoc and must not be told
    ; that JSDoc types belong in comments.
    procedure check_type_reference_node(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_type_arguments(AstNode.type_arguments_of(node))
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindTypeReference
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsJSDoc) = 0
            {
                let type_arguments AstNode.type_reference_arguments_of(node)
                if type_arguments <> null
                {
                    let name_end AstNode.end_of(AstNode.type_reference_name_of(node))
                    var lpos 0
                    var lend 0
                    if this.list_range_of(type_arguments, &lpos, &lend)
                    {
                        if name_end <> lpos
                        {
                            if b.token_kind_at_position(name_end) = KindDotToken
                                this.grammar_error_at_pos(b.skip_trivia_at(name_end), 1, DiagJSDoc_types_can_only_be_used_inside_documentation_comments)
                        }
                    }
                    else
                        this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
                }
            }
        }
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.type_arguments_of(node))
        this.check_type_reference_or_import(node)
    }

    ; checkTypeReferenceOrImport, ported as its first line's stop. Behind it stand
    ; the type-argument constraint check and the deprecation suggestion, both of
    ; which read the resolved type.
    procedure check_type_reference_or_import(this, node: ref[AstNode])
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", AstNode.kind_of(node))

    ; checkImportType, whose first line is a recursion into the module specifier —
    ; a LiteralType, which has no arm at all, so the walk is silent — and whose
    ; other two are stops.
    procedure check_import_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.import_type_argument_of(node))
        if AstNode.import_type_attributes_of(node) <> null
        {
            ; getResolutionModeOverride with reportErrors — the `with { "resolution-mode":
            ; "import" }` clause validated against the module kind. checkImportAttributes
            ; reports the same dimension from the declaration side.
            this.record_unported("get-resolution-mode-override", KindImportType)
        }
        this.check_type_reference_or_import(node)
    }

    ; checkNamedTupleMember, whole but for its last line. Its three grammar reports
    ; are pure shape questions about the member's own tokens.
    ;
    ; ★ The first reports on the MEMBER and the other two on its TYPE, which is the
    ; distinction `[...a?: T]` against `[a: ...T]` needs: the optional-and-rest
    ; report is about the member's two tokens, and the other two are about a type
    ; that carries a marker belonging on the name.
    procedure check_named_tuple_member(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node) <> null
        {
            if AstNode.question_token_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_tuple_member_cannot_be_both_optional_and_rest)
        }
        let tk Checker.kind_or_unknown(type_node)
        if tk = KindOptionalType
            this.grammar_error_on_node(type_node, DiagA_labeled_tuple_element_is_declared_as_optional_with_a_question_mark_after_the_name_and_before_the_colon_rather_than_after_the_type)
        if tk = KindRestType
            this.grammar_error_on_node(type_node, DiagA_labeled_tuple_element_is_declared_as_rest_with_a_before_the_name_rather_than_before_the_type)
        this.check_source_element(type_node)
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", KindNamedTupleMember)
    }

    ; getArrayElementTypeNode, whole. It is a SYNTACTIC question — *does this type
    ; node spell an array* — and answering it needs no type, which is what lets
    ; getTupleElementFlags tell a `...T[]` REST element from a `...T` VARIADIC one
    ; in a slice that owns no type system.
    function array_element_type_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k Checker.kind_or_unknown(n)
        if k = KindParenthesizedType
            return Checker.array_element_type_node_of(AstNode.parenthesized_type_inner_of(n))
        if k = KindArrayType
            return AstNode.array_element_type_of(n)
        if k = KindTupleType
        {
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(n)
            if AstNode.list_count(elements) <> 1
                return null
            let e AstNode.child_in_list(elements, 0)
            let ek AstNode.kind_of(e)
            if ek = KindRestType
                return Checker.array_element_type_node_of(AstNode.rest_type_inner_of(e))
            if ek = KindNamedTupleMember
            {
                if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(e) <> null
                    return Checker.array_element_type_node_of(AstNode.type_of(e))
            }
            return null
        }
        null
    }

    ; getTupleElementFlags, whole.
    function get_tuple_element_flags(node: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindOptionalType
            return ElementFlagsOptional
        if k = KindRestType
        {
            if Checker.array_element_type_node_of(AstNode.rest_type_inner_of(node)) <> null
                return ElementFlagsRest
            return ElementFlagsVariadic
        }
        if k = KindNamedTupleMember
        {
            if AstNode.question_token_of(node) <> null
                return ElementFlagsOptional
            if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node) <> null
            {
                if Checker.array_element_type_node_of(AstNode.type_of(node)) <> null
                    return ElementFlagsRest
                return ElementFlagsVariadic
            }
            return ElementFlagsRequired
        }
        ElementFlagsRequired
    }

    ; checkTupleType, whose ordering loop is ported and whose VARIADIC arm is where
    ; it stops.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE STOP HAS TO END THE LOOP, NOT SKIP AN ELEMENT, AND THAT IS THE ONLY
    ; PLACE IN THIS SLICE WHERE THE SHAPE OF THE ABSENCE MATTERS. A variadic element
    ; is the one case whose FLAGS depend on a type: the reference resolves it, errors
    ; and BREAKs when it is not array-like, and otherwise sets ElementFlagsRest on
    ; it — so `seenRestElement` after a variadic element is a fact this port cannot
    ; know. Emitting the loop's later reports anyway would put a diagnostic where the
    ; reference has none, which is the one thing diagcheck can fail on. Stopping the
    ; loop keeps our lines a SUBSET of the reference's, which is the relation it
    ; asserts.
    ;
    ; ★ The recursion and the final stop are BELOW the loop and run either way:
    ; checkSourceElements is unconditional in the reference too, and skipping it
    ; would hide every element's own arm behind a tuple.
    procedure check_tuple_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(node)
        let count AstNode.list_count(elements)
        var seen_optional_element false
        var seen_rest_element false
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let e AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
            let flags Checker.get_tuple_element_flags(e)
            if (flags & ElementFlagsVariadic) <> 0
            {
                this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", AstNode.kind_of(e))
                break
            }
            if (flags & ElementFlagsRest) <> 0
            {
                if seen_rest_element
                {
                    this.grammar_error_on_node(e, DiagA_rest_element_cannot_follow_another_rest_element)
                    break
                }
                set seen_rest_element: true
            }
            else
            {
                if (flags & ElementFlagsOptional) <> 0
                {
                    if seen_rest_element
                    {
                        this.grammar_error_on_node(e, DiagAn_optional_element_cannot_follow_a_rest_element)
                        break
                    }
                    set seen_optional_element: true
                }
                else
                {
                    if (flags & ElementFlagsRequired) <> 0
                    {
                        if seen_optional_element
                        {
                            this.grammar_error_on_node(e, DiagA_required_element_cannot_follow_an_optional_element)
                            break
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        this.check_source_elements(elements)
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", KindTupleType)
    }

    ; checkTemplateLiteralType, whose per-span recursion is ported and whose
    ; assignability check against the template constraint is the stop.
    ;
    ; ★ The loop is written in the reference's own order — recurse, resolve, check —
    ; so the tag a span's own subtree records comes first and the stop only speaks
    ; for a span whose type walks silently. record_unported keeps the FIRST tag,
    ; which is what makes writing it in order rather than hoisting it correct.
    procedure check_template_literal_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let spans AstNode.template_literal_type_spans_of(node)
        let count AstNode.list_count(spans)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let span AstNode.child_in_list(spans, i)
            this.check_source_element(AstNode.template_literal_type_span_type_of(span))
            this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", KindTemplateLiteralTypeSpan)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", KindTemplateLiteralType)
    }

    ; checkMappedType, whose grammar check and three recursions are ported.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TYPE PARAMETER OF A MAPPED TYPE IS THE SECOND CALLER THAT MAKES
    ; checkSourceElement's TypeParameter ARM LIVE, and slice 60 predicted exactly
    ; this: it wrote the arm INERT, with the note that *the first slice that does
    ; walk into one — checkMappedType's own type parameter, or an InferType's —
    ; would report a hole for an arm that exists*. Both of those callers are in this
    ; slice.
    ;
    ; ★ `if mappedTypeNode.Type == nil { c.reportImplicitAny(...) }` is BELOW the
    ; three recursions and above the stop; it needs the any type and the widening
    ; machinery, and it is unreachable in front of getTypeFromMappedTypeNode anyway.
    procedure check_mapped_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_mapped_type(node)
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.mapped_type_parameter_of(node))
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.mapped_type_name_type_of(node))
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.type_of(node))
        this.record_unported("get-type-from-mapped-type-node", KindMappedType)
    }

    ; checkGrammarMappedType, whole — a mapped type's member list is the parser's
    ; recovery slot and any entry in it is an error.
    function check_grammar_mapped_type(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let members AstNode.member_list_of(node)
        if AstNode.list_count(members) = 0
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.child_in_list(members, 0), DiagA_mapped_type_may_not_declare_properties_or_methods)
    }

    ; checkInferType, whose grammar report and recursion are ported and whose
    ; identical-constraints check is the stop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE GRAMMAR REPORT IS AN ANCESTOR WALK AND ITS PREDICATE IS ABOUT THE
    ; PARENT, NOT THE NODE. FindAncestor starts AT the node and asks of each step
    ; whether ITS parent is a conditional type whose extendsType is that step — so
    ; `T extends (infer U) ? …` is legal, because the walk climbs out of the
    ; parenthesized type and finds the relation one level up. Reading the predicate
    ; as *my parent is a conditional type* would reject it.
    ;
    ; ★ The stop is `len(symbol.Declarations) > 1`, i.e. a SECOND `infer U` with the
    ; same name — so the check is inert for every well-formed conditional type and
    ; the report stands for a shape the corpus barely contains. It is
    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration all the same, and that is the tag.
    procedure check_infer_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var n: ref[AstNode]? node
        var found false
        while n <> null
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindConditionalType
            {
                if AstNode.conditional_extends_type_of(parent) = n
                {
                    set found: true
                    break
                }
            }
            set n: parent
        }
        if found = false
            this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_infer_declarations_are_only_permitted_in_the_extends_clause_of_a_conditional_type)
        let type_parameter AstNode.infer_type_parameter_of(node)
        this.check_source_element(type_parameter)

        ; ★★★ SLICE 65: THIS ARM WAS NOT BLOCKED AT ALL, and the guard is why. The
        ; reference asks getSymbolOfDeclaration for the inferred type parameter and
        ; then `if len(symbol.Declarations) > 1` — so the whole
        ; declaredTypeLinks/areTypeParametersIdentical block behind it belongs to an
        ; `infer T` whose T is declared TWICE, which is the shape
        ; `T extends infer U ? infer U : never` has and almost nothing else does.
        ; With one declaration the function finishes at
        ; registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck, which is not ported for the reason
        ; check_type_alias_declaration's header gives. ★It is the sharpest of the
        ; four sites the head's tag named: three of them were re-pointed at the
        ; function that really stops them and this one had nothing to point at.
        let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(type_parameter)
        if symbol = null
            return
        if Symbol.declaration_count(symbol) > 1
            this.record_unported("get-declared-type-of-type-parameter", KindInferType)
    }

    ; getTypePredicateParent, whole — a kind test on the parent plus the identity
    ; test that the predicate IS that parent's return type annotation.
    function type_predicate_parent_of(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        var eligible false
        if pk = KindArrowFunction
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindCallSignature
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindFunctionDeclaration
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindFunctionExpression
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindFunctionType
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindMethodDeclaration
            set eligible: true
        if pk = KindMethodSignature
            set eligible: true
        if eligible = false
            return null
        if AstNode.type_of(parent) <> node
            return null
        parent
    }

    ; checkTypePredicate, whose first report is ported and whose second line is the
    ; stop.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REPORT IS THE ONE THIS ARM CAN REACH AND IT IS A `c.error`, NOT A
    ; GRAMMAR ERROR — so it survives a file with parse diagnostics, and a predicate
    ; written where the grammar allows the syntax but the semantics do not (`let x:
    ; a is string`) reports here rather than in the parser.
    ;
    ; ★ Everything below it — the parameter-index checks, the rest-parameter report
    ; and the assignability of the predicate's type to its parameter's — is behind
    ; getSignatureFromDeclaration, which is the signature table.
    procedure check_type_predicate(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let parent Checker.type_predicate_parent_of(node)
        if parent = null
        {
            this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_type_predicate_is_only_allowed_in_return_type_position_for_functions_and_methods)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("get-signature-from-declaration", KindTypePredicate)
    }

    ; checkJSDocType, whole but for the half of checkJSDocTypeIsInJsFile that needs
    ; a type string.
    procedure check_jsdoc_type(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_jsdoc_type_is_in_js_file(node)
        this.check_each_child_source_element(node)
    }

    ; checkJSDocTypeIsInJsFile, split at the one line that needs a type.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO HALVES REPORT DIFFERENT THINGS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A TYPE
    ; QUESTION. A `?T` or `T!` in a .ts file gets a message that QUOTES the type it
    ; would have meant (`'?' at the start of a type is not valid TypeScript syntax.
    ; Did you mean to write 'T | null'?`), so it needs getTypeFromTypeNode and
    ; TypeToString and stops here; a `*` or a JSDoc type literal gets the plain
    ; TS8020 and is complete. ★And the whole function is a NO-OP in a JavaScript
    ; file, which is where these kinds normally live — so the ported half is exactly
    ; the case that is an error.
    procedure check_jsdoc_type_is_in_js_file(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            return
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindJSDocNonNullableType
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", k)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSDocNullableType
        {
            this.record_unported("get-type-from-type-node", k)
            return
        }
        this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagJSDoc_types_can_only_be_used_inside_documentation_comments)
    }

    ; ── slice 52: the function declaration ───────────────────────────────────

    ; The six kinds whose checkSignatureDeclaration grammar arm is
    ; checkGrammarFunctionLikeDeclaration — the reference's own `case` list, kept
    ; as a list because the seventh (KindIndexSignature) has a DIFFERENT check and
    ; every other kind that reaches that switch has none.
    function kind_has_function_like_grammar_check(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return true
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        false
    }

    ; checkSignatureDeclaration, whole, with three of its steps written down rather
    ; than ported and each saying which kind of absence it is.
    procedure check_signature_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindIndexSignature
        {
            ; checkGrammarIndexSignature — the parameter-count, modifier, name,
            ; type-annotation and return-type checks of `[k: string]: T`.
            ;
            ; ★★★ SLICE 52 WROTE *no arm of this slice can reach it (an index
            ; signature is a class or interface MEMBER, and neither container's
            ; members are walked yet)*, AND SLICE 61's checkTypeLiteral IS THE ARM
            ; THAT REACHES IT — an object TYPE LITERAL is the third container, and
            ; its members walk is one line. The report has been the whole of the
            ; check all along and is only now observable;
            ; checker_index_signature_reachable.ts is the witness, and the unit's
            ; tag moving from `check` at TypeLiteral to this one is the proof.
            this.record_unported("check-grammar-index-signature", k)
        }
        else
        {
            if Checker.kind_has_function_like_grammar_check(k)
                this.check_grammar_function_like_declaration(node)
        }

        let function_flags b.get_function_flags(node)

        ; `if functionFlags&ast.FunctionFlagsInvalid == 0` guards two
        ; checkExternalEmitHelpers calls — the __await/__asyncGenerator helper for
        ; an async generator below ES2018, and __awaiter for an async function below
        ; ES2017. BOTH ARE DEAD, and for the reference's own default rather than for
        ; a gap in this port: languageVersion is GetEmitScriptTarget(), which
        ; answers ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 when Target is unset, so
        ; `languageVersion < ES2018` is false. That is the same read that makes the
        ; use-strict gate above always TAKEN — one unset option, two opposite
        ; consequences, which is why each is argued at its own site.

        this.check_type_parameters(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))

        ; checkUnmatchedJSDocParameters — UNREACHABLE, and it takes two conditions
        ; rather than one. getAllJSDocTags returns nil unless the node carries
        ; NodeFlagsHasJSDoc, and check_source_element_worker reports
        ; `check-jsdoc-comments` and RETURNS for any node that does — but that gate
        ; alone is NOT the argument, because the function walks UPWARDS through
        ; GetNextJSDocCommentLocation and an ancestor's tags would count. That walk
        ; goes to the parent only for eight kinds (PropertyAssignment,
        ; ExportAssignment, PropertyDeclaration, VariableDeclaration,
        ; SatisfiesExpression, ReturnStatement, VariableStatement,
        ; ExpressionStatement, and a VariableDeclarationList's first declaration),
        ; and a function declaration's parent is a SourceFile, a Block or a
        ; ModuleBlock — none of them. So the walk stops at the node itself and the
        ; gate decides. ★It expires for a DIFFERENT arm the moment one whose parent
        ; can be a VariableStatement lands: a function EXPRESSION.

        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.parameters_of(node))

        let return_type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if return_type_node <> null
            this.check_source_element(return_type_node)

        ; `if c.noImplicitAny && returnTypeNode == nil` — a switch with arms for
        ; ConstructSignature and CallSignature only, so no kind this slice reaches
        ; can report there. ★And noImplicitAny is TRUE under this harness, not
        ; false: it is a strict-FAMILY option, so an unset value falls through to
        ; `Strict != TSFalse` (strict_null_checks' header has the three readings of
        ; "unset" this slice met). So the two arms are live code behind a live
        ; option, and what the slice that ports either of them owes is the arm, not
        ; the option.

        if return_type_node <> null
        {
            ; Two type checks on the annotation, and the flag tests are
            ; EQUALITY tests against a composite (FunctionFlags.scaly's header says
            ; why): a generator that is not invalid, and an async function that is
            ; not a generator. Both resolve the annotation through
            ; getTypeFromTypeNode, so both report — separately, because the second
            ; is also the whole of checkAsyncFunctionReturnType and the two arrive
            ; in different slices.
            if (function_flags & (FunctionFlagsInvalid | FunctionFlagsGenerator)) = FunctionFlagsGenerator
                this.record_unported("check-generator-return-type", k)
            else
            {
                if (function_flags & FunctionFlagsAsyncGenerator) = FunctionFlagsAsync
                    this.record_unported("check-async-function-return-type", k)
            }
        }

        ; `if !ast.IsIndexSignatureDeclaration(node) { c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(node) }`
        ; — observable only through checkUnusedIdentifiers, which checkUnused gates
        ; off under this harness. checkBlock carries the same line for the same
        ; reason.
    }

    ; checkFunctionOrMethodDeclaration's prefix — two calls, and the third thing it
    ; does is where this slice stops.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SYMBOL CHECK RUNS FOR EVERY FUNCTION DECLARATION, NOT FOR SOME, and
    ; that is why the report is unconditional rather than behind the guard the
    ; reference writes. `c.hasBindableName(node)` is `!ast.HasDynamicName(node) ||
    ; c.hasLateBindableName(node)`, and HasDynamicName is `name != nil &&
    ; IsDynamicName(name)` — a computed or late-bound name. A function
    ; declaration's name is an Identifier or absent, so the left disjunct is TRUE
    ; on every one of them and checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol is reached. Writing
    ; the guard and reporting inside it would suggest a shape the corpus does not
    ; contain.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT STANDS BEHIND IT IS THE BODY, AND THAT IS THE SUCCESSOR. The
    ; reference's next line is `c.checkSourceElement(node.Body())` — a Block, an arm
    ; this port HAS since slice 50 — so the whole statement population of every
    ; function in the corpus is one report away. It is deliberately not taken here:
    ; the rule this column is built on (slice 49) is that the ported prefix runs up
    ; to the first thing the slice does not implement and the report NAMES that
    ; thing, and reaching past checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol to pick up a later
    ; step would make the tag a statement about what was convenient. The bill for
    ; the successor is that one function: the symbol's declaration list walked for a
    ; duplicate implementation, an overload without one, and the modifier/abstract
    ; agreement across declarations.
    ;
    ; ★ Between the two lies `functionFlags := ast.GetFunctionFlags(node)`, whose
    ; readers are all behind the symbol check, and the computed-property-name arm —
    ; `if node.Name() != nil && ast.IsComputedPropertyName(node.Name())`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THAT ARM WENT LIVE IN SLICE 62 AND THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE HAD
    ; PREDICTED IT: it read *belongs to this function's OTHER caller: a method's
    ; name can be `[Symbol.x]`, a function declaration's cannot* — and the other
    ; caller is checkMethodDeclaration, which this file now has. So the report is
    ; here rather than in the method arm, at the reference's own line, and it is
    ; unreachable for the FunctionDeclaration caller for exactly the reason the old
    ; sentence gave. Nothing about a function declaration's tag moves.
    procedure check_function_or_method_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_decorators(node)
        this.check_signature_declaration(node)
        ; The reference's comment at the line is the whole argument for asking the
        ; kind rather than hasDynamicName: *do not use hasDynamicName here, because
        ; that returns false for well known symbols* — `[Symbol.iterator]() {}` is
        ; a computed name whose expression IS late-bindable, and it must reach
        ; checkComputedPropertyName all the same.
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindComputedPropertyName
            this.record_unported("check-computed-property-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        ; ★★★ SLICE 68 PORTS THE GUARD THE OLD REPORT STOOD IN FRONT OF, and the
        ; guard is why the report above was unconditional while this one is not.
        ; `hasBindableName` is `!HasDynamicName(node) || hasLateBindableName(node)`:
        ; a function declaration's name is an Identifier or absent, so the left
        ; disjunct is true on every one of them — but a METHOD reaches this function
        ; too, and `[expr]() {}` with a non-literal expr is the shape that needs
        ; isLateBindableName, i.e. checkComputedPropertyName. That is the same stop
        ; check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name reaches, and it is asked here in the
        ; reference's own order.
        if b.has_dynamic_name(node)
            this.record_unported("is-late-bindable-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        else
        {
            let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
            if symbol <> null
            {
                let sym symbol as ref[Symbol]
                ; ★★ THE TWO CALLS ARE TWO SYMBOLS AND THE REFERENCE'S COMMENT SAYS
                ; WHY: the LOCAL symbol carries the declarations as written, and the
                ; EXPORT symbol is what makes the modifiers of all exported
                ; declarations agree. `localSymbol` is the node's own when it has one
                ; and the declaration symbol otherwise.
                var local_symbol AstNode.local_symbol_of(node)
                if local_symbol = null
                    set local_symbol: sym
                ; ★ The JavaScript guard is the reference's, with its own reason: a JS
                ; file does no semantic analysis, so redeclaring a function there is
                ; not a duplicate. 151 of this corpus's units are JS or JSX, so the
                ; guard is live rather than decorative.
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsJavaScriptFile) = 0
                    this.check_function_or_constructor_symbol(local_symbol as ref[Symbol])
                if Symbol.has_parent(sym)
                    this.check_function_or_constructor_symbol(sym)
            }
        }
        ; ★★★ AND THIS IS WHERE THIS PORT FIRST WALKS INTO A FUNCTION BODY. There is
        ; no unported step between the symbol check and `checkSourceElement(body)` —
        ; the arm the body needs (Block) has been in this file since slice 50 — so
        ; stopping here would mean inventing a report that names a function this port
        ; HAS, which is the one thing the unported column may never say. It is
        ; measured rather than assumed: 15 of the 42 function units of stage 1 move
        ; past this line into the expression and variable-type rows, and it adds ONE
        ; diagnostic over the corpus, still a subsequence.
        ;
        ; ★★★ IT ALSO RETIRES A PREMISE FOUR EARLIER SLICES ARGUED FROM. Slice 63's
        ; g15/g19/g16/g17 bounded that slice's whole unreachability to the single fact
        ; that *nothing in this port walks into a function body*; from here on that
        ; sentence is false, and the rows it justified are reachable. See §3.5dn.
        ;
        ; ★ The walk is UNCONDITIONAL — a report does not stop it — for the reason
        ; check_source_elements gives at its own loop: diagcheck compares the C
        ; section of every unit whether or not the dump is suppressed, so the
        ; statements after the first report are exactly where an invented diagnostic
        ; would hide.
        this.check_source_element(AstNode.body_of(node))
        ; checkAllCodePathsInNonVoidFunctionReturnOrThrow's argument is
        ; getReturnTypeFromAnnotation — the type-node dimension — so the arm stops at
        ; the call rather than inside it. The two blocks behind it (the JSDoc
        ; full-signature report and the implicit-any/generator pair) are named in
        ; check_function_declaration's note.
        this.record_unported("get-return-type-from-annotation", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkFunctionDeclaration, whole — one call plus two the call above stops.
    ;
    ; ★ checkGrammarForGenerator (the `*` on an ambient or overload declaration) and
    ; checkCollisionsForDeclarationName (a row this column already has from the
    ; class, enum and module arms) both follow the line above, so neither is
    ; reached; they are named here rather than left out so that the slice which
    ; retires the report above finds them already written down.
    procedure check_function_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_function_or_method_declaration(node)
    }

    ; ── slice 68: checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol — the OVERLOAD check ───────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST SLICE IN TEN WHOSE PRODUCT IS A DIAGNOSTIC RATHER THAN A TYPE,
    ; and that is the whole argument for taking it before the work list's larger
    ; head. Slice 67 closed with two candidates — `get-type-of-variable-or-parameter-
    ; or-property` 4 695 and this row's 3 807 — and noted that the matched count had
    ; not moved for nine slices because what those slices made was an object type
    ; `type_to_string` cannot print. This function makes no type at all: it walks a
    ; symbol's DECLARATION LIST and reports fifteen distinct codes about the overload
    ; set, so diagcheck goes back to being the positive instrument it was in slices
    ; 48–64 instead of the purely negative one slices 66 and 67 had.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS ONE FUNCTION AND TWO CALL SITES, both of which stop here today: the
    ; class arm (checkClassLikeDeclaration, where slice 67 left it) and the function/
    ; method arm (checkFunctionOrMethodDeclaration). The reference has a third,
    ; checkConstructorDeclaration, which sits behind `checkSourceElement(node.Body())`
    ; on a class member and is reached from neither.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE SLICE DOES **NOT** DO, and it is the worker's last branch:
    ; `getSignaturesOfSymbol` / `getSignatureFromDeclaration` /
    ; `isImplementationCompatibleWithOverload`, i.e. the assignability comparison
    ; between an implementation signature and each of its overloads (TS2394). That is
    ; the signature dimension and a work-list row of its own; everything in front of
    ; it is syntax over the declaration list.
    ;
    ; ★★★ A MESSAGE ARGUMENT IS NOT COMPARED AND A RELATED-INFO CHAIN IS NOT EITHER.
    ; The reference's C line is `C pos end code` (tests/oracle/types.go), so
    ; `Function_implementation_name_must_be_0`'s name argument and the
    ; `Consider_adding_a_declare_modifier_to_this_class` related diagnostics attached
    ; through SetRelatedInfo are invisible to every instrument this package has. They
    ; are left unbuilt rather than approximated, and named here so that the slice
    ; which gives this port a message renderer finds both sites written down.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND `c.error(nil, …)` IS NOT A DIAGNOSTIC OF THIS FILE. NewDiagnosticForNode
    ; leaves the file nil when the location is nil, and GetDiagnostics answers
    ; `collection.GetDiagnosticsForFile(sourceFile.FileName())` — so such a report
    ; lands in the GLOBAL list and never appears among the reference's C lines. Five
    ; arms of this family can be handed a nil location (the four that report on
    ; `GetNameOfDeclaration(overload)` alone, and the multiple-constructor loop), and
    ; each goes through error_on_node_or_null, which drops it. Emitting it instead
    ; would be an INVENTED line, which is the one failure diagcheck's subsequence
    ; relation exists to catch.

    ; getCanonicalOverload, the first of the reference's four closures.
    ;
    ; ★★ `overloads` IS `symbol.Declarations` AT EVERY CALL SITE, which is why this
    ; takes the symbol and not a list: the worker passes `declarations` to both
    ; agreement checks, and the per-file group the flag check builds is the whole
    ; list here (see check_flag_agreement_between_overloads for the proof).
    function canonical_overload(symbol: ref[Symbol], implementation: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let first Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, 0)
        if implementation = null
            return first
        if first = null
            return first
        ; implementationSharesContainerWithFirstOverload
        if AstNode.parent_node_of(implementation) = AstNode.parent_node_of(first)
            return implementation
        first
    }

    ; c.error(location, …) at the five sites where the reference may hand it a nil.
    ;
    ; ★ See the family header: a nil location makes a GLOBAL diagnostic, which
    ; GetDiagnosticsForFile does not return, so the reference's own C lines do not
    ; carry it. Dropping it is the faithful answer and not a leniency.
    procedure error_on_node_or_null(this, n: ref[AstNode]?, code: int)
    {
        if n = null
            return
        this.error_on_node(n as ref[AstNode], code)
    }

    ; ast.IsPropertyNameLiteral.
    function is_property_name_literal(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindIdentifier
            return true
        if k = KindStringLiteral
            return true
        if k = KindNoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
            return true
        if k = KindNumericLiteral
            return true
        false
    }

    ; The four kinds `checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbolWorker` accumulates over, and
    ; the same four its two reporting loops re-walk — see the worker's note on why
    ; `functionDeclarations` is a second walk here rather than a list.
    function is_function_or_constructor_declaration(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        false
    }

    ; `name.Text() == subsequentName.Text()` for the two arms of
    ; reportImplementationExpectedError that ask it — the private-identifier pair and
    ; the property-name-literal pair.
    ;
    ; ★ The two text accessors are disjoint by kind and neither answers for the
    ; other's kinds, so the dispatch is on the KIND rather than on a null text: an
    ; Identifier's text read through literal_text_of is null, and two nulls would
    ; compare EQUAL — a wrong answer that looks like a right one.
    function name_text_equals(a: ref[AstNode], b: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let ka AstNode.kind_of(a)
        var identifier_shaped false
        if ka = KindIdentifier
            set identifier_shaped: true
        if ka = KindPrivateIdentifier
            set identifier_shaped: true
        var data_a: pointer[char] null
        var len_a 0
        var data_b: pointer[char] null
        var len_b 0
        if identifier_shaped
        {
            set data_a: AstNode.identifier_text_of(a)
            set len_a: AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(a)
            set data_b: AstNode.identifier_text_of(b)
            set len_b: AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(b)
        }
        else
        {
            set data_a: AstNode.literal_text_of(a)
            set len_a: AstNode.literal_text_length_of(a)
            set data_b: AstNode.literal_text_of(b)
            set len_b: AstNode.literal_text_length_of(b)
        }
        if data_a = null
            return false
        if data_b = null
            return false
        if len_a <> len_b
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < len_a
        {
            if (*(data_a + i) as int) <> (*(data_b + i) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; reportImplementationExpectedError, the third closure. `isConstructor` is its
    ; one capture and becomes a parameter (§3.5e).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NODE IT LOOKS AT IS THE **NEXT SIBLING**, found through the parent's
    ; child walk rather than through a statement list, because the two are not the
    ; same list for a class member or an interface member. §3.9d's index pair is what
    ; the reference's ForEachChild visitor becomes here.
    procedure report_implementation_expected_error(this, node: ref[AstNode], is_constructor: bool)
    {
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if name <> null
        {
            if Binder.node_is_missing(name)
                return
        }
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var subsequent_node: ref[AstNode]? null
        if parent <> null
        {
            let p parent as ref[AstNode]
            let child_total AstNode.child_count(p)
            var i 0
            var seen false
            while i < child_total
            {
                let child AstNode.child_at(p, i)
                if seen
                {
                    set subsequent_node: child
                    set i: child_total
                }
                else
                {
                    if child = node
                        set seen: true
                    set i: i + 1
                }
            }
        }
        ; ★ The reference's own comment is the whole reason for the position test:
        ; extra nodes between overloads that could not be parsed into a valid node
        ; leave a subsequent node that is not really consecutive.
        if subsequent_node <> null
        {
            let s subsequent_node as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.pos_of(s) = AstNode.end_of(node)
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(s) = AstNode.kind_of(node)
                {
                    let subsequent_name AstNode.name_of(s)
                    var error_node: ref[AstNode]? subsequent_name
                    if error_node = null
                        set error_node: s
                    if name <> null
                    {
                        if subsequent_name <> null
                        {
                            let n1 name as ref[AstNode]
                            let n2 subsequent_name as ref[AstNode]
                            let k1 AstNode.kind_of(n1)
                            let k2 AstNode.kind_of(n2)
                            var same_name false
                            if k1 = KindPrivateIdentifier
                            {
                                if k2 = KindPrivateIdentifier
                                    set same_name: Checker.name_text_equals(n1, n2)
                            }
                            ; ★★★ THE SECOND DISJUNCT IS THE SLICE'S ONE TYPE QUESTION
                            ; AND IT IS A STOP. `isTypeIdenticalTo(
                            ; checkComputedPropertyName(name),
                            ; checkComputedPropertyName(subsequentName))` needs the
                            ; expression dimension; the `||` short-circuits, so it is
                            ; asked only when the first disjunct answered false and
                            ; both names are computed.
                            if same_name = false
                            {
                                if k1 = KindComputedPropertyName
                                {
                                    if k2 = KindComputedPropertyName
                                    {
                                        this.record_unported("check-computed-property-name", k1)
                                        return
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                            if same_name = false
                            {
                                if Checker.is_property_name_literal(k1)
                                {
                                    if Checker.is_property_name_literal(k2)
                                        set same_name: Checker.name_text_equals(n1, n2)
                                }
                            }
                            if same_name
                            {
                                ; We can get here in two cases — mixed static and
                                ; instance class members, or something with the same
                                ; name defined before the overload set. Only the first
                                ; reports here; the binder owns the second.
                                var report_error false
                                let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
                                if nk = KindMethodDeclaration
                                    set report_error: true
                                if nk = KindMethodSignature
                                    set report_error: true
                                if report_error
                                {
                                    if b.is_static(node) = b.is_static(s)
                                        set report_error: false
                                }
                                if report_error
                                {
                                    if b.is_static(node)
                                        this.error_on_node_or_null(error_node, DiagFunction_overload_must_be_static)
                                    else
                                        this.error_on_node_or_null(error_node, DiagFunction_overload_must_not_be_static)
                                }
                                return
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(s)) = false
                    {
                        this.error_on_node_or_null(error_node, DiagFunction_implementation_name_must_be_0)
                        return
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        var target: ref[AstNode]? name
        if target = null
            set target: node
        if is_constructor
        {
            this.error_on_node_or_null(target, DiagConstructor_implementation_is_missing)
            return
        }
        ; Report different errors regarding non-consecutive blocks of declarations
        ; depending on whether the node in question is abstract.
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
            this.error_on_node_or_null(target, DiagAll_declarations_of_an_abstract_method_must_be_consecutive)
        else
            this.error_on_node_or_null(target, DiagFunction_implementation_is_missing_or_not_immediately_following_the_declaration)
    }

    ; checkFlagAgreementBetweenOverloads, the second closure.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE PER-SOURCE-FILE GROUPING IS ONE GROUP HERE, AND THAT IS A PROOF RATHER
    ; THAN A SIMPLIFICATION. The reference groups the overloads by
    ; GetSourceFileOfNode and computes a canonical flag set per group, so that
    ; overloads split between lib.d.ts and a user file need not agree on `export` or
    ; `declare`. This port binds ONE file — there is no program, no lib and no second
    ; SourceFile — so every declaration of a symbol walks up to the same file node,
    ; the single group IS the whole list, `canonicalFlagsForFile` equals
    ; `canonicalFlags` and `deviationInFile` equals `deviation`. The two variables are
    ; therefore one variable, and the day this port loads a lib is the day the
    ; grouping has to be written; it is named here so that day finds it.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference iterates a Go MAP over the groups, whose order is
    ; nondeterministic — invisible only because the diagnostics are sorted by position
    ; afterwards. With one group there is nothing to order.
    procedure check_flag_agreement_between_overloads(this, symbol: ref[Symbol], implementation: ref[AstNode]?, flags_to_check: int, some_overload_flags: int, all_overload_flags: int)
    {
        ; Error if some overloads have a flag that is not shared by all overloads. To
        ; find the deviations, XOR someOverloadFlags with allOverloadFlags.
        let some_but_not_all: int some_overload_flags xor all_overload_flags
        if some_but_not_all = 0
            return
        let canonical Checker.canonical_overload(symbol, implementation)
        if canonical = null
            return
        let canonical_flags this.effective_declaration_flags(canonical as ref[AstNode], flags_to_check)
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let overload Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if overload = null
                continue
            let o overload as ref[AstNode]
            let deviation this.effective_declaration_flags(o, flags_to_check) xor canonical_flags
            if (deviation & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                this.error_on_node_or_null(this.name_of_declaration(o), DiagOverload_signatures_must_all_be_exported_or_non_exported)
            else
            {
                if (deviation & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    this.error_on_node_or_null(this.name_of_declaration(o), DiagOverload_signatures_must_all_be_ambient_or_non_ambient)
                else
                {
                    if (deviation & (ModifierFlagsPrivate | ModifierFlagsProtected)) <> 0
                    {
                        var target this.name_of_declaration(o)
                        if target = null
                            set target: o
                        this.error_on_node_or_null(target, DiagOverload_signatures_must_all_be_public_private_or_protected)
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        if (deviation & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                            this.error_on_node_or_null(this.name_of_declaration(o), DiagOverload_signatures_must_all_be_abstract_or_non_abstract)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    ; checkQuestionTokenAgreementBetweenOverloads, the fourth closure.
    procedure check_question_token_agreement_between_overloads(this, symbol: ref[Symbol], implementation: ref[AstNode]?, some_have_question_token: bool, all_have_question_token: bool)
    {
        if some_have_question_token = all_have_question_token
            return
        let canonical Checker.canonical_overload(symbol, implementation)
        if canonical = null
            return
        let canonical_has_question_token Checker.is_optional_declaration(canonical as ref[AstNode])
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if d = null
                continue
            let overload d as ref[AstNode]
            if Checker.is_optional_declaration(overload) <> canonical_has_question_token
                this.error_on_node_or_null(this.name_of_declaration(overload), DiagOverload_signatures_must_all_be_optional_or_required)
        }
    }

    ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol — the memo, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS THE SECOND READER OF valueSymbolLinks IN THIS PORT AND THE FIRST WHOSE
    ; HIT IS ABOUT REPORTS RATHER THAN ABOUT WORK. Both call sites can reach the same
    ; symbol — a class declaration merged with a function's does — and without the bit
    ; the second visit would emit every diagnostic a second time. diagcheck's relation
    ; is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a duplicated line is a failure and not a leniency.
    procedure check_function_or_constructor_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol])
    {
        let links this.value_symbol_link_of(symbol)
        if links.function_or_constructor_checked
            return
        set links.function_or_constructor_checked: true
        this.check_function_or_constructor_symbol_worker(symbol)
    }

    ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbolWorker, whole but for its last branch.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ACCUMULATORS ARE THE FUNCTION. Everything the reference reports here is
    ; decided by ONE walk over the declaration list that fills nine of them, and the
    ; walk's own order is what `previousDeclaration` MEANS — so the loop is
    ; transcribed statement for statement rather than reorganised, and the four
    ; reporting blocks after it read only accumulators.
    ;
    ; ★★ `functionDeclarations` IS A SECOND WALK RATHER THAN A LIST, and the
    ; equivalence is exact rather than close: the reference appends exactly the
    ; declarations whose kind is one of the four, in declaration-list order, and the
    ; two loops that read it iterate it in that order. Re-applying the same kind test
    ; over the same list in the same order yields the same sequence — so the list is
    ; the walk, and a heap allocation whose only purpose is to remember a filter is
    ; not built.
    procedure check_function_or_constructor_symbol_worker(this, symbol: ref[Symbol])
    {
        let flags_to_check: int ModifierFlagsExport | ModifierFlagsAmbient | ModifierFlagsPrivate | ModifierFlagsProtected | ModifierFlagsAbstract
        var some_node_flags: int ModifierFlagsNone
        var all_node_flags: int flags_to_check
        var some_have_question_token false
        var all_have_question_token true
        var has_overloads false
        var body_declaration: ref[AstNode]? null
        var last_seen_non_ambient_declaration: ref[AstNode]? null
        var previous_declaration: ref[AstNode]? null
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        let is_constructor (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsConstructor) <> 0
        var duplicate_function_declaration false
        var multiple_constructor_implementation false
        var has_non_ambient_class false
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if d = null
                continue
            let node d as ref[AstNode]
            let in_ambient_context (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            var in_ambient_context_or_interface: bool in_ambient_context
            if in_ambient_context_or_interface = false
            {
                let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
                if pk = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                    set in_ambient_context_or_interface: true
                if pk = KindTypeLiteral
                    set in_ambient_context_or_interface: true
            }
            ; Check whether declarations are consecutive only if they are
            ; non-ambient: ambient declarations may be interleaved, and mixing
            ; ambient with non-ambient is a separate error already reported.
            if in_ambient_context_or_interface
                set previous_declaration: null
            if Parser.is_class_like_node(node)
            {
                if in_ambient_context = false
                    set has_non_ambient_class: true
            }
            if Checker.is_function_or_constructor_declaration(AstNode.kind_of(node)) = false
                continue
            let current_node_flags this.effective_declaration_flags(node, flags_to_check)
            set some_node_flags: some_node_flags | current_node_flags
            set all_node_flags: all_node_flags & current_node_flags
            let optional Checker.is_optional_declaration(node)
            if optional
                set some_have_question_token: true
            if optional = false
                set all_have_question_token: false
            let body_is_present Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(node)) = false
            if body_is_present
            {
                if body_declaration <> null
                {
                    if is_constructor
                        set multiple_constructor_implementation: true
                    else
                        set duplicate_function_declaration: true
                }
            }
            else
            {
                if previous_declaration <> null
                {
                    let prev previous_declaration as ref[AstNode]
                    if AstNode.parent_node_of(prev) = AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
                    {
                        if AstNode.end_of(prev) <> AstNode.pos_of(node)
                        {
                            if (AstNode.flags_of(prev) & NodeFlagsReparsed) = 0
                                this.report_implementation_expected_error(prev, is_constructor)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if body_is_present
            {
                if body_declaration = null
                    set body_declaration: node
            }
            else
                set has_overloads: true
            set previous_declaration: node
            if in_ambient_context_or_interface = false
                set last_seen_non_ambient_declaration: node
        }
        if multiple_constructor_implementation
        {
            var j 0
            while j < count
            {
                let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, j)
                set j: j + 1
                if d = null
                    continue
                let declaration d as ref[AstNode]
                if Checker.is_function_or_constructor_declaration(AstNode.kind_of(declaration))
                    this.error_on_node_or_null(declaration, DiagMultiple_constructor_implementations_are_not_allowed)
            }
        }
        if duplicate_function_declaration
        {
            var j 0
            while j < count
            {
                let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, j)
                set j: j + 1
                if d = null
                    continue
                let declaration d as ref[AstNode]
                if Checker.is_function_or_constructor_declaration(AstNode.kind_of(declaration))
                {
                    var target this.name_of_declaration(declaration)
                    if target = null
                        set target: declaration
                    this.error_on_node_or_null(target, DiagDuplicate_function_implementation)
                }
            }
        }
        ; ★ The related-info list (`Consider_adding_a_declare_modifier_to_this_class`,
        ; TS6506) is attached to each report rather than reported on its own, and a
        ; related diagnostic is not among the file's C lines — see the family header.
        if has_non_ambient_class
        {
            if is_constructor = false
            {
                if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsFunction) <> 0
                {
                    var j 0
                    while j < count
                    {
                        let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, j)
                        set j: j + 1
                        if d = null
                            continue
                        let declaration d as ref[AstNode]
                        var code 0
                        let dk AstNode.kind_of(declaration)
                        if dk = KindClassDeclaration
                            set code: DiagClass_declaration_cannot_implement_overload_list_for_0
                        if dk = KindFunctionDeclaration
                            set code: DiagFunction_with_bodies_can_only_merge_with_classes_that_are_ambient
                        if code <> 0
                        {
                            var target this.name_of_declaration(declaration)
                            if target = null
                                set target: declaration
                            this.error_on_node_or_null(target, code)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        ; Abstract methods can't have an implementation — in particular, they don't
        ; need one.
        if last_seen_non_ambient_declaration <> null
        {
            let last last_seen_non_ambient_declaration as ref[AstNode]
            if AstNode.body_of(last) = null
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(last, ModifierFlagsAbstract) = false
                {
                    if Checker.is_optional_declaration(last) = false
                        this.report_implementation_expected_error(last, is_constructor)
                }
            }
        }
        if has_overloads = false
            return
        this.check_flag_agreement_between_overloads(symbol, body_declaration, flags_to_check, some_node_flags, all_node_flags)
        this.check_question_token_agreement_between_overloads(symbol, body_declaration, some_have_question_token, all_have_question_token)
        if body_declaration = null
            return
        ; ★★★ THE SLICE STOPS HERE. getSignaturesOfSymbol, getSignatureFromDeclaration
        ; and isImplementationCompatibleWithOverload — the assignability comparison
        ; behind TS2394 — are the signature dimension and a work-list row of their own.
        this.record_unported("get-signatures-of-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(body_declaration as ref[AstNode]))
    }

    ; ── slice 62: the type-MEMBER arms ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Slice 61's members walk has been handing PropertySignature and MethodSignature
    ; to this switch ever since it landed — **341 and 81 units of stage 2**, the two
    ; largest destinations of that slice's whole tag arithmetic, and it named them as
    ; the successor rather than taking them. This is that successor.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO ARMS ARE ONE SLICE BECAUSE THE REFERENCE'S FOUR FUNCTIONS ARE TWO
    ; PAIRS, AND EACH PAIR SHARES ITS WHOLE BODY WITH A CLASS MEMBER.
    ; checkPropertySignature is one report and a call to checkPropertyDeclaration;
    ; the MethodSignature case shares checkMethodDeclaration with the class's method.
    ; So porting the signature half means porting the declaration half, and what
    ; separates them is four `IsPropertyDeclaration`/`IsMethodDeclaration` tests
    ; inside bodies that are otherwise identical.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE CLASS HALF IS INERT, WHICH IS SLICE 60's TYPE-PARAMETER ROW AGAIN.
    ; Nothing walks a class body's members — checkClassDeclaration stops at
    ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations — and nothing walks an object literal's
    ; properties, so KindPropertyDeclaration and KindMethodDeclaration cannot reach
    ; this switch today. They are wired all the same, and the table above says
    ; `true` for them, because the table is a claim about which kinds this port
    ; SPEAKS on: leaving them out would make the slice that first walks a class body
    ; report a hole for an arm that exists.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE PAIR BUYS IS MOSTLY A WALK, exactly as slice 61's arms were. A
    ; property signature's TYPE annotation and a method signature's PARAMETERS and
    ; RETURN type were unreachable until now, so the type surface inside every object
    ; TYPE LITERAL in the corpus is walked for the first time — through
    ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration for the property and through
    ; checkSignatureDeclaration for the method, both of them functions this file has
    ; had since slices 55 and 52.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE TYPE LITERAL IS THE ONLY CONTAINER THAT REACHES THEM, WHICH IS THE
    ; SLICE'S LARGEST SINGLE FACT. An INTERFACE body — the canonical place for a
    ; property signature — is not walked: checkInterfaceDeclaration reports
    ; `get-declared-type-of-symbol` (`check-exports-on-merged-declarations` until
    ; slice 65 ported it, which moved the tag and not the fact) and
    ; `checkSourceElements(node.Members())`
    ; is the LAST statement of that arm, with the whole declared type between them
    ; (getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol, checkInheritedPropertiesAreIdentical, the base-type
    ; assignability loop, checkIndexConstraints). So half of what both grammar
    ; functions below contain is UNREACHABLE — checkGrammarProperty's interface arm
    ; and its mapped-type report, checkGrammarMethod's interface and class arms — and
    ; it is unreachable behind the TYPE SYSTEM rather than behind a missing recursion.
    ; ★It is measured rather than argued: controls-slice62.sh's g19 adds that members
    ; walk as a PREMISE and g20 breaks the interface arm on top of it, which is slice
    ; 61's g26/g27 technique. ★checker_member_container_reach.ts is the fixture that
    ; makes the asymmetry visible from the outside: the same error in both containers,
    ; and only the type literal's is ours.
    ;
    ; ★ WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: the ACCESSOR pair. `case GetAccessor, SetAccessor:
    ; c.checkAccessorDeclaration(node)` is 1 unit of stage 2 and is not a smaller
    ; version of this arm — it brings checkGrammarAccessor, the get/set modifier and
    ; accessibility agreement across the symbol's two declarations, and
    ; checkAllCodePathsInNonVoidFunctionReturnOrThrow. It is named here rather than
    ; guessed at.

    ; checkPropertySignature, whole. One report of its own, and it is about a
    ; spelling the grammar admits anywhere and the language permits only in a class
    ; body: `interface I { #x: number }`.
    procedure check_property_signature(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(node)) = KindPrivateIdentifier
            this.error_on_node(node, DiagPrivate_identifiers_are_not_allowed_outside_class_bodies)
        this.check_property_declaration(node)
    }

    ; checkPropertyDeclaration, whole — the shared body of both property arms.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE GRAMMAR `&&` IS TWO CALLS AND A THIRD BEHIND THEM, and the order is
    ; load-bearing in the way slice 52's was: checkGrammarModifiers runs first and a
    ; report there suppresses checkGrammarProperty, which in turn suppresses the
    ; computed-property-name check. Reading it as three independent checks would
    ; produce up to three diagnostics where the reference produces one.
    ;
    ; ★★ setNodeLinksForPrivateIdentifierScope IS A WRITE WITH NO READER, so it is a
    ; comment and not a report — §3.5ao's shape, and the reader is nameable: it sets
    ; NodeCheckFlagsContainsClassWithPrivateIdentifiers on every enclosing block
    ; scope, and the only thing that reads that flag is checkWeakMapSetCollision,
    ; which this file already stops at (`check-weak-map-set-collision`). So the
    ; write's whole observable effect sits BEHIND an existing report, and a report
    ; here would claim the unit is unmeasured where the reference emits nothing. ★It
    ; also needs a nodeLinks table this port does not have, which is the same
    ; argument from the other side.
    ;
    ; ★ THE ABSTRACT-INITIALIZER REPORT IS PropertyDeclaration-ONLY — the reference
    ; says why at the line (*property signatures already report "initializer not
    ; allowed in ambient context" elsewhere*), and that elsewhere is
    ; checkGrammarProperty's interface and type-literal branches, which this slice
    ; ports. So the test is inert today for the same reason the whole class half is.
    procedure check_property_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var reported false
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
            set reported: true
        if reported = false
        {
            if this.check_grammar_property(node)
                set reported: true
        }
        if reported = false
            this.check_grammar_computed_property_name(AstNode.name_of(node))
        this.check_variable_like_declaration(node)
        ; setNodeLinksForPrivateIdentifierScope(node) — see the header.
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
            {
                if AstNode.initializer_of(node) <> null
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagProperty_0_cannot_have_an_initializer_because_it_is_marked_abstract)
            }
        }
    }

    ; checkMethodDeclaration, whole — the shared body of the method signature and the
    ; class/object-literal method.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PRIVATE-NAME REPORT IS THE ONE THAT REACHES A SIGNATURE, and its test is
    ; not the property's. checkPropertySignature asks *is this a property SIGNATURE*
    ; (a syntactic fact) while this one asks *is there a containing CLASS* — an
    ; ancestor walk — because a method with a private name is legal in a class body
    ; and a private name reaches this function from an interface, a type literal and
    ; an object literal alike.
    ;
    ; ★ The asterisk/`constructor` report is MethodDeclaration-only and inert with
    ; the rest of the class half; it is the second of the two places this file
    ; compares an identifier's text against `constructor`.
    procedure check_method_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_method(node) = false
        {
            this.check_grammar_computed_property_name(name)
            if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindMethodDeclaration
            {
                if AstNode.asterisk_token_of(node) <> null
                {
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindIdentifier
                    {
                        if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "constructor", 11)
                            this.error_on_node(name, DiagClass_constructor_may_not_be_a_generator)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        this.check_function_or_method_declaration(node)
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindMethodDeclaration
        {
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
            {
                if AstNode.body_of(node) <> null
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagMethod_0_cannot_have_an_implementation_because_it_is_marked_abstract)
            }
        }
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindPrivateIdentifier
        {
            if Checker.get_containing_class(node) = null
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagPrivate_identifiers_are_not_allowed_outside_class_bodies)
        }
        ; setNodeLinksForPrivateIdentifierScope(node) — check_property_declaration's
        ; header carries the argument for both call sites.
    }

    ; ast.GetContainingClass — `FindAncestor(node.Parent, IsClassLike)`, written out
    ; as the walk it is (§3.9d) and starting at the PARENT, in the shape
    ; get_containing_function above has.
    function get_containing_class(node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if node = null
            return null
        var p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        while p <> null
        {
            if Parser.is_class_like_node(p)
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; checkNodeDeferred, whole. The reference's `links.typeChecked` term is the
    ; guard against re-deferring a file that has already been checked once; one
    ; Checker here serves one file and runs once, so the flag can only be false at
    ; every call — written down rather than translated, because a field with one
    ; reachable value is not state.
    procedure check_node_deferred(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        deferred_nodes.add(node)
    }

    ; checkDeferredNodes. Slice 60 is the slice its old header named — *the arm that
    ; first defers a body must find the drain already in place rather than add it
    ; silently* — so the loop is real now and the set is no longer empty by
    ; construction. What it drains is type parameters and nothing else: the
    ; reference's own switch has ten arms and checkNodeDeferred has nine callers, of
    ; which one is ported.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference clears the list after the walk and this port does not need to
    ; (one file, one drain, one Checker), but the clear is kept: it is what makes
    ; the drain idempotent, and a second caller is the ordinary way this function
    ; grows.
    procedure check_deferred_nodes(this)
    {
        var i 0
        while i < deferred_nodes.get_length()
        {
            this.check_deferred_node(*(deferred_nodes.get_buffer() + i))
            set i: i + 1
        }
        deferred_nodes.clear()
    }

    ; checkDeferredNode's switch, with the one arm this port defers into. The other
    ; nine kinds cannot be in the list — nothing ported calls checkNodeDeferred on
    ; them — so a fallthrough is the reference's answer rather than a hole.
    procedure check_deferred_node(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindTypeParameter
            this.check_type_parameter_deferred(node)
    }

    ; checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements, over the list checkVariableLikeDeclaration
    ; fills. Slice 56 gave it a writer, so it stops being empty by construction and
    ; becomes a report.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TAG NAMES THE ARGUMENT'S CALLEE AND NOT THE TABLE, which is worth a
    ; line because the table is the bigger thing. The reference's test is
    ; `c.symbolReferenceLinks.Get(c.getSymbolOfDeclaration(node)).referenceKinds ==
    ; 0` — the reference-links table is the UNUSED-IDENTIFIER machinery, a whole
    ; dimension — but getSymbolOfDeclaration is evaluated first and is unported
    ; too, so naming it is the port's ordinary rule (the tag names the FIRST thing
    ; not implemented) and not a claim that the rest is cheap.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND IT IS UNREACHABLE — 0 UNITS OF EITHER CORPUS CARRY IT, and no shape
    ; slice 56's battery could construct reaches it either. The drain runs LAST,
    ; after every statement, while the only route to its list is a parameter,
    ; whose FunctionDeclaration reports at the end of its own arm; control t3
    ; removes the parameter term so a variable declaration defers too, and the
    ; enclosing declaration then reaches ITS hole first. **A deferred element does
    ; not remove a report from the unit — it hands the unit to its container's.**
    ; The report is emitted anyway for §3.5cz's f14 reason: not emitting it would
    ; claim the drain is ported. Control t6 removes it and moves nothing, which is
    ; the claim held to a number.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS ONE ROW PER FILE, NOT PER ELEMENT, and that is the list's doing
    ; rather than a simplification: the drain happens once, at the end of
    ; checkSourceFile, so a file with three renamed binding elements reports once.
    ;
    ; ★ The gate is `is_declaration_file = false` at the call site, which is the
    ; reference's. A `.d.ts` is nothing but body-less signatures, so the one file
    ; kind whose every parameter could reach the writer is the one kind the reader
    ; never runs on.
    ; ★★★ SLICE 65 RE-TAGS IT, and this is the site where the old tag was furthest
    ; from the truth. The drain's condition is
    ; `symbolReferenceLinks.Get(getSymbolOfDeclaration(node)).referenceKinds == 0`,
    ; so the symbol was never the obstacle — the REFERENCE-MARKING table is, and
    ; nothing in this port writes one (markPropertyAsReferenced and its neighbours
    ; are the whole of that machinery). The row still moves nothing, because the
    ; drain is unreachable for the reason above.
    procedure check_unused_renamed_binding_elements(this)
    {
        if renamed_binding_elements_in_types.get_length() = 0
            return
        this.record_unported("symbol-reference-links", KindBindingElement)
    }

    ; produceDeferredDiagnostics, over deferredDiagnosticCallbacks. Empty for the
    ; same reason again — addDeferredDiagnostic's callers are all inside arms that
    ; report — and kept for the same reason: a diagnostic that is deferred and
    ; never produced is invisible, so the drain belongs in place from the start.
    procedure produce_deferred_diagnostics(this)
    {
    }

    ; ── slice 65: the merged declarations, and the symbol every arm asks for ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO HEADS, ONE SLICE, BECAUSE ONE OF THEM CALLS THE OTHER. Slice 64 left
    ; `check-exports-on-merged-declarations` at 5 599 units and
    ; `get-symbol-of-declaration` at 5 593 — the two largest rows of the work list,
    ; between them more than half the corpus — and the choice between them is not a
    ; choice: checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations' SECOND line is
    ; `c.getSymbolOfDeclaration(node)`. A slice that took the merged-declaration
    ; head alone would have had to stop inside it at the smaller head, and a slice
    ; that took getSymbolOfDeclaration alone would have moved five arms one line.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND getSymbolOfDeclaration IS FOUR LINES. The row is 5 593 units deep
    ; because every check that needs a symbol goes through it, not because the
    ; function is large — `node.Symbol()`, then getMergedSymbol of
    ; getLateBoundSymbol. What the row's SIZE measures is how central the question
    ; is, and what the slice buys is therefore mostly in the arms behind it. That is
    ; the §3.5cd shape again read at the head rather than at the walk: **a work-list
    ; row's depth is a claim about how many callers are waiting, never about how
    ; much code the row is.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ THREE OF THE FOUR SITES THAT CARRIED THE `get-symbol-of-declaration` TAG
    ; ARE NOT BLOCKED BY IT, and finding that out is what porting it is for. The tag
    ; was written at the first call into the unported region, which is the file's
    ; convention, and for three of the four sites the very next line is a
    ; DIFFERENT unported function:
    ;
    ;   check_type_parameter          getDeclaredTypeOfTypeParameter
    ;   check_infer_type              nothing — the block behind it is guarded by
    ;                                 `len(symbol.Declarations) > 1`, so the arm
    ;                                 FINISHES for a single-declaration parameter
    ;   check_unused_renamed_...      symbolReferenceLinks.referenceKinds
    ;   check_variable_like_decl...   getTypeOfSymbol, two lines further on
    ;
    ; So one site advances, two are re-tagged with the function that actually stops
    ; them, and one turns out not to be stopped at all. ★It is §3.5ci's finding with
    ; the sign reversed: there, a marker named a blocker that never existed; here,
    ; three markers named a blocker that exists and is not theirs. **A tag placed at
    ; the first unported call is a claim about an ORDER, and the order changes under
    ; it every time a neighbour lands.**

    ; ast.GetDeclarationOfKind — the first declaration of this symbol with that
    ; kind, or null.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's returns a *ast.Node and its caller compares it with `!=
    ; node`; nothing reads the null case, so the null is the "no such declaration"
    ; answer rather than a hole.
    function declaration_of_kind(s: ref[Symbol], kind: int) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let n Symbol.declaration_count(s)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(s, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                if AstNode.kind_of(d) = kind
                    return d
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; getMergedSymbol, whole — and it is the IDENTITY under this harness.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARGUMENT IS THAT THE TABLE HAS NO WRITER, AND IT IS A CLAIM WITH A
    ; DATE ON IT (§3.5bg). `c.mergedSymbols` is written at exactly two sites in all
    ; of internal/checker — mergeSymbol's `recordMergedSymbol(target, source)` and
    ; cloneSymbol's `recordMergedSymbol(result, symbol)` — and both are reached only
    ; from the GLOBAL merge: initializeChecker's mergeSymbolTable over the lib and
    ; the other source files, and the module-augmentation pass. This port has one
    ; file, no lib, and neither pass, so the map is empty and every lookup misses.
    ; ★★The slice that ports either one owes this function its table, and the
    ; failure mode if it does not is silent: a lookup that always misses and a table
    ; that is never filled are the same code.
    ;
    ; ★ Written as a function rather than folded into its caller for that reason —
    ; the place where the table WOULD be consulted has to exist before it can be
    ; given one, and a caller that reads `symbol` directly is a caller that will not
    ; be found by grepping for the function name.
    function get_merged_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
        symbol

    ; getLateBoundSymbol. The GUARD is whole; the forcing branch behind it is not.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE GUARD IS THE WHOLE ANSWER FOR EVERY SYMBOL THIS SLICE'S CALLERS CAN
    ; HAND IT, and that is a fact about the callers rather than about the function:
    ; a symbol reaches the second line only if it is a CLASS MEMBER (Property,
    ; Method, GetAccessor, SetAccessor) whose name is the internal `computed` one,
    ; and the nodes going through getSymbolOfDeclaration here are interfaces,
    ; classes, enums, modules, type aliases, variable declarations, binding elements
    ; and parameters. None of them declares a class member.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE BRANCH IS WRITTEN AS A STOP RATHER THAN LEFT OUT, for §3.5ao's reason:
    ; `links.lateSymbol == nil && core.Some(symbol.Declarations, c.hasLateBindableName)`
    ; needs the TYPE of a computed name's expression (isLateBindableName asks for a
    ; string, numeric or unique-symbol literal type), and then forces
    ; getMembersOfSymbol or getExportsOfSymbol on the parent. Answering `symbol` for
    ; a computed class member would be a well-formed WRONG answer of exactly
    ; §3.5bk's kind — the late-bound symbol is a DIFFERENT symbol — so the arm
    ; reports instead. ★The lateBoundLinks side table has no port either, and it
    ; would be a table with no reader until that branch runs.
    function get_late_bound_symbol(this, symbol: ref[Symbol]) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsClassMember) = 0
            return symbol
        var cd null as pointer[char]
        let cl Binder.internal_name(host, "computed", &cd)
        if SymbolTable.names_equal(Symbol.name_data_of(symbol), Symbol.name_len_of(symbol), cd, cl) = false
            return symbol
        this.record_unported("get-late-bound-symbol", 0)
        symbol
    }

    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration, whole.
    ;
    ; ★ It answers null for a node the binder gave no symbol, as the reference does
    ; — and every caller in this slice dereferences the result, because the
    ; reference's callers do (`symbol.ExportSymbol`, `symbol.Flags`). Where a null
    ; can arrive the caller says so at its own line; this function does not invent a
    ; symbol to protect them.
    function symbol_of_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        let symbol AstNode.symbol_of(node)
        if symbol = null
            return null
        this.get_merged_symbol(this.get_late_bound_symbol(symbol))
    }

    ; getEnclosingContainer — FindAncestor from the node's PARENT for a container,
    ; written out as the walk it is (§3.9d).
    ;
    ; ★ It starts at the parent, not at the node, which matters for a
    ; ModuleDeclaration: its own container flags would otherwise answer itself.
    function enclosing_container(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        var p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        while p <> null
        {
            if (Binder.get_container_flags(p) & ContainerFlagsIsContainer) <> 0
                return p
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        null
    }

    ; getEffectiveDeclarationFlags, whole. The combined modifier flags, plus the two
    ; the AMBIENT context adds, masked with what the caller asked about.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `flags |= Export` TERM IS WHY AN AMBIENT DECLARATION CAN BE EXPORTED
    ; WITHOUT AN `export` KEYWORD, and it is the one term of this function the
    ; merged-declaration check depends on: inside `declare module "m" { ... }` every
    ; declaration is an export, so two declarations of one name that LOOK like one
    ; local and one export are two exports and do not collide. The condition is four
    ; conjuncts and each one removes a shape — a class or interface MEMBER (where
    ; ambient and export mean nothing), a container that is not an export context, a
    ; declaration that already carries `declare`, and a global-scope augmentation's
    ; body.
    ;
    ; ★ `getCombinedModifierFlagsCached` is the binder's combined_modifier_flags,
    ; extracted from has_combined_modifier in this slice: the predicate cannot serve
    ; a caller that ORs into the value.
    function effective_declaration_flags(this, n: ref[AstNode], flags_to_check: int) returns int
    {
        var flags b.combined_modifier_flags(n)
        if (AstNode.flags_of(n) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(n)
            let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
            var member_of_class_like false
            if pk = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                set member_of_class_like: true
            if pk = KindClassDeclaration
                set member_of_class_like: true
            if pk = KindClassExpression
                set member_of_class_like: true
            if member_of_class_like = false
            {
                let container Checker.enclosing_container(n)
                if container <> null
                {
                    var global_augmentation_body false
                    if pk = KindModuleBlock
                    {
                        let grandparent AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
                        if grandparent <> null
                        {
                            if Binder.is_global_scope_augmentation(grandparent)
                                set global_augmentation_body: true
                        }
                    }
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(container) & NodeFlagsExportContext) <> 0
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) = 0
                        {
                            if global_augmentation_body = false
                                set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsExport
                        }
                    }
                }
                set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAmbient
            }
        }
        flags & flags_to_check
    }

    ; getDeclarationSpaces, whole but for its ALIAS arm.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REFERENCE PANICS ON A KIND IT DOES NOT LIST, so §3.5bk applies with
    ; its usual force: a `return DeclarationSpacesNone` default would be a
    ; well-formed WRONG answer — no space means no intersection means no report —
    ; and the whole check would go quiet for a declaration kind nobody thought of.
    ; The default reports instead, with the kind in the detail, which is how the
    ; histogram would name it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ALIAS ARM IS THE ONE HOLE AND IT IS FOUR KINDS PLUS A FALLTHROUGH.
    ; ImportEqualsDeclaration, NamespaceImport and ImportClause resolve the alias
    ; and OR the spaces of everything the target declares — `c.resolveAlias`, i.e.
    ; the whole of getTargetOfAliasDeclaration, which is the `check-alias-symbol`
    ; row of the work list. The fallthrough from ExportAssignment/BinaryExpression
    ; joins them when the exported expression is an entity name AND the symbol
    ; carries the Alias flag, and the two halves of that test are not equally cheap:
    ; the flag read is ours already, the entity-name test is ported, so the arm can
    ; tell an alias export from a value export and only the alias half stops.
    function declaration_spaces(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType
        if k = KindJSDocTypedefTag
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType
        if k = KindJSDocCallbackTag
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType

        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if Binder.is_ambient_module(node)
                return DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace | DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            if b.get_module_instance_state(node) <> ModuleInstanceStateNonInstantiated
                return DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace | DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            return DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace
        }

        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType | DeclarationSpacesExportValue
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType | DeclarationSpacesExportValue
        if k = KindEnumMember
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType | DeclarationSpacesExportValue

        if k = KindSourceFile
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType | DeclarationSpacesExportValue | DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace

        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
        if k = KindBindingElement
            return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
        if k = KindImportSpecifier
            return DeclarationSpacesExportValue

        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return DeclarationSpacesExportType

        ; The export-assignment pair, whose `fallthrough` into the alias arm is the
        ; reference's only one in this switch. `!IsEntityNameExpression(expression)
        ; || symbol.Flags&Alias == 0` answers ExportValue and RETURNS; otherwise the
        ; three import kinds' body runs.
        var is_alias_arm false
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            set is_alias_arm: true
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            set is_alias_arm: true
        if is_alias_arm
        {
            var expression: ref[AstNode]? null
            if k = KindExportAssignment
                set expression: AstNode.expression_of(node)
            else
                set expression: AstNode.binary_right_of(node)
            if expression = null
                return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            if Binder.is_entity_name_expression(expression) = false
                return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            let symbol this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
            if symbol = null
                return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            if (Symbol.flags_of(symbol) & SymbolFlagsAlias) = 0
                return DeclarationSpacesExportValue
            this.record_unported("resolve-alias", k)
            return DeclarationSpacesNone
        }

        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
        {
            this.record_unported("resolve-alias", k)
            return DeclarationSpacesNone
        }
        if k = KindNamespaceImport
        {
            this.record_unported("resolve-alias", k)
            return DeclarationSpacesNone
        }
        if k = KindImportClause
        {
            this.record_unported("resolve-alias", k)
            return DeclarationSpacesNone
        }

        this.record_unported("declaration-spaces", k)
        DeclarationSpacesNone
    }

    ; checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations, whole — slice 64's largest head.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT IT CHECKS IS A DECLARATION *MERGE*, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A HEAD OF FIVE
    ; ARMS AND NOT A CHECK OF ONE KIND. `interface I {}` twice is one symbol with two
    ; declarations, and so is an interface merged with a namespace, a class with a
    ; namespace, an enum with an enum. The rule is that all declarations of one name
    ; agree on being exported or being local, per SPACE — so the function ORs each
    ; declaration's spaces into one of three accumulators (exported, non-exported,
    ; default-exported) and reports where two of them intersect.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST LINE IS `node.LocalSymbol()` AND THE SLOT DID NOT EXIST. See
    ; AstNode.local_symbol and the binder's declare_module_member for the whole of
    ; that: the slot had been refused with §3.5be's rule and a survey that covered
    ; internal/binder only, so *no reader in the whole reference* was a statement
    ; about one package. This function is the reader.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TWO SYMBOLS ARE ASKED IN THE ORDER THE REFERENCE ASKS THEM, and the
    ; order is the meaning: a node with a LOCAL symbol is a node that is itself
    ; exported (declareModuleMember gave it both halves), so the check runs on it
    ; directly. Only when there is no local symbol does it fall back to the node's
    ; own symbol and ask whether that symbol has an EXPORT — because a
    ; non-exported declaration can still be one of several declarations of a name
    ; another declaration exports, and that is exactly the collision being looked
    ; for. A pure local symbol returns.
    ;
    ; ★ `GetDeclarationOfKind(symbol, node.Kind) != node` is the once-per-list
    ; guard, and it is a NODE identity test rather than a bit: the first declaration
    ; of this kind runs the check and the others return, so a symbol with three
    ; interface declarations reports once per offending declaration rather than
    ; three times over. It needs no side table, which is why this head has none
    ; while its neighbours in the interface and enum arms do.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENTS ARE NOT PORTED, and that is the harness rather than
    ; a shortcut: `scanner.DeclarationNameToString(name)` fills the `{0}` of both
    ; codes, and the oracle's C section is `pos end code` and nothing else, so the
    ; text is invisible to every instrument here. Stated at the site because a
    ; missing argument reads exactly like a forgotten one.
    ; ast.GetNameOfDeclaration, asked of the BINDER — and it is asked rather than
    ; substituted, which is §3.5br's rule at its own site.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `AstNode.name_of` WOULD HAVE BEEN THE NARROWED ACCESSOR AGAIN. The
    ; reporting loop of checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations walks the symbol's
    ; declarations, and getDeclarationSpaces has arms for ExportAssignment and
    ; BinaryExpression — two kinds with NO name slot, whose name is their expression
    ; or their assigned-to left operand. Slice 29 paid for exactly this substitution
    ; once, on `export = x` twice, where the span was eleven characters instead of
    ; one; the difference survives here for the same reason it survived there, so it
    ; is spent up front rather than found by a corpus.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT TRANSLATES THE BINDER'S REPORT INTO THE CHECKER'S, which is the layering
    ; this file keeps everywhere else (see grammar_error_on_node and
    ; b.error_range_for_node): get_name_of_declaration is a PROCEDURE that reports on
    ; the BINDER for the three GetAssignedName kinds, and the binder's report is read
    ; at dump time BEFORE the check runs — so a hole met here would be invisible in
    ; both columns. The checker records its own.
    procedure name_of_declaration(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let name b.get_name_of_declaration(n)
        if b.is_unported()
        {
            this.record_unported("name-of-declaration-assigned", AstNode.kind_of(n))
            return null
        }
        name
    }

    procedure check_exports_on_merged_declarations(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        var symbol AstNode.local_symbol_of(node)
        if symbol = null
        {
            set symbol: this.symbol_of_declaration(node)
            if symbol = null
                return
            ; ★★★ THIS EARLY RETURN IS A CONDITION AND NOT A SHORTCUT, and the
            ; battery had to reach stage 2 to say so. The reference's comment reads
            ; *this is a pure local symbol (all declarations are non-exported) — no
            ; need to check anything*, which invites the reading that removing it
            ; would only waste a loop over one accumulator and two empties. Control
            ; g04 removes it: silent over 1 202 units, **RED 1 over 17 972**. An
            ; ambient container makes effective_declaration_flags answer Export for
            ; some of a purely local symbol's declarations and not for others, so the
            ; intersection is not empty and the check invents a report.
            if Symbol.export_symbol_of(symbol) = null
                return
        }

        if Checker.declaration_of_kind(symbol, AstNode.kind_of(node)) <> node
            return

        ; ★ Typed rather than `var x DeclarationSpacesNone`: an untyped binding
        ; PEELS its first operand as a type annotation, so a define constant in that
        ; position is read as a TYPE NAME and the initializer disappears.
        var exported_spaces: int DeclarationSpacesNone
        var non_exported_spaces: int DeclarationSpacesNone
        var default_exported_spaces: int DeclarationSpacesNone
        let count Symbol.declaration_count(symbol)
        var i 0
        while i < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, i)
            if d <> null
            {
                let spaces this.declaration_spaces(d)
                let effective this.effective_declaration_flags(d, ModifierFlagsExport | ModifierFlagsDefault)
                if (effective & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                {
                    if (effective & ModifierFlagsDefault) <> 0
                        set default_exported_spaces: default_exported_spaces | spaces
                    else
                        set exported_spaces: exported_spaces | spaces
                }
                else
                    set non_exported_spaces: non_exported_spaces | spaces
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }

        let non_default_exported_spaces exported_spaces | non_exported_spaces
        let common_exports_and_locals exported_spaces & non_exported_spaces
        let common_default_and_non_default default_exported_spaces & non_default_exported_spaces
        var intersects false
        if common_exports_and_locals <> 0
            set intersects: true
        if common_default_and_non_default <> 0
            set intersects: true
        if intersects = false
            return

        var j 0
        while j < count
        {
            let d Symbol.declaration_at(symbol, j)
            if d <> null
            {
                let spaces this.declaration_spaces(d)
                let name this.name_of_declaration(d)
                ; ★★★ THE NULL GUARD IS A DEVIATION THAT CANNOT BE OBSERVED, AND
                ; SAYING WHICH IS THE POINT. The reference calls `c.error(name, …)`
                ; with no guard, and NewDiagnosticForNode on a nil node builds a
                ; diagnostic with a nil FILE and a zero range — which addDiagnostic
                ; keeps, and which `GetDiagnosticsForFile(sourceFile.FileName())`
                ; then never returns, because it is filed under no file name at all.
                ; So the reference emits a report the oracle cannot see and this port
                ; emits none: the same answer through two different mechanisms, which
                ; is exactly the shape that must be written down rather than left to
                ; look like agreement.
                if name <> null
                {
                    if (spaces & common_default_and_non_default) <> 0
                        this.error_on_node(name, DiagMerged_declaration_0_cannot_include_a_default_export_declaration_Consider_adding_a_separate_export_default_0_declaration_instead)
                    else
                    {
                        if (spaces & common_exports_and_locals) <> 0
                            this.error_on_node(name, DiagIndividual_declarations_in_merged_declaration_0_must_be_all_exported_or_all_local)
                    }
                }
            }
            set j: j + 1
        }
    }

    ; ── the grammar checks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.hasParseDiagnostics(sourceFile), whole: `len(sourceFile.Diagnostics()) > 0`.
    ; ★ It is the PARSE list and not the JS-SYNTAX one: sf.Diagnostics() does not
    ; include the second list (tscaly_ast.scaly's dump has them as separate
    ; sections for exactly that reason), so a file whose only complaint is a
    ; TS8000-series JS-syntax error still gets its grammar checks.
    function has_parse_diagnostics(this) returns bool
        AstNode.diagnostic_count(file) > 0

    ; grammarErrorOnFirstToken, whole. The span is the FIRST TOKEN at the node's
    ; own pos — not GetErrorRangeForNode, so no declaration name and no
    ; trivia-skip-then-name is involved — which is the same distinction
    ; binder.error_on_first_token draws one phase earlier.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PARSE-DIAGNOSTIC GUARD IS NOT A TIDINESS PASS. A file that failed to
    ; parse has a tree the grammar checks would read as a stream of violations, so
    ; the reference suppresses ALL of them and answers false — and the caller reads
    ; that false as *no grammar error here*, which is how `checkVariableStatement`'s
    ; `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && …` chain keeps running. A port that
    ; reported anyway would add diagnostics on exactly the units the parser
    ; yardstick already calls broken.
    function grammar_error_on_first_token(this, n: ref[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        b.range_of_token_at_position(AstNode.pos_of(n), &start, &stop)
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
        true
    }

    ; grammarErrorAtPos, whole — the third of the three reporters, and the one that
    ; reports at a span NO NODE has.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT TAKES NO NODE. Upstream's first parameter is `nodeForSourceFile` and
    ; its only use is `ast.GetSourceFileOfNode(node)`: this checker has exactly one
    ; file and reads it from its own field, so a parameter for it would be a second
    ; thing to keep in step (the same reasoning `has_parse_diagnostics` above
    ; follows, where the reference threads the file and this does not).
    ;
    ; ★ `length` rather than an end, because that is the reference's shape and the
    ; two callers below both pass a literal — `len(",")` and a computed `end-start`.
    function grammar_error_at_pos(this, start: int, length: int, code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, start + length, code)
        true
    }

    ; ── three null-safe readers the grammar checks need ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ The reference reads `node.Parent.Kind` and `node.Parent.Flags` straight
    ; out of checkGrammarModifiers, six times, because in Go a nil Parent field is
    ; a readable zero — `AstNode.kind_of` here DEREFERENCES. The assumption behind
    ; the reference's spelling is true for every node these checks reach (a bound
    ; declaration has a parent), but an assumption that is true today and crashes
    ; when it stops being true is exactly the shape this port keeps paying for, so
    ; the question is asked instead of assumed. KindUnknown is 0 and matches no
    ; kind test, which is what makes the substitution exact.
    function kind_or_unknown(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        AstNode.kind_of(n)
    }

    function flags_or_none(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return NodeFlagsNone
        AstNode.flags_of(n)
    }

    function is_ambient_module_or_null(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
    }

    ; grammarErrorOnNode, whole:
    ;
    ;   if !c.hasParseDiagnostics(sourceFile) { c.error(node, message); return true }
    ;   return false
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS NOT grammar_error_on_first_token WITH ANOTHER SPAN. `c.error` goes
    ; through NewDiagnosticForNode, i.e. scanner.GetErrorRangeForNode — the same
    ; function the BINDER's error_on_node uses — so a declaration reports at its
    ; NAME and everything else at its own trivia-skipped start. The distinction is
    ; the one binder.scaly draws between its two reporters, one phase earlier, and
    ; the two sit ten lines apart there for the same reason they do here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE SPAN MACHINERY IS THE BINDER'S, WHICH IS WHERE SLICE 45's NOTE
    ; SAID THIS BILL WOULD ARRIVE. error_range_for_node's eight deferred arms carry
    ; the line *THE READER THAT EARNS THEM IS THE CHECKER … the checker dimension's
    ; first bill*; this is the caller it named. It answers false on one of those
    ; arms, and then the honest move is to report UNPORTED rather than to invent a
    ; span: a wrong span is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, and the
    ; fifth yardstick compares spans. ★For the kinds this slice reaches the arm
    ; cannot fire — a modifier is a keyword token and the five node kinds are not
    ; on the list — so the report is a guard against the next slice rather than a
    ; live path, which is exactly why it is written now.
    function grammar_error_on_node(this, n: ref[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        if b.error_range_for_node(n, &start, &stop) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-error-range", AstNode.kind_of(n))
            return false
        }
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
        true
    }

    ; c.error, whole: `NewDiagnosticForNode(location, message)` then addDiagnostic.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS grammar_error_on_node WITHOUT THE PARSE GUARD, and that one missing
    ; line is the whole difference between the two reporters. A grammar check is
    ; suppressed on a file that failed to parse — its tree would read as a stream
    ; of violations — while a SEMANTIC error is not, because the reference reports
    ; those regardless. So the two cannot share an implementation, and the span
    ; machinery they do share is the binder's error_range_for_node.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference returns the *Diagnostic, for the two callers that attach
    ; related information to it (addTypeOnlyDeclarationRelatedInfo). Neither is in
    ; this slice's prefix, and the yardstick compares no related information —
    ; tscaly_types dumps `C pos end code` — so this answers nothing. The first
    ; caller that needs the value is the one that owes the return type.
    procedure error_on_node(this, n: ref[AstNode], code: int)
    {
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        if b.error_range_for_node(n, &start, &stop) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("error-range", AstNode.kind_of(n))
            return
        }
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarModuleElementContext — slice 50's head ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ SIX LINES, AND IT IS THE HEAD OF SIX ARMS. Slice 49 could take the five
    ; kinds whose arm BEGINS with checkGrammarModifiers and had to leave the
    ; import/export/module family, because this function stands in front of that
    ; call there and may RETURN — porting the modifier check into those arms
    ; without it would have run it where the reference does not.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE RESULT IS NOT THE REPORT'S RESULT, and the distinction is load-bearing
    ; in exactly the case grammar_error_on_first_token exists for. Upstream answers
    ; `!isInAppropriateContext`, so on a file that failed to parse the report is
    ; suppressed and this still answers TRUE — the caller bails out either way,
    ; which is what its comment asks for (*just bail out to avoid cascading
    ; errors*). Answering the report's bool instead would run the whole arm on a
    ; broken tree.
    ;
    ; ★ The three appropriate parents are read null-safely (kind_or_unknown) for
    ; the reason its own note gives: Go reads a nil Parent as a zero and
    ; AstNode.kind_of dereferences.
    function check_grammar_module_element_context(this, node: ref[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
        var in_appropriate_context false
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if pk = KindModuleDeclaration
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if in_appropriate_context
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, code)
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarExportDeclaration, whole. `export type { T }` may not repeat the
    ; `type` on a specifier.
    function check_grammar_export_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.is_type_only_of(node) = false
            return false
        let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(node)
        if clause = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(clause) <> KindNamedExports
            return false
        this.check_grammar_type_only_named_imports_or_exports(clause)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeOnlyNamedImportsOrExports. The reference's two branches read
    ; the SAME field (`specifier.IsTypeOnly()`) and differ only in the message, so
    ; the port keeps one read and picks the code — which is the §3.5n test applied
    ; to a branch rather than to a record.
    ;
    ; ★ It reports on the FIRST type-only specifier and returns, so a clause with
    ; two of them answers one diagnostic. That is the reference's `return` inside
    ; the loop and not a dedup.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE IMPORT SIDE IS PORTED AND UNREACHABLE FROM THIS SLICE, which is worth
    ; saying so it does not read as measured: its only caller is
    ; checkGrammarImportClause, which sits behind checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration
    ; in checkImportDeclaration — the call this slice reports. It goes live with
    ; that one. Ported anyway, because this is ONE function and splitting it by
    ; caller would be a partial port of a function rather than a smaller slice.
    function check_grammar_type_only_named_imports_or_exports(this, named_bindings: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let els AstNode.elements_of(named_bindings)
        let n AstNode.list_count(els)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let specifier AstNode.child_in_list(els, i)
            var code: int DiagThe_type_modifier_cannot_be_used_on_a_named_export_when_export_type_is_used_on_its_export_statement
            if AstNode.kind_of(specifier) = KindImportSpecifier
                set code: DiagThe_type_modifier_cannot_be_used_on_a_named_import_when_import_type_is_used_on_its_import_statement
            if AstNode.is_type_only_of(specifier)
                return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(specifier, code)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; shouldCheckErasableSyntax, whole. ★ ErasableSyntaxOnly is unset under this
    ; harness, so this is false everywhere and its two readers carry LIVE code
    ; behind it rather than a report — see the field's note for why that is the
    ; right side of the line.
    function should_check_erasable_syntax(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if erasable_syntax_only = false
            return false
        Parser.is_in_js_file(node) = false
    }

    ; ── the class-like grammar checks — slice 51's head ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HEAD IS TWO LINES AND THE BILL IS A SIDE TABLE. checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration
    ; is `checkGrammarClassDeclarationHeritageClauses || checkGrammarTypeParameterList`,
    ; and BOTH halves report at a span that only a NodeList's own TextRange knows:
    ; `HasTrailingComma()` is `last.End() < list.End()` and an EMPTY bracketed list
    ; reports from `list.Pos()-len("<")` to `SkipTrivia(text, list.End())+len(">")`.
    ; This port's lists are plain Arrays, so §3.5bq's side table — built in slice 24
    ; for three JS-syntax arms of the PARSER — is the mechanism these checks need,
    ; and a Checker holds no Parser. It travels on the SourceFile now, beside the
    ; two diagnostic lists (ast.scaly's ListRange has the argument).
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND IT IS THE FIRST TIME ANY INSTRUMENT IN THIS PACKAGE CAN SEE A LIST'S
    ; RANGE. No yardstick dumps one — §3.5bq says so and that is exactly why the
    ; table was built with its own measurement rather than a test — so until this
    ; slice a wrong row and a right row compared equal everywhere. Three of the
    ; reports below are the first readers that can tell them apart.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PARSE GUARD IS HOISTED IN FRONT OF EVERY RANGE LOOKUP, and that is an
    ; equivalence rather than a shortcut. Each of these checks computes a span and
    ; then hands it to grammar_error_at_pos, which answers false on a file that
    ; failed to parse; computing the span has no side effects, so testing the guard
    ; first gives the same answer for every input. It buys two things. A MISSING
    ; list — parse_bracketed_list's sentinel, handed out when the opening bracket
    ; was absent — carries no row, and it cannot arrive here: the only way to get
    ; one is a failed parse_expected, which reported. And a missing row is then
    ; reported only where it could have changed an answer.
    function list_range_of(this, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], out_pos: pointer[int], out_end: pointer[int]) returns bool
        AstNode.list_range_of(file, list, out_pos, out_end)

    ; checkGrammarForDisallowedTrailingComma, whole. `list.HasTrailingComma()` is
    ; DERIVED upstream too — `len(Nodes) > 0 && last.End() < list.End()` — so the
    ; row is the only thing this needs that the elements do not already carry.
    ;
    ; ★ The message is a parameter here as it is upstream, because the two callers
    ; pass different ones; the reference's second argument (`list.Nodes[0]`) is the
    ; source-file lookup grammar_error_at_pos does not take.
    function check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(this, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], code: int) returns bool
    {
        let n AstNode.list_count(list)
        if n = 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(list, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        if AstNode.end_of(AstNode.child_in_list(list, n - 1)) >= lend
            return false
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(lend - 1, 1, code)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeParameterList, whole. It fires on a list that EXISTS and is
    ; EMPTY — `class C<> {}`, which parses cleanly, which is the whole reason the
    ; grammar check exists rather than a parse diagnostic.
    ;
    ; ★ `- len("<")` and `+ len(">")` are the reference's own spelling of the two
    ; brackets the list's range sits inside; the trivia skip in between is why the
    ; end cannot be taken from the range directly.
    function check_grammar_type_parameter_list(this, type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns bool
    {
        if type_parameters = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(type_parameters) <> 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(type_parameters, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        let start lpos - 1
        let stop b.skip_trivia_at(lend) + 1
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(start, stop - start, DiagType_parameter_list_cannot_be_empty)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForAtLeastOneTypeArgument — the same shape one message along.
    ; Upstream asks GetSourceFileOfNode(node) for the text and the report's file;
    ; this checker has one file and the parameter goes with it.
    function check_grammar_for_at_least_one_type_argument(this, type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns bool
    {
        if type_arguments = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(type_arguments) <> 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(type_arguments, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        let start lpos - 1
        let stop b.skip_trivia_at(lend) + 1
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(start, stop - start, DiagType_argument_list_cannot_be_empty)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeArguments, whole — the `||` of the two above, in that order.
    function check_grammar_type_arguments(this, type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(type_arguments, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true
        this.check_grammar_for_at_least_one_type_argument(type_arguments)
    }

    ; checkGrammarExpressionWithTypeArguments, whole. Its parameter is upstream's
    ; `Union[ExpressionWithTypeArguments, TypeQuery]`, and type_arguments_of answers
    ; both — which is one of the nine arms slice 30 widened it to (§3.5br).
    function check_grammar_expression_with_type_arguments(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.expression_of(node)) = KindImportKeyword
            {
                if AstNode.type_arguments_of(node) <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagThis_use_of_import_is_invalid_import_calls_can_be_written_but_they_must_have_parentheses_and_cannot_have_type_arguments)
            }
        }
        this.check_grammar_type_arguments(AstNode.type_arguments_of(node))
    }

    ; checkGrammarHeritageClause, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENT IS DROPPED, on checkGrammarModifiers' argument one
    ; screen up: `{0} list cannot be empty` is filled from
    ; scanner.TokenToString(node.Token), a message's CODE does not depend on its
    ; arguments, and the dump is `C pos end code`. TokenToString is therefore still
    ; unported, and the bill for it is named where that note names it.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference reaches `types.Nodes` unguarded after the empty test, so a nil
    ; types would panic there; the guard here is not a divergence but the question
    ; asked instead of assumed — parse_heritage_clause always builds the list, which
    ; is why upstream can write it that way.
    function check_grammar_heritage_clause(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let types AstNode.heritage_clause_types_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(types, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true
        if types = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(types) = 0
        {
            if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
                return false
            var lpos 0
            var lend 0
            if this.list_range_of(types, &lpos, &lend) = false
            {
                this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
                return false
            }
            ; grammarErrorAtPos(node, types.Pos(), 0, …) — a ZERO-WIDTH span at the
            ; list's own start, with the reference's own `why not error on the
            ; token?` left standing beside it.
            return this.grammar_error_at_pos(lpos, 0, DiagX_0_list_cannot_be_empty)
        }
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(types)
        while i < n
        {
            if this.check_grammar_expression_with_type_arguments(AstNode.child_in_list(types, i))
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarClassDeclarationHeritageClauses, whole but one loop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE JSDOC `@augments` WALK IS UNREACHABLE HERE, BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER
    ; THAN BY THE CORPUS. Upstream runs `for _, j := range node.EagerJSDoc(file)`
    ; over the extends clause's single type, and EagerJSDoc answers nil unless
    ; NodeFlagsHasJSDoc is set — while check_source_element_worker reports
    ; `check-jsdoc-comments` and RETURNS for any node carrying that flag, one call
    ; before this arm runs. So the loop's body cannot execute, and it is omitted
    ; with its condition written down rather than ported as dead code: what it
    ; needs beyond the walk is getIdentifierFromEntityNameExpression, the tag's
    ; ClassName and a three-argument message. ★It expires the moment the JSDoc
    ; comment check is ported — that gate is the whole of the argument.
    ;
    ; ★★ UPSTREAM PANICS ON A HERITAGE TOKEN THAT IS NEITHER `extends` NOR
    ; `implements`, and the else branch here simply takes the implements reading.
    ; parse_heritage_clause is reached only on one of those two keywords and writes
    ; the token it saw, so the third case does not exist on either side; a trap
    ; would be a claim about a shape the parser cannot build.
    ;
    ; ★ `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && classLikeData.HeritageClauses != nil` is
    ; a short-circuit whose LEFT side is the call, so the modifier check always
    ; runs — the same shape checkImportDeclaration's has, and the reason a class
    ; with no heritage clause at all still reports its modifier errors.
    function check_grammar_class_declaration_heritage_clauses(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var seen_extends_clause false
        var seen_implements_clause false
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
            return false
        let clauses AstNode.heritage_clauses_of(node)
        if clauses = null
            return false
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        while i < n
        {
            let clause AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.heritage_clause_token_of(clause) = KindExtendsKeyword
            {
                if seen_extends_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_extends_clause_already_seen)
                if seen_implements_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_extends_clause_must_precede_implements_clause)
                let types AstNode.heritage_clause_types_of(clause)
                if AstNode.list_count(types) > 1
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(AstNode.child_in_list(types, 1), DiagClasses_can_only_extend_a_single_class)
                set seen_extends_clause: true
            }
            else
            {
                if seen_implements_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_implements_clause_already_seen)
                set seen_implements_clause: true
            }
            ; The reference DISCARDS this result — it is a statement of its own,
            ; after the extends/implements bookkeeping — so a report inside one
            ; clause does not stop the walk over the rest.
            this.check_grammar_heritage_clause(clause)
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration, whole: two calls and an `||`.
    function check_grammar_class_like_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_class_declaration_heritage_clauses(node)
            return true
        this.check_grammar_type_parameter_list(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 57: the interface's grammar head ───────────────────────────────

    ; checkGrammarInterfaceDeclaration, whole. One loop over the heritage clauses,
    ; and it is the smaller half of the class version above: an interface may have
    ; ONE `extends` and no `implements` at all, so there is no
    ; extends-must-precede-implements order to keep and no single-base rule — an
    ; interface's extends clause is allowed several types.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CLASS VERSION CALLS checkGrammarModifiers ITSELF AND THIS ONE DOES
    ; NOT, which is why the two heads cannot share a body however alike the loops
    ; look. Upstream's checkGrammarClassDeclarationHeritageClauses opens with
    ; `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && …`; here the modifier check is the
    ; CALLER's line — `if !c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) { c.checkGrammarInterfaceDeclaration(…) }`
    ; — so folding it in would run it twice for the class and put the interface's
    ; heritage report behind a condition it does not have.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `implements` ARM RETURNS ON THE REPORT'S RESULT, and on a file that
    ; failed to parse that result is FALSE — so the loop walks on into the next
    ; clause rather than stopping. That is the reference's own shape
    ; (`return c.grammarErrorOnFirstToken(...)`, not `report; return true`), and it
    ; is the same distinction check_grammar_module_element_context's header argues
    ; from the other side, where the answer must NOT be the report's.
    ;
    ; ★ Upstream PANICS on a heritage token that is neither `extends` nor
    ; `implements`; the else branch takes the implements reading, for the reason
    ; the class version's header gives — parse_heritage_clause is reached only on
    ; one of those two keywords and writes the token it saw, so the third case does
    ; not exist on either side.
    ;
    ; ★ checkGrammarHeritageClause's result is DISCARDED here as it is in the class
    ; version, and for the same reason: it is a statement of its own after the
    ; extends bookkeeping, so a report inside one clause does not stop the walk.
    function check_grammar_interface_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let clauses AstNode.heritage_clauses_of(node)
        if clauses = null
            return false
        var seen_extends_clause false
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        while i < n
        {
            let clause AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.heritage_clause_token_of(clause) = KindExtendsKeyword
            {
                if seen_extends_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_extends_clause_already_seen)
                set seen_extends_clause: true
            }
            else
                return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagInterface_declaration_cannot_have_implements_clause)
            this.check_grammar_heritage_clause(clause)
        }
        false
    }

    ; ── slice 52: the function-like grammar checks ───────────────────────────

    ; checkGrammarParameterList, whole. Four of its five reports are about the
    ; REST parameter and the fifth is the optional/required order; every span is a
    ; node this port already has, so nothing here needs the side table the type
    ; parameter list above needs.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TRAILING-COMMA CALL IS THE ONLY ONE WHOSE RESULT IS DISCARDED, and
    ; that is the reference's own shape: a rest parameter with a trailing comma
    ; reports TS1013 and the walk CONTINUES into the question-mark and initializer
    ; tests of the same parameter, so a `...a?,` yields two diagnostics. Reading
    ; that call as a `return` would drop the second.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AMBIENT GUARD IS ON THE PARAMETER, NOT ON THE FILE. `parameter.Flags
    ; & NodeFlagsAmbient == 0` — the flag is propagated down from the `declare`, so
    ; in a .d.ts every parameter carries it and the trailing comma is allowed there.
    ; A file-level test would be the same answer for the corpus's declaration files
    ; and a wrong one for a `declare function` in a .ts.
    ;
    ; ★ isOptionalDeclaration is ast.HasQuestionToken, which is
    ; `IsQuestionToken(node.QuestionToken())` — a null test plus a kind test, and
    ; question_token_of already answers null for anything that is not one (its
    ; postfix arm checks the kind). So the second arm's own `parameter.QuestionToken
    ; != nil` is not a second question: it is the same one, re-asked because
    ; upstream's helper took the node and its body wants the token.
    function check_grammar_parameter_list(this, parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) returns bool
    {
        if parameters = null
            return false
        var seen_optional_parameter false
        let parameter_count AstNode.list_count(parameters)
        var i 0
        while i < parameter_count
        {
            let parameter AstNode.child_in_list(parameters, i)
            let index i
            set i: i + 1
            let dot_dot_dot AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(parameter)
            let question AstNode.question_token_of(parameter)
            let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(parameter)
            if dot_dot_dot <> null
            {
                if index <> parameter_count - 1
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(dot_dot_dot, DiagA_rest_parameter_must_be_last_in_a_parameter_list)
                if (AstNode.flags_of(parameter) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                    this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(parameters, DiagA_rest_parameter_or_binding_pattern_may_not_have_a_trailing_comma)
                if question <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(question, DiagA_rest_parameter_cannot_be_optional)
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagA_rest_parameter_cannot_have_an_initializer)
            }
            else
            {
                if question <> null
                {
                    set seen_optional_parameter: true
                    ; `parameter.QuestionToken.Flags & NodeFlagsReparsed == 0` — a
                    ; REPARSED `?` is the bracketed name of a JSDoc `@param [x]`
                    ; tag, which is not a syntax error to report at. ★It cannot be
                    ; reached from this slice's arm and is ported anyway: a `@param`
                    ; tag puts NodeFlagsHasJSDoc on the function, and
                    ; check_source_element_worker returns on that flag one call
                    ; earlier. So no unit separates the guard from its absence —
                    ; §3.5cv's `@augments` shape, expiring with the same slice.
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(question) & NodeFlagsReparsed) = 0
                    {
                        if initializer <> null
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagParameter_cannot_have_question_mark_and_initializer)
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    if seen_optional_parameter
                    {
                        if initializer = null
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagA_required_parameter_cannot_follow_an_optional_parameter)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarArrowFunction's GUARD, and nothing behind it — the shape
    ; check_decorators has, and for the same reason: the guard is what makes the
    ; rest unreachable from this slice's one arm, so porting it is what lets the
    ; `||` chain above it run to completion.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS IS WHERE THE ECMA LINE MAP WOULD BE PAID FOR, AND IT IS NOT REACHED.
    ; Behind the guard stand two reports: the `.mts`/`.cts` reserved-syntax warning
    ; for a single type parameter without a constraint (which needs
    ; tspath.FileExtensionIsOneOf, and a list's HasTrailingComma, i.e. §3.5cv's side
    ; table), and *line terminator not permitted before arrow*, whose condition is
    ; GetECMALineOfPosition on both ends of the `=>` token — the LAST of
    ; error_range_for_node's eight deferred arms (§3.5bx). An arrow function is not
    ; a statement, so no arm of this slice can produce one; the report names the
    ; function rather than either half, because which of them comes first depends on
    ; the file's extension.
    function check_grammar_arrow_function(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindArrowFunction
            return false
        this.record_unported("check-grammar-arrow-function", KindArrowFunction)
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarForUseStrictSimpleParameterList, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LANGUAGE-VERSION GATE IS LIVE, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE READING OF THE
    ; ONE `check-decorators` PARKED ON. That report stands behind an option the
    ; harness leaves UNSET and whose default is FALSE, so the branch is dead; this
    ; gate is `c.languageVersion >= core.ScriptTargetES2016`, and languageVersion is
    ; `compilerOptions.GetEmitScriptTarget()`, which answers
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard — ES2025, the HIGHEST standard target — when Target
    ; is unset. So an unset option makes this branch always TAKEN. Reading "the
    ; option is unset" as "the branch is dead" is a wrong answer half the time, and
    ; which half depends on the reference's own default. The same default is what
    ; kills the two checkExternalEmitHelpers calls in check_signature_declaration:
    ; `languageVersion < ES2018` is false at ES2025.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE RELATED INFO IS DROPPED AND THE DIAGNOSTICS ARE NOT. Upstream attaches
    ; `'use strict' directive used here` to each parameter report and
    ; `Non-simple parameter declared here` / `and here` to the directive's, through
    ; AddRelatedInfo — which ast.NewDiagnostic keeps ON the diagnostic rather than in
    ; the list, so no dump reaches it (the same argument check_grammar_modifiers'
    ; header makes about a message's ARGUMENTS). What the dump does see is one TS1346
    ; per non-simple parameter plus one TS1347 on the directive, and that is what
    ; this emits.
    ;
    ; ★ The filter is walked TWICE upstream (once to report on each parameter, once
    ; to attach the related info in index order) and once here, because the second
    ; walk produces nothing this port keeps. `core.Filter` therefore needs no list:
    ; the FIRST non-simple parameter decides whether anything is reported at all, so
    ; the loop counts and reports in one pass and the directive's report follows it.
    function check_grammar_for_use_strict_simple_parameter_list(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        ; c.languageVersion >= core.ScriptTargetES2016 — always true here, see the
        ; header. Written as the condition it is rather than dropped, because a
        ; harness that reads @target turns it back into a question.
        let body AstNode.body_of(node)
        if body = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(body) <> KindBlock
            return false
        let use_strict_directive b.find_use_strict_prologue(AstNode.statements_of(body))
        if use_strict_directive = null
            return false
        let parameters AstNode.parameters_of(node)
        if parameters = null
            return false
        var non_simple 0
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(parameters)
        while i < n
        {
            let parameter AstNode.child_in_list(parameters, i)
            set i: i + 1
            var is_non_simple false
            if AstNode.initializer_of(parameter) <> null
                set is_non_simple: true
            if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(parameter))
                set is_non_simple: true
            if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(parameter) <> null
                set is_non_simple: true
            if is_non_simple
            {
                set non_simple: non_simple + 1
                this.error_on_node(parameter, DiagThis_parameter_is_not_allowed_with_use_strict_directive)
            }
        }
        if non_simple = 0
            return false
        this.error_on_node(use_strict_directive, DiagX_use_strict_directive_cannot_be_used_with_non_simple_parameter_list)
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarFunctionLikeDeclaration, whole: a five-term `||`, and the longest
    ; chain of grammar checks this port has run end to end (slice 51's
    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration is two).
    ;
    ; ★ The last term's `ast.IsFunctionLikeDeclaration(node)` guard is what keeps a
    ; CallSignature or a FunctionType — which have no body — out of the use-strict
    ; check; the two kinds that would reach it with a body and are not function-like
    ; DECLARATIONS do not exist.
    function check_grammar_function_like_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_type_parameter_list(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_parameter_list(AstNode.parameters_of(node))
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_arrow_function(node)
            return true
        if AstNode.is_function_like_declaration(node) = false
            return false
        this.check_grammar_for_use_strict_simple_parameter_list(node)
    }

    ; ── slice 62: the grammar of a type MEMBER ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ONE TERM NEITHER checkGrammarProperty NOR checkGrammarMethod CAN
    ; ANSWER, AND IT IS THE SAME TERM FIVE TIMES. `isNonBindableDynamicName` is
    ; `ast.IsDynamicName(node) && !c.isLateBindableName(node)`, and the right
    ; conjunct resolves the computed name's EXPRESSION — checkComputedPropertyName
    ; for a `[…]` name, checkExpressionCached for an element access — so it is the
    ; type dimension, one call deep, in a function whose other five reports are
    ; purely syntactic.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `&&` SHORT-CIRCUITS, WHICH IS WHY THE REPORT IS NARROW RATHER THAN
    ; PER MEMBER. Every member whose name is a plain identifier, a string, a number
    ; or a computed name over a LITERAL answers false at `IsDynamicName` and asks
    ; nothing — so the hole is exactly `[expr]` with a non-literal expr, which the
    ; binder already decides for its own late-binding (`b.is_dynamic_name`, ported
    ; in slice 30 and read here rather than written again).
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND IT ANSWERS **TRUE**, WHICH IS THE DIRECTION THAT SUPPRESSES. Every
    ; caller reads a true as *I have reported* and stops — so a true costs
    ; diagnostics and a false invents them: `checkGrammarProperty` would go on to
    ; the ambient initializer and the definite-assignment block, `checkGrammarMethod`
    ; to the computed-property-name check and the generator-constructor report, and
    ; diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, which forgives a missing line and fails
    ; on an invented one. §3.5cw's rule read from the other end: where the port
    ; cannot decide, pick the branch that speaks LESS.
    function check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(this, node: ref[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        if b.is_dynamic_name(node) = false
            return false
        ; isLateBindableName, and behind it the whole of checkComputedPropertyName.
        ; `IsEntityNameExpression(expression)` — the term that decides whether the
        ; report actually fires — sits BEHIND it and is not asked, because the answer
        ; is already unknowable.
        this.record_unported("is-late-bindable-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarComputedPropertyName, whole. Two lines: a kind test that makes the
    ; function a no-op for every name that is not computed, and one report about a
    ; COMMA expression inside the brackets.
    ;
    ; ★ It is NOT the dynamic-name check above and the two must not be confused: this
    ; one is decided by the expression's own kind and reports on the EXPRESSION,
    ; while that one needs the expression's TYPE and reports on the NAME. The
    ; reference calls them from the same `if`, which is what makes the pair look like
    ; one question.
    function check_grammar_computed_property_name(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(node) <> KindComputedPropertyName
            return false
        let expr AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(expr) <> KindBinaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.binary_operator_kind(expr) <> KindCommaToken
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(expr, DiagA_comma_expression_is_not_allowed_in_a_computed_property_name)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForInvalidQuestionMark and checkGrammarForInvalidExclamationToken,
    ; both whole and both one line: the POSTFIX slot holds a `?` or a `!` and the
    ; caller says which of the two it forbids and with which message. ast.scaly's
    ; question_token_of explains why the slot has to be tested for its kind rather
    ; than read.
    function check_grammar_for_invalid_question_mark(this, postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?, code: int) returns bool
    {
        if postfix_token = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(postfix_token) <> KindQuestionToken
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(postfix_token, code)
    }

    function check_grammar_for_invalid_exclamation_token(this, postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?, code: int) returns bool
    {
        if postfix_token = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(postfix_token) <> KindExclamationToken
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(postfix_token, code)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForGenerator, whole — the `*` of a generator in a place a body
    ; cannot follow.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS NAMED AND NOT WRITTEN BY SLICE 52, at check_function_declaration's
    ; own header: *checkGrammarForGenerator … follows the line above, so it is not
    ; reached; named here rather than left out so that the slice which retires the
    ; report above finds it already written down*. It is written now for a different
    ; caller — checkGrammarMethod, whose call is in FRONT of every stop — and the
    ; note there still holds unchanged: a function declaration still stops before it.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference reads the asterisk off `node.BodyData()` and panics for a kind
    ; that has none; `asterisk_token_of` answers null for exactly those kinds, so the
    ; nil test and the panic collapse into one null test (§3.5bk).
    function check_grammar_for_generator(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let asterisk AstNode.asterisk_token_of(node)
        if asterisk = null
            return false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(asterisk, DiagGenerators_are_not_allowed_in_an_ambient_context)
        if AstNode.body_of(node) <> null
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(asterisk, DiagAn_overload_signature_cannot_be_declared_as_a_generator)
    }

    ; checkGrammarProperty, whole. Its shape is a three-way branch on the PARENT —
    ; a class, an interface, a type literal — and the parent is what decides both
    ; which message a computed name gets and whether an initializer is admitted at
    ; all.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FIRST REPORT IS ABOUT A NODE THAT IS NOT THIS ONE, and it is the
    ; reason `member_list_of` exists in ast.scaly today: `{ [K in T]: U; x: number }`
    ; parses as a MAPPED type with a recovery member list, so a property whose
    ; computed name is a binary `in` expression reports on `node.Parent.Members()[0]`
    ; — the first member of the container, which need not be this property. ★It is
    ; the same diagnostic checkGrammarMappedType reports at the same node (slice 61),
    ; and the two are separate paths to it rather than one: that one fires for a
    ; mapped type WITH members, this one for a property whose own name carries the
    ; `in`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TWO `panic`s ARE NOT PORTED AND THE ARGUMENT IS ABOUT THE PARSER, not
    ; about a guess: `!ast.IsPropertySignatureDeclaration(node)` inside the interface
    ; and type-literal branches dies on a PropertyDeclaration there, and neither
    ; container's member production can build one — parseTypeMember has five member
    ; forms and a property DECLARATION is not among them. So the population is empty
    ; and what stands in its place is the initializer report the reference reaches
    ; after it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE `!` BLOCK IS PropertyDeclaration-ONLY AND IS THEREFORE INERT TODAY, in
    ; the shape slice 60's type-parameter arm was: nothing walks a class's members
    ; yet, so no PropertyDeclaration reaches this function. It is transcribed because
    ; the arm it belongs to is transcribed, and because a definite-assignment
    ; assertion is where a property's postfix slot stops being a `?`.
    ;
    ; ★ `IsAutoAccessorPropertyDeclaration` is `IsPropertyDeclaration && HasAccessorModifier`
    ; — so it is inert for the same reason, and it is the only reader of
    ; ModifierFlagsAccessor in this file.
    function check_grammar_property(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let property_name AstNode.name_of(node)
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(property_name) = KindComputedPropertyName
        {
            let expr AstNode.expression_of(property_name)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(expr) = KindBinaryExpression
            {
                if AstNode.binary_operator_kind(expr) = KindInKeyword
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.child_in_list(AstNode.member_list_of(parent), 0), DiagA_mapped_type_may_not_declare_properties_or_methods)
            }
        }
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(property_name) = KindStringLiteral
            {
                if Checker.literal_text_equals(property_name, "constructor", 11)
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(property_name, DiagClasses_may_not_have_a_field_named_constructor)
            }
            if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(property_name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_a_class_property_declaration_must_have_a_simple_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
                return true
            if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAccessor)
                {
                    if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_question_mark(AstNode.postfix_token_of(node), DiagAn_accessor_property_cannot_be_declared_optional)
                        return true
                }
            }
        }
        else
        {
            let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
            if pk = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            {
                if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(property_name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_an_interface_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
                    return true
                let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagAn_interface_property_cannot_have_an_initializer)
            }
            if pk = KindTypeLiteral
            {
                if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(property_name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_a_type_literal_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
                    return true
                let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagA_type_literal_property_cannot_have_an_initializer)
            }
        }

        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            this.check_ambient_initializer(node)

        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            let postfix AstNode.postfix_token_of(node)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(postfix) = KindExclamationToken
            {
                if AstNode.initializer_of(node) <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(postfix, DiagDeclarations_with_initializers_cannot_also_have_definite_assignment_assertions)
                if AstNode.type_of(node) = null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(postfix, DiagDeclarations_with_definite_assignment_assertions_must_also_have_type_annotations)
                var out_of_context false
                if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent) = false
                    set out_of_context: true
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    set out_of_context: true
                if b.is_static(node)
                    set out_of_context: true
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
                    set out_of_context: true
                if out_of_context
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(postfix, DiagA_definite_assignment_assertion_is_not_permitted_in_this_context)
            }
        }

        false
    }

    ; The literal twin of identifier_text_equals — same loop, the other text slot.
    ; ast.scaly's literal_text_of header says why the two cannot be one function.
    function literal_text_equals(n: ref[AstNode], s: pointer[const_char], len: int) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.literal_text_length_of(n) <> len
            return false
        let t AstNode.literal_text_of(n)
        if t = null
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if (*(t + i) as int) <> ((*(s + i) as char) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarMethod, whole. Its first line is the function-like grammar check
    ; and the rest is a branch on the PARENT, exactly as checkGrammarProperty's is —
    ; and the messages differ per container for the same reason.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE WHOLE MIDDLE BLOCK IS MethodDeclaration-ONLY AND IS INERT TODAY: an
    ; object literal's methods are reached through checkExpression and a class's
    ; through a members walk, and neither is ported. It is transcribed because the
    ; arm is (slice 60's argument for the type-parameter row), and because one of its
    ; reports is the only place in this file that reports on a `}` that is not
    ; there — `grammarErrorAtPos(node, node.End()-1, len(";"), X_0_expected, "{")`,
    ; a one-character span at the semicolon that stands where a body should be.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE class-like BRANCH ASKS TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS
    ; REACHABLE FROM A SIGNATURE. The ambient half is about a computed name in a
    ; `declare class`, the overload half about a method DECLARATION with no body; a
    ; MethodSignature in a class body is not a thing the parser builds, so what
    ; reaches this function today is the interface and the type-literal branch.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's own comment on the class-like branch is worth keeping: the
    ; computed-name restriction is technically about property declarations and
    ; accessors too, and it lands here because those two disallow computed names
    ; outright and accessors are refused in ambient contexts anyway.
    function check_grammar_method(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_function_like_declaration(node)
            return true
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
        {
            if pk = KindObjectLiteralExpression
            {
                ; We only disallow a modifier on a method declaration when it is a
                ; property of an object literal — and `async` alone is admitted, so
                ; the test is on the LIST rather than on the presence of modifiers.
                ; `modifiers != nil && !(len == 1 && Nodes[0] == async)` — and the
                ; test is on the LIST being PRESENT rather than on it being
                ; non-empty, which is the reference's own spelling. The parser
                ; leaves the slot null when there are no modifiers, so the two
                ; readings coincide on every tree it builds; the null test is
                ; transcribed because it is the one the reference makes.
                let modifiers AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
                if modifiers <> null
                {
                    var only_async false
                    if AstNode.list_count(modifiers) = 1
                    {
                        if AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.child_in_list(modifiers, 0)) = KindAsyncKeyword
                            set only_async: true
                    }
                    if only_async = false
                        return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagModifiers_cannot_appear_here)
                }
                let postfix AstNode.postfix_token_of(node)
                if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_question_mark(postfix, DiagAn_object_member_cannot_be_declared_optional)
                    return true
                if this.check_grammar_for_invalid_exclamation_token(postfix, DiagA_definite_assignment_assertion_is_not_permitted_in_this_context)
                    return true
                if AstNode.body_of(node) = null
                    return this.grammar_error_at_pos(AstNode.end_of(node) - 1, 1, DiagX_0_expected)
            }
            if this.check_grammar_for_generator(node)
                return true
        }
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                return this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_an_ambient_context_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
            if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            {
                if AstNode.body_of(node) = null
                    return this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_a_method_overload_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
            }
            return false
        }
        if pk = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_an_interface_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
        if pk = KindTypeLiteral
            return this.check_grammar_for_invalid_dynamic_name(name, DiagA_computed_property_name_in_a_type_literal_must_refer_to_an_expression_whose_type_is_a_literal_type_or_a_unique_symbol_type)
        false
    }

    ; ── slice 56: checkGrammarBindingElement ─────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Four reports, all of them about the REST element, and the whole function is
    ; behind `node.DotDotDotToken != nil` — so a binding element without a `...`
    ; runs the two tests and answers false, which is what the overwhelming
    ; majority of the corpus's binding elements do.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SECOND `if node.DotDotDotToken != nil` IS EQUIVALENT TO FOLDING IT
    ; INTO THE FIRST, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. The first draft of this comment
    ; claimed the opposite — that the two blocks must be kept apart or a report
    ; would move — and it is wrong: every path out of the first block that skips
    ; the second is a `return`, so the initializer test is reached in exactly the
    ; same states either way. The two are kept apart because that is the
    ; reference's shape and this is a transcription, NOT because behaviour depends
    ; on it, and control g8 is the row that says so with a number. ★It is worth
    ; the paragraph because §3.5cz's merge lesson makes the opposite reflex
    ; cheap — *a merge is sound only while what lies between is dead* — and here
    ; nothing lies between at all. **The lesson has a converse: a shape that must
    ; be transcribed for fidelity is not thereby a shape that carries a defect.**
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TRAILING-COMMA CHECK'S RESULT IS DISCARDED, exactly as upstream
    ; discards it, and it sits BETWEEN two returning reports — so `[...r,]` inside
    ; a pattern can collect TWO diagnostics (the comma and, if the rest element
    ; also carries a property name, TS2566). Reading the block as a chain would
    ; drop the second, which is checkGrammarParameterList's discarded result
    ; (slice 52) in the same position.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE INITIALIZER REPORT IS grammarErrorAtPos AND ITS SPAN IS THE `=`, one
    ; character at `initializer.Pos() - 1`. That is a span NO NODE has, which is
    ; the reason that reporter exists (§3.5cw) — and the reference's own comment
    ; says so at the line: *Error on equals token which immediately precedes the
    ; initializer*. ★★AND THE SUBTRACTION IS WHITESPACE-INDEPENDENT, which the
    ; first draft of this line got backwards: `Pos()` is the FULL start, i.e. the
    ; END of the previous token, and the previous token IS the `=`, so `Pos() - 1`
    ; is its last character however much space follows. That is what lets the
    ; reference do this arithmetic without a scan;
    ; checker_binding_element_rest_initializer.ts spaces one line out and packs
    ; another to show it.
    ;
    ; ★ `node.Parent.ElementList()` is the parent PATTERN's element list, read
    ; through elements_of, which answers for both binding-pattern kinds. A binding
    ; element's parent is always one of the two, so the null direction is
    ; unreachable rather than tolerated — and list_count/child_in_list answer 0 and
    ; null for it, which makes `node <> last` true and the first report fire, i.e.
    ; the loud direction.
    function check_grammar_binding_element(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let dot_dot_dot AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node)
        if dot_dot_dot <> null
        {
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
            let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
            var last: ref[AstNode]? null
            if n > 0
                set last: AstNode.child_in_list(elements, n - 1)
            if node <> last
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_rest_element_must_be_last_in_a_destructuring_pattern)
            this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(elements, DiagA_rest_parameter_or_binding_pattern_may_not_have_a_trailing_comma)
            if AstNode.property_name_of(node) <> null
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagA_rest_element_cannot_have_a_property_name)
        }
        if dot_dot_dot <> null
        {
            let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
            if initializer <> null
                return this.grammar_error_at_pos(AstNode.pos_of(initializer) - 1, 1, DiagA_rest_element_cannot_have_an_initializer)
        }
        false
    }

    ; ── slice 54: the variable DECLARATION's grammar ─────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarVariableDeclaration, whole and COMPLETE except for one term of
    ; checkAmbientInitializer's disjunction, named at its line. Five steps, and
    ; four of them are a `return` of a report, so the order below is the
    ; reference's rather than a convenience.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `__esModule` TERM IS REACHABLE, AND WHAT MAKES IT SO IS THE STUB
    ; PROGRAM'S ANSWER RATHER THAN THE REAL COMPILER'S — WHICH CORRECTS A CLAIM
    ; THIS FILE HAS CARRIED SINCE SLICE 49. Two places above say
    ; `program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile … the stub program does not have` / `does
    ; not answer`; it HAS it and it answers `core.ModuleKindNone`, i.e. **0**. The
    ; guard is `< core.ModuleKindSystem`, and System is **4**, so the term is
    ; TRUE for every file under this harness. Not merely reachable in principle —
    ; measured on the way in: `export var __esModule = 1` gets TS1216 out of the
    ; reference, and so does `export var [__esModule] = [1]`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE REAL COMPILER WOULD ANSWER THE OTHER WAY, which is why reading
    ; the reference's implementation instead of the ORACLE would have got this
    ; backwards. `GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile` there is `GetEmitModuleKind()` when
    ; the file's implied format is None — and with `Module` unset that is
    ; `GetEmitScriptTarget()` = ES2025 ⇒ **ModuleKindES2022 = 7**, and `7 < 4` is
    ; false. So this is a FIFTH reading of an unset option to set beside §3.5cw's
    ; three and §3.5cx's fourth, and it is a different KIND of reading: the value
    ; comes from the harness's stub, not from the option's default, and the two
    ; disagree. The rule that falls out of it: **an option's default is read from
    ; the constructor, but a PROGRAM's answer is read from the program the oracle
    ; builds** — tests/oracle/types.go, not internal/compiler/program.go.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CONJUNCTION IS NOT REORDERED and does not need to be. Because the
    ; first term is a constant true here it is written as the comment it is, with
    ; the value that makes it true, and the two terms that can actually vary are
    ; asked. A port that had reported unported on the program layer would have
    ; buried a live report behind a hole that is not there.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DEFINITE-ASSIGNMENT TERM IS THE ONE REPORT OF THIS FUNCTION THAT IS
    ; NOT ON THE DECLARATION. `grammarErrorOnNode(node.ExclamationToken, …)` points
    ; at the token, and error_range_for_node has no arm for a token kind, so the
    ; span is the token's own trivia-skipped start — which is what the reference's
    ; TS1263 at the `!` of `let b!: number = 1` measures.
    ;
    ; ★ The three-way message choice collapses to three CODES and no argument, so
    ; §3.5ct's argument applies unchanged: the dump is `C pos end code`.
    function check_grammar_variable_declaration(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let node_flags Binder.combined_node_flags(node)
        let block_scope_kind node_flags & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
        {
            if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_may_not_have_binding_patterns)
            if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_may_not_have_binding_patterns)
        }

        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var grandparent: ref[AstNode]? null
        if parent <> null
            set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        let gk Checker.kind_or_unknown(grandparent)
        var in_for_in_or_of false
        if gk = KindForInStatement
            set in_for_in_or_of: true
        if gk = KindForOfStatement
            set in_for_in_or_of: true

        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if in_for_in_or_of = false
        {
            if (node_flags & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            {
                ; The result is DISCARDED here, as it is upstream — the flow past
                ; this point does not depend on it — so an unported report inside
                ; does not stop the three checks below either.
                this.check_ambient_initializer(node)
            }
            else
            {
                if initializer = null
                {
                    if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
                    {
                        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(parent) = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_destructuring_declaration_must_have_an_initializer)
                    }
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                }
            }
        }

        let exclamation_token AstNode.exclamation_token_of(node)
        if exclamation_token <> null
        {
            var not_permitted false
            if gk <> KindVariableStatement
                set not_permitted: true
            if type_node = null
                set not_permitted: true
            if initializer <> null
                set not_permitted: true
            if (node_flags & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                set not_permitted: true
            if not_permitted
            {
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagDeclarations_with_initializers_cannot_also_have_definite_assignment_assertions)
                if type_node = null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagDeclarations_with_definite_assignment_assertions_must_also_have_type_annotations)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagA_definite_assignment_assertion_is_not_permitted_in_this_context)
            }
        }

        ; `c.program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile(sourceFile) < core.ModuleKindSystem`.
        ; The oracle's program answers ModuleKindNone = 0 and System is 4, so the
        ; term is a constant TRUE under this harness — see the header above, and
        ; note that it is the ORACLE's answer and not the real compiler's.
        if grandparent <> null
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(grandparent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(grandparent, ModifierFlagsExport)
                {
                    this.check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(name)
                }
            }
        }

        if block_scope_kind = 0
            return false
        this.check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(name)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForEsModuleMarkerInBindingName, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT RETURNS OUT OF THE LOOP ON THE FIRST ELEMENT THAT HAS A NAME, which
    ; is upstream's `return` inside the `for` and not a `continue` — so a pattern
    ; whose first named element is innocent hides a `__esModule` behind it. That is
    ; transcribed rather than tidied: it is the reference's answer, and the
    ; measurement that fixed the shape of this function is the same probe as the
    ; header's — `export var { __esModule: q } = …` gets NO report, because a
    ; binding element's NAME is `q` and the property name is not asked.
    function check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(this, name: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if name = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "__esModule", 10)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node_skipped_on_no_emit(name, DiagIdentifier_expected_esModule_is_reserved_as_an_exported_marker_when_transforming_ECMAScript_modules)
            return false
        }
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
        while i < n
        {
            let element AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
            set i: i + 1
            let en AstNode.name_of(element)
            if en <> null
                return this.check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(en)
        }
        false
    }

    ; grammarErrorOnNodeSkippedOnNoEmit, whole. It is grammar_error_on_node plus
    ; `d.SetSkippedOnNoEmit()`, a flag the emitter reads to drop the diagnostic
    ; when nothing is emitted.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FLAG IS NOT CARRIED, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A SHRUG.
    ; The oracle prints every line `c.GetDiagnostics` answers, and GetDiagnostics
    ; does not filter on it — so a skipped-on-no-emit diagnostic is in the C
    ; section like any other, which is how the reference's TS1216 was seen at all.
    ; The flag becomes observable on the day something in this repository EMITS,
    ; and until then a field for it would be a field nothing reads.
    function grammar_error_on_node_skipped_on_no_emit(this, n: ref[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
        this.grammar_error_on_node(n, code)

    ; checkGrammarNameInLetOrConstDeclarations, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT ALWAYS ANSWERS FALSE FOR A PATTERN and true only for a bare `let`, and
    ; the asymmetry is upstream's: the recursion's result is DISCARDED, so a
    ; pattern reports once per offending element and still answers false to its
    ; caller. That is the opposite discipline to the `__esModule` walk above, four
    ; lines away in the reference, and the two are deliberately not unified.
    ;
    ; ★ The text test is against the SPELLING `let`, not against the keyword's
    ; token kind: this position is an identifier by the time the parser is done
    ; with it (`let let = 1` parses), so what is left is a name comparison.
    function check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(this, name: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if name = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "let", 3)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(name, DiagX_let_is_not_allowed_to_be_used_as_a_name_in_let_or_const_declarations)
            return false
        }
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
        while i < n
        {
            let element AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
            set i: i + 1
            let en AstNode.name_of(element)
            if en <> null
                this.check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(en)
        }
        false
    }

    ; An identifier's stored text against a FIXED ASCII spelling. The reference
    ; writes `name.Text() == "__esModule"`; this port keeps an identifier's text as
    ; (buffer, length) rather than as a String, so the compare is a length test and
    ; a byte loop.
    ;
    ; ★ BYTES rather than runes, and it is exact: both spellings are ASCII, and the
    ; stored text is already the ESCAPE-DECODED value, so `let`
    ; arrives here as `let` and compares equal — which is the reference's answer
    ; too, since it compares the same decoded text.
    function identifier_text_equals(n: ref[AstNode], s: pointer[const_char], len: int) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(n) <> len
            return false
        let t AstNode.identifier_text_of(n)
        if t = null
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if (*(t + i) as int) <> ((*(s + i) as char) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; checkAmbientInitializer, whole for the ONE kind this slice reaches. The
    ; reference's switch has three arms (VariableDeclaration, PropertyDeclaration,
    ; PropertySignature) over two accessors each, and both accessors here answer for
    ; all three kinds already (`type_of`, `initializer_of`), so the switch collapses
    ; to nothing.
    ;
    ; ★ The `default: panic` goes with it, and the argument is about the CALLERS
    ; rather than the accessors: this function has exactly three call sites and each
    ; passes one of those three kinds, so the panic's population is empty. It is not
    ; that the accessors could not be handed a fourth kind — they would answer null
    ; and this would return false where the reference dies — it is that nothing
    ; hands them one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ONE TERM OF THE FIVE-WAY DISJUNCTION NEEDS THE TYPE MACHINERY, AND THE
    ; WHOLE CORPUS REACHES IT ONCE — IN ITS OWN FIXTURE.
    ; `isInitializerSimpleLiteralEnumReference` asks
    ; `checkExpressionCached(expr).flags & EnumLike` — but only for a PROPERTY
    ; ACCESS or an ELEMENT ACCESS, so for every other initializer shape the term is
    ; false without asking anything, and the disjunction is decided by the four
    ; kind tests around it. An ambient const initialized from a property access is
    ; rare enough that `is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference` is **1 unit
    ; of 17 863** on stage 2 and that unit is
    ; `checker_variable_ambient_initializer_enum_ref.d.ts` — the fixture IS the
    ; corpus here (§3.5cw's use-strict-prologue shape).
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS A REPORT AND NOT A `return false`, AND THE BATTERY IS WHY. Guessing
    ; *not an enum reference* agrees with the reference on every unit in reach —
    ; controls-slice54.sh's f13 makes that guess and comes back UNGATED — because no
    ; ambient const in the corpus is initialized from a real enum member. A guess
    ; that no instrument can distinguish from a port is the most expensive kind, so
    ; the term reports. Measured on the way in: `declare namespace P { const g =
    ; N.a }` gets TS1254 out of the reference, so the far side of the hole is real.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE HOLE IS ASKED IN THE PLACE THE ANSWER IS USED, not where the reference
    ; computes it. Upstream evaluates `isInvalidInitializer` eagerly and then throws
    ; it away in the else branch — where the report is TS1039 whatever the
    ; initializer is — so asking it only inside the const-and-untyped branch is the
    ; same answer with one fewer way to be wrong. What that costs is the
    ; checkExpressionCached CALL, which can report at its own sites; those are the
    ; expression dimension and are unported everywhere, so no line of ours moves.
    ;
    ; ★ `isConstOrReadonly` reads `readonly` through the COMBINED modifier flags, so
    ; a `readonly` the grammar has already refused on a variable statement still
    ; sets it — which is why the term is not simplified away for a kind that cannot
    ; carry the modifier legally.
    function check_ambient_initializer(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if initializer = null
            return false
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        var is_const_or_readonly false
        if this.is_declaration_readonly(node)
            set is_const_or_readonly: true
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            if this.is_var_const_like(node)
                set is_const_or_readonly: true
        }
        if is_const_or_readonly = false
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagInitializers_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
        if type_node <> null
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagInitializers_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)

        if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(initializer)
            return false
        if Checker.is_initializer_signed_numeric_literal(initializer)
            return false
        let ik AstNode.kind_of(initializer)
        if ik = KindTrueKeyword
            return false
        if ik = KindFalseKeyword
            return false
        if Checker.is_initializer_bigint_literal_expression(initializer)
            return false
        ; isInitializerSimpleLiteralEnumReference, the one term this port cannot
        ; answer — see the header. It is false for every initializer that is not one
        ; of these two kinds, so the report is asked here and nowhere wider.
        if ik = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference", ik)
            return false
        }
        if ik = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference", ik)
            return false
        }
        this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagA_const_initializer_in_an_ambient_context_must_be_a_string_or_numeric_literal_or_literal_enum_reference)
    }

    ; The second half of isInitializerStringOrNumberLiteralExpression: a PREFIX
    ; MINUS over a numeric literal. It is NOT ast.is_signed_numeric_literal, which
    ; admits a leading `+` as well — this one is minus-only, and a `+1` initializer
    ; in an ambient const IS refused by the reference.
    function is_initializer_signed_numeric_literal(expr: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) <> KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(expr) <> KindMinusToken
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(expr)) = KindNumericLiteral
    }

    ; isInitializerBigIntLiteralExpression, whole.
    function is_initializer_bigint_literal_expression(expr: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) = KindBigIntLiteral
            return true
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) <> KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(expr) <> KindMinusToken
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(expr)) = KindBigIntLiteral
    }

    ; isDeclarationReadonly, whole. `GetCombinedModifierFlags` is the binder's
    ; has_combined_modifier — the modifier twin of combined_node_flags, and the two
    ; sit together there because upstream has them as ONE generic over the flag
    ; getter, which Scaly cannot spell (the note at combined_node_flags says so).
    function is_declaration_readonly(this, declaration: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if b.has_combined_modifier(declaration, ModifierFlagsReadonly) = false
            return false
        b.is_parameter_property_declaration(declaration) = false
    }

    ; isVarConstLike, whole.
    function is_var_const_like(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let block_scope_kind Binder.combined_node_flags(node) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
            return true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
            return true
        block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
    }

    ; ── slice 53: the variable declaration list's grammar ────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList, whole. Five terms, and each is a
    ; `return` — so the first one that reports ends the function, which is what
    ; makes the order below the reference's rather than a convenience.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EMPTY-LIST REPORT IS UNREACHABLE FROM THIS SLICE'S ONLY CALLER, AND
    ; SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF WRITING IT. The check fires on a list that EXISTS
    ; and has no elements, and the parser produces exactly one such list —
    ; `parse_variable_declaration_list`'s missing-list branch, for `for (let of X)`
    ; — which reaches this function only through checkForInOrForOfStatement, an arm
    ; this slice does not port. Every other empty declarations list is the
    ; delimited-list parser answering nothing, which means parse_expected reported,
    ; which means has_parse_diagnostics is true and grammar_error_at_pos answers
    ; false. So the term is transcribed, and the two other callers upstream
    ; (checkForStatement and checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement) are what will
    ; exercise it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SPAN NEEDS THE LIST'S OWN RANGE, which is §3.5cv's side table again,
    ; and the missing list had no ROW until this slice. `createMissingList` is
    ; `parseEmptyNodeList` — `newNodeList(NewTextRange(p.nodePos(), p.nodePos()),
    ; nil)` — i.e. a zero-length range at the look-ahead position, and our missing
    ; branch built the Array without recording one. The row is added at the same
    ; point the reference computes it. Nothing in this port could see the
    ; difference before, which is why it had to be added by READING the reference
    ; rather than by a failing unit; the next slice is the first reader.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AWAIT-USING TAIL IS REACHED BY THE CORPUS — 10 units of stage 2's
    ; 17 855 carry the tag, which is more than the fixture and less than a slice —
    ; AND IT ANSWERS **TRUE**, and the direction
    ; is the argument. checkGrammarAwaitOrAwaitUsing is a chapter of its own — the
    ; module kind, the implied node format of the file, IsInTopLevelContext,
    ; IsEffectiveExternalModule and a related-info attachment — and its result
    ; decides whether checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement runs at
    ; all. Answering FALSE would run a check the reference may skip and could put a
    ; TS1156 where the reference has none, which is the one thing diagcheck can
    ; fail on. Answering true can only make our C section SHORTER, which the
    ; subsequence relation permits by construction. The unit is unported either
    ; way, so the choice costs nothing and removes a way to be wrong.
    function check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(this, declaration_list: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if declaration_list = null
            return false
        let declarations AstNode.variable_declarations_of(declaration_list)
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(declarations, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true

        if AstNode.list_count(declarations) = 0
        {
            if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
                return false
            var lpos 0
            var lend 0
            if this.list_range_of(declarations, &lpos, &lend) = false
            {
                this.record_unported("list-range", KindVariableDeclarationList)
                return false
            }
            return this.grammar_error_at_pos(lpos, lend - lpos, DiagVariable_declaration_list_cannot_be_empty)
        }

        let block_scope_flags AstNode.flags_of(declaration_list) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        var is_using false
        if block_scope_flags = NodeFlagsUsing
            set is_using: true
        var is_await_using false
        if block_scope_flags = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
            set is_await_using: true

        if is_using or is_await_using
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(declaration_list)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindForInStatement
            {
                if is_using
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_a_using_declaration)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_an_await_using_declaration)
            }
            if (AstNode.flags_of(declaration_list) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            {
                if is_using
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_await_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
            }
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindVariableStatement
            {
                let gk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
                var in_clause false
                if gk = KindCaseClause
                    set in_clause: true
                if gk = KindDefaultClause
                    set in_clause: true
                if in_clause
                {
                    if is_using
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_case_or_default_clauses_unless_contained_within_a_block)
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_await_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_case_or_default_clauses_unless_contained_within_a_block)
                }
            }
        }

        if is_await_using
        {
            this.record_unported("check-grammar-await-or-await-using", KindVariableDeclarationList)
            return true
        }

        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement, whole, and it is
    ; COMPLETE — nothing behind it is deferred, which is what lets the
    ; VariableStatement arm become the third of the switch's arms to run to its
    ; last line (the EmptyStatement and DebuggerStatement pair are the other two).
    ;
    ; ★★ IT ALWAYS ANSWERS FALSE. The reference's `return false` is not an
    ; oversight: this is the last term of checkVariableStatement's `&&` chain, its
    ; result is discarded, and the report it makes is a SEMANTIC error rather than a
    ; grammar one — `c.error`, not grammarErrorOnNode, so no parse guard. That is
    ; why it goes through error_on_node and why a file that failed to parse still
    ; gets a TS1156.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MESSAGE TAKES THE KEYWORD AS AN ARGUMENT AND THE FOUR ARMS COLLAPSE
    ; TO ONE CODE, which is check_grammar_modifiers' argument (§3.5ct) applied to a
    ; four-way switch: the dump is `C pos end code`, `let`/`const`/`using`/`await
    ; using` all produce TS1156, and a keyword string this port cannot print is a
    ; second thing to keep in step for no comparison. What is NOT dropped is
    ; upstream's `default: panic("Unknown BlockScope flag")` — the switch is over a
    ; masked value that is already known non-zero, so a fifth answer means the
    ; BlockScoped mask and the four constants have drifted apart, and the marker
    ; says so rather than reporting a diagnostic that looks right.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND THE MARKER IS UNREACHABLE FOR A REASON THAT IS WORTH WRITING DOWN,
    ; because it is not the usual *no corpus gets here*. The mask is three bits and
    ; the four constants cover 1, 2, 4 and 6 — so 3, 5 and 7 are the panic's
    ; population, and reaching one needs TWO of the bits set at once. The parser
    ; sets exactly one arm per list, and combined_node_flags ORs the list's flags
    ; with the STATEMENT's, which could add a bit if any OTHER flag used bits 0..2
    ; — and none does: NodeFlags.scaly's own header says those three positions are
    ; the variable-declaration aliases and nothing else. So this is a claim about
    ; the FLAG TABLE, and it expires if a flag is ever added at a low bit.
    ;
    ; ★ The combined flags come from the DECLARATION LIST, not from the statement —
    ; `let` sits on the list — and Binder.combined_node_flags walks up from there
    ; through the statement, OR-ing, which is why the same answer arrives whichever
    ; of the two the reference happens to hand it.
    function check_grammar_for_disallowed_block_scoped_variable_statement(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.container_allows_block_scoped_variable(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
            return false
        let list AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
        if list = null
            return false
        let block_scope_kind Binder.combined_node_flags(list) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        if block_scope_kind = 0
            return false
        var known false
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsLet
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
            set known: true
        if known = false
        {
            ; ★ The detail is the KIND and deliberately not the flag value, even
            ; though the flag is the interesting datum: tests/triage.py prints that
            ; column as a kind number, and NodeFlagsBlockScoped is 7, which is a
            ; real kind. A marker that reads as a wrong kind is worse than one that
            ; reads as a right one.
            this.record_unported("block-scope-flag-unknown", KindVariableStatement)
            return false
        }
        this.error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_can_only_be_declared_inside_a_block)
        false
    }

    ; containerAllowsBlockScopedVariable, whole. Seven kinds answer false — the
    ; six loop-and-branch statements whose body may be a single statement, plus
    ; `with` — and a LabeledStatement asks its own parent, which is the recursion
    ; upstream writes.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS ONE LINE IS WHAT SUPPRESSES TS1156 ON THE WHOLE CORPUS, and it is
    ; worth stating because the first draft of the control battery got it backwards.
    ; The caller RUNS on every variable statement — it is the last term of a chain
    ; slice 53 completes — so the report is not out of reach because the code is
    ; unexecuted; it is out of reach because every parent in reach (Block,
    ; ModuleBlock, SourceFile) falls through to the permissive answer.
    ; controls-slice53.sh's f10 was written expecting no change from making this
    ; answer false and came back RED 345; f11 gates the kind list itself. What no
    ; control can redden is the RECURSION: it would have to walk up to a refusing
    ; kind, and there is none within reach.
    ;
    ; ★ The parent is read null-safely for kind_or_unknown's reason: a
    ; VariableStatement at the top of a file has a SourceFile parent, but the
    ; recursion's own step can walk off the end of a label chain in a tree the
    ; parser built after an error, and KindUnknown falls out of every arm to the
    ; permissive answer the reference gives an unlisted kind.
    function container_allows_block_scoped_variable(this, parent: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var p parent
        while true
        {
            let k Checker.kind_or_unknown(p)
            if k = KindIfStatement
                return false
            if k = KindDoStatement
                return false
            if k = KindWhileStatement
                return false
            if k = KindWithStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForInStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForOfStatement
                return false
            if k = KindLabeledStatement
            {
                set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
                continue
            }
            return true
        }
        true
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarModifiers ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HISTOGRAM NAMED THIS ONE FUNCTION RATHER THAN ELEVEN ARMS. Slice
    ; 48's work list reads VariableStatement 3 940, ClassDeclaration 3 022,
    ; FunctionDeclaration 2 255, ImportDeclaration 1 601, InterfaceDeclaration
    ; 1 342, ModuleDeclaration 1 282, TypeAlias 699 on stage 2 — and
    ; checkGrammarModifiers is the first grammar check of every one of them, so
    ; nothing behind it can be reached without it. 356 lines of grammarchecks.go.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENTS ARE NOT COMPUTED, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER
    ; THAN A SHORTCUT. Sixteen of the reports below are one of three parameterised
    ; messages — `{0} modifier already seen`, `{0} modifier cannot be used with
    ; {1} modifier`, `{0} modifier must precede {1} modifier` — which upstream
    ; fills from visibilityToString and scanner.TokenToString. A message's CODE
    ; does not depend on its arguments, and the dump is `C pos end code`: two
    ; diagnostics agreeing on those three numbers print identically. That is the
    ; same argument DiagnosticList.sort_by_position makes for stopping its
    ; comparison at the code, and it is why visibilityToString and TokenToString
    ; are not ported here. ★It is also the one thing that would have to change if
    ; a later yardstick compared TEXT — named here so that slice finds the bill
    ; instead of discovering it as a diff.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO COMPILER OPTIONS DECIDE WHETHER PARTS OF THIS FUNCTION EXIST AT ALL,
    ; and both are read off the harness the way slice 48 read AllowUnreachableCode.
    ; `c.legacyDecorators` is `ExperimentalDecorators == core.TSTrue` and the
    ; oracle sets neither, so it is FALSE — which makes the accessor-pair decorator
    ; check unreachable and makes NodeCanBeDecorated's parameter arm answer false
    ; before it asks anything. `VerbatimModuleSyntax` is likewise unset, so the
    ; CommonJS top-level-export test is dead. Both are written at their place with
    ; a report behind the option rather than omitted, because an OMITTED step is
    ; invisible and a reported one is not. ★This paragraph used to add *it
    ; additionally needs program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, which the stub program
    ; does not have*, and slice 54 measured that claim false: the oracle's program
    ; answers ModuleKindNone for it, which is a VALUE and not a hole (§3.5cy).
    function check_grammar_modifiers(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return false
        ; ★★ THE GUARD BELOW ASKS WHETHER *THIS CALL* REPORTED, not whether the
        ; checker has ever reported, and the difference is a whole file. Since
        ; slice 49 the statement loop no longer stops at the first unported arm
        ; (check_source_elements says why), so every statement after the first
        ; arrives here with the flag ALREADY set — and a blanket `is_unported()`
        ; test would return before checking any of them, silently, leaving
        ; diagcheck measuring one statement per unit. Measured on
        ; checker_modifier_already_seen.ts: one line instead of four.
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        if this.report_obvious_decorator_errors(node)
            return true
        if this.report_obvious_modifier_errors(node)
            return true
        ; Either reporter may have met a construct this slice does not implement.
        ; Continuing would report the rest of the function's opinion about a node
        ; whose first question was never answered.
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return false

        if AstNode.is_this_parameter(node)
            return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagNeither_decorators_nor_modifiers_may_be_applied_to_this_parameters)

        ; blockScopeKind — read from the DECLARATION LIST, not from the statement:
        ; `using` and `await using` are flags of the list, and three arms below
        ; report on them.
        var block_scope_kind: int NodeFlagsNone
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableStatement
        {
            let dl AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
            if dl <> null
                set block_scope_kind: AstNode.flags_of(dl) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        }

        var last_static: ref[AstNode]? null
        var last_declare: ref[AstNode]? null
        var last_async: ref[AstNode]? null
        var last_override: ref[AstNode]? null
        var first_decorator: ref[AstNode]? null
        var flags: int ModifierFlagsNone
        var saw_export_before_decorators false

        ; The reference's comment, kept because the whole leading/trailing
        ; distinction below is unreadable without it: *We parse decorators and
        ; modifiers in four contiguous chunks: [...leadingDecorators,
        ; ...leadingModifiers, ...trailingDecorators, ...trailingModifiers]. It is
        ; an error to have both leading and trailing decorators.*
        var has_leading_decorators false

        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let modifier *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.is_decorator(modifier)
            {
                var grandparent: ref[AstNode]? null
                let dparent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
                if dparent <> null
                    set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(dparent)
                if this.node_can_be_decorated(node, dparent, grandparent) = false
                {
                    var overload false
                    if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindMethodDeclaration
                    {
                        if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(node))
                            set overload: true
                    }
                    if overload
                        return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_decorator_can_only_decorate_a_method_implementation_not_an_overload)
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
                }
                if legacy_decorators
                {
                    ; `else if c.legacyDecorators && (node.Kind == KindGetAccessor
                    ; || node.Kind == KindSetAccessor)`, whose body asks
                    ; GetAllAccessorDeclarationsForDeclaration — the symbol's
                    ; declaration list walked for the get/set PAIR — and reports
                    ; TS1207 on the second of them. Unreachable under this harness
                    ; (the option is unset, see the header), and reported rather
                    ; than dropped so that an options record arriving later turns
                    ; it into a port instead of into a silent wrong answer.
                    if Checker.is_accessor_kind(AstNode.kind_of(node))
                    {
                        this.record_unported("grammar-legacy-accessor-decorators", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                        return false
                    }
                }

                ; `flags&^(ExportDefault|Decorator) != 0` — *if we've seen any
                ; modifiers aside from `export`, `default`, or another decorator,
                ; then this is an invalid position*. Written as a SUBSET test
                ; because Scaly has no complement in this position and a
                ; complement constant would be a second thing to keep in step:
                ; the difference is non-empty exactly when flags is not a subset.
                if (flags & (ModifierFlagsExportDefault | ModifierFlagsDecorator)) <> flags
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)

                if has_leading_decorators
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsModifier) <> 0
                    {
                        ; The reference panics here if firstDecorator is nil — a
                        ; debug assertion, and it holds by construction:
                        ; has_leading_decorators is set only on the iteration that
                        ; also sets first_decorator.
                        ;
                        ; ★ Upstream spells the report out (hasParseDiagnostics,
                        ; c.error, AddRelatedInfo, return true / return false)
                        ; instead of calling grammarErrorOnNode, and the ONLY
                        ; difference is the related info — a second span attached
                        ; to the diagnostic, which ast.NewDiagnostic keeps on the
                        ; diagnostic rather than in the list, so it never reaches
                        ; a dump. What is left is grammarErrorOnNode exactly.
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagDecorators_may_not_appear_after_export_or_export_default_if_they_also_appear_before_export)
                    }
                }

                set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsDecorator

                ; if we have not yet seen a modifier, then these are leading
                ; decorators
                if (flags & ModifierFlagsModifier) = 0
                    set has_leading_decorators: true
                else
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                        set saw_export_before_decorators: true
                }

                if first_decorator = null
                    set first_decorator: modifier
            }
            else
            {
                let mk AstNode.kind_of(modifier)
                let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
                let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)

                ; Three placement tests that run before the switch, in the
                ; reference's order.
                if mk <> KindReadonlyKeyword
                {
                    if nk = KindPropertySignature
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_member)
                    if nk = KindMethodSignature
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_member)
                    if nk = KindIndexSignature
                    {
                        var index_ok false
                        if mk = KindStaticKeyword
                        {
                            if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                                set index_ok: true
                        }
                        if index_ok = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_index_signature)
                    }
                }
                var in_out_or_const false
                if mk = KindInKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if mk = KindOutKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if mk = KindConstKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if in_out_or_const = false
                {
                    if nk = KindTypeParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_parameter)
                }

                ; NodeFlagsReparsed on the MODIFIER: a modifier the JSDoc reparser
                ; synthesized carries no source order, so every *must precede*
                ; test is skipped for it. Read once, because eleven arms ask it.
                let reparsed (AstNode.flags_of(modifier) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0

                if mk = KindConstKeyword
                {
                    if nk <> KindEnumDeclaration
                    {
                        if nk <> KindTypeParameter
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_class_member_cannot_have_the_0_keyword)
                    }
                    if nk = KindTypeParameter
                    {
                        var host_ok false
                        if AstNode.is_function_like_declaration(parent)
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindFunctionType
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindConstructorType
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindCallSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindConstructSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindMethodSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if host_ok = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_type_parameter_of_a_function_method_or_class)
                    }
                }

                if mk = KindOverrideKeyword
                {
                    ; The reference's note: *if node.kind === Parameter,
                    ; checkParameter reports an error if it's not a parameter
                    ; property.*
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsOverride
                    set last_override: modifier
                }

                var visibility false
                if mk = KindPublicKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if mk = KindProtectedKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if mk = KindPrivateKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if visibility
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessibilityModifier) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAccessibility_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    ; ★ THE `else` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE ONE ARM OF THIS
                    ; FUNCTION THAT DOES NOT FLATTEN INTO INDEPENDENT TESTS.
                    ; Upstream's chain ends `else if flags&Abstract != 0 { if
                    ; private {…} else if !reparsed {…} } else if
                    ; IsPrivateIdentifierClassElementDeclaration(node) {…}` — so a
                    ; REPARSED non-private accessibility modifier beside `abstract`
                    ; falls out of the chain reporting NOTHING, and in particular
                    ; never reaches the private-identifier test. Every other arm
                    ; here is a run of returns and is equivalent flattened; this
                    ; one is not.
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                    {
                        if mk = KindPrivateKeyword
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if reparsed = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        if AstNode.is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(node)
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAn_accessibility_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | AstNode.modifier_to_flag(mk)
                }

                if mk = KindStaticKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsStatic
                    set last_static: modifier
                }

                if mk = KindAccessorKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if nk <> KindPropertyDeclaration
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_accessor_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_property_declaration)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAccessor
                }

                if mk = KindReadonlyKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    ; The reference's note again: *if node.kind === Parameter,
                    ; checkParameter reports an error if it's not a parameter
                    ; property* — which is why Parameter is admitted here.
                    var readonly_host false
                    if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindPropertySignature
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindIndexSignature
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if readonly_host = false
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_readonly_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_property_declaration_or_index_signature)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsReadonly
                }

                if mk = KindExportKeyword
                {
                    ; The VerbatimModuleSyntax arm. Unset under this harness (see
                    ; the header), which is the whole of the hole — the claim that
                    ; program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile is a second one was
                    ; measured false in slice 54 (§3.5cy).
                    if verbatim_module_syntax
                    {
                        this.record_unported("grammar-verbatim-module-syntax", nk)
                        return false
                    }
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                    {
                        if nk <> KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_class_elements_of_this_kind)
                    }
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsExport
                }

                if mk = KindDefaultKeyword
                {
                    var container parent
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindSourceFile
                        set container: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
                    var bad_container false
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindModuleDeclaration
                    {
                        if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(container) = false
                            set bad_container: true
                    }
                    if bad_container
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagA_default_export_can_only_be_used_in_an_ECMAScript_style_module)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) = 0
                    {
                        if reparsed = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if saw_export_before_decorators
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(first_decorator, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsDefault
                }

                if mk = KindDeclareKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                    {
                        if nk <> KindPropertyDeclaration
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_class_elements_of_this_kind)
                    }
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    if (Checker.flags_or_none(parent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    {
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagA_declare_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_already_ambient_context)
                    }
                    if AstNode.is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(node)
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAmbient
                    set last_declare: modifier
                }

                if mk = KindAbstractKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    var abstract_host false
                    if nk = KindClassDeclaration
                        set abstract_host: true
                    if nk = KindConstructorType
                        set abstract_host: true
                    if abstract_host = false
                    {
                        var member false
                        if nk = KindMethodDeclaration
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindGetAccessor
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindSetAccessor
                            set member: true
                        if member = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_abstract_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_class_method_or_property_declaration)
                        var in_abstract_class false
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
                        {
                            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(parent, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
                                set in_abstract_class: true
                        }
                        if in_abstract_class = false
                        {
                            if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAbstract_properties_can_only_appear_within_an_abstract_class)
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAbstract_methods_can_only_appear_within_an_abstract_class)
                        }
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsPrivate) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        {
                            if last_async <> null
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_async, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        }
                        if reparsed = false
                        {
                            if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        }
                    }
                    if AstNode.is_private_identifier(AstNode.name_of(node))
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAbstract
                }

                if mk = KindAsyncKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    var ambient false
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        set ambient: true
                    if (Checker.flags_or_none(parent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        set ambient: true
                    if ambient
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAsync
                    set last_async: modifier
                }

                var in_or_out false
                if mk = KindInKeyword
                    set in_or_out: true
                if mk = KindOutKeyword
                    set in_or_out: true
                if in_or_out
                {
                    var in_out_flag: int ModifierFlagsOut
                    if mk = KindInKeyword
                        set in_out_flag: ModifierFlagsIn
                    ; `node.Kind != TypeParameter || parent != nil && !(…)` — the
                    ; second half is guarded on a non-nil parent, so a type
                    ; parameter with no parent passes.
                    var wrong_host false
                    if nk <> KindTypeParameter
                        set wrong_host: true
                    if parent <> null
                    {
                        var variance_host false
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                            set variance_host: true
                        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                            set variance_host: true
                        if AstNode.is_type_or_js_type_alias_declaration(parent)
                            set variance_host: true
                        if variance_host = false
                            set wrong_host: true
                    }
                    if wrong_host
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_type_parameter_of_a_class_interface_or_type_alias)
                    if (flags & in_out_flag) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (in_out_flag & ModifierFlagsIn) <> 0
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOut) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | in_out_flag
                }
            }
        }

        let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if nk = KindConstructor
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_static, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_override, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_async, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            return false
        }
        var import_kind false
        if nk = KindImportDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if nk = KindJSImportDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if nk = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if import_kind
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_declare, DiagA_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_an_import_declaration)
        }
        if nk = KindParameter
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsParameterPropertyModifier) <> 0
            {
                if Binder.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(node))
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_property_may_not_be_declared_using_a_binding_pattern)
                if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node) <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_property_cannot_be_declared_using_a_rest_parameter)
            }
        }
        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
            return this.check_grammar_async_modifier(node, last_async)
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarAsyncModifier, whole. Four kinds may carry `async`; everything
    ; else that reached here reports at the modifier.
    function check_grammar_async_modifier(this, node: ref[AstNode], async_modifier: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return false
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(async_modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_here)
    }

    ; reportObviousDecoratorErrors / findFirstIllegalDecorator. The pair is the
    ; DECORATOR half of the two questions ast.can_have_illegal_decorators exists
    ; to answer, and the port already carries the predicate (slice 24 wrote it for
    ; checkJSDecoratorSyntax): a node on that list may never carry a decorator, so
    ; the FIRST one it carries is reported wherever it sits.
    function report_obvious_decorator_errors(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let decorator Checker.find_first_illegal_decorator(node)
        if decorator = null
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(decorator, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
    }

    function find_first_illegal_decorator(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if AstNode.can_have_illegal_decorators(node) = false
            return null
        Checker.find_first_decorator(node)
    }

    ; core.Find(node.ModifierNodes(), ast.IsDecorator) and its twin for
    ; ast.IsModifier. Two functions rather than one with a predicate parameter:
    ; Scaly's only escaping-closure form is `return \x: …` (§3.9e), and the port's
    ; standing answer to a callback argument is one function per call shape.
    function find_first_decorator(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return null
        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let m *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            if AstNode.is_decorator(m)
                return m
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    function find_first_modifier(node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return null
        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let m *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            if Parser.is_modifier_kind(AstNode.kind_of(m))
                return m
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; reportObviousModifierErrors / findFirstIllegalModifier / findFirstModifierExcept.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THIRD GROUP OF findFirstIllegalModifier IS A `panic`, AND IT IS
    ; PORTED AS A REPORT. Upstream ends its inner switch with *Unhandled case in
    ; findFirstIllegalModifier* — reachable only for a node kind that carries
    ; modifiers, sits inside neither a module block nor a source file, and is none
    ; of the eight the switch names. None of the five kinds this slice's arms
    ; reach can be that node, so the arm is a guard for the next slice rather than
    ; a live path; a report is what keeps it from becoming a silent `nil`.
    function report_obvious_modifier_errors(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let modifier this.find_first_illegal_modifier(node)
        if modifier = null
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(modifier, DiagModifiers_cannot_appear_here)
    }

    function find_first_modifier_except(node: ref[AstNode], allowed_modifier: int) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let modifier Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if modifier = null
            return null
        if AstNode.kind_of(modifier) = allowed_modifier
            return null
        modifier
    }

    function find_first_illegal_modifier(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        ; Group one: the kinds whose modifiers are all legal here — the accessors,
        ; the members, the module and import/export elements, the two function
        ; expressions, a parameter, a type parameter and a JS type alias.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_illegal_modifier(k)
            return null
        ; Group two: the kinds that may carry NO modifier at all.
        if Checker.kind_admits_no_modifier(k)
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        ; The default group. A declaration at the top level of a file or of a
        ; namespace body is exempt outright; everything else is judged per kind.
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
            return null
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
            return null
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAsyncKeyword)
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAbstractKeyword)
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAbstractKeyword)
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindVariableStatement
        {
            let dl AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
            if dl <> null
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(dl) & NodeFlagsUsing) <> 0
                    return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAwaitKeyword)
            }
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        }
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindConstKeyword)
        this.record_unported("find-first-illegal-modifier", k)
        null
    }

    function kind_has_no_illegal_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    function kind_admits_no_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMissingDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.NodeCanBeDecorated. It lives here rather than in ast.scaly because two
    ; of its five arms ask HasAbstractModifier / HasAmbientModifier, i.e.
    ; Binder.has_syntactic_modifier — a method on the binder, since a node's
    ; modifier flags are computed rather than cached in this port. The checker is
    ; the object that holds a binder.
    function node_can_be_decorated(this, node: ref[AstNode], parent: ref[AstNode]?, grandparent: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        ; *private names cannot be used with decorators yet* — legacy only, and
        ; the option is unset here (see check_grammar_modifiers' header).
        if legacy_decorators
        {
            if AstNode.is_private_identifier(AstNode.name_of(node))
                return false
        }
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        ; class declarations are valid targets
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        ; class expressions are valid targets for native decorators
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return legacy_decorators = false
        ; property declarations are valid if their parent is a class declaration
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            if parent = null
                return false
            if legacy_decorators
                return Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
            if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent) = false
                return false
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
                return false
            return b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAmbient) = false
        }
        ; if this method has a body and its parent is a class declaration, this is
        ; a valid target
        var method_like false
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            set method_like: true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            set method_like: true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set method_like: true
        if method_like
        {
            if parent = null
                return false
            if AstNode.body_of(node) = null
                return false
            if legacy_decorators
                return Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
            return Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
        }
        if k = KindParameter
        {
            ; The reference's TODO: *ParameterDeclaration decorator support for ES
            ; decorators must wait until it is standardized.* So the whole arm is
            ; legacy-only, and under this harness it answers here.
            if legacy_decorators = false
                return false
            ; What is left needs ast.GetThisParameter — the parent's first
            ; parameter, tested for being `this` — and the parent's body. Reported
            ; rather than written, for the reason the accessor-pair branch above
            ; is: unreachable while the option is unset, and a report is what turns
            ; the option's arrival into a port.
            this.record_unported("node-can-be-decorated-parameter", k)
            return false
        }
        false
    }
    ; ── the grammar check the file itself gets ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarSourceFile, whole:
    ;
    ;   return node.Flags&ast.NodeFlagsAmbient != 0 &&
    ;       c.checkGrammarTopLevelElementsForRequiredDeclareModifier(node)
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 47 DEFERRED THE SECOND HALF ON ONE PREDICATE AND SAID SO, AND
    ; THIS IS THAT BILL PAID. `ast.IsDeclarationNode` is `node.DeclarationData()
    ; != nil` — a property of the node LAYOUT, not a list anyone can read off a
    ; switch — so its note read *a hand-written kind list here would be a silent
    ; wrong answer in both directions: a missing kind drops a TS1046 the reference
    ; reports, an extra one invents a diagnostic where a plain statement stands*.
    ; It is now DERIVED (DeclarationKinds.scaly, generated by closing the
    ; reference's struct-embedding graph over DeclarationBase and verified against
    ; the reference's own DeclarationData()), and the derivation is what shows how
    ; right the deferral was: seven of the fifty-six kinds are ones no reader would
    ; have guessed, BinaryExpression and ObjectLiteralExpression among them.
    function check_grammar_source_file(this) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(file) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return false
        this.check_grammar_top_level_elements_for_required_declare_modifier()
    }

    ; ★ The OR is the reference's own, and it is the proof that a VariableStatement
    ; is NOT a declaration node: upstream writes `ast.IsDeclarationNode(decl) ||
    ; decl.Kind == ast.KindVariableStatement`, which would be dead text if the
    ; predicate already admitted it. The derived table agrees — 244 is absent from
    ; it — so the two readings of the reference confirm each other.
    function check_grammar_top_level_elements_for_required_declare_modifier(this) returns bool
    {
        let stmts AstNode.statements_of(file)
        let n AstNode.list_count(stmts)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let decl AstNode.child_in_list(stmts, i)
            var considered AstNode.is_declaration_node(decl)
            if AstNode.kind_of(decl) = KindVariableStatement
                set considered: true
            if considered
            {
                if this.check_grammar_top_level_element_for_required_declare_modifier(decl)
                    return true
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarTopLevelElementForRequiredDeclareModifier. The reference's own
    ; comment on the grammar it enforces is worth keeping, because the exempt list
    ; below is not derivable from anything else:
    ;
    ;   A declare modifier is required for any top level .d.ts declaration except
    ;   export=, export default, export as namespace, interfaces and imports:
    ;
    ;     DeclarationElement:
    ;        ExportAssignment
    ;        export_opt   InterfaceDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   TypeAliasDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ExternalImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   AmbientDeclaration
    function check_grammar_top_level_element_for_required_declare_modifier(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.kind_is_exempt_from_declare_modifier(AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return false
        var exempting: int ModifierFlagsAmbient | ModifierFlagsExport | ModifierFlagsDefault
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, exempting)
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagTop_level_declarations_in_d_ts_files_must_start_with_either_a_declare_or_export_modifier)
    }

    ; The kind half of the condition above, as a function for the reason
    ; ast.scaly's is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node is one.
    function kind_is_exempt_from_declare_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The whole body of the EmptyStatement and DebuggerStatement arms and the first
    ; line of the ExpressionStatement one, which is why a slice aimed at the
    ; statement histogram starts here.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONCE-BIT IS THE POINT OF THE FUNCTION, not bookkeeping around it. The
    ; reference's own comment: *If we are in a block, we only want to really report
    ; an error once to prevent noisiness* — so the bit lives on the BLOCK for the
    ; second diagnostic and on the NODE for the first, and a port that dropped it
    ; would emit one TS1036 per statement of an ambient block instead of one per
    ; block. ★And the bit is set to the RESULT of the report, not unconditionally:
    ; when grammarErrorOnFirstToken is suppressed by a parse diagnostic, nothing is
    ; marked and the next statement asks again — which is the same answer, since
    ; the suppression is a property of the file rather than of the statement.
    ;
    ; ★ The else branch of the reference is an assertion it has commented out
    ; ("we must be parented by a statement ... our parent will have already done
    ; it"), so there is nothing to port there and the function falls through.
    function check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return false
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if this.has_reported_ambient(node) = false
        {
            var in_signature AstNode.is_function_like(parent)
            if Checker.is_accessor_kind(AstNode.kind_of(parent))
                set in_signature: true
            if in_signature
            {
                let reported this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_implementation_cannot_be_declared_in_ambient_contexts)
                if reported
                    this.mark_reported_ambient(node)
                return reported
            }
        }
        var in_block false
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(parent)
        if pk = KindBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_block: true
        if in_block
        {
            if this.has_reported_ambient(parent) = false
            {
                let reported this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagStatements_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
                if reported
                    this.mark_reported_ambient(parent)
                return reported
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsAccessor, which is two kinds.
    function is_accessor_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        false
    }

    function has_reported_ambient(this, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let len reported_ambient.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if *(reported_ambient.get_buffer() + i) = n
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    procedure mark_reported_ambient(this, n: ref[AstNode])
        reported_ambient.add(n)

    ; ── the diagnostics, as GetDiagnosticsForFile answers them ──────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE ONE THING THIS SLICE OWES THE NEXT ONE IS A SORT IT DOES NOT
    ; WRITE. GetDiagnosticsForFile sorts the collection before answering, so a port
    ; that emitted in DISCOVERY order would differ from the reference as a wrong
    ; SPAN on every line after the first — a difference that reads as a checker
    ; defect and is a missing sort. (tests/checktypes.py checks that invariant on
    ; the oracle's side for exactly this reason, and slice 46's c2 proves it can
    ; fail: 213 findings.) It is NOT written here because no arm of this slice
    ; produces a diagnostic — the list is empty in every unit — and a sort with
    ; nothing to sort is machinery no instrument can reach, which is the shape
    ; §3.5be argues against. The FIRST arm that reports owes it, and what it owes
    ; is written out so it is a transcription rather than a rediscovery:
    ;
    ;   CompareDiagnostics is path, then pos, then end, then code, then the message
    ;   ARGUMENTS and the message chain, and the sort is STABLE. The path is one
    ;   file here. The arguments cannot be compared — this port carries no message
    ;   text — and they cannot MATTER either: two diagnostics agreeing on pos, end
    ;   and code are the same three numbers in the dump, so any order between them
    ;   prints identically. That is the argument for stopping at the code, and it
    ;   is an argument rather than an approximation.
    function diagnostic_count(c: ref[Checker]) returns int
        c.diagnostics.length()

    function diagnostic_at(c: ref[Checker], i: int, out_pos: pointer[int], out_end: pointer[int], out_code: pointer[int]) returns bool
        c.diagnostics.at(i, out_pos, out_end, out_code)
}
